{{alliance:Alliance Alliance}}

## Legacy
Alliance Alliance existed for barely a month, but its split ended the Coalition and provoked the merger that created the Federation, the alliance that dominated the rest of 2018 (the [[federation-era|Federation era]]). One participant, Slayne, later tied the Federation's creation directly to it: "I created Fed because I (along with some others) created Double AA."{{cite:thread-237070-p03|p3 #45, 14 Nov 2018}} Two of its seven guilds — Paladins United and Fantasy — were inside the Federation before the month was out.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 17, composed 30 Aug 2018}}

Alliance government in this period was written down, argued over and enforced, and it was documented far more thoroughly than the wars it was meant to regulate. The [[emperium-of-wynn|Emperium of Wynn]] had a five-tier constitution before it had a campaign{{cite:gdoc-1u97hsmpzu2lapij|"The Emperial Truth", introduction}}; the [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]] alliance pooled its members' foreign policy in a council acting by unanimity{{cite:thread-89529|post #1, opening post (thread opened 10 Sep 2015)}}; and the war blocs of 2018 ran admissions, expulsions, territory maps and rule changes through the same mechanism.

## Voting

Decisions were taken by formal vote and the same mechanism handled admissions, expulsions, territory maps and constitutional changes alike.{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|voting practice, 2016–2019}} One vote per member guild leader was the principle, and the counts do not always bear it out: the [[coalition|Coalition]] recorded seventeen votes on one admission in January 2018 and eighteen on one refusal, against fifteen member guilds on its own card of 11 January and fourteen by February.{{cite:coalition-voting-record-january-2018-discord-private|the recorded totals, 15 Jan – 8 Feb 2018}}{{cite:coalition-standing-documents-january-2018-discord-private|the card as posted, 11 Jan 2018}} Ten of its results were written down between 15 January and 8 February, and they are set out at [[coalition-voting|voting in the Coalition]]. The Coalition was running admission votes regularly by January 2018: one admission passed by a wide majority, a readmission carried roughly ten to three, and a bid was rejected roughly six to three on grounds of inactivity, failure to hold territory, and objections to a particular member.{{cite:coalition-founding-2017-discord-private|admission votes, January 2018}}

Both alliances admitted guilds faster than they could write them down. On 12 February 2018, the day the [[coalition|Coalition]] admitted [[emorians|Emorians]], another member took a territory off the new guild because it was not yet on the shared roster; on 27 February the [[the-federation|Federation]] made the same mistake against [[illustratus|Illustratus]], attacking a member because its own membership list was out of date.{{cite:emorians-guild-profile-discord-private|12 Feb 2018}}{{cite:federation-first-counterattack-2018-discord-private|27 Feb 2018}} Fifteen days apart, in two alliances, the roster was the last thing to learn who belonged.

Thresholds were themselves contested. [[the-federation|The Federation]] raised its voting threshold from a simple majority to two-thirds on 19–20 February 2018, immediately after an admission vote was closed and announced at nine votes in favour and another guild leader publicly disputed that it should have carried.{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|threshold change, 19–20 Feb 2018}} Six weeks later that threshold was the alliance's central political grievance: [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]]'s leader argued it handed permanent control to the bloc of guilds that had arrived together from the Coalition, pressed to change it, and the argument escalated into the longest war the alliance fought.{{cite:buildcraftia-war-2018-discord-private|Federation leadership channels, March 2018}} The rule adopted to settle one procedural quarrel produced a larger one.

Procedure could also be used against the alliance that wrote it. The Federation's vote to expel BuildCraftia carried an opt-out for member guilds unwilling to go to war against its leader personally, and the guild withdrew before the count concluded, so that the record would show a departure rather than a removal.{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|expulsion vote provisions, March 2018}}

That motion was counted twice and the two counts nearly agree. The contemporaneous one is the better figure: eight minutes after the motion opened, the leader of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] reported ten abstentions, ten in favour and one against, called the single no a placeholder, and noted that one guild had not voted at all.{{cite:federation-buildcraftia-expulsion-march-2018-discord-private|the count reported eight minutes in, 30 Mar 2018}} The exported tally is a later measurement — twenty-five reactions from twenty-two accounts, thirteen for, one against and eleven abstaining, which becomes twelve for expulsion, none against and ten abstaining once the account sitting on all three options comes out. A second account sits on two of the three, expel and abstain; taking that one out as well would give eleven, none and nine. An account on every option is a placeholder holding the emoji for others, which is what a participant said it was, and an account on two is not the same thing.{{cite:federation-buildcraftia-expulsion-march-2018-discord-private|exported and corrected tallies, motion of 30 Mar 2018}} The threshold stated on the motion was two thirds of the guilds not abstaining, so ten abstentions, close to half the alliance, lowered what expulsion needed rather than obstructing it.{{cite:federation-buildcraftia-expulsion-march-2018-discord-private|the abstention arithmetic worked through before the declaration, 30 Mar 2018}}

Somebody using a ballot put the same objection at the time. On 6 August 2018 a member of [[the-divine-swords|The Divine Swords]], looking back at a leadership vote of his own guild's, checked the voting channel and found he had apparently voted for the candidate he did not remember supporting.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|6 Aug 2018, the mark found}} He allowed that he might have added the mark afterwards, and the losing candidate answered that it had definitely been afterwards.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|6 Aug 2018, the mark added afterwards}} Another voter said he had simply marked whichever option was already ahead.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|6 Aug 2018, following the lead}} Both describe a mark placed for a reason other than preference, and one of them a mark placed after the count.

A vote that failed could simply be put again. A Federation ballot on forming a pact with Minerva closed on the evening of 26 September 2018, and two leaders reported afterwards that it had not passed.{{cite:federation-admissions-2018-discord-private|26 Sep 2018, the vote fails}} Shortly before the close the alliance's territory coordinator had said it looked clear.{{cite:federation-admissions-2018-discord-private|26 Sep 2018, looked clear}} The question was put a second time, and on 1 October the same coordinator reported that it was highly likely to pass though very close, and about two hours later that it had.{{cite:federation-admissions-2018-discord-private|1 Oct 2018, the second vote}} The alliance announced a treaty with the guild that day, and gave its terms by reference rather than setting them out, saying only that the guild had qualified for a treaty similar to the one [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] had had before.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|10/1/2018}} That was the arrangement of 8 September 2018, under which Titans Valor attacked the Confederation and Federation guilds left its ground alone except when reclaiming their own assigned territory.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the motion}} What changed between the two Minerva votes was never stated. [[terra|Terra]] re-ran a lost admission vote the following year, and there the reason was stated: the land terms had been altered.{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|re-run admission vote, September 2019}}

## Rules written after the event

Almost nothing in these constitutions was drafted in the abstract. A three-minute anti-sniping rule existed because one warring member was reliably slow to defend his own ground; a ban on alt guilds and multi-account warring, with a grace period, followed the exposure of two such guilds; and written alliance rules were first proposed after a dispute over one member's conduct.{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|origins of alliance rules, 2018}} At the end of January 2018 the Coalition adopted a ten-minute reclaim window — a guild had ten minutes to retake its own lost territory before allies could take it, and had to wait ten minutes before attacking ground just recovered from an enemy.{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|Coalition reclaim rule, late January 2018}} Some participants later blamed that rule for the alliance's collapse a fortnight afterwards, though the contemporaneous accounts of the walkout give the territory redistribution as its cause.{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|later accounts of the Coalition collapse}}{{cite:coalition-fracture-2018-discord-private|Coalition general channel, 16 Feb 2018}} Reading a rulebook of this period backwards gives a reasonably complete list of what had recently gone wrong.

The Federation went on to legislate against private diplomacy. On 1 July 2018, after a secret non-aggression pact between a member guild and an enemy guild was exposed, its guild leaders voted to prohibit member guilds from holding war-related pacts or alliances with any guild outside the alliance, while expressly allowing community-only ones; two days later the ban was written into a shared document of alliance rules alongside a prohibition on leaking war information.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Federation guild-leader vote, 1–3 Jul 2018}}

The rule was restated at the end of August and passed over twelve days after that. Late on 27 August 2018 a member asked the leadership channel whether agreements with the opposing alliance were permitted, and the alliance's founder said they were not; asked whether a ceasefire counted as well, he answered that it was the same thing.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|27 Aug 2018, the question and the answer}} A member explained the reasoning to a leader who asked what was meant: such an arrangement leaves the enemy free to strike part of the alliance while the rest is protected.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|27 Aug 2018, the reasoning}} He added that he was sure Federation guilds had made such deals before, and that it had annoyed the guilds left exposed.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|27 Aug 2018, the earlier deals}} On 8 September the alliance concluded an arrangement of exactly that shape at its own level, where the rule on member guilds did not reach.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the motion}} Nobody in either conversation referred to the other; setting them together is this article's.

## Territory

The central policy question for every alliance was whether territory should be assigned to member guilds or left open to whoever claimed it first.{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|assignment debates, 2018}} Assignment reduced friendly fire and hoarding, and it also put a committee in charge of who prospered. The Coalition moved in three steps over five weeks — an assignment scheme for one province in January 2018, a vote in early February to assign a second province that had been open to first claim, and a larger redistribution across two regions a week after that — and the third precipitated its collapse.{{cite:coalition-founding-2017-discord-private|territory policy, January–February 2018}}

Its successor adopted the same mechanism within days. The Federation rolled out a formal territory-assignment system on 21 February 2018{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018}}, and it produced friction immediately: guild leaders objected that the map had been drawn without consultation, a ratifying vote was called in response, and that vote was accidentally ended partway through by the leader running it.{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|territory assignment, February 2018}} Allocation was tied to contribution, with the most valuable ground going to the guilds carrying the fighting — the arrangement the guilds that walked out of the Coalition said had been rigged against them.{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|allocation and contribution, 2018}}

Alliances also legislated against their own members' weakness. The Federation operated a standing rule making the assigned territory of any guild holding fewer than thirty territories available for allied guilds to attack, which meant a member losing a war became a target for its own side.{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|the thirty-territory rule, 2018}}

## Writing the procedure afterwards

[[terra|Terra]] settled the largest war of 2019 by polling itself in a channel. Asked between 8 and 10 September whether they were voting on which beaten guilds could keep land, a member posted his own allocation guild by guild and others added to it underneath; no procedure governed it.{{cite:terra-terms-of-surrender-2019-discord-private|8–10 Sep 2019}} A fortnight later the same alliance produced a written claims process.{{cite:terra-institutions-2019-discord-private|September 2019}} It was built by the people who had just done it the other way.

## A territory loan

On 15 September 2019 a member guild's leader lent territories he held to a guild outside the alliance and put an expiry on it: eight in the morning GMT on 22 September.{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|15 Sep 2019}} Asked the next day why only a week, he said it was loaned land.{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|16 Sep 2019}} Nothing else in this record works that way — territory was granted, allocated, claimed or taken, and this is the one occasion it was lent, with an hour on it.

It doubled as probation. A member observed that the first redrawing of the map was twelve days away and that if the borrower kept helping it would deserve permanent claims by then.{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|15 Sep 2019}} On 22 September, the morning the loan expired, the alliance opened a vote on admitting the borrower; nobody at the time connected the two, and how the vote went was not written down.{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|22 Sep 2019}}

The [[terra|Terra]] treaty with [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] of 17 September forbade each side to send members into subguilds or foreign guilds to attack the other's claims — the first time the standard evasion was closed in writing.{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|17 Sep 2019}} Four days later the same idea arose inside the alliance, when a member said he would attack a neutral guild under a different tag rather than his own. Leaders told him it would put him at war with the Foxes, that the neutral would find out, and that he was trying to get around the agreement.{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|21 Sep 2019}}

## Membership

Membership was conditional and negotiated. Guilds were admitted on terms — sharing a named territory, repairing relations with a specific member, accepting observers, or submitting to a period of council oversight — and readmission after a departure did not restore a guild's former holdings.{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|admission terms, 2016–2019}} The [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]] admitted a guild in November 2018 on written ground rules covering leaking, respect for the alliance's existing pacts and an obligation to defend the Canyon on request{{cite:aesir-pact-2018-discord-private|admission conditions, 17 Nov 2018}}, and operated a follower status below full membership for guilds it had not yet decided to admit.{{cite:aesir-pact-2018-discord-private|follower status, 12–21 Nov 2018}} [[the-consortium|The Consortium]] created a comparable rank, Distant Ally, on the day it broke up.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|Distant Ally vote, 2 Apr 2019}}

Guild-level government ran on the same pattern. The [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] legislated an inactivity policy giving ten days' grace before removal and a rank system with explicit experience and donation thresholds for each grade, then applied both to its existing roster.{{cite:kingdom-of-foxes-government-2016-discord-private|membership legislation, December 2016 – January 2017}} The Emperium of Wynn made a High Council seat require a written application rather than appointment by a member guild's leader, a change made after the council had been suspended for inactivity.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|High Council restructure, September 2017}}

## What expelling a guild was thought to cost

[[the-federation|The Federation]] had a written test by April 2018. A motion of the 4th asked its leaders whether to expel the guilds that had not claimed or attempted to claim the territory assigned to them, or that had been assigned none because they had shown they could neither claim nor defend it, and it named four.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|4 Apr 2018, the motion}} The four went. Five months later, arguing over a fifth, the alliance used none of it: what it had instead was an argument about the price of removing a guild, and it went against the man making it.

Membership and territory had come apart months before. Landlessness was a standing a guild could ask for: one member told the leaders on 4 April 2018 that it was content to hold nothing, [[emorians|Emorians]] was admitted by vote on the 11th having asked not to be assigned any territory until the next allocation, and on the 22nd a guild rebuilt from another's remains asked not to be given any until it could defend them.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|4–22 Apr 2018}} On 10 September 2018 the Federation's territory coordinator asked the leadership channel whether he should be assigning any land to White Lotus at all, said he had not been, and put it to the guild directly whether a revival was expected or whether it should be left suspended.{{cite:federation-inactive-members-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018}} A guild could keep its seat, its roles and its place on the roster while being left off the map entirely — and the arrangement was asked for rather than imposed.

The expulsion vote eight days later turned on a calculation. A leader said he was voting against expulsion purely to avoid the pattern in which the alliance removed a guild and the guild promptly became active and attacked it; that had happened too many times, and he did not enjoy taking chances.{{cite:federation-inactive-members-2018-discord-private|18 Sep 2018}} Another supplied the condition that made it arithmetic rather than superstition: it had happened with the others because those guilds already had a fighting force, and this one had never had anybody but its leader.{{cite:federation-inactive-members-2018-discord-private|18 Sep 2018}}

A middle course was floated and abandoned: expel the guild but grant it a truce, so its leader could return when he had time. The guild's own representative asked what the point of expelling it would then be, and nobody answered him.{{cite:federation-inactive-members-2018-discord-private|18 Sep 2018}}

The vote passed and the guild was expelled. Asked in the general channel on 27 September why it had been kicked, and pressing for a real answer after a joke, a member was told that it was inactive, did nothing, was taking up space and was dead weight.{{cite:federation-inactive-members-2018-discord-private|27 Sep 2018}}

Two months later White Lotus attacked the alliance's successors. Members of the Aesir bloc recorded on 22 November that it had gone for the Molten Heights and lost, one of them supposing it wanted the Light Realm because it had once held it; on the 26th somebody called for help because it was trying to take their Light Realm assignment.{{cite:federation-inactive-members-2018-discord-private|22–26 Nov 2018}} Nobody at the time connected those attacks to the September vote, and a member explained them on other grounds — that the guild was going after ground it used to own.

## Self-imposed limits

Several of these mechanisms worked against the alliances that adopted them. [[war-syndicate|War Syndicate]] observed a self-imposed rule against attacking the province of Wynn, and the Coalition took the entire Wynn mainland by walking through the gap it left.{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|the Wynn prohibition}}{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 14, composed 22 Mar 2018}} Dominant alliances also debated capping their own success: Coalition leaders discussed deliberately limiting how much of the map the alliance held, measuring themselves against the alliance they had just displaced.{{cite:coalition-founding-2017-discord-private|leadership discussion, early 2018}}

## Whether a guild could vote twice

The question of who held a vote was raised across four years and never settled. In 2015 a leader of [[ordo-malleus|Ordo Malleus]], on adding a member guild's co-leader to his private top tier, checked before greeting him whether that guild now held two votes, and recorded his relief at being told it held one.{{cite:ordo-malleus-informal-power-discord-private|2015}}

Enforcement, where it existed, was one member telling another in the channel that they could only vote once.{{cite:who-gets-a-vote-discord-private|24 Jan 2018}} The single occasion on which an alliance actually changed a voting rule addressed the opposite problem: [[the-federation|the Federation]] made silence count as abstention, because guilds that did not vote were putting a two-thirds majority out of reach.{{cite:who-gets-a-vote-discord-private|7 Mar 2018}} The rule was written into the first motion its new voting channel carried, on 7 March 2018, as a line requiring every guild to register an option and taking a guild silent for a day to have abstained; two days later it was put to the leaders as a motion of its own, which had to go without an abstain option in order to ask the question, and carried twenty-one to five with no account on more than one option.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|7 and 9 Mar 2018, the abstention rule and its ratification}} The motion set the alliance's practice aside twice over in order to ask: besides having no abstain option it said that whichever option held the majority would simply be taken, so the rule creating the two-thirds arithmetic was itself decided without it. Nothing comes off that tally: no account on the motion sat on more than one option, and the guild that moved it voted on it like the rest.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|9 Mar 2018, the terms of the motion and its tally}}

Guilds voting twice were ruled on and never voted on. That a guild held one vote was stated in the Federation's leaders' channel on 18 February 2018, two days after it was founded, by an officer deciding which of two members of one guild to count.{{cite:federation-vote-procedure-february-2018-discord-private|18 Feb 2018, one vote per guild}} It went to the voting channel as a rule on 10 March, twenty-two minutes into a map ratification in which two accounts had already been asked to take back a second vote, one of them having reacted to yes and no together.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|10 Mar 2018, the rule issued mid-ratification}} It did not hold. On the second map motion a fortnight later an account reacted to both options and was asked in the leadership channel to remove one while the motion was still open. Both reactions were there when the tally was exported, and whether the request was refused, or the reaction taken off and put back afterwards, was never established.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|23-24 Mar 2018, the double vote}}

Ballots were also seeded, which is why a count taken off one has to be corrected before it means anything. Of the fourteen motions the Federation's voting channel carried in March 2018, seven have an account on more than one option and five have one on every option offered; two accounts between them cover all five, and neither had moved the motion it sat on.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|multi-option reactions across the fourteen March motions}} A member gave the reason six minutes before the expulsion motion of 30 March went up: he had reacted to all three options on a second account so that the options would be there for other people to use.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|30 Mar 2018, the explanation}} The alliance's own founder had said the same thing seventeen days earlier and about himself. Asked in the leadership channel on 13 March 2018 why guilds such as [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] appeared to get two votes, and whether that was not against the rule issued three days before, he answered that he ordinarily voted for all of the options when he added the marks to a motion; another member called such marks placeholders in the same minute, and the questioner withdrew the point.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|13 Mar 2018, the founder's account of the practice}} The cost of it fell on the smallest members, and one of them said so in the same exchange: the representative of [[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]] answered that it was better the founder placed the marks, because if she placed them her guild would not get a vote at all, having no co-leader to cast one.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|13 Mar 2018, the KingdomPhoenixes answer}} One account could build a ballot or vote in it and not both, so one vote per guild, in a room where ballots were assembled by hand, took the vote from any guild too small to send two people; reading the two remarks together is this article's. The permission to place the marks was itself held by a few members, and on 8 March, a day after the voting channel was created, its removal was proposed as a punishment for members who added joke marks to motions.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|8 Mar 2018, the permission}} The month's motions are set out at [[federation-ballots-march-2018|the Federation's ballots in March 2018]]. The effect is arithmetical rather than partisan. Each option on those five motions carries one reaction that was never a vote, so a raw count overstates the losing side by about one per option, and it does so most where the margin is narrowest. The correction is to take out the accounts sitting on *every* option, which are the placeholders and nothing else; an account on some options but not all cannot be told apart from a guild voting twice, and a count that turns on one has to be given both ways.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|the seeding correction, March 2018}} Moving a motion is not itself a reason to discount a mark: across all fourteen of the month's motions there is no case of the mover sitting on every option.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|movers and their marks across the fourteen March motions}} The alliance's leader, who had issued the one-vote rule himself, sits on two options on three of the month's motions.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|the leader on two options, March 2018}} Eight of the fourteen carry an edit stamp, and two of those bear on their own result: one was edited thirty-one hours after it went up, after its vote had run, and another four hours into a window of twenty-four.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|edit stamps across the fourteen March motions}} A stamp records only the last change and never what it changed, so for those two the wording the guilds reacted to is simply not recoverable.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|edited motions, March 2018}}

By September 2018 the seats had drifted from the guilds. A member who had moved between guilds was still voting in the seat of the one he had left; a leader remarked that half the leadership had mentioned it and that he did not even have the right guild's colour, and a third said it was fine so long as it was changed back eventually.{{cite:who-gets-a-vote-discord-private|2 Sep 2018}} Nothing was done.

A fortnight later a duplicate was caught. Immediately after the Federation voted [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] back into the alliance, a member observed that both of Aphelion's leaders had voted and both had voted in favour; another checked and said it made no difference, nine to three being a majority either way.{{cite:titans-valor-guild-profile-discord-private|19 Sep 2018}} It is the only occasion where a duplicate is noticed aloud, tested against the result and let stand on the strength of the test.

Three weeks later somebody answered that reasoning. On 12 October 2018 a Federation leader told two members of the same guild that one of them had to withdraw a vote in favour. One replied that the count stood at twelve to four and removing theirs would change nothing — the September argument, now offered by the person being challenged. Another leader answered that the point was not whether it would change the outcome but that it was a rule.{{cite:who-gets-a-vote-discord-private|12 Oct 2018}} It is the only place in four years where anyone states that the rule binds independently of whether breaking it alters a result.

On 13 September 2019 a [[terra|Terra]] leader proposed the rule outright — one vote per guild, because a guild holding two made the ballot unfair. The answer was not that he was wrong: it was that duplicates had to be removed by hand, that people never listened, and that co-leaders always tried to vote anyway.{{cite:who-gets-a-vote-discord-private|13 Sep 2019}} The man who gave it had been in the room in 2015 when the question was first asked.{{cite:who-gets-a-vote-discord-private|2015 and 13 Sep 2019}} Four years, four alliances, one person willing to say the rule held for its own sake, and it was never written down.

The same question at a larger scale was never put. Through September 2019 [[the-simple-ones|The Simple Ones]] and four or five allied and subordinate guilds acted as a single body inside [[terra|Terra]], spying inside the enemy alliance together and negotiating the settlement together{{cite:espionage-as-institution-discord-private|8 Sep 2019}}, then declaring neutrality and withdrawing together on 21 September{{cite:subguilds-discord-private|21 Sep 2019}} — in an alliance that counted its votes one to a guild.{{cite:who-gets-a-vote-discord-private|September 2019}} The duplicate anyone noticed was one person holding two votes in a single guild's seat; the arrangement nobody mentioned held five or six. Setting the two beside each other is this article's.

The same argument had been made about territory, once, and made plainly. On the evening of 28 February 2018 the Federation adopted its first written rule, on subguilds refused admission: such a guild would hold no role, no seat in the leaders' channel and no assigned territories, but its parent could hand over some of its own and let it fight.{{cite:federation-vote-procedure-february-2018-discord-private|28 Feb 2018, the subguild rule}} Asked whether a subguild's wars would then earn its parent more ground, the alliance's founder said they would not, because otherwise every guild would split itself into subguilds.{{cite:federation-vote-procedure-february-2018-discord-private|28 Feb 2018, the reason given}} That is the multiplication problem put by a participant, in terms, twelve days after the alliance was founded. Nobody in the room said the same of votes, and nobody ever did.

## The charge that the votes were not real

On 14 July 2018 a Federation member reported to the leadership channel that the leader of the opposing alliance was saying the alliance's voting was rigged — that whatever one faction inside it voted for passed, and that the other member guilds had no say.{{cite:federation-late-july-2018-discord-private|14 Jul 2018}} The response in the channel was to ask on what basis the claim was made, rather than to deny it.

The charge was not baseless in structure, whatever its merits in fact. Days earlier the [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]]' Viceroy had asked the leadership channel whether anyone had noticed there were four community alliances inside the Federation, naming [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]], the [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]] and Vestige.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|18 Jul 2018}} He could not name the fourth, and another member supplied it: a pact between Lunatic Mox and [[ex-nihilo|Ex Nihilo]].{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|leadership channel, 18 Jul 2018}}

The alliance could not agree what one of them was. Asked in that exchange what Vestige was, the Viceroy said it lay between Verinian Trials and [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]].{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|18 Jul 2018}} Ten days later a member put the same question in the general channel and a different member answered that Vestige was the Lunatic Mox and Ex Nihilo alliance, like Valkyrie and Aesir.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|28 Jul 2018}} Both were given confidently to someone who did not know, neither was corrected, and nothing in the record chooses between them.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|18–28 Jul 2018}} The exchanges settle the category and not the membership: Vestige was a pact among member guilds rather than a guild of its own. Blocs within the alliance existed and did not correspond to how votes were counted. The same accusation had been made from inside four months earlier by a member guild, and answered at length.{{cite:federation-map-disputes-march-2018-discord-private|March 2018}}

A third description was given in August and contradicted on the spot. On 22 August 2018, during a jokey conversation in the general channel about how the Federation might one day fall, a member described Vestige as two small guilds and one very large one; a member of it answered in the next message that they were a single body rather than three, and was told he did not lead it.{{cite:federation-vestige-2018-discord-private|22 Aug 2018}} The whole passage is banter — the same member joked that he might turn out to have had bad intentions towards the alliance all along and would stage a rising of traitors — and the joke was not a plan.{{cite:federation-vestige-2018-discord-private|22 Aug 2018, the banter}}

Two days later the argument about whether it was one thing or three settled itself. Within an hour of a new guild appearing on 24 August, a member identified it as Vestige uniting into a single guild.{{cite:federation-vestige-2018-discord-private|24 Aug 2018, the identification}} Others in the same exchange asked whether it was a splinter of [[lunatic|Lunatic]] and when [[ex-nihilo|Ex Nihilo]]'s leader had joined it; the answer given was that he had joined just then.{{cite:federation-vestige-2018-discord-private|24 Aug 2018, the questions}} The guild founded that day out of those two was [[odysseia|Odysseia]]{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|24 Aug 2018}}; joining the two is this article's, and it fits the second of the two July answers without settling between them.

Three weeks after that the alliance spoke of Vestige in the past tense. On 14 September, when somebody joked in the leadership channel about forming a third alliance inside the Federation, a member said Vestige had tried to be that, a guild leader answered that it had simply become the new guild, and a third agreed; a former member of it added that they had been going to approach [[thenolifes|TheNoLifes]] about joining and that it had then died.{{cite:federation-vestige-2018-discord-private|14 Sep 2018}} What the three conversations establish is that the bloc existed until 24 August 2018 and ceased to exist by becoming a guild. Its membership was never stated the same way twice and is not recoverable.

The alliance had considered the risk in those blocs and accepted it. Asked on 24 July 2018 what people made of arrangements like Valkyrie, the Viceroy answered that the [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]] was a pact and [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]] an alliance.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|24 Jul 2018, pact and alliance}} The member who had asked put the objection herself — that a quarrel between two guilds draws in every guild bound to either.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|24 Jul 2018, the objection}} He replied that they had been doing it for months with no trouble arising, and that he thought it a good thing.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|24 Jul 2018, the answer}}

## Nobody could hold the map

Between early September and mid-October 2018 four people in succession drew [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s territory map, and the alliance lost all four.{{cite:federation-mapmakers-2018-discord-private|September–October 2018}} The first had joined the committee mainly to understand how the map was made, and asked to be removed once he had.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|September 2018}}

The second deleted a vote he was winning. Asked on 25 September whether the map vote had to be unanimous, he said it did not — two thirds was enough — that it would have passed easily, and that it had stood at fourteen to one when he removed it, one guild against.{{cite:federation-mapmakers-2018-discord-private|25 Sep 2018}} Asked directly why, he said he wanted the alliance left in no doubt that the dissenting guild and its leader were a problem within it; another member replied that this could be dealt with at any other time and should not be allowed to stop the map.{{cite:federation-mapmakers-2018-discord-private|25 Sep 2018}}

He had also said what made the job intolerable. The process they had adopted did not allow a small part of a map to be redone once it had passed, so a detail got wrong stood.{{cite:federation-map-round-september-2018-discord-private|26 Sep 2018}} A map with a known error in it could be lived with or stopped, and stopping it meant deleting the vote — a connection this article draws and he did not.

He resigned the next day and gave a reason that was not about any one guild: that guild leaders leave whoever holds the job no choice but to side with one of them and offend another.{{cite:federation-mapmakers-2018-discord-private|26 Sep 2018}} The post was vacant at once — a member observed that the alliance now had no map-maker and offered to back somebody else for it, and a third said he had volunteered first.{{cite:federation-mapmakers-2018-discord-private|26 Sep 2018}} The volunteer was drawing the map two days later, and on 14 October a second member joined him at it rather than replacing him.{{cite:federation-mapmakers-2018-discord-private|28 Sep and 14 Oct 2018}}

In the same conversation a guild leader asked why map-making was not simply opened to the leaders, granting that it might start arguments.{{cite:federation-mapmakers-2018-discord-private|26 Sep 2018}} It was the third such request in seven months, after the March one and the September canvass — and the first to be granted. On 10 October the new map-maker told the leadership channel that the alliance's founder had given him access so that he could discuss the map with the leaders, that he knew many of them wished to be more involved, and that he meant to keep the process open.{{cite:federation-mapmakers-2018-discord-private|10 Oct 2018}} Nobody referred back to the asking.

It opened by one step and no further. Four days later the map was to be settled privately between the two map-makers and then posted for the leaders to comment on.{{cite:federation-mapmakers-2018-discord-private|14 Oct 2018}}

## A guild half in and half out

Eight days before [[the-federation|the Federation]] dissolved, it could not say whether one of its members was inside it. [[emorians|Emorians]] had begun moving to a second guild account named Audux, and the alliance had agreed the guild would not need a vote to swap across.{{cite:federation-guild-swap-2018-discord-private|22 Oct 2018}} The reason offered in the channel — internal struggles and an absent leader — was one Federation member's second-hand account of another guild's affairs, and the guild gave none of its own.{{cite:federation-guild-swap-2018-discord-private|22 Oct 2018, the reason given}}

By 2 November the move had started and not finished, and a member asked whether the new guild was technically in the alliance at all. The man who had drawn the map answered that technically nothing would stop anyone going and wiping it, and it could do nothing in reply: anything not in the Federation was an enemy in reality, and this was not in the Federation.{{cite:federation-guild-swap-2018-discord-private|2 Nov 2018}} Asked whether that made it technically an enemy, he said it was extremely complicated — no vote was needed for the swap, but the old guild was still in use, so the guild's official identity was not yet the new one.{{cite:federation-guild-swap-2018-discord-private|2 Nov 2018}}

A member then objected that one of that guild's people was warring in the new account: swapping was fine, he said, but he did not accept both being warred at once.{{cite:federation-guild-swap-2018-discord-private|2 Nov 2018}} That is the subguild question arriving from the opposite direction — not a member running a second guild to gain an advantage, but a member becoming a second guild and the alliance unable to say which of them it had admitted.

Membership attached to the guild account rather than to the people in it, which is why a change of account could put a member outside the alliance while everyone concerned stayed inside it. Nobody in the record puts it that way; it is this article's reading of why the exchange was as difficult as it was.

## The register of enemies

The largest alliance in the game kept its list of enemies the way it kept most things. On the evening of 2 July 2018 a member said the register was due a revision, posted one running to fourteen guilds, and asked whether any should be added or taken off; the alliance's founder said it looked good to him, and the additions then came one member at a time.{{cite:federation-enemy-list-july-2018-discord-private|2 Jul 2018}}

It was not supposed to work that way. Thirteen minutes earlier, at 17:37 on the alliance's own clock, the member who kept the list had told the advisory council that it badly needed revising and that he could not change it without the guild leaders voting on who counted as an enemy now, adding that he would probably just open a vote.{{cite:federation-rule-and-practice-july-2018-discord-private|2 Jul 2018, 17:37, the rule stated}} At 17:50 he posted the revision in the leaders' channel instead, and the founder approved it in a line at 17:51.{{cite:federation-rule-and-practice-july-2018-discord-private|2 Jul 2018, 17:50 and 17:51}} No vote was put and nobody objected.{{cite:federation-rule-and-practice-july-2018-discord-private|2 Jul 2018, no vote}} The procedure needed a day and a channel full of guild leaders, and the list needed changing that evening. Three minutes after the approval a member of the council proposed naming the whole opposing alliance at once rather than guilds one at a time, and nobody answered him.{{cite:federation-rule-and-practice-july-2018-discord-private|2 Jul 2018, 17:54, the proposal}}

The test for inclusion was stated aloud, once. A leader said one guild had been left off — only a subguild, granted, but a considerable nuisance and an enemy in every sense.{{cite:federation-enemy-list-july-2018-discord-private|2 Jul 2018}} Somebody else said he had never heard of them. Another guild went on for whose subguild it was, which is ownership rather than conduct — the same device the alliance tolerated on its own map when the owner was a member.{{cite:federation-enemy-list-july-2018-discord-private|2 Jul 2018}}

Being on the list was not a label. Since April 2018 member guilds had been bound by vote to attack the guilds named on it, so a name added in passing by whoever was in the channel committed the alliance to a war.{{cite:federation-obligation-to-attack-2018-discord-private|3–4 Apr 2018}} What that was worth three months on shows better in what the alliance was asking for than in the register: on 19 July a notice went to the whole membership complaining that guilds tagged to reclaim lost territory had been tagged three times before anything was done.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|1:52 PM, 7/19/2018}}

The next morning, in the same channel, members could not say what they were fighting. Asked what the opposing alliance amounted to, or whether there was an alliance there at all, they settled on this: the guilds attacking them were not in one alliance; the man they thought of as leading them had left an earlier grouping because it had grown too toxic; and if he led these, he had not meant to.{{cite:federation-enemy-list-july-2018-discord-private|3 Jul 2018}} One member, asked whether an opposing bloc still existed, said it had been finished for a while and mentioned that he had deleted it himself.{{cite:federation-enemy-list-july-2018-discord-private|3 Jul 2018}}

Sixteen hours separate the fixed list of fourteen from that conversation. This article sets them side by side; nobody at the time did.

The list went on growing on the same footing. On the morning of 4 July a leader told the council that HouseBaratheon belonged to the opposing alliance and might be worth adding, and that its own leader had confirmed the affiliation to him earlier that day; two members answered that they had taken it for an unaffiliated guild.{{cite:federation-rule-and-practice-july-2018-discord-private|4 Jul 2018, the affiliation}} A guild went onto the register of enemies on a third party's report of what its leader had said, and on nothing else the alliance held. Sixteen minutes later the same leader corrected the tag he had given it: it ends in the letter i and he had written the visually similar letter l, and he asked for the brackets as well.{{cite:federation-rule-and-practice-july-2018-discord-private|4 Jul 2018, the tag corrected}}

## What membership was for, from outside

Azeron applied to [[the-federation|the Federation]] on 22 October 2018, posting to the voting channel, and its application is the only one in this record that says what belonging was for from the applicant's side. It described the alliance as home to many guilds and as rich, powerful and kind-hearted — noting in brackets that some guilds were not included in the kind-hearted part — and asked to join for the help of friends, an assigned territory, and its free-for-all ground not being attacked. The Federation, it said, was the dream.{{cite:federation-admissions-under-suspicion-2018-discord-private|22 Oct 2018}}

Four minutes later the channel had decided it was a spy. A member called it a subguild of [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]], the application shaky, and the guild an infiltrator; no evidence for any of that appears in the exchange.{{cite:federation-admissions-under-suspicion-2018-discord-private|22 Oct 2018}} The same member had said a moment earlier that the applicant's leader disliked his guild because they went around calling it a subguild of the enemy — so the accusation and the grievance were the same object.{{cite:federation-admissions-under-suspicion-2018-discord-private|22 Oct 2018}}

One leader held out on the only ground the alliance had ever really used: he had seen them war, they were doing a good job, they would be a qualified member — at least they warred. The answer was that they were bad at war and that a heavy defence would kill them.{{cite:federation-admissions-under-suspicion-2018-discord-private|22 Oct 2018}} Neither position is unreasonable and the disagreement is about what membership was for.

Mid-vote, a leader remarked that Valkyrie was swaying yes, and that it was time to overrule.{{cite:federation-admissions-under-suspicion-2018-discord-private|22 Oct 2018}} He is the same man who set out two evenings later why a map could not be redrawn against a bloc.

The count was reported at eight to three, and the man reporting it noted that because one guild's leader had voted, the vote cast by another member of that guild counted only as a placeholder — one vote per guild, and the leader's is the one that stands.{{cite:federation-admissions-under-suspicion-2018-discord-private|22 Oct 2018}} Which way the eight ran is not stated; the founder referred the next evening to everyone having voted no to that guild.{{cite:federation-admissions-under-suspicion-2018-discord-private|23 Oct 2018}}

In the middle of the same conversation the leaders argued about their own members' affiliations from a diagram somebody had posted weeks earlier, one saying parts of it were inaccurate but that it got the point across.{{cite:federation-admissions-under-suspicion-2018-discord-private|22 Oct 2018, the diagram}} They disputed whether [[odysseia|Odysseia]] belonged to [[aesir-pact|Aesir]], one saying they had no proof it did not.{{cite:federation-admissions-under-suspicion-2018-discord-private|22 Oct 2018, Odysseia's affiliation}} Another observed that his own guild did not appear on it at all.{{cite:federation-admissions-under-suspicion-2018-discord-private|22 Oct 2018, a missing guild}} Two evenings later the same channel was told that those groupings decided every map vote. That an alliance treating its internal blocs as the decisive fact about its voting could not enumerate them is this article's observation; nobody made it at the time.

The next evening a second application was judged, and the founder named the inconsistency himself. He said the applicant was joining only for territory and hated the Federation.{{cite:federation-admissions-under-suspicion-2018-discord-private|23 Oct 2018, the founder's objection}} The alliance's former map-maker said he was voting no because he was unwilling to surrender further territory to guilds whose usefulness was not yet established.{{cite:federation-admissions-under-suspicion-2018-discord-private|23 Oct 2018, the cost of admission}} The founder then pointed out that the guild under discussion had less than half the war record of the one rejected the day before, which everyone had voted against for reasons he called random.{{cite:federation-admissions-under-suspicion-2018-discord-private|23 Oct 2018, the inconsistency}} Both judgements are the Federation's characterisations of guilds outside it, and neither applicant's account survives beyond its application.

The month's other application asked for no territory at all. On 29 September 2018 the leader of Foxerial asked the leadership channel about adding his guild, saying that the guild was only himself, that he would hold no territories, and that he would attack only guilds outside the alliance.{{cite:federation-admissions-2018-discord-private|29 Sep 2018, the offer}} A member answered in the same register with a remark about defending with the heaviest possible mobs, and no decision was recorded.{{cite:federation-admissions-2018-discord-private|29 Sep 2018, the answer}} The exchange is light in tone and may be no more than that, but in an alliance where membership meant an assignment it is the only proposal of membership without one.

The capture log has him doing what he offered. On 20 September Foxerial took Cinfras County Lower from [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] at 10:00 UTC and Cinfras from [[imperial|Imperial]] at 10:14, and had lost both by 11:44.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Foxerial exchanges with times, 20 Sep 2018 UTC}} Across 28 and 29 September it took ground from BuildCraftia and [[kangronomicon|Kangronomicon]] more than twenty times and lost nearly all of it within the hour, most of it to Federation guilds.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Foxerial exchanges by counterpart, 28–29 Sep 2018 UTC}}

## The bloc veto

The man who drew the map explained on 23 October 2018 why it could not be redrawn, seventeen days before the alliance dissolved. Cut the territory of a guild belonging to the [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]] or [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]], he said, and one could be reasonably certain every guild in that grouping would vote the map down; given the number of guilds in the alliance, only one more was then needed on top of them — two in Aesir's case — and the map failed at once.{{cite:federation-blocs-within-2018-discord-private|23 Oct 2018}} The territories of the larger guilds therefore could not really be rearranged without severe backlash.{{cite:federation-blocs-within-2018-discord-private|23 Oct 2018}}

He admitted the premise before he gave the reason. The assumption behind any map was that whoever drew it gave the best ground to the guilds that had done most for the alliance, and everybody in that channel knew it did not really hold.{{cite:federation-blocs-within-2018-discord-private|23 Oct 2018}}

What he wanted was that a bloc either stop functioning as one or leave the Federation and run itself properly as a war alliance.{{cite:federation-blocs-within-2018-discord-private|24 Oct 2018}} The alliance never made that choice.

## The one inquiry

Late on 20 July 2018, asked whether [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]]'s leader had lost his ranks, the Kingdom of Foxes' Viceroy told the channel that he had received news the man might have approached the opposing alliance, that he could not verify it, and that he had removed the roles until he could speak to him.{{cite:federation-late-july-2018-discord-private|20 Jul 2018}} A member asked whether that meant the guild was no longer an ally.

It is the only occasion in the record of the Federation suspending a member's standing pending an inquiry, in an alliance that had no disciplinary procedure to invoke. The reasoning given was more careful than the act: the information was unverified and said to be so, the roles were removed as a precaution rather than a penalty, and the stated intention was to speak to the man before deciding anything.

## Cheating, and the absence of a way to establish it

These alliances had a clear idea of what cheating was and almost no means of showing that any of it had happened. The [[emperium-of-wynn|Emperium of Wynn]] announced in May 2017 that hacks, cheats and duplicate accounts would be dealt with — the only written anti-cheating provision any alliance in this record produced, from the alliance that wrote everything else down as well.{{cite:cheating-and-enforcement-discord-private|May 2017}}

Nothing follows it. Accusations surface throughout the period and none of them is ever resolved: they are made in war channels during fighting, repeated years later as banter by people who say in the same breath that they were never proven, and dropped. On 7 September 2019, mid-raid, members trying to work out how an enemy guild had taken a territory ahead of four of their own captains were told that a report about macro use had been made to the game's moderators and had produced no result. The next suggestion in the channel was that they should use macros too.{{cite:cheating-and-enforcement-discord-private|7 Sep 2019}} That exchange is the whole enforcement problem in one place: no adjudication available, and no reason not to do the thing.

Adjudication was available twice in 2018, and both times it came from outside. In May [[the-federation|the Federation]] was accused on the game's public forums of feeding — arranging for territory to fall to a guild rather than fighting for it — and one of its members answered the charge there.{{cite:thread-226798|post #1, 26 May 2018}} Its guild leaders checked the answer before it went out.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|26 May 2018, the draft}} Five days later a Wynncraft administrator closed the thread, declining to change the war rules{{cite:thread-226798-p08|post #143, jpresent, 30 May 2018}} and saying that keeping track of who was feeding whom would be more than the moderation team could handle.{{cite:thread-226798-p08|post #143, the reasons given}} The alliance's own advisory channel had produced two incompatible definitions of the offence the day after the post went up{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|27 May 2018}}, and its leaders then settled on the position that the word described nothing that existed — see [[the-feeding-accusation|the feeding accusation]].{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|31 May 2018}}

A private dispute went the same way. The Federation's founder was reported to the moderators in August 2018 for taking a guild he had acquired in a swap, and was told he would be banned unless he handed it back.{{cite:libertas-as-property-2018-discord-private|28 Aug 2018}} A member of the game's staff set the settlement instead: a price of ten stacks of liquid emeralds, with a replacement guild and the old name going to the other party.{{cite:libertas-as-property-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018}} The alliance's part was to raise the money among its member guilds, which is described at [[libertas|Libertas]].{{cite:libertas-as-property-2018-discord-private|30 Aug – 1 Sep 2018}}

Where the line actually sat is easier to see in what nobody called cheating. One guild's conspicuous wealth was explained by a bug that left it holding a territory it could not lose, and nobody proposed that it give anything back.{{cite:cheating-and-enforcement-discord-private|2017–2019}} When the game changed its own war rules in March 2018 and small guilds were left unable to defend themselves, the guilds attacking them pressed the advantage, and nobody called that cheating either.{{cite:cheating-and-enforcement-discord-private|March 2018}} What drew the word, on this article's reading of those episodes, was using software the game did not provide; exploiting what the game did provide, however plainly unintended, did not. Nobody in the record states the distinction that way.

One thing was acted on at once. On 8 March 2018 [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s leader told the leaders' channel that he had watched a member assign himself the server bot's role to take administrator permissions on the alliance's own Discord, and that it had been fixed.{{cite:cheating-and-enforcement-discord-private|8 Mar 2018}} The member did not dispute it. His objection was that it had been said in public chat, and another leader answered that the leaders' channel was not very public.{{cite:cheating-and-enforcement-discord-private|8 Mar 2018, the objection}} No vote followed and no expulsion — a detection, a statement, a fix, and an argument about venue.

In May the [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]]' Viceroy gave the Federation's advisory council an alternate account he had kept inside an enemy alliance's war channels as the reason his advice about a spy should carry.{{cite:cheating-and-enforcement-discord-private|16 May 2018}} The only reply was a member asking which server he meant, and nobody remarked on it.{{cite:cheating-and-enforcement-discord-private|16 May 2018, the response}} Espionage against an enemy was a credential; interfering with the alliance's own machinery was named aloud in the leaders' channel. Reading the two as one line is this article's, and [[espionage-and-infiltration|espionage and infiltration]] carries the May exchange in full.

This article does not name any accused guild or player. Every accusation in the underlying material is unproven, most were made by opponents during fighting, and the community that made them had no process capable of testing one.

## The war that had no commander

The alliance's largest war was directed by nobody in particular. On the first day of the March 2018 fighting the member who would normally have coordinated it was unavailable, and guilds settled on a tactic of concentrating against one enemy guild; on the morning of 3 March, seeing that it was making no progress, the member directing that effort agreed to let the Kingdom of Foxes' Viceroy coordinate instead.{{cite:federation-march-2018-discord-private|3 Mar 2018}} Direction of the war passed from one person to another because the first conceded it.

The next day a member proposed that the alliance create an elected Supreme Commander, and the objections were specific rather than indifferent: that the post would obviously go to somebody from one of the two largest guilds; that a member should consider how he would feel being directed by a commander drawn from another guild; and that the idea was sound except for the question of how such a person would get anyone to follow him.{{cite:federation-march-2018-discord-private|4 Mar 2018}} The alliance did not decline unified command in principle. It could not answer who would hold it, or why anybody would obey.

## Who could still read the room

Standing in an alliance was a role on a Discord server, granted and removed by hand, and four times in seven months it outlived the membership it belonged to. It was noticed the first time: on 13 January 2018 a member asked the [[coalition|Coalition]]'s general channel whether a named player was in [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] at all, was told he was not, and a third observed that somebody had got in undetected.{{cite:roles-outlive-membership-2018-discord-private|13 Jan 2018}}

April shows the machinery. A member reported to [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s leadership that someone in DogsAmongUs was leaking to a newly formed guild, and a leader ordered everyone in that guild above captain stripped of their roles before anybody knew who it was.{{cite:roles-outlive-membership-2018-discord-private|16 Apr 2018}} The man was then identified: he had left DogsAmongUs in order to found the guild he was leaking to, and had kept his alliance role and captain rank throughout. Asked why the roles had been left alone, the leader responsible answered that he had expected him to leave of his own accord.{{cite:roles-outlive-membership-2018-discord-private|16 Apr 2018, after the identification}}

That is the only stated reason in the record for access outliving membership, and it names the procedure: there was none. A defector to [[hackforums|HackForums]] lost his roles the same night on 21 July, because the founder happened to see him do it.{{cite:roles-outlive-membership-2018-discord-private|21 Jul 2018}} On 12 August the leader of [[libertas|Libertas]] — a guild nobody present could establish was a member — held [[imperial|Imperial]]'s roles and could read the territory assignment committee.{{cite:roles-outlive-membership-2018-discord-private|12 Aug 2018}}

Reading the four as one failure is this article's. The alliance could not revoke what it had granted for the same reason it could not list its members: both waited on somebody noticing.{{cite:roles-outlive-membership-2018-discord-private|13 Jan – 12 Aug 2018}}

## What was never written down

Not every alliance wrote itself a constitution. [[the-federation|The Federation]], the largest of them, had none: in May 2018 a member proposed drafting a formal charter, observing that the alliance had run for a long time on rules announced informally and never set down.{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|May 2018}} What it had instead was a voting threshold, a territory-assignment practice and a body of decisions — which worked until its members stopped accepting the decisions, and by the end of July 2018 they were transferring assigned territory between themselves without the alliance's involvement.{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|July 2018}}

Its founder said as much when a vote turned on a procedure nobody in the room could state. On the evening of 5 September 2018, working out why the readmission of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] had failed, he raised as something that had never come up before what should be done when a leader who had not voted cast a vote after the twenty-four hours were up.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|5 Sep 2018, the question}} A leader asked whether it was not a standing rule that guilds which had not voted within the day counted as abstaining, and he answered that they made all of it up as they went along.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|5 Sep 2018, the answer}} Pressed, he said he liked to keep things flexible, did not want an alliance run on rigid rules, and thought strict rules produced more argument rather than less.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|5 Sep 2018, the elaboration}} He had stated the procedure in the same channel without hesitation six days earlier — two thirds quickly and a motion passed at once, otherwise the alliance waited a day and took the count as it then stood.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the rule stated}} The abstention rule he was being asked about was the one he had proposed himself six months earlier.{{cite:who-gets-a-vote-discord-private|7 Mar 2018}} Nobody in the September conversation referred to March; setting the two together is this article's.

One rule the alliance did adopt got no further than the vote that made it. Between 26 August and 1 September 2018 it voted itself a mandatory interval before a refused applicant could be considered again, settling on two weeks by fifteen votes to two after a first ballot on four lengths produced no majority.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|26 Aug and 1 Sep 2018, the two votes}} No announcement of it was made and no rules document carried it.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|26 Aug – 18 Sep 2018, no announcement and no rules document}} It was applied once: a readmission application of 18 September opened by observing that two weeks had passed, posted fourteen days after the motion that had refused the guild.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|18 Sep 2018, the application}}

