The one alliance of the period that outlasted them all, [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]], described itself throughout as a community alliance and was never established for the purpose of war.{{cite:thread-89529|post #1, opening post (thread opened 10 Sep 2015)}}

When a [[the-federation|Federation]] guild took back a territory belonging to an ally, it chose how heavily to defend the ground before handing it over, and a defence heavy enough to keep the enemy out could be too heavy for the owner's own newer fighters to beat.{{cite:federation-allied-defence-2018-discord-private|August–September 2018}}

The practice had been publicly accused of cheating before the alliance wrote any rule about it. On 26 May 2018 a Federation member answered the charge on the game's forums, describing member guilds retaking an ally's ground and defending it lightly so that the owner could reclaim it more easily.{{cite:thread-226798|post #1, 26 May 2018}} Defending lightly also spared the recapturing guild the cost of holding land it would lose again.{{cite:thread-226798|post #1, the justification}} A member of an opposing guild answered that the line between defending lightly and feeding was a grey one.{{cite:thread-226798|post #5, ChuckeyTheBear, 26 May 2018}} He described a recaptured Federation territory left under a hundred low-level mobs and, once his side began attacking the recapturing guild instead, under nine hundred.{{cite:thread-226798|post #5, the siege}} A Wynncraft administrator closed the thread on 30 May without changing the war rules — see [[the-feeding-accusation|the feeding accusation]].{{cite:thread-226798-p08|post #143, jpresent, 30 May 2018}}

The alliance was asking about this by hand five weeks before it issued the August notice: on 10 July 2018 a member asked the whole membership in the announcements channel to use a thousand mobs on one guild's territories, "just so we don't burn as much money defending".{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Wynn, 7/10/2018}}

## The motion of 13 March 2018

It had been voted on already, five months earlier and from the other direction. On 13 March 2018 the representative of a small member guild put a motion to the Federation's voting channel: some guilds were defending allies' territory by placing the highest-level defenders on it, which made it very hard for smaller guilds, usually fighting alone, to take their own ground back.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|13 Mar 2018, the complaint}} There should therefore be a limit, it said, on the level of defence a guild may place when defending an ally's territory.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|13 Mar 2018, the limit proposed}} Fifteen guilds voted for the limit, four against and six abstained.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|the motion's tally, 13 Mar 2018}} The guild that moved it left the alliance five days later. On the evening of 18 March 2018 its representative told the war channel that almost everyone there hated him and that he was leaving to rejoin [[war-syndicate|War Syndicate]] if anyone was left in it{{cite:march-2018-observations-verified-discord-private|18 Mar 2018, the departure announced}}, and was told the Federation was the [[coalition|Coalition]] and War Syndicate combined, every surviving War Syndicate guild being already inside it.{{cite:march-2018-observations-verified-discord-private|18 Mar 2018, the answer given}} Identifying the departing representative with the guild that moved the motion rests on a remark in a second channel fourteen minutes later naming the guild that had left, and the join is this article's.{{cite:march-2018-observations-verified-discord-private|18 Mar 2018, the guild named in the second channel}} Nothing comes off that tally: no account on the motion sat on every option, and the mark placed by the guild that moved it was a vote like anybody else's.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|multi-option reactions and movers' marks across the fourteen March motions}}

The alliance found out on 7 July 2018 that it had a rule and had lost it. A member of the leadership channel said he was fairly sure no such rule existed.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|7 Jul 2018, the denial}} The leader of [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]] reproduced the motion, dated it to 13 March and said the vote had passed, and a third member said he had not known the rule existed.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|7 Jul 2018, the motion produced}} Six weeks later the alliance wrote the notice below, which asks the owner what it wants rather than setting any limit.

## The rule of 19 August 2018

On 19 August 2018 a member posted the same notice to the leadership channel and to the alliance's noticeboard a minute apart.{{cite:federation-allied-defence-2018-discord-private|19 Aug 2018, the two postings}} It asked that guild leaders' requests about defences be taken into account on allied land, and allowed that all defences had to remain situational.{{cite:federation-allied-defence-2018-discord-private|19 Aug 2018, the request}} It said the alliance's purpose was to hold its ground, so defending properly on ally-assigned land mattered{{cite:federation-allied-defence-2018-discord-private|19 Aug 2018, the reason given}}, and directed that where the owning guild was in a position to reclaim, its leader or captains should be asked what defence they wanted.{{cite:federation-allied-defence-2018-discord-private|19 Aug 2018, the direction}}

The rule was to ask the owner.

## Thirteen messages, seven answers

The noticeboard the rule pointed at was audited the same day and did not hold the answers. A member counted its messages and reported that of thirteen, only seven said anything about how a guild wished its territory defended.{{cite:emorians-august-2018-discord-private|19 Aug 2018, the count}} At least one of those was probably out of date, its author no longer being in the guild it concerned, and two of the others said the same thing.{{cite:emorians-august-2018-discord-private|19 Aug 2018, stale and duplicated}} The leader of [[emorians|Emorians]] answered that the point was efficiency and streamlining between guilds, and that asked at that moment he could name only one guild that wanted its ground lightly defended — and only because its representative kept raising it and shouting at other members.{{cite:emorians-august-2018-discord-private|19 Aug 2018, the answer}} The member replied by asking whether remembering four guilds was really so hard.{{cite:emorians-august-2018-discord-private|19 Aug 2018, four guilds}}

The same argument had already had a public round that morning in the war channel. The representative of [[the-hive|The Hive]] posted a message telling members to be thankful for the alliance, because many guilds in it would be nowhere without it, and that his own guild's territories were being attacked too and it would not be defending more lightly.{{cite:emorians-august-2018-discord-private|posted 19 Aug 2018, written the evening of the 18th}} Forty-six minutes later the representative of [[ex-nihilo|Ex Nihilo]] answered that this amounted to calling a whole guild ungrateful for asking allies to defend more reasonably, and that over-defending allied territory was counterproductive.{{cite:emorians-august-2018-discord-private|19 Aug 2018, the reply in the war channel}} The Hive's representative said he had not been aiming the message at anyone at first, but had felt like tagging people once a particular guild blamed his.{{cite:emorians-august-2018-discord-private|19 Aug 2018, the aim}} The message carried its own earlier header, so its words were of the 18th and only its appearance was of the 19th.{{cite:emorians-august-2018-discord-private|the re-posted header, 18–19 Aug 2018}}


## The reference sheet

Twelve days later the alliance stopped relying on asking. On 31 August 2018 its territory coordinator posted a per-guild sheet to the noticeboard so that anyone who cared how people wanted their land defended would not have to scroll for it, listing ten member guilds and what each of them wanted.{{cite:federation-allied-defence-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018}} The alliance's founder relayed six of the entries to the whole membership the next day, in the first of his weekly summaries.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/1/2018}}

Those six show the shapes the answers took. One banned particular mob levels outright, one made its answer depend on whether it was under siege, two set ceilings in the hundreds, one invited bosses if the defender wanted them, and one asked for "more mobs for more money".{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/1/2018, the six entries}}

Across the full ten the answers were not commensurable at all: one asked for defence in proportion to what the ground was worth, one was a plea rather than a figure, one was defined by pointing at another guild's entry, and one asked that nobody interfere unless asked first.{{cite:federation-allied-defence-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the entries}} Reading that as why no single standard could be enforced is this article's.

## It did not settle it

The sheet was reposted in revised form on 24 September 2018 because it had been buried, with one guild dropped, one added and several entries changed{{cite:federation-allied-defence-2018-discord-private|24 Sep 2018}} — one of them reversing completely, from wanting the heaviest defence available to wanting no help at all unless it asked for it.{{cite:federation-allied-defence-2018-discord-private|the two versions compared, 31 Aug and 24 Sep 2018}} The argument the notice of 19 August had been written to end was still running in the last week of September.{{cite:federation-allied-defence-2018-discord-private|September 2018}}

## The rules were not repealed

By October 2018 [[the-federation|the Federation]] had a written answer to most of the ways its members could hurt each other. Guilds were not to attack each other's assigned ground; a guild recapturing an ally's territory was not to defend it so heavily that the owner could not take it back, and there was a channel where each guild published the level it wanted, and this sheet; guilds were not to make their own arrangements with guilds outside the alliance; and a guild falling below a stated number of territories forfeited protection.{{cite:federation-allied-defence-2018-discord-private|the rule and the sheet, Aug – Sep 2018}}{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|the thirty-territory rule, 2018}} None of it was repealed in the alliance's last month. All of it stopped happening.

On 15 October a member asked a leader of another member guild in the war channel to stop putting high-level defenders on their own guild's assigned ground, said the noticeboard already told every captain to defend under a stated level there, and asked that their ground be left alone entirely even when an enemy held it.{{cite:federation-rules-unenforced-october-2018-discord-private|15 Oct 2018, the complaint}} Told that the guilds concerned already knew, they said they would take it to that person's leader — and were told that the person being complained about was that leader.{{cite:federation-rules-unenforced-october-2018-discord-private|15 Oct 2018, the leader}} Three days later, when a similar complaint reached the leadership channel, two leaders advised that the way to get such things fixed was to approach guild leaders privately.{{cite:federation-rules-unenforced-october-2018-discord-private|18 Oct 2018, the advice}}

Eight days after that the alliance voted the noticeboard out of existence, on the argument that nobody read it. The motion carried nine to two as the reactions stand, and eight to one after removing an account sitting on both options; the channel's last message is dated the day before the motion, and the complaints went on in the channels where they had always gone on.{{cite:federation-last-motions-2018-discord-private|26 Oct 2018, the motion and the tally}}{{cite:federation-rules-unenforced-october-2018-discord-private|26 Oct 2018, the channel}} Whether the channel was ever actually deleted was not shown. The other motions of the alliance's last month are at [[federation-last-motions-2018|the Federation's last motions]].

## A member guild taking a member's ground

On the afternoon of 20 October 2018 a member reported in the war channel that Selchar had passed from [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] to [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]]. A leader answered within the hour that this was now actually treason: taking the place once would have been a symbolic thing, but they were still attacking, [[aphelion|Aphelion]] had taken it too, and both those guilds were warring through members placed in them by Titans Valor.{{cite:federation-rules-unenforced-october-2018-discord-private|20 Oct 2018, treason}} Nothing followed it.

The log has the hour. Between 20:50 and 22:01 UTC that day Selchar changed hands eight times between four guilds, all of them Federation members: an account carrying Titans Valor's tag took it twice from Paladins United, Aphelion took it from that account, [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]] took it once, and Paladins United took it back three times.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Selchar exchanges, 20 Oct 2018 20:50–22:01 UTC, raw — the declaration in force cannot be applied to the window}} That account appears nowhere else in the whole log: its entire record is four captures of that one territory in that one hour.{{cite:territory-exchanges|complete record of the attacking account, all dates, UTC}}

In the same conversation another member described the leader of Titans Valor helping on undefended territory belonging to a guild outside the alliance and then defending it with thirty snipers and nine hundred filler mobs, after being asked three or four times to stop.{{cite:federation-rules-unenforced-october-2018-discord-private|20 Oct 2018, the over-defending}} That is the offence the notice of 19 August was written against, committed on behalf of a guild the alliance had no arrangement with at all. Six years later, in October 2024, the same leader said in the same server that they had held four Federation guilds at once.{{cite:federation-rules-unenforced-october-2018-discord-private|Oct 2024, the retrospective}} It is that person's own account of their own conduct, given long afterwards; reading the 2018 complaint through it is this article's.

## Complicit in both

The clearest statement of what had happened came from somebody who had already left. On 1 November 2018 the leader of [[thenolifes|TheNoLifes]] answered the charge that his guild had refused to help during a large attack by setting out what it had done: only one leader had asked it to stop taking free-for-all territory; it had gone on to take ten territories from [[the-hive|The Hive]] because that guild was no longer an ally; the alliance's founder had then told it to stop and it had; and he resented being treated as somebody who acted without reason.{{cite:federation-rules-unenforced-october-2018-discord-private|1 Nov 2018, the defence}} Earlier the same morning a member had said in that channel that this was not the kind of alliance anyone wanted.{{cite:federation-rules-unenforced-october-2018-discord-private|1 Nov 2018, the morning}}

Ten understates it. From 27 October, the day TheNoLifes' captures on The Hive begin, to the morning of the message on 1 November, it took ninety-six territories from that guild across forty-four distinct places, and The Hive took ninety-six back.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges between TheNoLifes and The Hive, 27 Oct – 1 Nov 2018 UTC, raw: 96 each way over 44 territories — the declaration of 12 Oct cannot be applied, its surviving text being a revision of 2 Nov}}

That afternoon the guild's former head of war, who had left it a week before, wrote in the same channel that not attacking The Hive was itself against Federation rules, that members were not allowed to make pacts with guilds outside the alliance, and that everyone was being complicit in both.{{cite:federation-rules-unenforced-october-2018-discord-private|1 Nov 2018, the afternoon}} Nothing in the channel answers it. Nine days later the alliance [[the-federation-dies|dissolved in a morning]].

Between 22 January and 11 February 2018 [[iceresistance|IceResistance]], the leader of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]], assembled at least two multi-guild arrangements which he presented as treaties, each a standing understanding among several guilds that one named guild would be treated as a shared target until holding territory became impossible for it.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Coalition general and war channels, January–February 2018}} He posted as Ice, the handle the [[emperium-of-wynn|Emperium of Wynn]] had matched to his in-game name in its own announcements the previous September, and as hot pink emperor by late February 2018.{{cite:emperium-announcements|9/1/2017}}{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|the same account across the January and February 2018 messages}}

## The announcement

The first surfaced in an unlikely place. On 22 January 2018 its author set out its purpose at length in the [[coalition|Coalition]]'s own general channel, confirming that the object was to keep a Coalition member guild off the territory map, to an audience that included the guild it was aimed at.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Coalition general channel, 22 Jan 2018}} The target was Hall of Fame [HoF], a Coalition member since the alliance's December 2017 declaration of war.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Coalition channels, January 2018}}{{cite:drew1011-storytime|roster at the declaration of war, part 14, composed 22 Mar 2018}} He described the arrangement as a private understanding among several guilds that carried no obligation to be published, and said the name was his own coinage rather than anything the participants had agreed to.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Coalition general channel, 22 Jan 2018}} Coalition leadership rejected the distinction: a standing multi-guild commitment to target one guild was an alliance whatever it was called.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Coalition leadership channel, January 2018}}

The arrangement was a loose grouping of smaller guilds — SDU, ANO and Immortals Light, that is [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]], [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] and Immortals Light — and by the Coalition's own account the attacking was carried by two of them, Titans Valor and Immortals Light.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Coalition war channel, 4 Feb 2018}}

## The name

The author's name for it, the Alovilatious Treaty, appears in the Coalition's war channel both while the arrangement was running and afterwards, when members referred back to it.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Coalition war channel, 2018}} Within about an hour of the disclosure the Coalition had replaced it with a nickname derived from a pasta shape, and it was the nickname rather than the original that subsequently circulated.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Coalition general channel, 22 Jan 2018}} That nickname and its successors run through the general, leadership and war channels of the Coalition, [[the-federation|Federation]] and [[emperium-of-wynn|Emperium]] servers between January and October 2018, most heavily in February.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|three servers, January–October 2018}} When a second campaign opened on 10 February 2018 against the [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]], [[drew1011|Drew1011]] assigned it a second pasta nickname in the Coalition's leadership channel as a matter of routine.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Coalition leadership channel, 10 Feb 2018}}

Nothing suggests the participating guilds ever used either name of themselves, which leaves the episode known under vocabulary supplied entirely by its targets — and the author's own name for it surviving because the people mocking it wrote it down.

## The campaigns

The first campaign did not lapse of its own accord. On 7 February 2018 Hall of Fame declared in the Coalition's war channel that it had beaten the campaign outright, and by June 2018 members of a different alliance gave the same outcome: the guild had been attacked daily over the Cinfras area, had held, and was afterwards regarded as having won.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Coalition war channel, 7 Feb 2018; later accounts, June 2018}} How long the attacks ran was disputed among those describing it afterwards, one putting it at about a month and another at about a week.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|later accounts, 2018}} The campaign was expensive for its target regardless of the outcome: the guild reported losing the large majority of its bank over the period it was under attack.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Coalition channels, February 2018}}

In the second campaign, against the Kingdom of Foxes from 10 February 2018, the targets were agreed between two people rather than directed by one; Coalition members attributed its trigger to an insult from a Kingdom of Foxes member.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Coalition leadership and war channels, February 2018}} It ran in the last week of the Coalition's existence; the alliance broke apart on 16 February.{{cite:coalition-fracture-2018-discord-private|Coalition general channel, 16 Feb 2018}}

## The organiser's denial

The existence of the first arrangement was denied twice in Federation channels five weeks after it was announced, and the denials came from the man who had assembled it. On 21 February 2018, in the alliance's war channel, he denied that it had ever existed; on 28 February, in the leadership channel, he read [[drew1011|Drew1011]]'s written record of the 10 February action back to him, rejected it as fabricated, and demanded that the history be corrected.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Federation war and leadership channels, 21 and 28 Feb 2018}}

The messages announcing the January arrangement, setting out its purpose, and denying five weeks later that any treaty had existed all belong to one account, which changed handle from Ice to hot pink emperor between 22 January and 21 February 2018.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|the same account across the January and February 2018 messages}} Titans Valor, the guild he led, was by then a Federation member carrying the tag ANO, and was one of the three guilds that had done the attacking under the January arrangement.{{cite:thread-206094-p02|#34, 11 Dec 2017, quoting the opening post}}{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Coalition war channel, January–February 2018}}

Neither party disputed that something had happened on 10 February. What they disputed was whether it deserved the word treaty — and the objection came from the one person in a position to know.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Federation leadership channel, 28 Feb 2018}} It reads either as a warning that the treaty vocabulary describes loose target-sharing the participants themselves would not have recognised under that name, or as what a man says once an arrangement has become an embarrassment; nothing settles which. The denial was not a single outburst but a position held across two channels and two weeks, and both alliances went on to write rules against exactly the kind of arrangement he said had never happened.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Federation channels, February and July 2018}}

The disagreement was possible because the record and its subject were being made in the same rooms at the same time: Drew1011 was compiling a dated written history of the alliance era while the events it covers were still weeks old, and passages of it circulated in leadership channels during the period described.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Coalition and Federation leadership channels, early 2018}}{{cite:drew1011-storytime|parts 13–17, composed March–July 2018}}

## Afterwards

Both surviving alliances went on to legislate against arrangements of this kind. On 1 July 2018, after a secret non-aggression pact between a member guild and an enemy guild was exposed, the Federation's guild leaders voted to prohibit member guilds from holding war-related pacts or alliances with any guild outside the alliance, while expressly allowing community-only ones; the ban was written into a shared rules document two days later, alongside a prohibition on leaking alliance war information.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Federation guild-leader vote, 1–3 Jul 2018}} Concentrated attack by several guilds on one target, the mechanism these arrangements organised, is described further at [[territory-warfare|territory warfare]].{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|concentrated attack, 2018}}

**Angels of Eternal** [AoE] belonged to [[the-federation|the Federation]] for five months of 2018 and fought it for the three that followed. It was admitted for its war players, given no ground of its own, and left the day after its leader did.

## From the other side

It came out of the wreckage of [[war-syndicate|War Syndicate]]. In January 2018 it was named among the guilds assembled into a successor grouping under that bloc's remains,{{cite:angels-of-eternal-discord-private|January 2018}} and in the same month a [[coalition|Coalition]] representative acknowledged a pact with it that had stopped him intervening while its allies attacked one of his own guilds.{{cite:angels-of-eternal-discord-private|January 2018}} It first appears in the capture log on 4 January 2018.{{cite:territory-exchanges|first recorded capture, Angels Of Eternal, 4 Jan 2018}}

## Admitted in a minute

The Federation admitted three guilds during the week it was founded, and the records of the three admissions do not resemble each other. [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] was voted through eight to two. [[fantasy|Fantasy]] was admitted on written conditions. Angels of Eternal was introduced in a chat channel on 18 February 2018 by a member who described it as a guild from his side of the alliance with some very good war players, and two leaders agreed inside a minute, one reasoning that if it would help them, there was no reason not to.{{cite:angels-of-eternal-discord-private|18 Feb 2018}} No vote appears anywhere in the exchange.

## No ground of its own

By June 2018 the guild held no assigned territory at all and was warring only in areas the alliance had left unallocated. Its case was put to the Federation's leadership on 20 June by the leader of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]]: that the guild was still a member and fought in free-for-all areas only because it had been given nothing of its own; that it had held a great deal of ground before and remained war-active; that its members felt mistreated and were reconsidering; and that if it went over to the rival alliance it would become a real threat. He asked the leadership not to make leaving easy for them.{{cite:angels-of-eternal-discord-private|20 Jun 2018}} Another leader proposed at once that it be assigned territory.{{cite:angels-of-eternal-discord-private|leadership channel, 20 Jun 2018}}

The capture log contradicts him. He used the alliance's formal term — the guild was warring FFA, meaning the twenty-one territories the Federation had by then declared open to everyone — and on that ground it was barely fighting at all. Of its 304 captures in July, 242 fell outside the declared list, as did 66 of its captures from [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] and 79 from [[ex-nihilo|Ex Nihilo]] across June and July.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures by month and opposing guild, excluding declared free-for-all territories, Angels Of Eternal, Jun–Jul 2018}}{{cite:holders-of-le-announcements|free-for-all list circulated to member guilds, March 2018}}

That is not a mistake so much as an argument. The speaker was making the case for giving the guild territory, and a guild reduced to fighting over common ground is the strongest form that case can take — a characterisation by an interested party, made while the fighting it described was going on, and not borne out by where the fighting actually happened.

## The defection, and the month it was busiest

Its leader left for [[hackforums|HackForums]] on 21 July 2018 and began attacking Federation territory; within days the guild was being named among the enemy coalition, and in September it took part in the Confederation's assault.{{cite:angels-of-eternal-discord-private|21 Jul – Sep 2018}}

August 2018 was the heaviest month the guild ever had: 973 captures, 571 of them outside the published free-for-all list — well over twice its next-heaviest month measured the same way, and peaking at 105 off-list captures on 17 August.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures by month and day, excluding published free-for-all territories, Angels Of Eternal, Aug 2018}} Across August and September its off-list captures ran 271 from Ex Nihilo, 84 from [[the-divine-swords|The Divine Swords]], 48 from [[odysseia|Odysseia]], 42 from [[lunatic|Lunatic]], 38 from Kingdom Foxes and 32 from [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]] — Federation guilds, every one.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures by opposing guild, excluding published free-for-all territories, Angels Of Eternal, Aug–Sep 2018}}{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Federation free-for-all lists, 6 Aug and 8 Sep 2018}} That is contested ground rather than churn, and it was taken from the alliance it had belonged to five weeks earlier.

## Out within a day of its leader

The guild left the Federation on 22 July 2018, the day after its leader went to [[hackforums|HackForums]]. The alliance announced it in one line, alongside [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] leaving and Aphelion joining.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 7/22/2018}}

So the June warning was answered inside five weeks. Its advocate had told the leadership that a guild given nothing of its own might go over to the rival alliance and become a real threat; by late July it had gone, and by August it was taking more ground than it ever had while outside the alliance it had belonged to.{{cite:angels-of-eternal-discord-private|20 Jun 2018}} Its own fighting fell away afterwards — 15 captures in the whole of October across six days, only four of them outside the free-for-all list.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures by day, excluding published free-for-all territories, Angels Of Eternal, Oct 2018}}

