## The Emperator election

The alliance held two contested elections in 2017; neither was recorded on the forums.

![A Discord announcement by Paladin A'renos calling the vote for Emperator between Nitrogen2Oxygen and Drew1011, with the reaction tallies showing D = 30 and N = 31. From Drew1011's storytime account.](/images/chronicles/storytime/37-image.webp){{cite:drew1011-storytime|Emperator election screenshot, part 12}}

By Drew1011's narrative the contest opened when A'renos announced he no longer had time to run the alliance; three candidates stood — Nitrogen2Oxygen, joshaftel (ElegantDeath) and Drew1011 — and joshaftel withdrew to act as Drew1011's campaign manager in exchange for the promise of the Community Leader post. The vote ran a week and ended 30–31 in Nitrogen2Oxygen's favour.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 12, composed 26 Feb 2018}} Drew1011's application was an essay setting out his guild and alliance offices from 2015 onward.{{cite:the-emperial-truth|Drew1011's Emperator application essay}}

The alliance's own channels date it. A'renos opened the vote on 2 August 2017 — "the time has come for the alliance to elect the @Emperator. Today and for 3 days, the vote will be held in here" — with N to vote for Nitrogen2Oxygen and D for Drew1011, and the reaction counts under the message read D 30 and N 31.{{cite:emperium-announcements|marker 12, screenshot of the #voting channel, 2 Aug 2017}} That is three days rather than the week the narrative recalls, but the margin is the one it gives.

The vote closed on 4 August 2017 and the winner delivered an address to the membership.{{cite:emperium-constitution-2017-discord-private|4 Aug 2017}}

A second, better-dated poll followed. On 31 August 2017 Nitrogen2Oxygen asked the alliance to choose a Shadow Advisor between Shy_Phoenix, Angry_will, Drew1011 and Whimsicality; the tallies were 4, 5, 19 and 4.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|Shadow Advisor poll screenshot dated 08/31/2017, part 12}} Drew1011 held the post by 5 September 2017.{{cite:thread-202724|post #7, Aya quoting the opening post, 5 Sep 2017}}

![The Shadow Advisor poll of 31 August 2017, in which Drew1011 was elected 19 votes to 5, 4 and 4. From Drew1011's storytime account.](/images/chronicles/storytime/38-image.webp){{cite:drew1011-storytime|Shadow Advisor poll screenshot dated 08/31/2017, part 12}}

### A claim to have rigged it

On 26 February 2018 the losing candidate posted an instalment of his account of the election in an alliance channel. Beneath it the next day, a member wrote that he was the one who had rigged it, and that he had done so because another of the alliance's officers did not want that candidate to win.{{cite:kingdom-of-foxes-in-the-emperium-2017-discord-private|27 Feb 2018, the claim}} Asked how, he ruled out two methods put to him and then described making a second Discord account, bringing it into the alliance's server, giving it the roles a member of his guild would have carried, and voting with it for the winner.{{cite:kingdom-of-foxes-in-the-emperium-2017-discord-private|27 Feb 2018, the method}} The losing candidate answered with dismay and nothing else.{{cite:kingdom-of-foxes-in-the-emperium-2017-discord-private|27 Feb 2018, the reply}} Some hours later the candidate who had won said he had known the man was not acting on his behalf, and the man answered that he had been the winner's campaign manager.{{cite:kingdom-of-foxes-in-the-emperium-2017-discord-private|27 Feb 2018, the winner's reply}} The instalment itself, written the day before, has someone the losing candidate believed he had recruited to his own side in fact managing his opponent's.{{cite:kingdom-of-foxes-in-the-emperium-2017-discord-private|26 Feb 2018, the instalment's account of the campaign}}

Nothing followed the claim. No inquiry was opened, no denial was entered, the result was never revisited, and the alliance whose office was at stake had been finished for four months.{{cite:kingdom-of-foxes-in-the-emperium-2017-discord-private|February 2018}} Whether any fraudulent vote was cast was never established. What can be set beside it is the count: the margin was one reaction, and the largest lead either side held at any point was five votes — figures from the instalment of the 26th, which also has the poll running a week where the alliance's own channels ran it over three.{{cite:kingdom-of-foxes-in-the-emperium-2017-discord-private|26 Feb 2018, the instalment's figures}} Setting the two side by side is this article's doing and not the record's. The instalment was carried in two alliance channels a day apart, and the dates above are the first posting rather than the copy.{{cite:storytime-two-editions-2018-discord-private|the February instalments in both channels}}

An allegation of the same kind had already cost somebody his guild. A member accused in May 2017 of voting for himself in the Community Leader poll from more than one forum account was expelled by the guild he belonged to.{{cite:emperium-announcements|marker 4, screenshot of #general, 13 May 2017}}

### An unresolved question

Three sources describe the top of the alliance in October 2017 and no two agree.

- **Drew1011's forum profile** records the title "Emperator of the Emperium of Wynn (15 October 2017 — 16 October 2017)", a reign of a single day.{{cite:forums-wynncraft-com-members-drew1011-2157|Drew1011 forum profile title chain}}
- **Drew1011's own narrative** says he ran for Emperator and **lost**, 30–31, and that he afterwards won a by-election for Shadow Advisor. He never claims to have been Emperator.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 12, composed 26 Feb 2018}}
- **The second thread**, the only contemporaneous source, names Nitrogen2Oxygen as Emperator on 5 September 2017{{cite:thread-202724|post #7, Aya quoting the opening post, 5 Sep 2017}} and **Angry_will** as Emperator in the opening post as last revised at 3:59 PM on 17 October 2017, with Drew1011 as Shadow Advisor in both.{{cite:thread-202724|post #1, OP as last revised 17 Oct 2017}}

The three cannot be reconciled. A one-day Emperatorship between Nitrogen2Oxygen's departure and Angry_will's installation would fit every recorded date, since the Shadow Advisor was constitutionally the co-leader — but no source states that such a succession occurred. Angry_will, who was also the alliance's Economy Leader, appears in no other source.

The alliance's own channels make a one-day tenure unremarkable for that autumn. Leadership changed hands three times in eight weeks as the membership drained away, the office of Emperator passing between four holders in succession, one of whom passed it on within a day of receiving it.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|leadership succession, autumn 2017}}
## Why there was an Emperator

The office was a constitutional consequence rather than a founding one. The alliance capped itself at ten to fifteen guilds, counted anything beyond ten as an overflow, and required that an overflow of five or more "must create a brother alliance" carrying the same government and electing its own three leaders. Only then did an Emperator become necessary: a figurehead over both halves, barred from holding any other leadership post, elected by both alliances and the wider community, holding "over 50% of power" and removable only by an eighty-per-cent vote or by standing down.{{cite:emperium-announcements|6/11/2017, quoting the constitution}}

A’renos put the rule into effect on 11 June 2017, when the alliance passed seventeen guilds: a second Triarch would be raised, "meaning that instead of 3 Leaders there will be 6", and an Emperator elected who "can be anyone and everyone (other than Arenos)".{{cite:emperium-announcements|6/11/2017}} Members were asked which guilds they wanted to be grouped with, and how the split should be decided — by the Shadow Advisor, by the guild leaders, or by the High Council.{{cite:emperium-announcements|6/11/2017}}

The same day two guilds were branded under the constitution’s heresy clause. Pickles and Arcana were declared "Excommunicated Traitors" for "trying to bring down the alliance", with the announcement asking that it "be seen by everyone that these guilds are not friendly".{{cite:emperium-announcements|6/11/2017}}

The split stayed pending for six weeks. In late July the alliance said it would take place once the Emperator had been elected, and that the result would be a war alliance and a community alliance with six leaders between them.{{cite:emperium-constitution-2017-discord-private|late July 2017}} The Emperator was elected on 4 August. Whether the two alliances were ever separated was never recorded.

Two of the alliance's own leaders later described the office as subordinate in practice. In August 2018 the candidate who had won that vote pasted the alliance's own written definition of the office into a leaders channel, with his own name set beneath it as the holder, and dismissed it.{{cite:kingdom-of-foxes-in-the-emperium-2017-discord-private|22 Aug 2018, the definition}} The candidate he had beaten replied that the founder had structured the alliance to give himself total control, and that even the head of state answered to the founder's advisory office.{{cite:kingdom-of-foxes-in-the-emperium-2017-discord-private|22 Aug 2018, the reply}} Both were by then hostile to the founder, and one of them had held the office he was describing.

## Founding and the first map

The alliance held its first organisational session in April 2017, with representatives of its founding member guilds introducing themselves, and member guilds joined by swearing an oath.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|first session, April 2017}} Its first substantive act was cartographic: a colour-coded map dividing the territory into numbered zones for member guilds to claim, with some areas marked off-limits and others designated free-for-all or event zones.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|territory map, April 2017}} The constitution later gave that work to Land Consolidators sitting under the war order.{{cite:gdoc-1u97hsmpzu2lapij|The Emperial Truth, Ordo Malleus and Land Consolidators}}

Three offices were filled by election over six weeks in the spring — a War Leader on 30 April 2017, then the Community and Economy Leaders — together forming the body the alliance called the Triarch, which the High Council confirmed.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|elections, April–May 2017}} On 26 May 2017 the holder of the advisory office put himself forward for a new supreme position of Emperator, and the vote passed roughly fifteen to three.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|Emperator vote, 26 May 2017}} The objection raised against it was procedural: that whether to put him to a vote was the High Council's decision rather than his own, and that an advisory post carried no power to determine his fitness for the office — anyone, the challenger argued, could announce they wanted to be Emperator and call a vote on themselves.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|26 May 2017}}

The office was then rebuilt rather than re-run. On 5 June the same officeholder said in the high council channel that a real Emperator would have to be voted in, and set out a procedure — apply for the rank, a shortlist of five, a vote by both alliances, then the choice — referring members to a written constitutional document for the detail; on 10 June he said he would not be the Emperator himself.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|5–10 Jun 2017}} The published rules of 11 June are the result: the office would be elected, open to anyone in the community other than A'renos, and a candidate had to apply, be voted in by the whole community and finally be approved.{{cite:emperium-announcements|6/11/2017}} The May vote is better read as the thing that provoked the constitution than as the first holding of the office the constitution created.

## Offices and elections

Positions were filled by open application through a form — High Council, Judge, Community Organiser, General, Land Consolidator, Event Planner and Captain — rather than by appointment, and every guild was asked to supply two council members alongside a leader.{{cite:emperium-announcements|4/18/2017 and 4/26/2017}} Six trial judges were named on 22 April and further rounds recruited through June.{{cite:emperium-announcements|4/22/2017 and 6/1/2017}}

The Triarch was elected and re-elected in public. Hitoro won the Community Leader contest on 12 May 2017, taking 29 votes of about 81 against joshaftel’s 27, Uriah’s 11, Jack_Riddle’s 10 and Angry_will’s 4;{{cite:emperium-announcements|5/12/2017 and marker 3, the poll screenshot}} izzy4234 won Economy Leader on 20 May.{{cite:emperium-announcements|5/20/2017}} A second round opened in June with seven candidates for three posts — Angry_will and izzy4234 on economy, Tntdestroyd and SiuChau on war, and Rani, Szlili and Uriah on community — the economy and war votes held in Discord and the community vote on the forum thread.{{cite:emperium-announcements|6/17/2017}}

Guilds were admitted the same way, by a vote with a closing time. LE Flowers joined on 1 May 2017, IceBlue Team on 8 May, and TitanZ and the Divine Souls in mid-June.{{cite:emperium-announcements|5/1/2017, 5/8/2017, 6/14/2017 and 6/15/2017}}{{cite:emperium-voting|9/5/2017}}

The alliance also ran a service arm. The Blades of the Emperium — "The Demon Hunters" — were established on the forums in May 2017 as paid boss-slayers and guides for the wider community, applicants required to screenshot the thanks they received.{{cite:emperium-announcements|4/24/2017 and 5/8/2017}}

## The crisis of May 2017

Six weeks in, the leadership fell out. On 26 May 2017 izzy4234 announced that A’renos and DownDeeper "have not been the most 'cooperative' together in this alliance" and that a new war leader would take DownDeeper’s place on the Triarch.{{cite:emperium-announcements|5/26/2017}} Two days later A’renos warned that "[r]umors, lies and false accusations will not be tolerated here",{{cite:emperium-announcements|5/28/2017}} and the day after that answered a statement several members had made about him concerning events "over 1 year ago": he would neither confirm nor deny it, and anyone who wished to could "follow DownDeeper out of the door".{{cite:emperium-announcements|5/29/2017}}

He set the alliance six numbered objectives that month — guild sizes, standing down inactive guilds, council members per guild, events, more judges and organisers, and a war-build thread — with a deadline of 27 June. A fortnight before it, five of the six were still "WORK IN PROGRESS".{{cite:emperium-announcements|5/26/2017 and 6/8/2017}}

## What it built in three months

The alliance's first two announcements, on 7 April 2017, were a constitution and a roster.{{cite:emperium-announcements|4/7/2017}} Inside three months it had assembled more formal machinery than any other Wynncraft alliance in the record.

- **A written constitution**, the Emperial Truth, amended on 3 July to accommodate new offices.{{cite:emperium-announcements|7/3/2017}}
- **Three elected ministries** — war, community and economy, together the Triarch — filled by open application and a vote of the whole membership, with fixed rounds of re-election. Seven candidates stood for the three posts in June.{{cite:emperium-announcements|June 2017}}
- **A judiciary.** Trial judges were appointed on 22 April, a fortnight after founding, and by 21 June the bench ran to four ranks: Inquisitor, Judge, Exemplar and High Judicator.{{cite:emperium-announcements|4/22/2017 and 6/21/2017}} The same announcement graded the alliance's war ranks in tiers awarded by guild leaders and the founder — honours rather than places in a chain of command.{{cite:emperium-constitution-2017-discord-private|21 Jun 2017}}
- **A legislature.** Every guild sent representatives to a High Council, guilds that had not appointed theirs were named in an announcement, and a Speaker was elected from 10 July.{{cite:emperium-announcements|7/10/2017}}
- **Taxation.** The elected Economy Leader announced guild taxes, then dues assessed per guild, then where in the game he would be waiting to collect them.{{cite:emperium-announcements|guild taxes and alliance dues, 2017}}
- **An elected head of state.** The office of Emperator was defined on 11 June: open to anyone but the founder, entered by application, voted on by the whole community and confirmed by the founder, its holder's word to be law and its holder to hold half the power in the alliance.{{cite:emperium-announcements|6/11/2017}}
- **A published work programme**, a numbered list of what needed improving, reissued with each item marked complete, partly complete or in progress.{{cite:emperium-announcements|work programme, 2017}}
- **Monthly honours.** A Member of the Month was chosen by a vote of the community and a Guild of the Month named beside him, the prizes paid into guild banks.{{cite:emperium-constitution-2017-discord-private|monthly honours, 2017}}

Two of its constitutional habits have no parallel elsewhere. **Tied elections were settled by contest rather than a second ballot.** When the war leadership vote tied on 20 June the two candidates were given a week to prove which was the stronger at war; when the economy vote tied at twelve apiece, the candidates were set to compete over who could gather more for the alliance, the winner to take the office.{{cite:emperium-announcements|6/20/2017}} The test was fitness for the particular office rather than a further count of the same votes.

And it decided it was too large and planned to divide itself. On 11 June, holding seventeen guilds, it announced that an alliance should hold ten to fifteen and that a split was therefore needed — "no the alliance wont be breaking apart" — and asked members which guilds they wished to be grouped with.{{cite:emperium-announcements|6/11/2017}} The [[coalition|Coalition]] broke apart nine months later for the opposite reason: it admitted four guilds in a fortnight after saying it would admit none, and could not fit them on the map.

## The alliance that wrote everything down, and failed first

Its own diagnosis, given on 24 July 2017, named none of that machinery. What it named was participation: guilds with no involvement in the alliance, officers not doing the work their offices required, and a council gone idle.{{cite:emperium-announcements|7/24/2017}} The same day the founder issued instructions to his officers: list and remove the guilds taking no part, warn office-holders that inactive roles would be cut, hold fresh elections if the council stayed idle, and tighten the rules on how member guilds operated. The alliance had to carry on or it would die and its people would leave, he told them, and anyone who did not follow the instructions would lose their position.{{cite:emperium-constitution-2017-discord-private|24 Jul 2017}} Public meetings were still being held in late August.{{cite:emperium-constitution-2017-discord-private|late August 2017}} On 16 October a leader announced that he did not have the time to rebuild the alliance, and handed it on.{{cite:emperium-announcements|10/16/2017}} It had run six months.

[[the-federation|The Federation]] had no written constitution at all, ran on rules announced informally and never set down, and held the whole map for eight months and twenty-five days.{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|May 2018}} The comparison is this article's rather than the record's, and it cuts against the obvious reading: the alliance with a judiciary, a franchise, a tax system and an amendable constitution failed first, and said on the way down that what it lacked was people who turned up.

The Federation's silence was not simply an absence, either. Asked in August 2018 to replace its negotiated map with one that adjusted automatically to war activity, the [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]]' Viceroy refused and gave a reason: territory assignments were delicate, one mistake could kill an alliance, and a map that adjusted itself was exactly the mistake.{{cite:federation-territory-doctrine-2018-discord-private|23 Aug 2018}} He had watched [[coalition|the Coalition]] die of a redistribution six months earlier and cited it himself the same day. Where the Emperium wrote its rules down and the Federation did not, the second was in this instance holding a doctrine about what writing them down would do.

## The machinery in practice

The summer offensive was issued as a request. On 7 May 2017 the founder asked every member guild to take the territory that did not already belong to the alliance, leaving the province of Wynn until last and avoiding the ground already marked off-limits, and said plainly that this was a request and not an order.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|7 May 2017, the call}} A second leader turned it into a procedure within the hour, telling guilds to clear one region at a time and to post which region each was taking.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|7 May 2017, the procedure}} The next day the founder announced that the alliance's Discord would open to the wider community after the weekend and that nothing in it would be secret.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|8 May 2017, opening the server}} All war planning therefore had to be finished before the doors opened, and newcomers would get three warnings before a ban.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|8 May 2017, the conditions}}

Removal ran by ultimatum and then by ruling. On 27 May 2017 a leader named four member guilds in the leaders channel and gave them a day or two to report or face removal.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|27 May 2017}} On 7 June the Speaker reported a survey of the alliance's fifteen member guilds: twelve had answered him, and he named the three that had not.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|7 Jun 2017, the Speaker's survey}} The founder ruled within hours that two of the three were dead and that the third would stay in the alliance without a Discord presence.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|7 Jun 2017, the ruling}} Asked by the Speaker whether a vote was needed to remove them, he replied that they would not be voted out and that he was in contact with them through another service.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|7 Jun 2017, the question of a vote}}

The last purge was settled in a private channel in two minutes. On 4 October 2017 the alliance's leader and his second worked through the inactive members in the senior leaders' channel and agreed a test: judge the activity of the guild's leader rather than of the guild.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|4 Oct 2017, the criterion}} A guild whose leader was absent would never recover, they reasoned, while the rest were worth keeping in case the game's broken guild mechanics were repaired.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|4 Oct 2017, the reasoning}} Beasts [BST] and [[thenolifes|The No Lifes]] [TNL] were struck off on the spot and a third guild kept on longer.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|4 Oct 2017, the removals}}

Guilds that left gave reasons about the alliance rather than about a quarrel. Void of Amnesia applied on 17 May 2017 and was put to a vote the same evening, over an objection that it had only two high-level players.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|17 May 2017}} It resigned a month later, on 16 June, because the alliance was inactive.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|16 Jun 2017}} On 5 August the leader of another member guild announced its departure on the ground that it was not contributing much, and was wished well.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|5 Aug 2017}}

Its answer to being unable to grow was a second alliance rather than a split. On 17 August 2017 the founder told the High Council that since the Emperium could not get past fifteen guilds he would abandon the plan to divide it and build a separate alliance of identical structure instead, stocked only with new guilds, which he and the council would train and then feed into the Emperium.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|17 Aug 2017, the proposal}} Two council members objected at once, one of them saying he could see no purpose in it.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|17 Aug 2017, the objection}} No second body was ever founded.

It lost people it had trusted as readily as guilds it had not. On 13 July 2017 the High Council learned that a war captain who knew the alliance's war planning had joined another guild.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|13 Jul 2017}} Members judged the loss serious rather than funny, one saying the others were underestimating how bad it was, another cautioning that they did not yet know whether it was a betrayal at all.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|13 Jul 2017, the council's reaction}} Arcana [ARN] applied to join on 10 June 2017 and was branded an excommunicated traitor the following day.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|10 Jun 2017}}{{cite:emperium-announcements|6/11/2017}} By 14 July the leaders were counting it among the guilds hostile to them and putting the branding down to its conduct in the alliance's Discord, where the announcement had given the reason as an attempt to bring the alliance down.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|14 Jul 2017}} Whether it was admitted in between was never recorded.

The judiciary was argued out of existence a month before it was voted out. On 25 August 2017 the alliance's leader confirmed that one of its Judges had been dismissed.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|25 Aug 2017}} On 12 September that officer pressed for the judicial office to be revived and its applications reopened, and was refused, the leader having already closed them.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|12 Sep 2017, the refusal}} Two other senior figures argued in the channel that judges served no purpose where there was nothing to arbitrate, and that guild leaders and the council between them could do the same work.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|12 Sep 2017, the argument}} The ballot that abolished the bench opened four weeks later.

## The Speaker's office, August–October 2017

In the alliance's last three months the office that made the High Council work was vacated, refused, filled, suspended, and finally used against the body it spoke for. On 1 August 2017 the sitting Speaker posted that he was leaving the game for reasons outside it, that another Speaker would have to be elected, and that in the meantime the Speaker's powers might reasonably pass to the next Emperator.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-speaker-office-2017-discord-private|1 Aug 2017, the resignation}} Two days earlier the serving leader had told the leaders' channel that a further week of silence from him might force a demotion.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-speaker-office-2017-discord-private|30 Jul 2017}} The leader thanked him, altered his roles, and within a minute said a new Speaker was needed and wondered aloud whether a particular member should take it by default.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-speaker-office-2017-discord-private|1 Aug 2017, the succession}}

An application for the vacancy was refused four days later in a single line. On 5 August the founder, then holding the alliance's senior advisory office, told the High Council channel that he could not accept it because the applicant had in the meantime become War Leader.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-speaker-office-2017-discord-private|5 Aug 2017}} He cited no rule, nobody argued, and no vote on it was ever taken. When the office was filled again went unrecorded. On 14 September 2017 the serving leader announced in the High Council channel that the Speaker's role was being temporarily revoked for unexplained inactivity and that an explanation would restore it; the Speaker answered about an hour later that he had sent a private message about it.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-speaker-office-2017-discord-private|14 Sep 2017}}

A fortnight after being suspended the same officer dissolved the council's existing membership. On 29 September he told the alliance's leaders' channel that any High Council members their guilds had held before were out, that anyone who still wanted a seat had to apply through the announcements channel, and that each guild had a free entry and could put its own members forward as well.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-speaker-office-2017-discord-private|29 Sep 2017}}

The Speaker of that autumn was the leader of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]], whose expulsion on 11 October 2017 ended the alliance.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-speaker-office-2017-discord-private|the account across the alliance's channels}} The same account ran [[alovilatious-treaty|the multi-guild targeting arrangements of January 2018]] from inside the [[coalition|Coalition]]'s own server, and was banned from that server on 25 January 2018.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-speaker-office-2017-discord-private|the Coalition ban, 25 Jan 2018}}{{cite:undated-observations-verified-discord-private|the ban of 25 Jan 2018 and the ground given for it}}

## Constitutional activity and decline

The alliance's constitutional business intensified as it emptied. In June 2017 the holder of the advisory office proposed splitting it into two brother alliances under a shared structure headed by an elected Emperator, so that the failure of one would leave the other standing.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|brother alliances, June 2017}}{{cite:emperium-announcements|6/11/2017}} A proposal followed to establish an independent War Department run by guild owners, which would have removed the Judges and the High Council from war decisions entirely; it met strong resistance and was not adopted.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|War Department proposal, June 2017}} The Speaker election of July 2017 was contested procedurally rather than politically, earlier candidates withdrawing amid a dispute over whether the advisory officeholder could simply appoint the role rather than put it to a vote.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|Speaker election, July 2017}} The Economy Leader's proposal of a mandatory monthly contribution from each member guild drew objection both to the amount and to the decision to call it a tax.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|contribution proposal, 2017}}

In July 2017 the alliance declined a membership application after two near-even High Council votes, on the stated grounds that it was too unstable to take on new members.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|membership vote, July 2017}} By early August the High Council had shrunk to around sixteen active members, prompting a recruitment drive aimed at getting member guilds to fill their allotted seats{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|High Council recruitment, August 2017}}, and a proposal to merge all the alliance's smaller member guilds into a single large guild was rejected unanimously.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|merger proposal, 2017}}

Two decisions state the position more precisely than any summary. On 31 August 2017 the High Council voted against filling the War Leader position at all, noting that warring was inactive and that every previous holder of the office had left the alliance; the role was put on hold rather than contested.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|War Leader vote, 31 Aug 2017}} Four weeks later, with the membership visibly draining away, the leadership published a full rewrite of the power structure defining five ranked tiers, from the Emperator and the advisory office at the top down to honorary members, demoting the Triarch and Speaker offices in the process.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|constitutional rewrite, late September 2017}} The High Council was placed on hiatus for inactivity in September and then restructured so that a seat required a written application rather than appointment by a member guild's leader — done by the Speaker on 29 September 2017, a fortnight after his own role had been suspended.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|High Council restructure, September 2017}}{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-speaker-office-2017-discord-private|29 Sep 2017}}

The expulsion that ended the alliance was decided by four leaders in a private channel. On the evening of 11 October 2017 they counted three to one, with one abstention, to ban the member who had called the previous day's ballot to remove the head of state, and the ban was carried out at once; no public vote on it was ever held.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|11 Oct 2017, the leadership vote}}{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|expulsion, October 2017}} Within the hour a leader reported that Tranquility [PaT] was leaving over the removal of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]], argued that losing what he called the alliance's best warring guild was not worth it, and proposed readmitting it on condition that its leader was not made Speaker again — noting that the man had been complaining outside the alliance's own server that it was run as a dictatorship.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|11 Oct 2017, the aftermath}} Titans Valor's own history gives the same date for its expulsion and records Peace and Tranquility leaving with it.{{cite:gdoc-1fev2xrzlfbeo2ll|Oct 2017}} A former holder of the senior office, by then holding no formal position, repeatedly attempted to direct the alliance afterwards — demanding status reports, pressing for the removed guild to be re-invited with a written apology, and reposting the alliance's old recruitment thread — and the serving leaders refused to recognise any authority.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|autumn 2017}} On the morning of 10 October 2017 a member relayed to the leaders' channel that he was asking every guild for a written account of its recent activity and of the alliance's direction by the following day, and one leader answered that nothing worth reporting had happened.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-speaker-office-2017-discord-private|10 Oct 2017, the demand}} That afternoon the serving leader told the same channel that the man had no right to tell them what to do, and reissued the same deadline in his own name, complaining that only one guild report had reached him.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-speaker-office-2017-discord-private|10 Oct 2017, the refusal and the reissue}} By 4 November 2017 the remaining leaders concluded that the alliance had ceased to exist, its former member guilds having regrouped elsewhere, and on 3 December the Discord server was pruned to three people.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|4 Nov and 3 Dec 2017}}

One of its leaders, discussing in September 2017 why war-focused alliances did not last, observed that an earlier alliance built solely around war had survived about four months.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|leadership discussion, September 2017}}

## A’renos on what it was for

He set out the alliance’s purpose at length on 21 May 2017, and placed himself outside it: "I am the Shadow Advisor of this alliance, but I am not its leader. I oversee what happens, and I advise." The goals he gave were "[t]he unification of the whole guild community of Wynn as well as the destruction of all guild hate", and he traced them to a defeat: "When [[ordo-malleus|Ordo Malleus]] fell apart, I was saddened by what happened, and I have made great improvements into making sure those mistakes do not happen again."{{cite:emperium-announcements|5/21/2017}}

The same address named the sitting Triarch as DownDeeper, izzy4234 and Hitoro, and closed on the line he posted as an image beside it: "They can destroy what i care about, but i can remake it."{{cite:emperium-announcements|5/21/2017}}

## The channels

The alliance’s announcement channel opens on 7 April 2017, a month before its forum thread, with the constitution posted as a link and a roster of eleven guilds named with their leaders and representatives: Libertas, UltimateXeons, NeoPancakes, Sinners, Beasts, The Mage Legacy, Jaguars, Wynn Theory, Boom, BuildCraftia and Shadow Kittens.{{cite:emperium-announcements|4/7/2017}}

Offices were filled by open application through a form rather than appointment, for High Council, Judge, Community Organiser, General, Land Consolidator, Event Planner and Captain{{cite:emperium-announcements|4/18/2017}}, and six trial judges were named on 22 April — among them three from [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]], including [[whimsicality|Whimsicality]].{{cite:emperium-announcements|4/22/2017}} The pinned role list of 21 September 2017 gives the government at its height: Nitrogen2Oxygen as Emperator, [[drew1011|Drew1011]] as Shadow Advisor, Angry_will on economy, [[iceresistance|IceResistance]] on community, and the war leader’s post empty.{{cite:emperium-info|9/21/2017}}

Nine rules were pinned beside it, four of them about conduct and one — "Do not attack any territories owned by a guild within the alliance" — the only military clause.{{cite:emperium-info|9/26/2017}}

Decisions were taken by reaction vote in a channel of their own, and loosely. A vote of 27 August 2017 set the triarch’s shifts at two months and abolished the war advisor outright; another lowered the alliance’s taxes, with a note that "angry’s vote counts as 3" and that "all visitor votes on the taxes will be deleted".{{cite:emperium-voting|8/27/2017 and 8/29/2017}} Guild applications were put to the same channel with a closing time.{{cite:emperium-voting|9/5/2017}} When a member was accused of using multiple forum accounts to vote for himself in the community leader poll, the guild he was in expelled him.{{cite:emperium-announcements|marker 4, screenshot of #general, 13 May 2017}}

## The last ballots

The alliance put its own arrangements to its members throughout, and the pace tells the story of its last three months. On 27 August 2017 it settled a batch of constitutional questions in a single day — ministerial terms fixed at two months, an advisory post abolished, the voice channels kept, taxes lowered — and published the results two days later.{{cite:emperium-voting-record-2017-discord-private|27–29 Aug 2017}} Guild applications went to the membership on a standard form with the results announced: one refused on 7 September 2017, Peace and Tranquility [PaT] admitted on the 13th, a returning guild readmitted on the 23rd by seven votes to one, and a further guild admitted on 11 October by the same margin.{{cite:emperium-voting-record-2017-discord-private|admission votes, 7 Sep – 11 Oct 2017}} Tranquility had applied on the alliance's second forum thread the day before its vote was taken.{{cite:thread-202724-p02|p2 #26 and #27, 12 Sep 2017}} The tallies were small against a body the alliance elsewhere put above a hundred.{{cite:emperium-voting-record-2017-discord-private|guild admission votes, 2017}} Offices were still being filled by published application in the alliance's final month: two applications for Speaker went up in full on 28 September 2017, each answering a standard set of questions on guild leadership and prior experience, and the vote closed the following day.{{cite:emperium-voting-record-2017-discord-private|28–29 Sep 2017}} The franchise was uneven by design in at least one respect — one member's vote counted as three — and votes cast by visitors on the tax question were deleted.{{cite:emperium-voting-record-2017-discord-private|franchise and the tax vote, 2017}} A member whose conduct had been questioned had his continued membership put to the whole community, and the vote acquitted him.{{cite:emperium-voting-record-2017-discord-private|conduct vote, 2017}}

On 27 September the Emperator told the alliance that he felt he was making it too much like a dictatorship, and opened a vote on what should be done with the written constitution — with deletion among the options.{{cite:emperium-voting-record-2017-discord-private|27 Sep 2017}}

Then the machinery failed on itself. On 10 October a member called for a vote to remove the Emperator from office. The motion set the bar at ninety per cent of the membership{{cite:emperium-voting|10/10/2017}} — not the eighty per cent of a vote across both alliances the constitution had fixed in June.{{cite:emperium-announcements|6/11/2017, quoting the constitution}} Which of the two governed was never argued. It was closed the same day: with the vote standing at nine to nothing and the alliance and its honorary ranks numbering one hundred and sixteen, removal could not be reached.{{cite:emperium-voting-record-2017-discord-private|10 Oct 2017}} Nine people voted, all nine voted to remove him, and he stayed — not because the alliance decided he should, but because the provision for vacating the office demanded a turnout the alliance no longer had.

The dismantling took five more days. The judges were put to a vote on 11 October and were gone by the 15th, a judiciary appointed a fortnight after the founding abolished five days before the end.{{cite:emperium-voting-record-2017-discord-private|11–15 Oct 2017}} The officer who ran the ballot said immediately afterwards that the alliance had finished removing things and would now start adding them.{{cite:emperium-voting-record-2017-discord-private|15 Oct 2017}} On the 15th a survey asked members where they wanted the alliance to go, and a member posted an invitation to a different server; on the 16th a leader said he had not the time to rebuild it.{{cite:emperium-voting-record-2017-discord-private|15–16 Oct 2017}}

An alliance that had written down a constitution, a judiciary and a franchise dismantled all three by referendum in its final three weeks — and its franchise, asked to remove the man who had said he was becoming a dictator, could not remove anybody.

## Decline and fall

The leadership thinned through the late summer. On 4 September 2017 Nitrogen2Oxygen wrote in the first thread: "Such a sad time. Arenos leaves, [ElegantDeath] leaves and now my bot gets hecked"{{cite:thread-188024-p08|p8 #149, 4 Sep 2017}} — the alliance's thread-keeper and its Community Leader gone within weeks, alongside a compromised Discord bot that had mass-banned visitors.{{cite:thread-188024-p08|p8 #148, 4 Sep 2017}} The replacement thread he promised was posted the same day.{{cite:thread-202724|post #2, Drew1011, 4 Sep 2017}}

The community guild-timeline image records "10/17/17 Emperium Falls".{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, community guild-timeline image (imgur seklYAQ)}} Drew1011, then its Shadow Advisor, wrote that by mid-October "the Emperium was completely dead at that point", and dates his own move to found the [[coalition|Coalition]] to the same weeks.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 13, composed 4 Mar 2018}} The first thread's own last two posts are dated 17 October 2017: Paladin A'renos writing "bumity bump bump lets get this back up", and the reply "necro!!! alert alert!!".{{cite:thread-188024-p08|p8 #154 and #155, 17 Oct 2017}}

## Events

The constitution devoted more space to events than to war. It listed royal balls, Nether PVP tournaments, races, skin design and parkour challenges, drop parties and a Demon Hunter and Explorer Service{{cite:gdoc-1u97hsmpzu2lapij|"The Emperial Truth", Community Events}}, alongside a Guinness Wynn Records, a member and guild of the month, yearly member awards and a guide to guilds titled "How to Guild - An Idiots guide to guilds"{{cite:gdoc-1u97hsmpzu2lapij|"The Emperial Truth", Community Events, list}}; among special events it recorded that an in-game marriage had already been held.{{cite:gdoc-1u97hsmpzu2lapij|"The Emperial Truth", Special Events}} The ball was inherited outright: "This event has been regarded as the best event created by the Ordo Malleus, and will be implemented in the Emperium."{{cite:gdoc-1u97hsmpzu2lapij|"The Emperial Truth", Balls}}

Six war events were sketched, among them a whole-map free-for-all, a King of the Hill and a Capture the Capital{{cite:gdoc-1u97hsmpzu2lapij|"The Emperial Truth", War Events, list}}, each to be given a story so that guilds fought a scenario rather than a scoreboard{{cite:gdoc-1u97hsmpzu2lapij|"The Emperial Truth", War Event Explanation}}. Referees were to photograph the map through the day, and penalties fell on guilds that left territories undefended until the last minute or bribed others to hold land with token defences.{{cite:gdoc-1u97hsmpzu2lapij|"The Emperial Truth", War Event Explanation, penalties}} One worked example set UltimateXeons as raiders of the canyon against [[libertas|Libertas]] as the defenders of Almuj{{cite:gdoc-1u97hsmpzu2lapij|"The Emperial Truth", War Events, example 1}}; another opened the Nether portal against the province of Wynn, with victory to whichever side held Nemract, Troms and Ragni.{{cite:gdoc-1u97hsmpzu2lapij|"The Emperial Truth", War Events, example 2}} The Nether tournament had its own rules: sixteen entrants in four groups of four, referees in each, up to two builds banned in advance, and a prohibition on standing still to farm points off a passive player.{{cite:gdoc-1u97hsmpzu2lapij|"The Emperial Truth", Nether Event}}

## Legacy

The Emperium's people outlasted it, and several of them reassembled elsewhere. Nitrogen2Oxygen resurfaced as DiamondDeities' representative in [[aesir-pact|the Aesir Pact]] in 2018 alongside [[simplyinfinity|simplyinfinity_]] of Sins of Seedia;{{cite:thread-227373|post #1, opened 5 Jun 2018, last revised 21 Jul 2018}} IceResistance's [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] became one of the longest-running guilds in the record; and IceBlueTeam, TheNoLifes and Sins of Seedia all reappear in later alliance rosters. Four guilds named in Drew1011's roster of [[war-syndicate|War Syndicate]] at the December 2017 declaration of war — BuildCraftia, Sins of Seedia, Titans Valor and Beasts — were Emperium members during 2017, Tranquility applied that September, and the Emperium's War Leader in mid-2017 was TnTdestroyd, whom the same account names as War Syndicate's unofficial leader. The possible continuity between the two blocs is set out on the War Syndicate page. As an institution the Emperium left less: no successor claimed the name, and its constitution of Emperators, Judges and Brother Alliances was not imitated by the war blocs that followed.

The Empire of Nemract ran community events through 2021, and its guild thread carried the Empire branding until July 2023.

## History
The union was formed on 17 August 2020 by [[The Simple Ones]] [ILQ] and Mystic Woods.{{cite:community-guild-timeline}} From 30 November 2020 it was branded “The Empire of Nemract … | ILQ + Aeu”{{cite:thread-278123|the thread title carrying the ILQ + Aeu branding}}; the “Aeu” tag belonged to **Admiralia**.{{cite:thread-278123|post #1 (OP as last edited 18 Apr 2023)}} Admiralia was a member from that rebrand; whether Mystic Woods was renamed or replaced at that point was never recorded. Its empress held court titles (“Empress of Nemract, Queen of Aphelion”), and the Empire hosted community events through 2021.

On 30 November 2020 the union ran to five guilds. Beside The Simple Ones and Admiralia it named Mystic Woods [MYC], Nefarious Ravens [NFR] and a guild tagged [Apa], and it was aligned with, though not a member of, the [[Artemis]] alliance.{{cite:thread-278123|post #7, 30 Nov 2020, quoting the opening post}} Nefarious Ravens signed the Treaty of Damask with The Simple Ones on 21 May 2021 and declared independence.{{cite:community-guild-timeline|21 May 2021}}

By November 2021 the Empire described itself as “a group of guilds under one foreign policy, united to stand and fight together” and was a member of [[Valhalla]]. Only The Simple Ones and Admiralia were named as member guilds by then, and recruits had to hold a class of at least combat level 40.{{cite:thread-278123-p15|p15 #283, 22 Nov 2021, quoting the opening post}} The alliances named in the last state of the thread, in April 2023, were Kanzeon, the Concorde and [[smtn elf]].{{cite:thread-278123|post #1 (OP as last edited 18 Apr 2023); Kanzeon and the Concorde appear in no other archived source}}

### The court

The Empire was governed through a court of titled officers. Its thread names twelve of them under noble titles — the empress **XeraAndromeda** as owner, Crown Prince GrandAdmiralAce_ and Chancellor Count MightyShaper as chiefs, and nine more ranked count, viscount, viscountex, baroness or baron — each carrying post-nominal letters for the Empire's own orders, awarded in up to three grades{{cite:thread-278123|post #1 (OP as last edited 18 Apr 2023)}}.

The court grew as the Empire aged. In November 2020 it ran to six — the empress, the crown prince, two barons and two viscounts — none of them yet carrying post-nominals.{{cite:thread-278123|post #7, 30 Nov 2020, quoting the opening post}} A year later there were eleven, the orders were in use, and a second prince and a chancellor had been added.{{cite:thread-278123-p15|p15 #283, 22 Nov 2021, quoting the opening post}}

Recruits were asked to be "somewhat active in our community" and told that "participation in guild wars are completely optional though preferred", and those accepted were invited to [[The Simple Ones]], named as "our main guild"{{cite:thread-278123|post #1 (OP as last edited 18 Apr 2023)}}. The wars clause and the main-guild clause were both in the thread by November 2020.{{cite:thread-278123|post #7, 30 Nov 2020, quoting the opening post}}

The thread was locked between March 2022 and January 2023, and reopened on 8 January 2023 at its owner’s request; her first post back read “We are back gamers”.{{cite:thread-278123-p17|p17 #332–333, 8 Jan 2023}} It ran on under the Empire branding to a last post dated 18 July 2023.{{cite:thread-278123-p19|p19 #367, 18 Jul 2023}} No dissolution was ever announced. No war between The Simple Ones and Mystic Woods was ever recorded. They took ground from each other seventy-eight times between September 2019 and June 2026, over forty-eight distinct territories, thirty of them in September 2019 alone and twenty-eight after the union was declared.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges with The Simple Ones and Mystic Woods on opposite sides, by month, 2 Sep 2019 – 27 Jun 2026 UTC, raw: 78 across 48 territories, 30 in Sep 2019, 28 after 17 Aug 2020}}

## Membership
{{alliance:Empire of Nemract}}

Every alliance of the period both spied and was spied on, and the incidents are distributed evenly enough across guilds and years that no bloc stands out as unusually secretive or unusually porous.{{cite:espionage-and-infiltration-discord-private|2015–2019}}

## Alt accounts

The standard instrument was the alt account. Guild ownership was transferred through one presented as a relative of the buyer; an alt joined a rival guild through its public recruitment thread for the sole purpose of infiltrating it; and a guild leader entered an alliance's Discord under a second identity to obtain member roles and read its internal channel.{{cite:espionage-and-infiltration-discord-private|infiltration incidents, 2015–2018}} That last case ended in a ban, the alliance's leadership confirming the second identity and later citing the deception as the specific reason the person was barred from its server.{{cite:espionage-and-infiltration-discord-private|alliance Discord ban}}

Alt accounts were also used to hold guilds rather than merely to watch them. In October 2018 [[the-federation|the Federation]] found that one of its own member guilds was being run through the account of somebody who had already left the alliance, which its leaders called treachery outright.{{cite:federation-collapse-2018-discord-private|Federation leadership channels, 30 Oct 2018}} Where multi-accounting crossed into breaking the game's rules, alliances reported one another to the moderators: in January 2018 [[coalition|Coalition]] leadership reported a member for running additional guilds through alt accounts purely in order to war more, and those guilds were deleted.{{cite:espionage-and-infiltration-discord-private|moderator report, January 2018}}

### Nobody treated it as a scandal

The absence of objection is as consistent as the practice. In January 2018 the [[coalition|Coalition]] worked out that one of its new members was an alternate account run by an opposing leader to gather information; in the same week, and in the same channels, members of another guild described an alternate of their own that a rival had accepted through its public recruitment thread. Nobody suggested the two cases called for different judgements.{{cite:espionage-as-institution-discord-private|January 2018}} A member guild was expelled from the alliance on evidence handed over by the alliance's own enemy, and that was thought good enough.{{cite:espionage-as-institution-discord-private|2018}}

It shaped what guilds asked for. The first request one guild made on leaving [[luna|Luna]] for the new [[terra|Terra]] coalition in August 2019 was that its leadership roles be hidden, for fear of informants.{{cite:espionage-as-institution-discord-private|August 2019}} In the settlement after that war another guild argued for thirty-three territorial claims by explaining that it and four subguilds had been operating inside the defeated alliance for months; the objection it met was that thirty-three was too many.{{cite:espionage-as-institution-discord-private|September 2019}}

That no one in four years and three alliances objected in principle is this article's reading rather than the record's, since an absence is weak evidence taken one instance at a time.

### A credential rather than a confession

On 16 May 2018 [[the-federation|the Federation]] worked out that an opposing leader was sitting in its own war channel. [[drew1011|Drew1011]]'s advice to the advisory council was to give no sign of knowing: keep the channel looking ordinary, go on discussing the war in it, and say nothing specific or sensitive.{{cite:cheating-and-enforcement-discord-private|16 May 2018}}

A member asked whether it really mattered that the man was there. Drew1011 said it did — that an informant in war planning is worth something however small the advantage, and that this is not obvious to anyone who has never run one. Then he gave his evidence: the alternate account he had kept inside [[war-syndicate|War Syndicate]]'s war channels during the invasion of the previous December.{{cite:cheating-and-enforcement-discord-private|16 May 2018}}

He had built the remedy that morning. Hours before the exchange, on the same day, he created a restricted channel, told guild leaders to give the new role only to their most trusted chiefs and leadership, and pinned it; it replaced the open war-discussion channel for anything sensitive.{{cite:espionage-as-institution-discord-private|16 May 2018}} The sequence of the day was therefore to discover that an enemy was reading the war channel, build a smaller one he was not in, and only then explain to the council why it mattered.

The remedy shows what the alliance could not do. It had no way of assessing trust itself, so it delegated the vetting to each member guild's judgement about its own people — a single security perimeter drawn twenty-odd times over, separately, by twenty-odd guilds.{{cite:espionage-as-institution-discord-private|16 May 2018}}

What happened next is the point. The following message asks which of two servers he means; he answers; the member observes that he had not been around then. The conversation returns to whether the enemy's spy matters, and moves on. Nobody remarks on the propriety of it at all.{{cite:cheating-and-enforcement-discord-private|16 May 2018}} He was not admitting to something — he was citing it as the reason his advice should carry, and the channel took it that way.

## Leaks

Leadership channels leaked repeatedly, and were known to leak. The Coalition stripped a guild leader of his roles in February 2018 for passing alliance information to enemies, and separately removed a member guild after a rival leader supplied evidence that it had leaked internal material.{{cite:coalition-founding-2017-discord-private|discipline, February 2018}} The Federation spent days in May 2018 investigating a leak of its leader chat, which it knew about only because the recipient was circulating screenshots of it.{{cite:espionage-and-infiltration-discord-private|Federation leak investigation, May 2018}} [[the-consortium|The Consortium]] declared its leadership channel compromised in March 2019 after private discussion reached a rival alliance's leader.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|leak, 25 Mar 2019}}

The institutional response to a leak was consistently to create a smaller room rather than to change who was in the larger one. After the May 2018 leak the Federation built a new restricted channel limited to trusted chiefs and guild leaders, expressly to take sensitive business out of the open war channel.{{cite:espionage-and-infiltration-discord-private|Federation restricted channel, May 2018}} Screenshots were the period's evidentiary currency throughout — used to prove that a leak had happened, to prove what a rival alliance's leadership had said, and, in the Federation's case, circulated by the beneficiary of a leak, which is how the leak was found.{{cite:espionage-and-infiltration-discord-private|use of screenshots as evidence, 2018}}

## Moles and disinformation

The most consequential case was retrospective. Coalition members identified a figure inside the [[war-syndicate|War Syndicate]] alliance, known to them at the time only by a codename, as having fed them intelligence for months, and connected his exposure to the collapse of the guild he had founded.{{cite:espionage-and-infiltration-discord-private|Coalition accounts of the War Syndicate period}} The identification was made afterwards by the side he was said to have been helping.

It was routine well before the autumn. By May 2018 a Kingdom Foxes war captain had been caught passing information to the other side for about a week, a Federation-aligned member was relaying territory assignments into the rival alliance's channels, and Federation leaders who walked into the rival Discord themselves were recognised and banned within minutes.{{cite:enemy-alliances-2018-discord-private|May–July 2018}}

By September 2018 the two largest alliances were running agents inside each other, and the traffic went both ways inside a fortnight. The Federation admitted a guild at the start of the month and removed it within about two days, after screenshots showed its leader passing internal material to the rival alliance; the guild was then attacked and reduced.{{cite:federation-decline-2018-discord-private|September 2018}} The intelligence was acted on: the Federation's leadership circulated the rival alliance's plan to attack its two Wynn-province guilds on a named day, obtained from a source inside that alliance.{{cite:federation-decline-2018-discord-private|early September 2018}}

Infiltration also ran the other way, as deliberate disinformation. A Federation member operating inside the rival alliance in September 2018 fed it false information about when the Federation was active, so that a coordinated attack was scheduled for what was in fact the alliance's peak activity window.{{cite:espionage-and-infiltration-discord-private|disinformation, September 2018}} By the end of the period intelligence work had become a routine alliance function: the Federation was compiling informant reports on rival alliances' contingency plans in 2018, and in 2019 the [[terra|Terra]] coalition intercepted and decoded advance warning of a raid and told its guilds to prepare without revealing that they knew.{{cite:espionage-and-infiltration-discord-private|counter-intelligence, 2018–2019}}

## Disclosure

Not everything covert stayed covert, and several of the clearest cases were volunteered rather than discovered. In December 2016 a newly appointed advisor to the [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] told its council, unprompted, that she still had access to a rival guild's private council channel and had been passing on what she read there.{{cite:kingdom-of-foxes-government-2016-discord-private|State Council, December 2016}} A guild leader running one alliance's foreign relations disclosed to his own council in the same month that he had kept an alt account inside a rival guild during an earlier war.{{cite:espionage-and-infiltration-discord-private|December 2016}}

Guilds sometimes arrived at a new alliance still running operations against the old one, and said so. A guild admitted to the Federation in September 2018 disclosed that it was actively feeding intelligence out of the alliance it had just left, including that alliance's roster.{{cite:espionage-and-infiltration-discord-private|September 2018}} In September 2019, during the raid that broke [[luna|Luna]]'s hold on the map, a guild fighting on the Luna side revealed mid-operation that it had been informing for the attacking coalition since that coalition's founding, while another negotiated a separate non-aggression deal in the middle of the same battle.{{cite:terra-alliance-2019-discord-private|the raid of 7 Sep 2019}}

Infiltration was proposed out loud as well as confessed. On 13 September 2019 the leader of a [[terra|Terra]] member guild told the alliance's leaders' channel that he had written a genuine application to join [[imperial|Imperial]] in order to see that guild's community from the inside, and asked them what they thought.{{cite:terra-institutions-2019-discord-private|leaders' channel, 13 Sep 2019}} The first question put back to him was who would take over his own guild if the application succeeded; he named a member.{{cite:terra-institutions-2019-discord-private|leaders' channel, 13 Sep 2019}}

The costliest case ran the other way, from inside a guild to its destruction. On 15 October 2019 the leader of MasterWynnLords told Terra's leadership channel that somebody who had joined and appeared to be helping with a reclaim had instead drained eighty thousand from the guild bank and left.{{cite:terra-october-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 15 Oct 2019}} The same person then joined the main enemy guild in the region and took part in wiping MasterWynnLords from its Rymek holdings and its other claims.{{cite:terra-october-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 15 Oct 2019}} That is the victim's account, and nothing else about the episode was recorded.

## Founding

The guild was created on 24 June 2018, the same day its founder led a walkout from [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]] taking several of that guild's war captains with him.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|24 Jun 2018}}{{cite:hall-of-fame-guild-profile-discord-private|June 2018}} The date does not rest on anyone's memory: a member queried the game's own guild records in the alliance channel the following day, and the reply carried the creation date, the tag and a membership of eleven.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|guild record query, 25 Jun 2018}}

[[the-federation|The Federation]] admitted it four days later, the vote passing despite recorded resentment about the split that had created it, and announced the membership in its own channel on 30 June.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|28–30 Jun 2018}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 6/30/2018}}

## Inside the alliance

Its first significant act as a member was to expose another one. On 1 July 2018 it posted the screenshots that surfaced Hall of Fame's undisclosed non-attack pact with an enemy guild — the guild its own founder had left a week earlier — and the alliance voted the same day to prohibit member guilds from holding war pacts with outsiders.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|1 Jul 2018}}{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Federation guild-leader vote, 1–3 Jul 2018}} A leader of the guild remarked later that month, half in jest, that founding it had caused more disruption inside the alliance than the war being fought against the alliance from outside.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|July 2018}}

It absorbed a second guild within five weeks: Verinian Trials, which had left the Federation alongside Hall of Fame in July, merged into it in the last days of the month, the two guilds' combined war totals cited as evidence of the scale.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|late July 2018}} It reorganised its leadership into a council in early August on its founder's proposal, making a leader of the absorbed guild one of its own.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|August 2018}}

It was a standing target throughout. Members recorded the opposing coalition taking one Federation guild at a time and named this one as the current target; in July it was under attack from four guilds at once while holding its territory count steady, and a further assault that month named eight attacking guilds.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|July 2018}}

In late July it gave several of its own assigned territories to a member guild that held none, carrying the proposal by a vote among its own leadership — and another guild's leader objected that assignments were not the member guilds' to redistribute.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|late July 2018}}{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|July 2018}} That exchange is described from the alliance's side at [[the-federation|the Federation]].

## The end

On 24 August 2018 its leadership and active members left it, together with those of [[lunatic|Lunatic]], to found a combined guild called [[odysseia|Odysseia]]. The Federation declared Ex Nihilo out of the alliance the same day, its remaining owner intending to keep the emptied guild.

The alliance took longer to establish that than to lose the guild. On 25 August 2018 the leader of [[emorians|Emorians]] told the leadership channel that Ex Nihilo was already out and that [[lunatic|Lunatic]] was under new leadership.{{cite:federation-lunatic-mox-departure-august-2018-discord-private|25 Aug 2018, the leadership channel}} The alliance's map-maker, reading the same channel while redrawing the territory allocation, had to ask whether Ex Nihilo had left; she had not known.{{cite:federation-lunatic-mox-departure-august-2018-discord-private|25 Aug 2018, the map-maker}} Seventeen minutes later she asked the territory committee what the guild leaders thought of giving the ground Ex Nihilo had held to [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] on the next map, and two agreed within twenty minutes.{{cite:federation-lunatic-mox-departure-august-2018-discord-private|25 Aug 2018, the reallocation}} No departure notice was ever issued: a search of both announcement channels between 20 August and 15 September 2018 returns the one-line clarification about Lunatic of 31 August and the first weekly bulletin of 1 September, which records the fact in its own summary.{{cite:federation-lunatic-mox-departure-august-2018-discord-private|both announcement channels, 20 Aug – 15 Sep 2018}}{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|24 Aug 2018}}

Ezura led the guild [[validus|Validus]], and under him it founded [[concordia|Concordia]] — the first bloc assembled against the [[kangronomicon|Kangronomicon]]–[[nethers-ascent|Nether's Ascent]] control of the territory map — with a chief named [[tangler]] as its recruiter.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L119–126}}

Concordia peaked in October 2015, when around four of its guilds attacked Kangronomicon and Nether's Ascent simultaneously.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L119–126}} It was answered on 6 November by the creation of [[ordo-malleus|Ordo Malleus]], and two days later 3zPz of Historical [UEZ] posted a thread titled "Concordia Disbanded". Ezura contested the disbandment in the thread itself, "that was when most people began to consider the alliance dead".{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L203–204, composed 19 Jan 2018}}

Opponents accused the Validus leadership of hostile conduct in game and on the forums. Drew1011, who was among the accusers, later wrote that he had campaigned to persuade another guild's leader that Ezura "was a bad person", and considered Ezura's conduct to have cost Validus its allies.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L138–143, L194, composed 19 Jan 2018, re-posted 22 Feb 2018}} Nothing else about him was recorded.

Fantasy [Fux], created on 19 Jul 2017,{{cite:wynncraft-api|Wynncraft API v3/guild, retrieved 2 Sep 2026}} is chiefly remembered for one of the boldest acts of [[federation-era|the Federation era]]: going to war, alone, against the alliance that held the entire map.

## History

### The Coalition and the Trench War (January 2018)

Fantasy belonged to the [[coalition|Coalition]] before it helped found the alliance that formed against it. The Coalition's information post of 11 January 2018 lists it among fourteen enemy guilds rather than among its fourteen members, and the alliance's leadership channel was still confirming the departure five days later.{{cite:coalition-information|1/11/2018, member and enemy lists}}{{cite:the-trench-war-january-2018-discord-private|16 Jan 2018}} What followed was [[the-trench-war|the Trench War]]: about a week of fighting against [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] over the canyon, in which Fantasy attacked every territory the alliance had assigned and held each with a single mob, so that ground changed hands every half-hour and nobody kept it.{{cite:the-trench-war-january-2018-discord-private|January 2018}}{{cite:territory-exchanges|Fantasy–Kingdom Foxes exchanges 16–21 Jan 2018: 260 across 31 territories, median 28.9 minutes to the next exchange, UTC}}

The Coalition's leader gave the cause as a request for ground beyond the two Air Temple territories the guild already held, which he had undertaken to consider and then forgotten.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L1023–1024, part 17, composed 11 Jul 2018}} The first captures of the war were Kingdom Foxes taking Air Temple Lower and then Air Temple Upper off Fantasy in the small hours of 16 January.{{cite:territory-exchanges|first Fantasy–Kingdom Foxes exchanges, 16 Jan 2018, 01:43 and 01:52 UTC}} The Air Temple was the only ground Fantasy named when it asked to rejoin [[the-federation|the Federation]] nine months later, and wanting both of its territories rather than one was why it left again within a day.{{cite:federation-air-temple-2018-discord-private|11–12 Oct 2018}} Reading the two episodes together is this article's; nobody joined them at the time.

### Alliance Alliance and Aesir (early 2018)

Fantasy co-founded [[Alliance Alliance]] on 17 Feb 2018 alongside the five guilds leaving the [[Coalition]], and was out of it within two days.{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, the community guild-timeline image (imgur seklYAQ)}} [[the-federation|The Federation]] admitted it on 18 February on terms requiring its leader's other guild to take part{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|18 Feb 2018}}, and the founding allocation announced on the evening of the 21st gave it the mid canyon.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}}

[[drew1011|Drew1011]] wrote that [[viaire|Viaire]] had moved the guild across mid-battle on the 18th in exchange for keeping his canyon territories, after which "Fantasy immediately began targeting Hax".{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 17, composed 30 Aug 2018}}

The capture log dates the change of side to the same two days. On 17 February Fantasy took twenty-three canyon and Air Temple territories off three guilds that were in the Federation or joined it that week — eleven from [[holders-of-le|Holders of LE]], ten from [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]], two from [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]]. On the 19th and 20th it made eighty-five captures against Alliance Alliance, fifty-five of them from [[hackforums|HackForums]] and thirty from [[necronomicon|Necronomicon]], against two single captures elsewhere, one of them off a Federation guild.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Fantasy captures by defending guild, 17 and 19–20 Feb 2018 UTC}} No list of free-for-all ground had been published when this fighting happened — the first, of 21 February, named fourteen territories without setting down which — so the counts can be bounded but not filtered.{{cite:federation-first-map-2018-discord-private|free-for-all list, 21 Feb 2018}} None of the fifty-four territories taken from HackForums appears on the first list whose contents were written out, published on 10 March.{{cite:territory-exchanges|territories taken by Fantasy from HackForums, 19–20 Feb 2018 UTC, against the free-for-all list of 10 Mar 2018}}

All seven Alliance Alliance guilds carry the same closing date of 19–20 March 2018, the days the bloc fell from seventy-two territories to one; none of the seven has a departure date of its own.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}{{cite:territory-exchanges|Alliance Alliance from 72 territories to one, 19–20 Mar 2018}} For Fantasy that date is a month late, and the defection and the closing date are not two events. Five days later Fantasy co-created the [[Aesir Pact]] with [[Sins of Seedia]] and IceBlue Team — a pact of sister guilds 'especially dedicated to Gavel', led by Viaire among others.{{cite:thread-227373|post #1, opened 5 Jun 2018}}

### The lone war (mid-2018)

Fantasy held assigned Federation territory until the alliance announced its departure on 22 September 2018, the allocation rising to 33 territories in the last list that names it.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|6/4/2018 and 9/22/2018}} It then [[fantasy-turns-on-the-federation|fought the alliance through October]], harder in a fortnight than in the whole summer before, and was readmitted and gone again on the same night of 29 October.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|10/29/2018}} Its [[aesir-pact|Aesir]] membership ran unbroken through all of it.{{cite:thread-227373|post #1, opened 5 Jun 2018}} Twelve days after the rejoin the Federation [[the-federation-dies|cascaded apart]],{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, 13 Nov 2018}} and the Aesir guilds, its last defenders, quit after 14 hours of constant warring — Aesir died with it.{{cite:thread-236962|post #17, 11 Nov 2018}} The April 2018 leaderboard capture shows Fantasy at 27 territories.{{cite:pre2018-territory-snapshots|Wayback leaderboard capture, 16 Apr 2018}}

### 2019 and after

In 2019 Fantasy fought on the side that became [[Terra]] against the guilds that became [[Luna]], and almost all of that fighting came before either alliance had a name: 218 captures off the twelve Luna guilds and 227 lost to them in September, of which 188 and 191 fell between the 8th and the 14th, against thirteen and twenty-two for the rest of the month and one and three for the whole of October to December.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges between Fantasy and the twelve guilds of the Luna roster, by UTC day, Sep–Dec 2019, raw; the eighteen free-for-all territories declared 14 Sep 2019 are not netted out}} It joined Terra at the alliance's founding on 15 September, after that week rather than during it, and co-founded the [[Council of Canyon Kingdoms]] on 15 Sep 2019, staying to its collapse on 23 Nov 2019.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} After that the guild disappears from the alliance record entirely.

## Territory

Fantasy held more ground than most guilds while doing much less of the fighting: it peaked at 101 territories between 30 March and 8 October 2018 on 4,051 captures, against [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]]' 145 on nearly five times as many.{{cite:who-fought-2018-discord-private|Fantasy: 4,051 captures, peak 101}} The two measures are close to unrelated across the period, and both are explained at [[territory-warfare|territory warfare]].

## The Air Temple, and three minutes

Fantasy petitioned to rejoin [[the-federation|the Federation]] on 11 October 2018, posting to the alliance's voting channel. It asked to stand alongside its friends again, and for the Air Temple — the only ground it named, on the reasoning that holding it would buy time to come to its allies' defence.{{cite:federation-air-temple-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018}}

Within a minute of the petition going up a leader said they were certain to get in; within two, another said he did not want to hand the Air Temple to a guild that had only just joined.{{cite:federation-air-temple-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018}} The vote passed.

The next morning a leader wrote that it would soon be time to send the invitation. A minute later the alliance's founder reported that Fantasy's two leaders had just left the Discord, and a minute after that somebody explained why: they wanted both Air Temple territories.{{cite:federation-air-temple-2018-discord-private|12 Oct 2018}} The guild had asked for one piece of ground, been admitted, and left before the invitation was sent.

## Alliances

- [[Alliance Alliance]] — 17–18 Feb 2018 (co-founder){{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 17, composed 30 Aug 2018}}
- [[the-federation|Federation]] — 18 Feb – 22 Sep 2018; readmitted and gone again on 29 Oct 2018{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|18 Feb 2018}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|2/21/2018, 9/22/2018 and 10/29/2018}}
- [[Aesir Pact]] — 25 Mar 2018 – 10-11 Nov 2018 (co-founder){{cite:thread-227373|post #1, opened 5 Jun 2018}}
- [[Terra]] — 30 Aug 2019 – 15 Sep 2019 (roster approximate)
- [[Council of Canyon Kingdoms]] — 15 Sep 2019 – 23 Nov 2019 (co-founder)

## Background

By the spring of 2018 [[The Federation]] held the entire map, having taken it at the end of the [[Coalition Civil War]] on 20 March 2018{{cite:community-guild-timeline|20 Mar 2018 entry}}. [[Fantasy]] had been inside the bloc for a month by then: it was voted in on 18 February 2018, on terms requiring its leader's other guild to take part, and was given the mid canyon in the settlement of the 21st{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|18 Feb 2018}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|2/21/2018}}. The 20 March date it carries alongside the other [[alliance-alliance|Alliance Alliance]] guilds is the closing date all seven of that bloc's guilds carry, not a join date of its own{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}.

Five days after the 20th it co-founded the [[Aesir Pact]]{{cite:community-guild-timeline|25 Mar 2018 entry}}, the Federation's sister-guild pact "especially dedicated to Gavel."{{cite:thread-227373|post #1, 5 Jun 2018}}

## A member until September

Fantasy appears in every territory assignment the Federation published, from the first on 21 February 2018 to the last that names it on 4 June, and its allocation grew across them: 24 territories in March, 27 on 10 May, 33 on 4 June.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|2/21/2018, 3/10/2018, 5/10/2018 and 6/4/2018}} Its departure was announced to the alliance on 22 September 2018: Fantasy was no longer in the Federation, and had "merged into SDU kinda" — [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]], which stayed in.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|9/22/2018}} The hedge was doing work the announcement did not explain.

The explanation had been given in the leadership channel the day before. Asked why the two guilds would merge at all — one leader thought it looked worse than not merging — a member answered that Fantasy's leaders no longer wished to run a guild full time and had gone to the guild nearest them, and that what remained of Fantasy was left for [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]]. Another leader asked how many active fighters the guild actually had; the question went unanswered. A member said Fantasy would most likely be back.{{cite:fantasy-leaving-and-returning-2018-discord-private|21 Sep 2018}} So it was the leadership that moved and not the guild, which is what the qualifier was carrying and why nothing had to explain how a merged guild went on fighting.

It fought member guilds through the summer, but at nothing like the level of a war. With free-for-all ground taken out, weekly exchanges against members ran between five and 157 from May to mid-September.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Fantasy against the guilds in the 4 June 2018 assignment list, weekly, FFA of the list then in force excluded}} Fighting between members was ordinary in the alliance's later months: several separate wars between them broke out in November.{{cite:territory-exchanges|intra-Federation wars igniting 1 Nov 2018}}

## The eighteen days it disappeared

Fantasy's last capture before the gap was at 02:40 UTC on 23 September, the day after the announcement, and its next was at 18:58 UTC on 11 October. In between it does not appear in the log at all, on either side, while the map recorded 21,734 exchanges — about twelve hundred a day.{{cite:territory-exchanges|no exchanges involving Fantasy, 23 Sep – 11 Oct 2018}} It held no territory on 1 October.{{cite:territory-exchanges|holdings reconstructed from the capture log, 1 Oct 2018}}

## The war was in October

It asked to return before it came back. The petition went to the alliance's voting channel at 17:01 UTC on 11 October, naming the Air Temple as the ground it wanted{{cite:fantasy-leaving-and-returning-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018, 17:01 UTC}}{{cite:federation-air-temple-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018}}; the first capture of the eighteen-day silence broke it at 18:58 UTC, an hour and fifty-seven minutes later.{{cite:territory-exchanges|first capture after the gap, 11 Oct 2018 UTC}} The order rests on a message timed in one record and a capture timed in another, and holds only because both were put on the same clock to compare them.

What followed was heavier than anything in the summer by a factor of four: 269 exchanges with Federation guilds in the week of the 8th — all of them from the 11th onward — and 637 in the week of the 15th.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Fantasy against the guilds of the 4 June 2018 assignment list, weekly, unfiltered}} These are raw counts. The alliance's last free-for-all declaration went out on 12 October and was rewritten on 2 November, leaving only the later text, so no list can be applied to October without applying a document altered after the fighting it would describe.{{cite:federation-public-announcements|10/12/2018, edited 11/2/2018}} Both Air Temple territories had been taken off Fantasy on 14 September, while it was still a member, by [[kingdomphoenixes|Kingdom Phoenixes]].{{cite:territory-exchanges|Air Temple Lower and Upper, KingdomPhoenixes from Fantasy, 14 Sep 2018 UTC}}

It was readmitted at 15:29 on 29 October and gone again at 23:12, seven hours and forty-three minutes later, in an announcement whose author stopped mid-sentence to ask what word the alliance was supposed to use by that point.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|10/29/2018}} Five other membership changes were announced in the same sixteen hours: The Hive and Odysseia out, Minerva in, Nethers Ascent out; The Divine Swords was kicked the following afternoon.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|10/29/2018 and 10/30/2018, membership changes}} No reason for Fantasy's second departure was recorded anywhere in the alliance's channels.{{cite:fantasy-leaving-and-returning-2018-discord-private|29 Oct 2018}} That week is the heaviest of the whole record: 492 exchanges against member guilds, again unfiltered.{{cite:territory-exchanges|week of 29 Oct 2018, Fantasy against the 4 June assignment list, unfiltered}}

## Asking a second time

Between the petition of 11 October and the readmission of the 29th the leadership argued about a single territory, and it was put as a matter of precedent. Through the afternoon of 15 October 2018 the leader of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] asked the leadership channel repeatedly why the guild could not simply be given the upper Air Temple ground.{{cite:federation-fantasy-second-petition-october-2018-discord-private|15 Oct 2018, the question put}} He was answered that it would be unfair to member guilds that had worked through several maps for comparable territory.{{cite:federation-fantasy-second-petition-october-2018-discord-private|15 Oct 2018, the answer on precedent}} One leader said the alliance had no quarrel with the applicant provided it left that territory alone{{cite:federation-fantasy-second-petition-october-2018-discord-private|15 Oct 2018, no quarrel}}; another said flatly that it was not an ally, was not in the alliance, and had no arrangement of the kind another guild held.{{cite:federation-fantasy-second-petition-october-2018-discord-private|15 Oct 2018, not an ally}} The guild's own case, as the leader arguing for it gave it, was that it had separated again from the member guild it had merged into, had done the alliance no harm since, and simply wished to apply again.{{cite:federation-fantasy-second-petition-october-2018-discord-private|15 Oct 2018, the applicant's case}} Nothing else said the September merger had been undone, and the alliance's notice of 22 September had given that merger as the reason the guild was no longer a member.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|9/22/2018}}

The second petition went up on the morning of the 19th, and it came from inside the alliance rather than from the guild. A member asked the guild leaders what they thought of readmitting it on the understanding that it received the Air Temple ground, or else of a non-aggression arrangement of the kind another guild had been given; a leader answered within seconds that this was the previous vote and that the answer was still no.{{cite:federation-fantasy-second-petition-october-2018-discord-private|19 Oct 2018, the motion and the first reply}} That afternoon a leader reported that the result had not reached two thirds and that the guild was therefore finished, the threshold being stated for that motion rather than read off any standing rule.{{cite:federation-fantasy-second-petition-october-2018-discord-private|19 Oct 2018, the threshold and the result}} Titans Valor's leader went on arguing that a non-aggression arrangement would at least harm nobody.{{cite:federation-fantasy-second-petition-october-2018-discord-private|19 Oct 2018, the argument continued}} That evening a leader passed on, as a courtesy, that the applicant would not be attacking the alliance until its vote closed, and suggested the leaders bear it in mind — hours after the failure had been reported in the same channel, both messages on the alliance's own clock.{{cite:federation-fantasy-second-petition-october-2018-discord-private|19 Oct 2018, the offer relayed}} The tallies of both petitions are at [[federation-last-motions-2018|the Federation's last motions]].

## It left Aesir first

Five days before the readmission it left the pact it had helped found. On 24 October the [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]] told its members that Fantasy had gone on its own terms, that there were no tensions between them, that the guild wanted a different path and should be shown respect — and, in the same message, that the alliance was not weakened and there was now room to consider somebody new.{{cite:fantasy-leaving-and-returning-2018-discord-private|24 Oct 2018}} A departure, a reassurance and a vacancy in one announcement.

## Aftermath

The Federation [[the-federation-dies|died by internal collapse]] on 10 November 2018{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, 13 Nov 2018}}, and Fantasy went on fighting its guilds for four days after it had stopped existing. Against the guilds of the 4 June assignment list it made 44 exchanges on the 10th, 86 on the 12th and 113 on the 14th.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Fantasy against the 4 June 2018 assignment-list guilds, by UTC day, 10–14 Nov 2018, unfiltered}} No free-for-all list can be netted out of these. The Federation dissolved on the 10th, and for the rest of November nothing replaced its declarations — the alliance that held the ground afterwards issued its own instrument only in December, and that one named guilds rather than territories.{{cite:aesir-membership-record-2018-discord-private|November 2018}} Its two heaviest days of the month both fell after the dissolution. [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] and [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] took 150 and 85 of those exchanges between them over the five days from the 10th.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Fantasy's counterparts by exchange volume, 10–14 Nov 2018 UTC, raw; no free-for-all list was in force for the window}}

What people thought was happening was written down in the server of the bloc that had constituted itself the Federation's successor that morning. On 11 November one of its members worked out that nobody would now hold the map: the mega-alliance had been taken down, but the [[confederation|Confederation]] had not the strength to hold a map, so what remained was a fractured Federation plus the Confederation and there would never be full domination. Two others agreed.{{cite:after-the-federation-2018-discord-private|11 Nov 2018}} They were predicting rather than reporting, and the record bore them out. Three days before the end one of them had already warned in the same channel that warring at random hours to hold their own ground might itself bring the Confederation back.{{cite:after-the-federation-2018-discord-private|8 Nov 2018}}

None of them mentions Fantasy. The connection between what they expected and what the capture log shows Fantasy doing in those days is this article's.

{{map:2018-09-22|The map on the day Fantasy's departure was announced}}

[[the-federation|The Federation]] admitted Metric [Met] on 30 August 2018 after a month of argument{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/1/2018}}, announced its removal three days later{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/2/2018}}, and gave the six territories it had been assigned to another guild the day after that.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/3/2018}}

## The application

Late on 31 July 2018 the alliance's founder told the leadership channel that Metric's leader had asked to join for the third day running and had written the paragraph the alliance required of every guild that wanted in.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|31 Jul 2018}} He said he was not sure the man was serious, and would investigate before starting a vote.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|31 Jul 2018, the investigation}} Two members answered at once that they would rather have this guild than [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]], which had left the alliance nine days earlier.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|31 Jul 2018, the comparison}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 7/22/2018}}

## One fewer enemy

The case for taking the guild was put in writing to the guild leaders on 2 August. Its author acknowledged that many of them disliked the applicant and its leader, said he had spoken to the man and had his promise to stop the taunting, and put the leader's personal war count in the thousands.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|2 Aug 2018, the case}} If the alliance gave the guild room to grow it might become a powerful ally, he argued, and if it did not, admitting it would at least mean one fewer persistent enemy.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|2 Aug 2018, the argument}} The man had also agreed to help against three named opponents.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|2 Aug 2018, the undertaking}}

The objections were of two kinds. One guild's leader said she would not change her mind after what had been said to her members, and did not trust the change of manner.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|2 Aug 2018, the leader's objection}} Others said the applicant was effectively one man, that he needed a war force rather than a name, that wars in this game were decided by numbers, and that a guild could recruit without belonging to an alliance.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|2 Aug 2018, the objections}} A member noted that the applicant and Achte Shadow [ASh] between them had wiped his guild's territories more than once.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|2 Aug 2018, the wipes}} Another answered that this was the argument for admitting them, since a guild that had shown it could take a guild down would be an asset with the alliance behind it.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|2 Aug 2018, the answer}}

## The offer to absorb it

The vote opened late in the month and ran slowly. On 29 August, with the discussion turning on whether so small a guild could survive being targeted, one leader said it would manage if the alliance gave it a small allocation to begin with and let it earn more by holding that.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, the allocation proposal}} A member then said the alliance should simply absorb its smaller guilds into one another, which would spare them warring alone and would help with territory as well.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, the merger proposal}} [[aphelion|Aphelion]]'s representative took it up immediately and offered that Aphelion absorb the applicant, saying her guild needed members, that both guilds' chances of surviving would improve, that she was forcing nothing, and that the applicant's leader could keep his own guild's name if he wanted it.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, Aphelion's offer}}

Two leaders objected that a merger should not be reached for immediately, that a guild having a character of its own was worth something, and that merging was never a good time.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, the objections}} Whose name would survive was raised twice and never settled.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, the name}} Nothing came of the offer, and the applicant was admitted separately the following day.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018}} Five days earlier two member guilds had merged in exactly that way, [[ex-nihilo|Ex Nihilo]] and [[lunatic|Lunatic]] combining into [[odysseia|Odysseia]].{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|24 Aug 2018}}

## The spy objection

Later on 29 August a [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] officer said the vote kept looking worse, that he did not trust the applicant's leader, and that he was always nervous about spy guilds — adding that he supposed this was hypocritical.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, the spy objection}} The founder answered that he did trust the man and that the guild had not been attacking the Federation.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, the answer}} He sincerely doubted, he said, that anybody would let two months pass between applications, doing nothing in the interval, purely in order to spy.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, the two months}} Earlier in the same vote a member had said he was voting against because he had a bet riding on the guild not getting in; the founder told him that was not a reason to deny a guild, and he then gave a different one, that the guild was a nuisance when it attacked his own.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, the bet}}

Watching the tally at eight to five, the same officer remarked that he liked how the alliance voted in groups: among the guilds in favour one sub-alliance and then the other, and among those against nobody who belonged to a sub-alliance at all.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, the blocs}} A member answered at once that he had voted in favour and belonged to no sub-alliance, and the exchange turned into a joke about founding one.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, the answer to it}} Two months later whether the alliance's internal blocs decided its votes was an open argument in the same channel.{{cite:federation-blocs-within-2018-discord-private|October 2018}}

## The vote and the map

Early on 30 August the Fox officer confirmed that the vote had been open more than twenty-four hours, so that it became official once it was past two thirds.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the threshold}} In the same minute, saying he was doing it solely so that the map vote could be put up immediately afterwards, he changed his own vote in favour to push the guild through, and told the channel to put the map vote up.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the change of vote}} That threshold had been in force since February 2018.{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|threshold change, 19–20 Feb 2018}} Members were confirming the guild was in and welcoming its leader within the hour{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the welcome}}, and the founder later that morning put the result at about ten to five.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the result}}

## A second guild the same day

On 30 August a member reported to the leadership channel that a trade had been arranged between [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] and [[the-hive|The Hive]], four territories for four, with one further territory going to Achte Shadow [ASh].{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the trade}} Another leader confirmed the extra territory and a third welcomed Achte Shadow into the Federation in the same minute.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the welcome}} The member who reported the trade said the guild had earned it.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, earned it}}

Three and a half weeks earlier the alliance had named Achte Shadow among the guilds from which [[hackforums|HackForums]] was taking territory during the episode its members called feeding{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|6 Aug 2018}}, and on 2 and 10 August members recorded it attacking Federation guilds.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|2 and 10 Aug 2018}} The capture log has HackForums taking ground from Achte Shadow inside the three hours of 6 August the alliance was describing.{{cite:territory-exchanges|HackForums captures by defending guild, 6 Aug 2018 18:00–21:00 UTC, free-for-all list of 6 Aug 2018 excluded}}

How Achte Shadow was admitted was never written down. The alliance's weekly summary of 1 September named the guilds that had joined in the preceding nine days — [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] returning, Arisen, Metric and [[odysseia|Odysseia]] — and did not include it.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/1/2018}}

## Removed

The alliance announced the expulsion on 2 September: "Evidence arose that Metric [Met] only joined the alliance to spy and cause internal chaos and has been removed from the alliance as a result."{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/2/2018}} The next day [[nethers-ascent|Nether's Ascent]] joined and was assigned the six Rymek territories Metric had been given.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/3/2018}} The following week's summary put it more briefly: "Metric was kicked because they were only here to spy lmao, who would've guessed".{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/9/2018}}

What the guild had actually done was not stated in either announcement. In the leadership channel on 3 September members said they had never understood why it had been accepted, that it had been suspicious from the day it joined, that it had been foolish enough to expose itself within two days, and that the alliance had been perfectly fine without it.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018}} A member noted in the same conversation that two guilds had left the alliance and this one had just been removed{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the tally}}, and that evening the founder asked for it to be taken off the alliance's published list of members.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the list}}

The argument those messages sat inside was about the quality of what the alliance had been admitting, and a member guild's leader gave the answer: the Federation had accepted so much that it did not really have space, and he apologised for the offence it would cause in the room.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, no space}} The alliance's own summary of that week had opened two days earlier by saying that literally every guild suddenly wanted to join it.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/1/2018, the weekly update}}

## A waiting period for refused applicants

On the morning of 26 August 2018 the alliance's founder asked the leadership channel whether there should be a policy fixing how long a guild that had been turned down must wait before it could be considered again.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the proposal}} His reason was Metric: someone in the advisory channel had reported that the guild wanted to join, and it had been turned down before.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the reason given}} A permanent refusal was unreasonable, he said, because circumstances changed — but a guild ought to have to wait.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the case for it}}

The channel argued about the length for twenty minutes. A month was too long, the founder said, because a great deal could change in one and the alliance itself was only six months old; two members proposed three weeks, one proposed a week, and two argued for two weeks as a starting figure that could be adjusted later.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the lengths proposed}} He put it to the guild leaders at ten that morning with four options, and none of them carried: one vote for a week, five for two weeks, six for three and three for a month.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the tallies recorded on the motion}} That night he said he would put it again with only the two leading options, and did so on 1 September. Two weeks carried, by fifteen votes to two.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|1 Sep 2018, the tally recorded on the motion}}

The rule was never written down. The alliance announced it nowhere and no rules document carried it.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|26 Aug – 18 Sep 2018, no announcement and no rules document}} Its one recorded application was the [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] readmission bid of 18 September 2018, which opened by observing that two weeks had passed — fourteen days after the motion that had refused the guild.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|18 Sep 2018, the application}} The guild the rule was proposed for waited for nothing: Metric was admitted on 30 August, four days after the proposal and two days before the second vote adopted it, and expelled as a spy on 2 September.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/1/2018 and 9/2/2018}}

## Two guilds refused for want of room

Neither guild the alliance turned down in the same fortnight was refused on its record. Night Warriors [NHW] applied on 31 August 2018; its leader wrote that he had been in [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]], had admired how the Federation was run, had founded his own guild after Hall of Fame collapsed, and wanted the alliance behind it.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the application}} The leaders did not know who the guild was: one identified its leader as a former Hall of Fame recruit, and another posted its war record, forty successful wars out of fifty-nine.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the leadership channel}} It was refused by two votes to twelve, with none abstaining. The reactions left on the motion give three in favour, thirteen against and one abstention — seventeen marks from fifteen accounts, one of which, the guild that put the motion, sits on all three options. Taking that one out removes a mark from each column and leaves fourteen marks from fourteen accounts, which is what makes the arithmetic close: the single abstention was its own.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the motion as exported and the mover on every option}}{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the tally recorded on the motion}} It failed against a two-thirds threshold on either figure, and the expulsion motion put at 18:48 the same evening needed no such correction — the two are set beside each other at [[federation-ballots-august-2018|the Federation's ballots in August 2018]]. The alliance's weekly bulletin did not name it, recording only that one other guild had applied and been denied.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/1/2018}}

It was not the guild abbreviated NYN, whose stalled admission vote the alliance had been arguing over since July.{{cite:federation-remembering-buildcraftia-august-2018-discord-private|the two guilds distinguished}} That guild was deleted on 10 September 2018, its disappearance noticed in the leadership and general channels that day.{{cite:federation-remembering-buildcraftia-august-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, the deletion}} At least one member of the alliance ran the two together at the time.{{cite:federation-remembering-buildcraftia-august-2018-discord-private|the conflation}}

The Nezeract [NzT] applied on the evening of 8 September with a written document.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the application}} A member said at once that they would be spies, and the founder answered that their leader was the man who had been passing the Federation information from inside the opposing alliance — his claim, and nobody else's.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the spy exchange}} The leaders then gave their reasons for voting against: that the alliance was crowded, that they had never heard of the guild, and in one case a personal one.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the reasons}} It was refused by three votes to ten.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the tally recorded on the motion}}

Crowding was the reason the leaders gave among themselves throughout. On the morning of 31 August, as the fourth application in three days went up, the founder asked what had suddenly made every guild in the game want to join; a leader answered that they could not accept every application, and another said he was refusing from then on because his own guild would end up with about ten territories and that was all it could get.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, too many applications}} On 3 September, during the vote on [[nethers-ascent|Nether's Ascent]], a member guild's leader said the alliance had too many guilds in it and that she would gladly vote for a good guild if there were room, and another agreed that space was tight.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, no room}}

Times here are the alliance's own clock, which ran eight hours behind UTC that winter. Each count below is the figure posted in the channel as its motion closed.

## Thirteen motions in eleven days

[[the-federation|The Federation]] decided who belonged to it at better than a motion a day for its first fortnight, in one room, in front of its own guild leaders, and wrote the count down each time.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|the thirteen motions, 17-28 Feb 2018}} Thirteen motions went to the leaders' channel between 17 and 28 February 2018: ten on admitting a guild, two on the alliance's territorial map, and one that was not a motion at all.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|the thirteen motions classified}}

The first was put at 14:52:28 on 17 February, two hours and twenty-six minutes after the channel's first message, and it was on admitting Deimos.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|the first motion, 17 Feb 2018}} It closed two minutes and twenty-nine seconds later at four votes to nothing, and two further members voted after it had closed, at 14:59 and 15:02.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|17 Feb 2018, the close and the late votes}} A motion in this alliance closed when its mover chose to close it rather than when the room had finished voting. The alliance had been founded the evening before, without a vote of any kind — see [[the-federation-founding-night|the founding night]].{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018, 20:05-20:06}}

## The ten guilds

Nine of the ten admission motions carried and one was refused. Four closed in the first two days: Deimos, four to nothing on 17 February; [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]], seven to one the same day; [[iceblue-team|Ice Blue Team]], eight to two on the 18th; and [[dogsamongus|DogsAmongUs]], eight to one on the 18th.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|the four motions closing 17-18 Feb 2018}} Three more followed in two days: [[fantasy|Fantasy]] on a stated condition, eight to two, closing on the 19th; Kasai Shinrai, nine to nothing on the 19th; and [[the-hive|The Hive]], thirteen to nothing on the 20th.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|the three motions closing 19-20 Feb 2018}} Then the pace broke: [[illustratus|Illustratus]] was refused three to seven on the 25th, an invitation to New York Nibbas carried sixteen to one the same day, and As Darkness Falls was admitted fourteen to four on the 27th.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|the three motions closing 25-27 Feb 2018}}

The New York Nibbas motion asked only whether to issue an invitation, and the guild was still being voted on the following month — see [[federation-admissions-march-2018|admissions in March]].{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|25 Feb 2018, the invitation motion}} Illustratus was inside the alliance a fortnight after its refusal, having declared itself independent of its parent guild in the interval.{{cite:federation-first-month-2018-discord-private|admission votes, late February 2018}}

The totals rise as the alliance fills. The first motion closed on four votes and the last on eighteen.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|the first and last totals}} On 21 February a member told the war channel the alliance held twenty-one guilds, and the settlement published that evening named twenty-two.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, the figure given}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}} No motion in the month drew a total larger than eighteen.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|the month's totals}} What counted as a guild's vote was never settled here or in any other alliance of the period, so the gap between the two figures is not a turnout.{{cite:who-gets-a-vote-discord-private|24 Jan 2018}}

## A motion that was not one

One of the thirteen is a joke and can be identified as one mechanically. At 12:19 on 20 February the representative of [[imperial|Imperial]] put a motion whose two options were the same single word, so that neither could express disagreement with the other; the alliance's founder closed it five minutes afterwards at two to one.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, the joke motion}} It sits between the motion admitting The Hive and the motions on the map, in the same channel and format as the alliance's real business.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|20-21 Feb 2018, the sequence}} Nothing follows from it, and any count of the month's motions that includes it is wrong by one.

## The two map motions of 21 February

The alliance's founding territorial settlement was carried by the second of two map motions put the same afternoon. The first asked the leaders whether they approved the map in the announcements channel; it was put at 13:24 on 21 February and closed at 14:07:44, eleven in favour and five against.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, the first map motion}} A second went up at 14:15.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, the second map motion put}}

The settlement went to the announcements channel at 21:12 that evening, seventy-six seconds after the vote reached two thirds and one minute after the founder asked a leader who had not voted to do so.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}}{{cite:federation-vote-procedure-february-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, 21:11-21:12:30}} The motion carrying it was not closed until 20:35:36 on 24 February, fourteen in favour and none against — three days after the document it authorised had been published.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, the close}} A motion here could be published from and closed afterwards. The alliance's later ratifications are at [[federation-map-votes-2018|the map votes]].

## Refused by one alliance, admitted by the other

Ice Blue Team passed through both alliances in four days. The [[coalition|Coalition]] put a motion on admitting it at 10:37 on 14 February 2018 and closed it at 12:22:45, three in favour and six against.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|the Coalition motion, 14 Feb 2018}} The Federation put the same guild at 09:27 on the 18th and closed it at 10:55:54, eight in favour and two against.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|the Federation motion, 18 Feb 2018}} The day before the Coalition's refusal, one of its officers recorded in the war channel that Ice Blue Team had approached her asking to sign a peace treaty, and that when she explained the alliance would defend guilds under attack they turned out not to have known that As Darkness Falls was one of them.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|13 Feb 2018, the approach}} Nothing about the guild had changed in the four days between the two votes.

## The longest argument of the month

The motion on As Darkness Falls drew the longest debate of the month, and it was not about the guild. The case against was that the alliance already held enough guilds and did not need more, and that six territories were not hard to defend.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|26-27 Feb 2018, the case against}} The case for was made by a member who had fought that guild for the other side a few weeks earlier and vouched for its warring, and who argued that inactive member guilds should be pruned or have their allocations cut to make room.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|26-27 Feb 2018, the case for}} The founder recorded that the guild had been a Coalition member, had left to do things other than war, had been approached during the current war by a leader of the opposing bloc and had turned that approach down, and had said it would return to warring and wanted to join when it did.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|26-27 Feb 2018, the founder's account}} The vote opened at 18:30:36 on 26 February and closed at 12:23:04 on the 27th, fourteen to four.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|the ASF motion, 26-27 Feb 2018}}

The alliance recorded that admission in three rooms inside a minute. As the motion closed at 12:23:04 an officer of [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]] told the general channel that the guild was back in, and another member welcomed a returning player to the alliance.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|27 Feb 2018, the general channel at 12:23}} In the war channel that afternoon a Fox war-leader asked since when the guild had been back and was told it was that day.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|27 Feb 2018, the war channel at 16:25}} The word the members used was the wrong one. As Darkness Falls had been in the Coalition, not in the Federation, which was eleven days old; the vote of 27 February admitted it and readmitted nothing.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|27 Feb 2018, the guild's previous membership}}

## In and out in eight days

The first guild the alliance admitted was gone eight days later. Deimos was voted in on 17 February by the Federation's first motion; by midday on 25 February members of the general channel were saying they would go and invade its ground and that its territories had been split up.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|25 Feb 2018, the general channel}} The circumstances of the departure were never set down, and no leaving announcement was made.

What the episode produced was the alliance's first statement of a re-entry rule, and it was contradicted in the same minute. The representative of Imperial said that a guild which leaves is out, and named Deimos as the case; an officer of Hall of Fame answered that her own guild had left an alliance once and been taken back.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|25 Feb 2018, the rule and the answer}} She was describing her guild's departure from and return to the Coalition rather than anything the Federation had done.{{cite:coalition-explained-by-its-members-february-2018-discord-private|9 Feb 2018, the condition and the return}} How membership was governed afterwards is at [[alliance-governance|alliance governance]].

## The month's first admission

The Federation admitted a guild it was attacking, five days before it built a room to vote in. On 1 March 2018 a member told the alliance-wide channel that the alliance was fighting over the territory of Llevigar because Funbari Onsen [FBO] wanted it.{{cite:federation-funbari-onsen-admission-march-2018-discord-private|1 Mar 2018, the fighting over Llevigar}} The guild's leader sent a private message the next morning asking to join, and it reached the leadership channel at 10:28.{{cite:federation-funbari-onsen-admission-march-2018-discord-private|2 Mar 2018, the application relayed}} Six minutes later another member urged admission on the strength of a territory count nobody checked, putting the guild at about twenty-two.{{cite:federation-funbari-onsen-admission-march-2018-discord-private|2 Mar 2018, the twenty-two asserted}} The figure was right: reconstructing the map from the capture log at that minute gives Funbari Onsen twenty-two territories, taken on the back of 104 captures in the preceding week.{{cite:territory-exchanges|territories whose last recorded captor was Funbari Onsen at 2 Mar 2018 17:28 UTC, and its captures 25 Feb – 2 Mar 2018 UTC, raw}}

The ballot opened at 10:57 and closed at 18:43 the same day, twenty in favour and two against.{{cite:federation-funbari-onsen-admission-march-2018-discord-private|2 Mar 2018, the ballot opened and closed}} It ran in the leadership channel, because the voting channel was not created until 7 March.{{cite:federation-funbari-onsen-admission-march-2018-discord-private|2 Mar 2018, the channel used}} A formal admission vote of the whole leadership was therefore in use before the room built for it, which is why the fourteen motions of the voting channel are not the month's whole ballot record. Whether any account on this one sat on both options went unrecorded, and nobody in the channel restated the count, so neither of the corrections that the later tallies need can be applied to it.{{cite:federation-funbari-onsen-admission-march-2018-discord-private|the tally as it stands, 2 Mar 2018}}

The alliance went on attacking the guild for the eight hours the ballot was open, and said so four minutes after the result. Members spent the day asking whether it could be invited yet and whether it was with them or not.{{cite:federation-funbari-onsen-admission-march-2018-discord-private|2 Mar 2018, the pressing}} At 18:46 a member asked whether the decision had taken too long, another hoped the guild would not be too angry, and a third said the alliance had gone after it anyway despite his asking members to leave it alone.{{cite:federation-funbari-onsen-admission-march-2018-discord-private|2 Mar 2018, four minutes after the result}}

## A truce with no instrument behind it

For two days in the first week of March the alliance and its last enemy had an understanding and nothing else. On the morning of 6 March 2018, asked in the discussion channel beside the alliance's serialised history what [[paladins-united|Paladins United]]'s standing was, a leader answered that it was still being worked out and that for the present neither side was meant to attack the other.{{cite:federation-first-fortnight-of-march-2018-discord-private|6 Mar 2018, the standing described}} A vote to make peace with the guild on terms had failed the evening before, and no other instrument had replaced it.{{cite:federation-corkus-campaign-march-2018-discord-private|5 Mar 2018, the vote}}

The motion readmitting the guild went to the voting channel on the evening of the 7th.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|the Paladins United motion's tally, 7 Mar 2018}} The negotiation behind it, and the three different counts the vote produced, are at [[paladins-united|Paladins United]].

At 6:54 on the morning of the 8th the alliance told its whole membership that the guild "has been accepted in to the Federation" and was to be treated with the same respect as every other guild in it.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 3/8/2018 6:54 AM}} The two days of informal quiet had run between a failed peace and a successful admission.

## Too many guilds

The complaint began as an aside, framed against the alliance the Federation had replaced. On the evening of 3 March 2018 the leader of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] asked the war channel whether the alliance could clear out guilds that had gone wholly dormant, and said that this was not the [[coalition|Coalition]], which had kept a great many inactive members.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|3 Mar 2018, the complaint}} The Federation was the better alliance because the remnant of [[war-syndicate|War Syndicate]] was in it, he said; told that some guilds were simply in other time zones, he answered that some guilds were useless.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|3 Mar 2018, the comparison}}

He put it formally on 7 March: the alliance contained a large number of guilds, a few contributed nothing to its wars, and their membership should be terminated so that only guilds able to support the alliance remained.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|7 Mar 2018, the proposal}} He was answered inside four minutes, and not on the merits. The objection was that enemies would say the Federation had merely used its smaller guilds, that the smaller guilds were already worried about exactly that, and that the result would be more enemies rather than fewer.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|7 Mar 2018, the answer}} The same argument decided an expulsion vote six months later, when a leader said he was voting against removal to avoid the pattern in which the alliance expelled a guild and the guild promptly became active and attacked it.{{cite:federation-inactive-members-2018-discord-private|18 Sep 2018}} Setting the two occasions beside each other is this article's.

## The moratorium

The next morning the question went to the whole leadership in a softer form — not whom to expel, but whether to keep letting guilds in. A member posted to the voting channel that several leaders thought the alliance had too many guilds, and offered four options: to keep adding guilds and not thin the alliance down; to admit nobody further until some had been removed for inactivity or for treason against the alliance; an alternative to be sent to the mover privately; or abstention.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|8 Mar 2018, the motion and its options}} Nineteen accounts took the moratorium, one the option of carrying on as before, one the alternative and three abstained.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|the tally, 8 Mar 2018}} Nothing comes off that: no account on the motion sat on every option.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|multi-option reactions across the fourteen March motions}} The single mark against the moratorium belonged to an account that had marked the moratorium as well, and taking that one out leaves eighteen, none, one and three, with nothing at all recorded against it.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|the duplicate account, 8 Mar 2018}} An account on two options out of four cannot be told apart from a guild voting twice, so the motion has two defensible readings and neither is the answer. Why every March tally needs that subtraction is at [[federation-ballots-march-2018|the Federation's ballots in March 2018]].

## Two applications refused, neither of them on the moratorium

Three days later the alliance voted on admitting Germany Elite [GMY], and the case for it was made as replacement rather than addition. On 8 March a member had told the leadership channel that War Syndicate's mistake was refusing to add guilds, and that an alliance grew stronger the more it added.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|8 Mar 2018, the case for adding}} On the 11th, arguing for Germany Elite on the day of the vote, he said the Federation already contained guilds that had done nothing, that a no vote was how War Syndicate had fallen, and that active guilds should replace the dead ones rather than be added to them.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|11 Mar 2018, replacement rather than addition}} The motion drew fourteen in favour, ten against and two abstentions — short of two thirds, and so refused.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|11 Mar 2018, the Germany Elite tally}} This is the one March motion where the guild that moved it sat on two of the three options, its own and an abstention, which is the case the alliance's arithmetic could never settle: an account on some options but not all cannot be told from a guild voting twice. Taking both of its marks out gives fourteen, nine and one instead.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|multi-option reactions across the fourteen March motions, and the mover's marks on 11 Mar}} The refusal holds either way.

The following day [[house-of-sentinels|House of Sentinels]] applied and was refused in eight minutes, a third of the guilds having voted against.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|12 Mar 2018, the House of Sentinels motion}} Neither refusal mentions the moratorium.

## What Germany Elite was doing while it applied

The application had a private history, and the leadership channel heard it the day the vote ran. On the morning of 10 March 2018 a member reported that Germany Elite had asked to assist the alliance and wanted to join, argued that it showed more promise than several guilds already inside, and asked how a vote was started.{{cite:federation-germany-elite-march-2018-discord-private|10 Mar 2018, the approach relayed}} Shortly before four the next morning he told the alliance-wide channel that he had made an arrangement with the guild before he left the alliance, that it had never changed, and that the guild wanted placing in the alliance's divisions because it was willing to fight.{{cite:federation-germany-elite-march-2018-discord-private|11 Mar 2018, the arrangement disclosed}} What the arrangement was for was never legible; the message giving its object breaks off mid-word.{{cite:federation-germany-elite-march-2018-discord-private|11 Mar 2018, the line cut short}}

Between 11:21 and 11:35 on the 11th a member of the leadership channel said he would give the guild the answer no{{cite:federation-germany-elite-march-2018-discord-private|11 Mar 2018, the refusal}}, and then said that the last time the alliance had refused Germany Elite it had torn up an existing treaty and gone on the offensive.{{cite:federation-germany-elite-march-2018-discord-private|11 Mar 2018, the precedent}} The guild was fighting a member of the alliance while the request was live: early on 10 March the alliance-wide channel reported it warring [[emorians|Emorians]] for about four hours, heavily, with members offering each other wagers in emeralds on the outcome.{{cite:federation-germany-elite-march-2018-discord-private|10 Mar 2018, the war with Emorians}} By the 19th the war channel treated the rivalry as weather, expecting Germany Elite to fire on [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] again that day{{cite:federation-germany-elite-march-2018-discord-private|19 Mar 2018, the wars listed}} and describing it as habitually fighting for the Molten Heights.{{cite:federation-germany-elite-march-2018-discord-private|19 Mar 2018, the Molten Heights}}

### The report of 27 March

The alliance's largest guild recorded losing assigned ground to the refused guild by hand. At 07:52 on the export's clock on 27 March 2018 a Kingdom of Foxes officer pasted a territory report into the alliance-wide channel giving the Entrance to Rodoroc and Ranol's Farm as passing to Germany Elite, and named the Fox members who had been holding them.{{cite:federation-germany-elite-march-2018-discord-private|27 Mar 2018, the report typed into the channel}} It was typed by a member, not drawn from the automated feed.{{cite:federation-germany-elite-march-2018-discord-private|27 Mar 2018, a hand-typed report}}

Both territories were back in Fox hands before he finished typing. Germany Elite took the Entrance to Rodoroc at 14:21 UTC and lost it again at 14:32, and took Ranol's Farm at 14:37 and lost it at 14:44, while the report went up at 14:52 UTC.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges on the two territories, 27 Mar 2018 UTC, raw}} Over the whole day Germany Elite took the Entrance to Rodoroc four times and Ranol's Farm six, held neither longer than twenty-five minutes at a stretch, and ended the day holding neither.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Germany Elite's captures and holding spells on the two territories, 27 Mar 2018 UTC, raw}} Reading the report against the log is this article's.

Neither territory was open ground. The free-for-all declaration of 24 March was edited at 15:28 UTC on 27 March, thirty-eight minutes after the captures, so it cannot say what it contained that morning.{{cite:federation-germany-elite-march-2018-discord-private|the declaration's edit stamp, 27 Mar 2018}} The edit added two named territories at the alliance leader's request and changed nothing else.{{cite:free-for-all-amendments-2018-2019-discord-private|27 Mar 2018, the two additions}} Neither the Entrance to Rodoroc nor Ranol's Farm appears on the longer list, so neither can have stood on the shorter one before it.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|3/24/2018}} That the additions settle the question is this article's reading.

The proposal to bring the guild in came back the same day and drew the objection that it was openly hostile to Kingdom of Foxes.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|27 Mar 2018, the hostility objection}} The two guilds had spent that morning taking the same two territories off each other; nobody in the channel joined the two, and joining them is this article's.

## What the alliance did instead

The expulsion the moratorium was supposed to precede was proposed as a condition of the admission that failed. On 11 March 2018 a member told the leadership channel that if Germany Elite were accepted the question ought to go to a ballot on removing Death Reapers [DxR] and Immortalish [Smd].{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|11 Mar 2018, the proposal}} Another added [[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]] and said that removing it would free fifteen territories, but that its leader should be given one last chance.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|11 Mar 2018, the third guild}} Fifteen is what the alliance's map of the previous evening assigned that guild.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|3/10/2018}}

Germany Elite was refused that day, and the motion went to the voting channel the following evening anyway, naming only the first two guilds, on the grounds that they held no territory, were inactive and took up space.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|12 Mar 2018, the expulsion motion}} It carried ten to two with eleven abstentions, or ten to one with ten if the single account sitting on two of its three options is taken out as well; no account on it sat on every option, so the first figure removes only placeholders and the second removes a mark that cannot be told apart from a guild voting twice.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|the motion's tally, 12 Mar 2018}}{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|multi-option reactions across the fourteen March motions}} Nothing happened for three and a half weeks: on 17 March a member of the leadership channel was still asking why the two guilds were in the alliance.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|17 Mar 2018, the question repeated}} Their removal was ordered on 6 April 2018, by which time the alliance had run a fresh complaint, a fresh list and a fresh motion.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|6 Apr 2018, the removal}} What it did in April, and the two guilds it added to the list in the interval, is at [[the-federation-april-2018|the Federation in April 2018]].

## The question put directly

On 15 March the alliance answered the underlying question in terms. A motion asked whether, when a guild reported that several of its main warring members would be inactive for a substantial period, the alliance should expel that guild or defend its territories; a third option offered to reduce its territory instead.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|15 Mar 2018, the motion}} Defending carried with fifteen, expulsion took three and reducing territory three, with none abstaining.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|the motion's tally, 15 Mar 2018}} One account sat on every option offered and is taken out as a placeholder; a second sat on two of the four, and dropping that one as well would give fourteen, three and two.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|the seeding account and the two-option account on 15 Mar 2018}} An earlier reading of this tally subtracted the mover's mark too, which put defending at thirteen; the mover was a voter and the mark was a vote.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|movers' marks across the fourteen March motions}} Taken twice in a week, the alliance's position was that it was too large to keep admitting guilds and unwilling to remove the ones that had stopped fighting.

## The month's close

The admission question came back on 27 March 2018 and was dropped without a vote. A member proposed in the war channel that the alliance bring Germany Elite onto its side, saying the guild had finally got itself together and was eager.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|27 Mar 2018, the proposal}} The first objection was that the guild was openly hostile to [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]].{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|27 Mar 2018, the hostility objection}} The second was that the alliance was short enough of room that it could not make assignments, [[imperial|Imperial]] in particular being angry about it, so that adding guilds useful or otherwise made the problem worse.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|27 Mar 2018, the space objection}} The third was that adding too many allies would leave the alliance with no enemies left to fight.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|27 Mar 2018, the enemies objection}} The founder agreed about space and added that admitting a guild which hated an existing member was worse still.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|27 Mar 2018, the founder's answer}} Imperial had put its complaint about the allocation to the leadership channel three days earlier.{{cite:federation-map-disputes-march-2018-discord-private|24 Mar 2018, Imperial's complaint}}

## 27 April: the ballot nobody opened

On the afternoon of 27 April 2018 [[dogsamongus|DogsAmongUs]] took two territories from member guilds of [[the-federation|the Federation]] inside forty minutes: Temple of Legends from [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] at 20:42 UTC and City of Troms from [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]] at 21:21.{{cite:territory-exchanges|DogsAmongUs as attacker or defender, 27 Apr 2018 UTC: four exchanges across two territories}} It held neither. [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] took the first back at 21:05 and Sins of Seedia the second at 21:24, three minutes after losing it.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Temple of Legends and City of Troms, 27 Apr 2018 20:42–21:24 UTC}}

The two were not the same kind of ground. Temple of Legends had been on the alliance's own free-for-all list since 10 March and was on the list published five days before, where a capture was open to anybody and not an act of war against whoever had held it a minute earlier.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|3/10/2018, 3/24/2018 and 4/22/2018 6:22 PM}} It changed hands 1,185 times between sixty-nine guilds over April, May and June 2018, with a median holding time of thirty-eight minutes.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Temple of Legends exchanges, 1 Apr – 30 Jun 2018 UTC: 1,185 across 69 attacking guilds, median hold 38.5 minutes}} City of Troms was on none of the three lists.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|3/10/2018, 3/24/2018 and 4/22/2018 6:22 PM}}

In the war channel a member called at once for an expulsion ballot against DogsAmongUs for attacking allies, and pointed out that there were three free-for-all territories in the same jungle.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-april-june-2018-discord-private|27 Apr 2018, the demand}} The guild's leader offered the territory straight back and said he had only wanted to see his own banner on it; others told the complainant to calm down.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-april-june-2018-discord-private|27 Apr 2018, the offer and the answers}} A quarter of an hour later a member observed that the complainants looked to have been searching for a reason to move against DogsAmongUs in the first place, and the accused leader said he had privately thought the same.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-april-june-2018-discord-private|27 Apr 2018, the observation and the agreement}} The complainant answered that it put him in mind of an earlier episode, which he did not date, in which the leader now heading the rival alliance had invaded [[serpentem-empire|Serpentem Empire]] and taken all of its territories.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-april-june-2018-discord-private|27 Apr 2018, the earlier episode}} No motion went to the voting channel.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-april-june-2018-discord-private|no motion on the subject, Apr – Jun 2018}}

Which of the afternoon's two captures the complaint was about was never said, and one of them was on ground the alliance had declared open to everybody. Both territories were back with member guilds before the conversation finished.

## 5 May: a member nobody recognised

Late on 5 May 2018, on the export's clock, a member of [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]] posted the alliance's log line showing a territory in the Detlas suburbs passing from his guild to IceBlue Team and asked for an explanation. Members took a moment to establish which guild he was in.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-april-june-2018-discord-private|5 May 2018, the report and the confusion}} Shortly after midnight the leader of IceBlue Team answered that her people had not realised DiamondDeities belonged to the alliance, that the territory had been left very lightly defended, and apologised; the DiamondDeities member accepted it in one word.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-april-june-2018-discord-private|6 May 2018, the apology}} No alliance body was involved.

The capture is logged at 05:26 UTC on 6 May, seven hours ahead of the clock the messages carry.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Detlas Close Suburbs, IceBlue Team from DiamondDeities, 6 May 2018 05:26:25 UTC}} Detlas Close Suburbs was not on the free-for-all list; Detlas, which was, is a different territory.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|4/22/2018 6:22 PM}} And DiamondDeities had itself taken the territory from [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]], another member guild, six hours earlier.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Detlas Close Suburbs, DiamondDeities from Kingdom Foxes, 5 May 2018 23:18:46 UTC}}

The guild whose membership had to be established had been on the alliance's published allocation since 22 April, and its predecessor guild had been a member well before that.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|4/22/2018 2:34 PM}}{{cite:le-flowers-to-diamond-deities-2018-discord-private|8 Apr 2018}} An allied guild's leadership did not know it was in the alliance.

## 10 May: three minutes

At 21:12 on the export's clock on 10 May 2018 a member of the war channel posted the log line showing the Light Realm Entrance passing from TheNoLifes to Constellations. A member noted that Constellations was an ally; the member who had posted the line said that an ally had attacked an ally; the next message was about something else.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-april-june-2018-discord-private|10 May 2018, the exchange in the war channel}} The capture is logged at 03:37 UTC on 11 May, and TheNoLifes took the territory back eighty minutes afterwards.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Light Realm Entrance, Constellations from TheNoLifes 11 May 2018 03:37:53 UTC and returned 04:57:39 UTC}}

The alliance's new territory allocation had been open for votes for three hours at the moment of the remark and was announced eighty minutes later. It gives Constellations the Light Realm, and gives TheNoLifes the north sky islands.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-april-june-2018-discord-private|10 May 2018, the allocation then in its ballot}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|5/10/2018}} It was also the first capture Constellations had made anywhere in the Light Realm since the log began in January 2018.{{cite:territory-exchanges|first capture by Constellations on any territory named Light Realm*, 3 Jan – 11 May 2018 UTC: 11 May 03:37:53}} Reading the capture as a guild moving into ground it was about to be given is this article's; nobody in the channel said it.

## 23 June: reported by the guild that did it

On the evening of 23 June 2018 a member told the alliance's general channel that some of his own guild's people were attacking one of an allied guild's territories for amusement, and were so low-level they would be killed immediately.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-april-june-2018-discord-private|23 Jun 2018, the report}} A leader in the channel told them to stop at once and said that anyone seen attacking allied territory would be expelled from the guild.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-april-june-2018-discord-private|23 Jun 2018, the order}} Two minutes later the guild's leadership apologised directly to the injured guild's representative and said demotions were coming; a minute after that, that they had been issued and that none of it had been condoned.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-april-june-2018-discord-private|23 Jun 2018, the apology and the demotions}} A member asked how people of that level were captains at all. Half an hour later one of those responsible said they had wanted to see how quickly they would die, and that it would not happen again.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-april-june-2018-discord-private|23 Jun 2018, the explanation}} The whole of it, from the report to the demotions, took four minutes.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-april-june-2018-discord-private|23 Jun 2018, the interval}}

## The remedy nobody used

In March 2018 the alliance had been clear with itself: a grievance did not license attacking allies, and doing it was the shortest road out of the alliance — a rule stated in the war channel and never written into any document.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|6 Mar 2018, the rule stated}} Over the following three months four more member-on-member attacks followed and that road was not taken once. Every one of them was closed by the offending guild's own leadership, in the channel where it was raised, within minutes to hours; the single proposal for an expulsion vote, on 27 April, was made in the war channel and abandoned in the same conversation.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-april-june-2018-discord-private|the four cases, 27 Apr – 23 Jun 2018}} Against twenty-one motions put to the voting channel between 2 April and 30 June 2018, not one concerned attacking an ally.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-april-june-2018-discord-private|the voting channel, 2 Apr – 30 Jun 2018}}

Reading the four together as a working practice rather than as four failures of the rule is this article's. What the alliance did write down that spring was about defending an ally's ground rather than taking it, and is at [[allied-defence|allied defence]]; the March cases are at [[federation-attacking-allies-march-2018|attacking allies in March 2018]].

## The rule, and the case it was stated in

The rule was put in the war channel rather than in any document. On 6 March 2018, after a territory log showed the city of Troms passing from [[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]] to New York Nibbas [NYN], the war coordinator of [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] told the member responsible that a grievance did not license attacking allies, and that doing so was the surest route out of the alliance.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|6 Mar 2018, the rule stated}} Member guilds had to be left alone whatever one thought of their leaders, he added, volunteering that he was no admirer of one member guild's leader himself and did not take that guild's ground over it.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|6 Mar 2018, the dislike disclaimed}}

The answer was not a denial but a definition. The member said he had never forgotten what that guild had done to him and that the city was his; that he did not consider KingdomPhoenixes an ally; and that they had done nothing and attacked their own allies as well.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|6 Mar 2018, the answer}} Asked for proof of the second charge, he told the channel to ask two members how they had been removed from the earlier alliance.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|6 Mar 2018, the proof offered}}

The guild doing the attacking was not in the alliance. Asked how New York Nibbas had access to the leadership channel at all when a vote was supposed to be required, a member was told that a member guild had given its leader that guild's own role.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|6 Mar 2018, the role handed over}} The vote on admitting New York Nibbas opened in that channel later the same morning and failed the following afternoon.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|6–7 Mar 2018, the ballot}} That ballot, and the parking option it carried for a member who had voted twice, is at [[federation-ballots-march-2018|the Federation's ballots in March 2018]].

It was settled bilaterally. Later on 6 March the leader of KingdomPhoenixes offered the attacker a personal undertaking in the war channel: neither would attack the other, with free-for-all ground excepted as open to both.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|6 Mar 2018, the private undertaking}} She mentioned that a new map was due shortly since the alliance had recovered most of the territory, and the offer was accepted twenty-one minutes later.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|6 Mar 2018, the offer accepted}} No alliance body considered the matter and no sanction of any kind followed.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|6 Mar 2018, no sanction}} What the excepted pool was is at [[federation-free-for-all-territories|the free-for-all territories]].

## Whose rules reached whose children

The next day the question arrived in its structural form, and a member said in as many words that nobody knew whether subguilds were permitted at all.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|7 Mar 2018, nobody knew}} The leader of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] supplied the rule from memory: subguilds were allowed provided the subguild was not led by the parent leader's alternate account, and using a subguild to feed captured territory into the parent guild was strictly forbidden, while running several to help fight enemies was acceptable.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|7 Mar 2018, the subguild rule}} A member immediately widened the first condition from the leader's alternate account to any member's.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|7 Mar 2018, the condition widened}} That is the whole of the Federation's subguild law, settled in a chat channel a month before the alliance obtained the game's own written rule.

It was tested twice in the same hour. A member said he wished [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]] could afford the arrangement Titans Valor had — at least three proxy guilds each with a substantial treasury, coordinating attacks, with the main guild coming in to take the territories — and was told the plan was against the rules because it amounted to feeding.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|7 Mar 2018, the proxy-guild plan refused}} Another pointed out that the leader of [[fantasy|Fantasy]] had been ordered to disband his own subguild although he was not that guild's leader either.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|7 Mar 2018, the uneven application}} The answer was an account of [[illustratus|Illustratus]], whose founder had been expelled from Titans Valor when that guild was created, had wanted to come back to the game, and had been given permission to start a branch.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|7 Mar 2018, the Illustratus exception}}

Two members of the same channel then said they intended to attack Illustratus regardless, one adding that since the guild was said to be going to attack them they would strike first.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|7 Mar 2018, the announced attacks}} The leader of Titans Valor told the channel that Illustratus had agreed to conduct no attacks for the duration of the vote on its admission.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|7 Mar 2018, the undertaking reported}} That vote failed.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|the Illustratus motion's tally, 7 Mar 2018}}

## What made a guild an enemy

The test was unsettled and the alliance argued it on 1 March 2018. Told in the general channel that the alliance had fought off [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] and its allies again, a member asked who the allies were and was given a list of nine guilds, to which a tenth was added seconds later.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|1 Mar 2018, the list of ten}} Seven minutes on, a member said TheWeirdoSquad was not really an enemy; another answered that the guild took the Rodoroc territory and was an enemy on that ground.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|1 Mar 2018, the objection and the answer}} The first replied that taking Rodoroc was all it did, and that to him a guild counted as an enemy only if it was actively targeting the alliance.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|1 Mar 2018, the test proposed}} The alliance's register of enemies is at [[alliance-governance|alliance governance]].

## Nobody could be ordered

On 13 March 2018 a member told the war channel that two guild leaders should order their guilds never again to defend the territories of [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]], the defences placed having been derisory, and repeated the demand.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|13 Mar 2018, the demand}} The Viceroy of Kingdom of Foxes answered that he was not to be commanded to order his guild to do anything, that his guild was not expected to defend allied territory heavily and knew it, and that the defence in question had been a mistake rather than a policy.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|13 Mar 2018, the refusal}} The alliance put the general question to a vote the same day; the limit it carried is at [[allied-defence|allied defence]].

## The invasion of a member

On 24 March 2018 four member guilds began taking [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]]'s territory and no vote for it was ever taken.{{cite:federation-map-disputes-march-2018-discord-private|24 Mar 2018, the attack}} The day after it began, a member defended it in the alliance's general channel as retaliation: the only reason BuildCraftia had been attacked was that it had attacked an ally first, and had an ally attacked the complainant's assigned territories the alliance would have fought for him too.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|25 Mar 2018, the defence of the invasion}} The complainant's charge was that the alliance applied its rules to some members and not to others.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|25 Mar 2018, the charge}}

The charge was traded in both directions two days later. A member complained in the war channel about Kingdom of Foxes' handling of a war and asked whether they were merely testing a build; the guild's war coordinator confirmed that a new member had been testing one.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|27 Mar 2018, the complaint}} The coordinator answered that the complainant had himself attacked allied territory belonging to two guilds when he was angry; the complainant said that at the time he had been close to destroying the alliance in any way he could.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|27 Mar 2018, the mutual accusation}}

## The one case that worked

On the morning of 29 March 2018 a member of Paladins United reported in the war channel that [[imperial|Imperial]] had taken the city of Corkus from them.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|29 Mar 2018, the report}} The alliance's founder asked why; a member of Imperial said nothing had been said about any such plan; another traced it from the logs to a particular member and gave the time.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|29 Mar 2018, the investigation}} The founder said the territory would be retaken but that the alliance needed to know why it had happened so that it would not recur.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|29 Mar 2018, the founder's direction}} The reporting member retook it, said the attackers appeared to have mistaken the city for another territory, and noted that a second territory had gone the same way; an Imperial member apologised within an hour.{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|29 Mar 2018, the restitution and the apology}}

It is the only episode of the month in which report, question, investigation, restitution and apology ran end to end, and what distinguishes it from the others is not its size. Nobody had meant it.

## Twelve of seventeen

On the evening of 28 August 2018, with an admission motion open, [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s founder asked the leadership channel whether the threshold that applied was two thirds.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|28 Aug 2018, the threshold question}} A member answered by doing the arithmetic in the channel. He asked whether the alliance stood at seventeen guilds and said it had not reached the mark yet.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|28 Aug 2018, the count of guilds}} Two thirds of seventeen, he made it, was twelve.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|28 Aug 2018, twelve of seventeen}} Ten minutes later a member reported that the count had reached twelve of seventeen and the motion was through.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|28 Aug 2018, the count reported}} He was answered in the next message by somebody who told him he had forgotten to allow for a placeholder, and who added that unless every guild still to vote voted against it, it would carry anyway.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|28 Aug 2018, the correction}}

Four messages settle two things the alliance never wrote down. Two thirds was the test on an admission and not a threshold kept for maps.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|28 Aug 2018, two thirds on an admission}} And a mark on a motion was not necessarily a vote: at least one of the marks on that ballot was known in the room to be furniture, and the leaders took it out in their heads before deciding whether they had won.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|28 Aug 2018, the placeholder allowed for}}

The electorate was guilds rather than people, and a guild leader said so while conceding what it cost him. Earlier the same evening the leader of [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] opened his objection to the application by saying that because each guild held one vote his objection counted for nothing formally, and then made it: the applicant was very new, had four members, and though they fought well there would be long stretches when none of them was online.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|28 Aug 2018, the objection}} His own guild had been readmitted six days earlier.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|22 Aug 2018, the readmission}}

## Two mornings later the procedure itself was in dispute

On 30 August a guild leader thanked a colleague for explaining that there was a voting period at all, said he had had no idea of it, and said he thought there was a better way to run the alliance's votes.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the voting period}} In the same conversation a member proposed that votes be made anonymous.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the anonymity proposal}} Neither the period nor the anonymity was changed. The twenty-four-hour ballot the leader had not heard of had been voted in on 4 May, not quite four months earlier, by ten votes to nothing.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-april-june-2018-discord-private|4 May 2018, the time-limit motion}}

## Two motions on the last evening

Night Warriors [NHW] applied on the last day of the month and was refused.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the application}} The reactions left on the motion give three in favour, thirteen against and one abstention.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the motion as exported}} Fourteen accounts marked it.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the accounts on the motion}} The account that put the motion sits on all three options, so one mark comes off each and the corrected figure is two in favour, twelve against and no abstention.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the mover on every option}} The first figure is the reactions as they stood when they were exported; the second takes out the account holding every option. Against a threshold of two thirds the motion failed on either.

The expulsion motion put at 18:48 the same evening needs no such correction. It stands at fifteen in favour and two against from seventeen accounts.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, 18:48, the expulsion motion's tally}} No account sits on both options, and the member who moved it sits on neither.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the reactions by account on the expulsion motion}} It was edited eight seconds after it went up, striking out its first half.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the edit stamp on the expulsion motion}} An edit that fast is somebody correcting his own message before anybody could read it, and what it removed went unkept.

One channel, one evening, two motions, and only one of them needs the arithmetic done to it. That is the case against applying a blanket adjustment to a tally.

## How these counts were taken

An account sitting on every option offered was a placeholder holding the marks so that others could react, which a member of the alliance said outright on 30 March 2018.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|30 Mar 2018, the explanation}} Taking such an account out is the whole of the correction. Moving a motion is not itself a reason to discount a mark: on none of the fourteen motions the voting channel carried in March 2018 did the member who put a motion sit on every option of it.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|movers and their marks across the fourteen March motions}} The first motion in the channel where one did was the admission of [[aphelion|Aphelion]] on 10 April 2018.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-april-june-2018-discord-private|the Aphelion motion's reactions, 10 Apr 2018}} The Night Warriors motion is that pattern appearing again four and a half months later.{{cite:federation-vote-arithmetic-august-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the mover on every option}}

An account on some of a motion's options but not all of them is a different thing and cannot be told from a guild that marked one option and then thought better of it, so a count turning on such an account has to be given both ways.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|the seeding correction, March 2018}} How the March ballots were assembled is at [[federation-ballots-march-2018|the Federation's ballots in March 2018]], the quarter that followed at [[federation-ballots-april-june-2018|April to June]], and the correction itself at [[alliance-governance|alliance governance]]. The admissions and refusals these motions decided are at [[federation-admissions-august-2018|the Federation's admissions in August 2018]].

Eighteen motions went to [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s voting channel in July 2018.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|the voting channel, July 2018}} Twelve of them were neither a map ratification nor the attempt to remove three member guilds.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|the twelve ordinary motions}} Those twelve have never been counted. The map votes are at [[federation-map-votes-2018|the Federation's map votes]] and the expulsion motion of the 19th at [[the-federation-july-2018|the Federation in July 2018]].

## A rule about consorting with the enemy

The month opened with a motion that came out of a live quarrel forty minutes earlier. On the late morning of 1 July 2018 the leadership channel pressed one guild's leader to break a pact with an enemy guild and to show proof of having done it.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|1 Jul 2018, the quarrel}} Asked whether such arrangements would be allowed in future, a member ruled them out and then set out his own position: that the alliance ought not to make them with guilds outside it except where the matter was one of community rather than warfare.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|1 Jul 2018, the position stated}} The subject deserved a vote, he said, and he posted one eight minutes later.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|1 Jul 2018, the motion posted}}

Which of its three positions carried cannot be read off the motion. It offered prohibition for war and community dealings both, prohibition for war only, or no prohibition at all.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|1 Jul 2018, the three options}} Sixteen accounts marked a single option and none marked any other, and the option they took corresponds to none of the three the text sets out.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|1 Jul 2018, the reactions and the mismatch}} The motion was edited fifty-three seconds after it went up, and what an edit changed was never kept.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|1 Jul 2018, the edit stamp}} The count is certain and the answer is not.

What the alliance did next is on record independently. Two days later the ban was written into a shared document of alliance rules, prohibiting war-related pacts with guilds outside the alliance while expressly allowing community-only ones, alongside a prohibition on leaking war information.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Federation guild-leader vote, 1–3 Jul 2018}} That is the second of the three positions the motion offered. Reading the rules document as the outcome of this ballot is this article's; the motion itself records only a count on an option that is not one of its own. The episode the quarrel came from is at [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]], and the rule it left behind at [[alliance-governance|alliance governance]].

## A motion with nothing to vote against

On 2 July the alliance replaced the role it used to summon help in wars. The motion asked whether to create a new voluntary role doing the work of the existing Captain role, so that members who did not want to be tagged would not be, and sixteen accounts marked its only option, the mover among them.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|2 Jul 2018, the role motion}} A motion with one option cannot record a no, so nothing about the strength of the agreement follows from the figure; the new role was in use in the war channel five days later.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|7 Jul 2018, the role in use}} The reason had been given in the war channel through the middle of the 2nd: the Captain tag had drawn complaints, and the replacement was to be a role for war alone, one that could be opted out of and was meant to be carried only by members who wanted to help.{{cite:federation-rule-and-practice-july-2018-discord-private|2 Jul 2018, the reason and the terms}} Two members answered in that conversation that renaming the role would change nothing and that the same people would object to the new one, and a third pointed out that the old role could be given up as well.{{cite:federation-rule-and-practice-july-2018-discord-private|2 Jul 2018, the objections}} The alliance had been quarrelling about those tags for a fortnight, and went on doing so through the month.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 6/27/2018 and 6/29/2018}}

## One guild admitted, one refused

Renegade [ReA] was admitted on 7 July by eleven votes to three. Its leader posted the application to the voting channel himself at 13:07.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|7 Jul 2018, 13:07, the application}} Fourteen accounts marked the motion, none of them on more than one option, so nothing has to be taken out of the figure.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|7 Jul 2018, the reactions by account}} The result was announced in the leadership channel at 17:58 and the new leader was welcomed and told he could bring a co-leader in{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|7 Jul 2018, 17:58, the result}}, and the alliance announced the admission the same day.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|7/7/2018}}

Grim [GRI] was refused on the 11th, and this is the one motion of the month where an exported tally and a participant's memory of it can be set beside each other. The motion went up at 20:10 with options for yes, no and abstain.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|11 Jul 2018, 20:10, the Grim motion}} Sixteen accounts marked it, eight, five and three, with nobody on more than one option.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|11 Jul 2018, the reactions by account}} Fifteen days later, asked in the leadership channel what had become of that vote, a member said it had failed and gave the figures as eight for, five against and three abstaining.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|26 Jul 2018, the recollection}} The two agree exactly.

The same motion drew the only recorded objection to giving guilds an abstention at all. Immediately after it went up a member asked the leadership channel why an admission vote had an abstain option, and another agreed with him; the alliance's founder answered that abstentions let a vote finish faster when people wanted to abstain.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|11 Jul 2018, the objection to abstentions}}

What the refused guild did next is at [[federation-under-attack-july-2018|the Federation under attack]].

## A placeholder in miniature

A housekeeping motion of 8 July shows in one line why a Federation tally cannot be read as cast. It asked whether to add a channel for important notices.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|8 Jul 2018, the notices motion}} The reactions give thirteen in favour and one against.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|8 Jul 2018, the tally as exported}} The single account against also marked the option in favour, so it stands on every option the motion offered and is not a voter; taking it out gives twelve to nothing.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|8 Jul 2018, the account on both options}} The whole of the recorded opposition was furniture.

A motion posted on 14 July carries no reactions at all. Its text is a fragment continuing an earlier message, it gives no options, and no result was ever recorded.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|14 Jul 2018, the unmarked motion}} The voting channel had carried one in the same condition in March.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|the unmarked motion, March 2018}}

## The map-drawing franchise, refused

On 15 July the alliance was asked to let everyone contribute to a possible map of the Wynn province so that everyone would be satisfied with it, the motion giving the splitting of two regions between several guilds as its example.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|15 Jul 2018, the franchise motion}} Sixteen accounts marked it, seven in favour including the mover and nine against, none on more than one option, and it failed.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|15 Jul 2018, the reactions by account}}

It failed two days after a map had been ratified seventeen to two.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|poll results, 13 Jul – 16 Aug 2018}} Each of those maps was drawn in a committee channel and put to a private show of reactions among the same guild leaders before it reached the voting channel, and the private tallies ran much closer than the public ones.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|committee reaction tallies, 18 Jul – 29 Aug 2018}} The alliance declined to widen a franchise it was already exercising in two other rooms; reading the two records together is this article's.

## The survey, and the answer it got

On 18 July the Federation surveyed its own guild leaders, and the answer was almost perfectly uniform. Three positions were offered: content with how the alliance was run and with its direction; content but wanting small changes; and not content and wanting major change.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|18 Jul 2018, the survey and its options}} It went up four days after the alliance renamed itself.{{cite:federation-late-july-2018-discord-private|14 Jul 2018}} One account marked all three options and was a placeholder rather than a voter; taking it out, seventeen guild leaders chose the middle position and nobody chose either of the others.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|18 Jul 2018, the reactions by account}}

Three things were said about it in the channel within the hour. A member asked whether everyone had voted and was told it was a selective survey rather than one open to every leader, which limits what the figure measures; the founder said within a minute of it opening that the result was exactly what he had expected; and a member observed that nobody knew what the small changes in question were.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|18 Jul 2018, the three remarks}} Four days after it, two member guilds left.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 7/22/2018}}

Ninety minutes after posting it the member who had written it said what they had wanted from it: more transparency in the alliance's territory assignment process, even if that amounted only to a channel the rest could watch without having any real say.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|18 Jul 2018, the aim stated}} Two guild representatives used the opening at once to state which territories their guilds cared about keeping and which they did not.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|18 Jul 2018, the two representatives}} That evening the alliance's territory committee channel was opened to every guild leader, the founder giving as his reason that they had asked for more transparency.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|18 Jul 2018, the channel opened}} Nothing in either record says the survey was what he meant.

## A guild asking to be stripped

The last of the twelve is the strangest. On 26 July [[the-hive|The Hive]] [THI] asked the alliance to take almost all of its territory away.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|26 Jul 2018, the motion}} It proposed that the next map assign the guild three named territories and nothing else.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|26 Jul 2018, the three territories}} It was consistently a priority target for the opposition, it said, and the change would reduce the pressure on it, open ground for other guilds and for the free-for-all pool, and let it defend less and help its allies more — while conceding that its enemies might simply follow it onto whatever it kept.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|26 Jul 2018, the case made}} It asked the alliance's view before putting the change forward. Nine accounts approved, four objected and two recorded themselves indifferent.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|26 Jul 2018, the reactions}} The guild that had argued the small guilds' case inside the alliance was asking to be relieved of its own map.{{cite:the-hive-guild-profile-discord-private|March 2018}}

## How these counts were taken

An account sitting on every option a motion offered was a placeholder holding the marks so that others could react, which a member said outright on 30 March 2018, and taking such an account out is the whole of the correction.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|30 Mar 2018, the explanation}} Two of the July motions carry one.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|the placeholder accounts, July 2018}} Moving a motion is not by itself a reason to discount a mark: the movers of 2 and 15 July each marked a single option of their own motions, and those marks are votes.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|the movers and their marks, July 2018}} An account on some of a motion's options but not all of them cannot be told from a guild that marked one and thought better of it, and no July motion turns on one.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|multi-option reactions, July 2018}}

A tally is the reactions still attached years afterwards rather than the vote as cast, so direction and margin carry and an exact figure does not.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|the tallies as exported}} Where the alliance stated a threshold it stated it off the motion — the two thirds that defeated the expulsion of the 19th was given that evening in the leadership channel and appears nowhere in the motion's own text.{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|19 Jul 2018, the threshold given in answer}} How the March ballots were assembled is at [[federation-ballots-march-2018|the Federation's ballots in March 2018]], the quarter before this one at [[federation-ballots-april-june-2018|April to June]], and the month after at [[federation-ballots-august-2018|August 2018]].

## Somebody had to put the marks there first

[[the-federation|The Federation]] decided by having each guild leader react to one of a set of marks placed on a message, and the marks did not appear on their own. On the evening of 13 March 2018 a guild leader asked the leadership channel why guilds such as [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] appeared to get two votes, and whether that was not against the rule issued three days earlier.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|13 Mar 2018, the question put}} The alliance's founder answered that he ordinarily voted for all of the options himself when he added the marks to a motion.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|13 Mar 2018, the founder's answer}} Another member called such marks placeholders in the same minute, and the questioner withdrew the point.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|13 Mar 2018, the placeholders}} The appearance the questioner was reacting to had a second cause the answer did not reach. Kingdom of Foxes held two accounts in the leaders' channel{{cite:march-2018-observations-verified-discord-private|the two Kingdom of Foxes accounts, March 2018}}, and one of them said on 14 March that her vote overruled the other's.{{cite:march-2018-observations-verified-discord-private|14 Mar 2018, the vote that overruled}} It is the practice set out by the person doing it, seventeen days before the account of it given on 30 March.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|30 Mar 2018, the explanation}}

What it cost fell on the smallest members, and one of them said so in the same exchange. The representative of [[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]] answered that it was better the founder place the marks, because if she placed them her guild would not get a vote at all, having no co-leader to cast one.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|13 Mar 2018, the KingdomPhoenixes answer}} One account could build a ballot or vote in it and not both, so one vote per guild, in a room where ballots were assembled by hand, took the vote from any guild too small to send two people. Reading the two remarks together is this article's; each half was said and neither speaker drew the conclusion.

The permission was itself scarce. On 8 March 2018, a day after the voting channel was created, a member observed that a particular rank was needed to put the marks on a motion at all.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|8 Mar 2018, the rank required}} He complained that the marks came out in the wrong order, and proposed removing the permission from members who added joke marks to motions.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|8 Mar 2018, the sanction proposed}}

## The arithmetic nobody could do

The threshold was two thirds. What two thirds of what was worked out in public twice in three days. On 7 March 2018 the founder asked his own leadership channel how many votes a two-thirds majority needed if two guilds abstained, and was given a number with a hedge after it.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|7 Mar 2018, the question about abstentions}} On 9 March he stated the rule as two thirds of the whole guild total less those which had abstained, and put the number of guilds at twenty-two.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|9 Mar 2018, the rule and the electorate}} The leader of [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]] restated it as two thirds of all guilds rather than of those who had voted.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|9 Mar 2018, the rival formulation}}

The disagreement decided a motion that week. On 9 March the leader of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] told the channel that the vote he had moved stood at ten to two and that this was a four-fifths majority; the founder told him that was not how it worked.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|9 Mar 2018, the four-fifths claim}} The motion was the admission of [[illustratus|Illustratus]] as a guild in its own right, posted to the voting channel on 7 March, and it carries eleven in favour, four against and two abstaining.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|the Illustratus motion's tally, 7 Mar 2018}} Nothing comes off that: no account on it sat on every option, and a mover's own mark on this channel's motions is a vote.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|multi-option reactions and movers' marks across the fourteen March motions}} It failed. Twenty-two guilds less two abstentions is twenty and two thirds of twenty is fourteen, so it was three short; that figure is this article's arithmetic from the founder's own rule and electorate, and nobody in the channel produced it.

The readmission of [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] was announced with a figure the motion only half bears out. The founder reported the result to the leadership channel on the morning of 8 March 2018 as fourteen in favour and two abstentions, and said it met two thirds.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|8 Mar 2018, the announcement}} The motion, posted on the evening of the 7th, carries fifteen in favour, two against and two abstaining, with nothing to take off it; the two against went unmentioned.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|the Paladins United motion's tally, 7 Mar 2018}} The count the founder gave the channel that morning is the contemporaneous one and the better evidence of the vote as it was cast; the exported tally is the marks still attached years afterwards.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|declared counts against exported tallies, March 2018}} It carries an edit made twenty-three minutes after posting, so its final wording is not certainly the wording the guilds reacted to.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|the edit stamp, 7 Mar 2018}} The result stands either way: fourteen carries against a threshold of fourteen, and against eleven if the two against go back into the total.

## A place to put a second vote

The alliance's own ballot design allowed for double voting four days before it issued the rule against it. The vote on admitting New York Nibbas [NYN] was opened in the leadership channel on 6 March 2018 with three options: yes, no, and one for a member who had double voted and was moving the extra mark onto it.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|6 Mar 2018, the three options}} It ran overnight; on the afternoon of 7 March the founder checked it, observed that it was not a two-thirds majority, was told that it was not, closed it and pinned the result.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|7 Mar 2018, the closure}} Eleven were in favour, seven against, and five accounts sat on the double-voters' option.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|7 Mar 2018, the count}} He opened the motion readmitting Paladins United ninety seconds later, with the same three options.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|7 Mar 2018, the second motion}} The rule of one vote per guild reached the voting channel on 10 March.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|10 Mar 2018, the rule issued mid-ratification}}

## The minute a map was ratified

The threshold on the month's first territory map was settled while the count was closing. At 13:58 on 10 March 2018, on the export's own clock, a member of the leadership channel asked whether the standard was a simple majority or whether unanimity was wanted; the member conducting the ratification answered two thirds, and the alliance's founder said not unanimous.{{cite:federation-first-fortnight-of-march-2018-discord-private|10 Mar 2018, 13:58, the standard asked for}} The map was declared passed in the same minute, and a member asked whether the founder wanted to post it.{{cite:federation-first-fortnight-of-march-2018-discord-private|10 Mar 2018, the map declared passed}} The allocation went into the announcements channel at 2:11 that afternoon — thirteen minutes later, on the same clock.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Deleted User, 3/10/2018 2:11 PM}} That clock ran eight hours behind the capture log in the first ten days of March 2018, before daylight saving began on the 11th, so the same publication is timed at 22:11 where the map's ratification is given in [[federation-map-votes-2018|the Federation's map votes]].

In the minute before the close the alliance also refused canyon territory to [[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]] on the ground that its one active member had no time to fight, a decision taken as an aside while the count ran.{{cite:federation-first-fortnight-of-march-2018-discord-private|10 Mar 2018, the canyon refused}} The map published thirteen minutes later gave it fifteen territories in the Llevigar eastern plains, Bucie and the southernmost swamp, and none in the canyon.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Deleted User, 3/10/2018 2:11 PM}} What that cost the guild across the following week is at [[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]]. The two thirds is a fact about this map and no other: the standard was stated twice in the leadership channel that week and disputed both times.

## The ballot that came before the channel

The fourteen motions are the voting channel's record and not the month's. The Federation put the application of Funbari Onsen [FBO] to its whole leadership on 2 March 2018, five days before that channel existed.{{cite:federation-funbari-onsen-admission-march-2018-discord-private|2 Mar 2018, the application put to the leaders}} The ballot opened at 10:57 and closed at 18:43 the same day{{cite:federation-funbari-onsen-admission-march-2018-discord-private|2 Mar 2018, the ballot opened and closed}}, carrying twenty in favour to two against{{cite:federation-funbari-onsen-admission-march-2018-discord-private|2 Mar 2018, the count}}, and it was held in the leadership channel for want of anywhere else.{{cite:federation-funbari-onsen-admission-march-2018-discord-private|2 Mar 2018, the channel used}}

Neither correction the later tallies need can be applied to it. Whether any account on the motion sat on both options went unrecorded, and nobody in the channel gave the count again afterwards, so no contemporaneous figure stands beside the exported one.{{cite:federation-funbari-onsen-admission-march-2018-discord-private|the tally as it stands, 2 Mar 2018}} The practice preceded the room: admissions were put to every guild leader and counted before the alliance had a channel, a threshold argument or a rule about who might place the marks. The admission itself is at [[federation-admissions-march-2018|the Federation's admissions in March 2018]].

## Silence as abstention

The motion that made silence count as abstention had to set the alliance's practice aside in order to ask the question. Posted to the voting channel on 9 March 2018, it asked whether a guild that voted for nothing and did not abstain should count as abstaining, lowering the number of yes votes a motion needed, or as an assumed no.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|9 Mar 2018, the question put}} It offered no abstain option of its own and said that whichever option held the majority would simply be taken.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|9 Mar 2018, the terms of the motion}} Counting non-voters as abstaining carried twenty-one to five, and nothing comes off that tally either: no account on the motion sat on more than one option, and the guild that moved it voted on it like the rest.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|the abstention motion's tally, 9 Mar 2018}} It is the rule that carried the expulsion of [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] three weeks later on ten abstentions.{{cite:federation-buildcraftia-expulsion-march-2018-discord-private|the abstention arithmetic, 30 Mar 2018}}

## A motion with nothing on it

One motion of the month carries no marks at all, though a count for it was reported eight minutes after it went up. It asked whether [[house-of-sentinels|House of Sentinels]] should be admitted, and went to the voting channel at 11:30 on the export's clock on 12 March 2018.{{cite:house-of-sentinels-march-2018-discord-private|12 Mar 2018, the motion}} At 11:38 a member told the leadership channel that the vote was over and that a third of the guilds had voted against, which under a two-thirds threshold settled it.{{cite:house-of-sentinels-march-2018-discord-private|12 Mar 2018, the declaration}} Two members gave the count in the same minute, first as eight against to one in favour and then as seven against to none.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|12 Mar 2018, the two counts}} The exported message carries nothing.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|the House of Sentinels motion as exported}} Where the alliance's own members put a count down at the time, that count is the better one — and here they disagreed with each other inside a minute.

## Who could sway a vote

The alliance put the question of bloc power to itself at the end of the month and answered no. On 27 March 2018 a member posted an opinion vote to the voting channel asking who thought that Kingdom of Foxes or the guilds of the former [[coalition|Coalition]] had the power to sway votes in their favour.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|27 Mar 2018, the opinion vote}} Sixteen marked no and six yes once the one account sitting on both options — every option the motion offered — comes out; the mover's single mark is a vote and stays.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|27 Mar 2018, the tally}} No consequence was attached to it. The same charge was made in the leadership channel that day and answered at length by the Kingdom of Foxes' Viceroy, who put the number of guilds surviving from the Coalition at ten.{{cite:federation-map-disputes-march-2018-discord-private|27 Mar 2018, the answer}}

The correction these counts need — take out the accounts sitting on every option, which were placeholders holding the marks so that others could use them, and leave a mover's single mark alone, because it is a vote — is set out at [[alliance-governance|alliance governance]]. Of the month's fourteen motions, five carry an account on every option and seven an account on more than one; where an account sits on some options but not all it cannot be told from a guild voting twice, and a count that turns on one is given both ways.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|multi-option reactions across the fourteen March motions}}

Times here are the Discord export's own clock, which ran eight hours behind UTC in February 2018. Capture-log figures are UTC and say so.

## The two documents

The [[coalition|Coalition]] kept a standing card in its alliance-information channel listing its fourteen member guilds and, beneath them, fourteen guilds named as enemies of the alliance. It was posted on 11 January 2018, the day the alliance's replacement server was built, and last revised on 14 February; an edited Discord message kept only its final text, so the card's contents belong to the February date.{{cite:coalition-information|posted 1/11/2018 9:33 PM, last revised 14 Feb 2018}} Its own member list settles that: [[holders-of-le|Holders of LE]] and [[emorians|Emorians]] are on it, and both were admitted between 9 and 11 February.{{cite:coalition-last-fortnight-discord-private|9-11 Feb 2018}} As posted on 11 January the card had named fifteen member guilds and eleven enemies, with [[fantasy|Fantasy]] among the members and [[necronomicon|Necronomicon]] among the enemies; by 20 January Fantasy had been added to the enemy list and Necronomicon taken off it.{{cite:coalition-standing-documents-january-2018-discord-private|the card as posted, 11 Jan 2018}}{{cite:coalition-standing-documents-january-2018-discord-private|20 Jan 2018, the enemy list}} The date it does carry is two days before the alliance broke.

The second document is the Federation's founding territorial settlement, announced to the alliance at 9:12 PM on 21 February 2018, naming twenty-two member guilds and the region each was given.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}}

## Nine, six and seven

Set one against the other and the twenty-two divide three ways.

Nine had been Coalition members: [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]], [[imperial|Imperial]], [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]], LE Flowers, [[holders-of-le|Holders of LE]], [[serpentem-empire|Serpentem Empire]], DeathReapers, [[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]] and Immortalish.{{cite:coalition-information|member list as revised 14 Feb 2018}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}} One of the nine sits there awkwardly: KingdomPhoenixes walked out with the other five on 16 February, and it holds the South Canyon in the settlement five days later with no readmission recorded anywhere.{{cite:coalition-fracture-2018-discord-private|16-21 Feb 2018}}

Six were on the enemy list: [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]], [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]], [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]], [[dogsamongus|DogsAmongUs]], [[fantasy|Fantasy]] and [[the-divine-swords|The Divine Swords]].{{cite:coalition-information|enemy list as revised 14 Feb 2018}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}}

Seven appear on neither: The Hive, Kasai Shinrai, [[angels-of-eternal|Angels of Eternal]], [[iceblue-team|Ice Blue Team]], TheEnchanted, Deimos and [[illustratus|Illustratus]].{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}} Two of the seven need a qualification. Kasai Shinrai was not new: it had been a Coalition guild at the declaration of war in December 2017 and was off the card by February.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|roster at the declaration of war, part 14, composed 22 Mar 2018}} And Illustratus held ground before any vote let it in — it first appears in the capture log on 19 February, two days before the settlement gave it Nemract, and the vote on its admission failed three to seven on 25 February.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Illustratus, first exchange 19 Feb 2018 UTC}}{{cite:federation-first-month-2018-discord-private|admission votes, late February 2018}}

The arithmetic closes at the other end too. Of the Coalition's fourteen members, the five absent from the settlement — [[hackforums|HackForums]], [[paladins-united|Paladins United]], [[house-of-sentinels|House of Sentinels]], [[emorians|Emorians]] and [[kangronomicon|Kangronomicon]] — are exactly the five that formed [[alliance-alliance|Alliance Alliance]], and five is the figure the alliance's founder gave when he was asked about the walkout six months afterwards.{{cite:coalition-information|member list as revised 14 Feb 2018}}{{cite:coalition-collapse-as-remembered-discord-private|August 2018}} Of its fourteen named enemies, eight are absent from the settlement.

Eight guilds were left in the Coalition on the evening of the walkout.{{cite:coalition-fracture-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018}} Five days later its successor named twenty-two.

## Titans Valor, 16 to 21 February

Every step of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]]'s admission is dated. It was proposed on [[the-federation-founding-night|the founding night]] and refused: asked whether the guild was in the alliance, the Coalition's leader said no twice and another member said no twice more.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018, 20:12}} On the morning of 17 February a member asked the general channel whether the alliance was on good terms with it and was told to leave it alone for the moment, that it was attacking [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]], and simply not to talk to it.{{cite:federation-from-the-enemy-list-2018-discord-private|17 Feb 2018, morning}} The capture log has that fighting from outside: the two guilds took Llevigar, Llevigar Gate East and West, the Llevigar plains, Orc Lake and Green Camp off each other in turn between 19:21 and 22:13 UTC that day.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Titans Valor and Hall of Fame, exchanges both ways, 17 Feb 2018 19:21-22:13 UTC}}

At 17:30 that afternoon the head of [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]] put its admission to the leaders' channel, opening by asking the room to hold its complaints. The objections were personal and the case made for it was not: that it was war-active, that it was not in fact an enemy, and that it was better used than opposed. One leader agreed on condition that the alliance could expel it if it caused trouble; another observed that the rival bloc had not taken it in either.{{cite:federation-from-the-enemy-list-2018-discord-private|17 Feb 2018, 17:30}} It was announced as a member at 19:14 and given a role three minutes later.{{cite:federation-from-the-enemy-list-2018-discord-private|17 Feb 2018, 19:14-19:17}}

At 09:59 the next morning it attacked a member guild, and the response in the war channel was to invite it.{{cite:federation-from-the-enemy-list-2018-discord-private|18 Feb 2018, 09:59}} The capture log names the ground and fixes the minute on the other clock: Titans Valor took Orc Road from Hall of Fame at 17:59:47 UTC on 18 February.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Titans Valor takes Orc Road from Hall of Fame, 18 Feb 2018 17:59:47 UTC}}

On 20 February its leader negotiated his allocation with the founder directly and was told which region his guild would receive; another leader complained the following morning that it had been given too much.{{cite:federation-from-the-enemy-list-2018-discord-private|20-21 Feb 2018}} At 21:11 on the 21st the founder told the leaders' channel that the map stood one vote short of the two-thirds majority that motion required, and asked that leader to vote. He voted inside the minute, and the settlement went out to the whole alliance at 21:12:30.{{cite:federation-vote-procedure-february-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, 21:11-21:12:30}} Titans Valor was given the Light Realm, the north-eastern ocean and Rymek.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}} Five days earlier the alliance had refused it, and four days earlier it had been told not to speak to it.

Where the guild stood on the map across that week cannot be settled from the capture log. No enumerated list of free-for-all territories covers February 2018: the Coalition marked its open ground on a map rather than naming it, and the Federation had no map at all before the settlement of the 21st.{{cite:coalition-announcements|1/16/2018, free-for-all ground shown on the map only}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}} No capture count for the window can have that churn taken out of it.

## Drafting the settlement

Three people drafted it in a private group message. On the evening of 20 February a leader told the guild leaders' channel that three named members were working out the assignments, and half an hour later that they had a working map.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, the drafting}} Asked to show it, the founder said to send it in the group message rather than to the channel.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, where to send the map}}

The criteria were four: what a guild had contributed to the war, its guild level, what it could get out of a region, and whether it could hold what it was given.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, the four criteria}} Equality was not among them; a member of the leadership proposed in the same exchange that the Gavel regions go to the guilds that had helped most and Wynn to those that had helped less.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, Gavel and Wynn}} Wynn had to be assigned at all, the founder said, because there were by then too many guilds to leave it open.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, why Wynn was assigned}}

Guilds negotiated, and about content rather than number. On the morning of the 21st [[imperial|Imperial]]'s representative said his guild had expected the Light Forest and Aldorei because it had held them before, and that it fought as much as the guild being offered them instead.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, the Imperial claim}} He was told he could have Aldorei only if [[holders-of-le|Holders of LE]] took part of his Light Forest, and answered that the question was not how many territories a guild had but which of the game's quests ended inside them — a criterion nobody had written down.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, the quests argument}} Twenty-six minutes later he proposed the Quartz Mines instead, taking nothing from anyone, and two leaders agreed within a minute.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, the Quartz Mines swap}}

The map was pinned in the leaders' channel at 06:53 that morning, fourteen hours before it went to the whole alliance at 21:12.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, 06:53}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}} It moved in between: at 08:03 the founder told the war channel that more changes were needed because some leaders were unhappy, and at 08:25 a leader said five named guilds could each probably be given one or two more.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, 08:03-08:25}}

## Eight territories in a swamp

Late on 23 February [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]]'s representative told the war channel that his guild had been handed eight low-level territories in ground it did not want, that it would not accept them, and that the alliance would break up quickly.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|23 Feb 2018, the challenge}} The founder answered that guilds had been given what they had contributed and that most people had wanted to give BuildCraftia less.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|23 Feb 2018, the founder's answer}} A [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] moderator then told him his guild had not been in the alliance during the period he was claiming credit for, that the alliance had not existed then, and that better ground had to be earned inside the present membership.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|23 Feb 2018, earned on present membership}}

At 01:27 on the 24th members asked whether the guild had left; the founder said its leader had walked out of a private leadership conversation and was apparently going to [[hackforums|HackForums]] and [[paladins-united|Paladins United]], and members began discussing redistributing the swamp.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, 01:27}} At 02:13 a member corrected the room: the departure was not official, and the guild's other leader would have to agree.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, 02:13}} BuildCraftia was still a member four days later.

It took allied ground rather than waiting for more. On 25 February the guild Elegant was reported angry because BuildCraftia had invaded its territories.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|25 Feb 2018, Elegant}} On the 27th a leader named greed as the reason and another said the point was to demonstrate the guild could hold more than it had been given; the ground belonged to [[serpentem-empire|Serpentem Empire]] and LE Flowers.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|27 Feb 2018, the invasions}} That afternoon it put the threat in writing: it would join the other side if it did not get the swamp. A leader answered that a guild could not demand an immediate result, demand more land and threaten to betray its allies at once; the representative answered that he did expect a result immediately.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|27 Feb 2018, the threat}}

Money ended it, not ground. [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]]'s leader offered Serpentem Empire a share of Corkus instead, saying Hall of Fame could not hold it anyway; within the hour BuildCraftia paid thirty-two liquid emeralds into the banks of the two guilds it had taken from.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|27 Feb 2018, the transfer}} No allocation changed.

The alternative had been argued down three days before. Members set the [[war-syndicate|War Syndicate]] against the settlement — it had given every member guild twenty-four territories whatever it did — and were answered that the equal model had worked only because that alliance refused new members, and would now leave dormant guilds holding ground while the active ones carried the war.{{cite:federation-allocation-negotiated-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, the equal-shares alternative}}

## Turned away for want of room

On 28 February a leader told the guild leaders' channel that Wrath of Poseidon and, separately, Brilliance had asked to join, and that he had told them both to wait until there was more territory to give; the founder agreed.{{cite:federation-recruitment-and-refusal-february-2018-discord-private|28 Feb 2018, the two applications}} Neither was refused on anything to do with what it was: one came from the [[illustratus|Illustratus]] grouping the leader himself had come from, the other was led by a former officer of [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]].{{cite:federation-recruitment-and-refusal-february-2018-discord-private|28 Feb 2018, the two guilds described}} It is the gate [[federation-admissions-february-2018|the month's admission motions]] kept running into, applied where nobody was voting.

A guild could also be decided against without a vote. On the morning of 20 February a leader asked whether they were going to talk to [[blacklisted|Blacklisted]] about an alliance or simply eliminate it; the founder answered that they would eliminate it, having already put the question of joining to the guild's fighting officers and been refused.{{cite:federation-recruitment-and-refusal-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, Blacklisted}} No motion was put. Four days later a member made the opposite case about New York Nibbas in the general channel — that the alliance should invite it rather than let it become an enemy — and was told to bring it to the leaders' channel as a proposal.{{cite:federation-recruitment-and-refusal-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, New York Nibbas}} A motion to issue that invitation carried sixteen to one the next day.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|25 Feb 2018, the invitation motion}} Reading the second as the outcome of the first is this article's.

## The admissions doctrine

The alliance stated its admissions doctrine on its second day, and it was still applying the same one six months later. Arguing for Titans Valor on 17 February the head of Sins of Seedia told the leaders' channel that having the guild inside was better than having it against them, and that it was war-active and not actually an enemy; another leader said she had no objection to its leader and that if he wanted to help he should be allowed to.{{cite:federation-from-the-enemy-list-2018-discord-private|17 Feb 2018}} In August 2018 the alliance made that argument again, to admit two guilds that were attacking its own members.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|August 2018}}

The reversal was not confined to guilds hostile only on paper. Sins of Seedia, whose head announced the merger on the founding night, is on the enemy card.{{cite:coalition-information|enemy list as revised 14 Feb 2018}}{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018}} BuildCraftia is on it and received the western half of the swamp in the settlement.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}} Fantasy is on it having left the Coalition in January and fought [[the-trench-war|the Trench War]] against it.{{cite:the-trench-war-january-2018-discord-private|January 2018}} It was voted into the Federation on 18 February, on terms requiring its leader's other guild to take part.{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|18 Feb 2018}}

The membership was held loosely enough that members did not agree who was in it. On the evening of 17 February one enumerated the opposing side for another as Paladins United, HackForums, [[emorians|Emorians]], [[kangronomicon|Kangronomicon]], House of Sentinels, [[necronomicon|Necronomicon]], Fantasy and Titans Valor, hedging that he was going from memory and was forgetting guilds.{{cite:federation-from-the-enemy-list-2018-discord-private|17 Feb 2018, evening}} Titans Valor had been announced as a member that same evening, and Fantasy was voted in the following day. Both hold assigned regions in the settlement published four days later.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}}

## The only ground its guilds could contest

Most of [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s map was assigned: a region to a guild, and allies left it alone. A short list of territories belonged to nobody, and any member guild could take one from any other.{{cite:free-for-all-territories-discord-private|the three categories, Feb 2018}} They were the only ground inside the alliance on which its own guilds were allowed to compete.

The first list, of 21 February 2018, named fourteen territories and was posted twice in the leadership channel inside twenty minutes.{{cite:free-for-all-territories-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, fourteen territories}} From 10 March the alliance published the list beside its guild-by-guild allocation, and it grew as the assigned map shrank.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|3/10/2018, 3/24/2018 and 4/22/2018 6:22 PM}} It was alliance business rather than public business: on 26 July 2018 a member asked whether the list could be given to anybody outside the Federation at all.{{cite:free-for-all-territories-discord-private|26 Jul 2018, the request}}

## The pool in the spring

Three published lists fall between the amendment of 27 March and the audit of September, and the pool moves across them by ones. The list of 22 April 2018 names twenty-two territories; that of 10 May names twenty-three, the addition being Twisted Ridge; that of 4 June names twenty-one, dropping the swamp and dark forest transition, Wybel Island and Twisted Ridge and adding Raider's Base Upper. Every other entry is common to all three, and each list prints its own count against what is in it.{{cite:federation-allocation-counts-recounted-2018-discord-private|the three lists compared, 22 Apr, 10 May and 4 Jun 2018}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|4/22/2018 6:22 PM, 5/10/2018 and 6/4/2018}}

One of the three removals has a reason behind it. On 5 June 2018 the alliance's map-maker told the advisory council that Wybel Island had been replaced as free-for-all ground because it had been of little use.{{cite:federation-map-by-image-june-2018-discord-private|5 Jun 2018, why Wybel Island came out}} It had entered the pool ten weeks earlier by an edit rather than a vote, when the alliance's leader asked on 27 March for it to be added to a published list which did not carry it and the announcement was edited two and a half minutes later.{{cite:federation-map-making-march-2018-discord-private|27 Mar 2018, the two requests and the edit stamp}}

How the list was published changed in the middle of the run. On 22 April the allocation and the free-for-all list went up as two messages four hours apart; from 10 May they were one message, with the open list as its closing line — a line that begins with a bracketed tag of the same shape as a guild's.{{cite:federation-allocation-counts-recounted-2018-discord-private|the publication of the three lists}} The separate April list carries no edit stamp, unlike the allocation posted four hours before it, and is the only one of the three that can be dated to its own day without qualification.{{cite:federation-allocation-counts-recounted-2018-discord-private|the free-for-all list of 22 Apr 2018}}

## The rule of 17 June 2018

From the middle of June the pool could grow without anybody publishing anything. On 17 June 2018 the voting channel carried a motion, described in its own text as a revision put again to close loopholes found in an earlier vote, providing that assigned territory a guild could not hold, and made no effective attempt to reclaim within four days, became free-for-all ground; that the rule reached only guilds holding fewer than thirty assigned territories; that it reached only guilds which still held their own assigned land and wanted to fight elsewhere; that the open period ended and began again if the owner reclaimed its ground, after which the territory was not to be attacked; and that enemy guilds could be attacked there regardless. It carried nine to two.{{cite:federation-map-by-image-june-2018-discord-private|17 Jun 2018, the motion and its tally}} The motion went up at 00:01 on the export's clock and was edited at 07:42, so its final wording is not certainly the wording the guilds reacted to, and whether it was ever applied to a named territory was never set down.{{cite:federation-map-by-image-june-2018-discord-private|the edit stamp on the motion of 17 Jun 2018}}

It is the only instrument of the period that altered the pool by rule rather than by list or by map. From that week [[the-federation|the Federation]] could change what was open in three ways: by publishing a list, by drawing a map — the four it drew in June were images with no list attached — or by nobody doing anything for four days.{{cite:federation-map-by-image-june-2018-discord-private|the four image maps of Jun 2018}} The June ballots are at [[federation-map-votes-2018|the Federation's map votes]].

## Twenty-one before, twenty-one after

The pool's size was in dispute before its contents changed. On the morning of 16 July 2018 a member told the advisory channel that the alliance was already short of free-for-all ground{{cite:federation-free-for-all-list-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, the shortage}}, that trading two more territories away to settle the dispute over Corkus Island would make it worse{{cite:federation-free-for-all-list-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, the Corkus trade}}, and that he was not on the map team and had no direct say.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-list-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, the map team}} Another put the pool at twenty-one against about ten under the [[coalition|Coalition]], and said no guild would ever hold all twenty-one at once.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-list-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, twenty-one against about ten}} A third asked why Guild Hall had been made an assigned territory when it had been free-for-all before{{cite:federation-free-for-all-list-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, Guild Hall}}, and a fourth proposed taking Avos Temple out and putting another territory in.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-list-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, the substitution proposed}}

Two of the pool's territories sat on Corkus and were the sticking point in the dispute over the island. The guild seeking sole control was told it could have all of Corkus except those two{{cite:federation-free-for-all-list-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, the two Corkus territories}}, and its written proposal had asked for them by name.{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, the proposal}}

Nine days later the pool changed by map. The alliance's map-maker described the change as taking Factory Entrance and Avos Temple out and putting Mine Base Plains in{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-2018-discord-private|25 Jul 2018, the substitution}}, and the map went to the announcements channel the next afternoon as images with no list attached.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 7/26/2018 2:11 PM}} On the afternoon of the 27th a member asked the war channel which free-for-all territories had changed, saying he could not face going through them all and that there were still twenty-one of them{{cite:federation-free-for-all-list-july-2018-discord-private|27 Jul 2018, 17:05 alliance clock, the question}}; two minutes later he was told that Avos Temple and Factory Entrance had gone and that Guild Hall and Mine Base Plains had come in.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-list-july-2018-discord-private|27 Jul 2018, 17:07 alliance clock, the four territories named}} Four movements against a list of twenty-one leave twenty-one, which is the count he gave: the map exchanged the pool's contents rather than shrinking it.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|6/4/2018}}

The second addition is the weaker half of that answer. It came from one member{{cite:federation-free-for-all-list-july-2018-discord-private|27 Jul 2018, one member's answer}}, and another in the same exchange produced a copy of a list he said he did not know to be current.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-list-july-2018-discord-private|27 Jul 2018, the list nobody could vouch for}} Two of the four movements are carried by the next declaration and two are not. Mine Base Plains, named on no Federation list published before that summer, is on the list of 6 August{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 8/6/2018 3:51 PM}}; Avos Temple, on every list from 10 March onward, is absent from it.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|3/10/2018 and 6/4/2018}} Factory Entrance came back by the announcement of 5 August, which described it and Mine Base Plains as free-for-all again.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 8/5/2018 8:13 PM}} Guild Hall was never named on any declaration the alliance published.

The map moved with the pool. Guild Hall had not changed hands since 7 July and changed hands four times in fifty-three minutes on the 27th{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges on Guild Hall, 1–31 Jul 2018 UTC: none 7–26 Jul, four between 14:35 and 15:28 on 27 Jul}}; Mine Base Plains, untouched since the 20th, passed through ten guilds in the thirty-eight hours from the evening of the 26th.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges on Mine Base Plains, 1 Jul – 5 Aug 2018 UTC: none 20–26 Jul, then 16 exchanges among 10 attackers, 26 Jul 21:37 to 28 Jul 11:01}} The map as a whole was busy in the same week, at between 560 and 1,503 exchanges a day, so those two were quiet while the rest was not.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges per UTC day, 20–29 Jul 2018: 560 to 1,503}} Avos Temple, named as a removal, changed hands twenty-one times over 27–29 July and finished each of those days with [[imperial|Imperial]], the guild that had asked for the whole island.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges on Avos Temple, 27–29 Jul 2018 UTC: 21, the last capture of each day by Imperial}}

Reading that churn as the pool's composition rather than as anybody's hostility is this article's. Setting the arguments of 16 July beside the movements nine days later is this article's too: two of the four territories had been named in that conversation, moving the way it had proposed, and nothing joined the two at the time.

## The audit of 8 September

At a quarter to two in the morning on 8 September 2018 the member who kept the list posted a new one to both of the alliance's public channels, prefaced by "Oof since i messed up the last list".{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 9/8/2018 1:46 AM}}{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Viaire, 9/8/2018 1:46 AM}} As the document now reads it names sixteen territories grouped by province: seven in Gavel, one in the Ocean province and eight in Wynn.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 9/8/2018 1:46 AM}} The list before it, published on 6 August to the public channel alone, named twenty-two and printed its own count.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 8/6/2018 3:51 PM}}

Sixteen was not the number that morning. Both copies were edited on 11 September, and only the edited text came down.{{cite:free-for-all-amendments-2018-2019-discord-private|the edit audit, 11 Sep 2018}} What the list said before that can be read back out of the members' own conversation, because they began reading it aloud to each other within six minutes of it going up.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the general channel}} One of them welcomed Jofash Tunnel as a long-overdue addition; Jofash Tunnel is in the edited text and not on the August list, so that entry did not move.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, Jofash Tunnel}} The same member twice treated Thanos as being outside the pool — once directly, and once in an arithmetic that only works if it is, counting the eight territories in the game yielding more than two million quest experience and making three of them free for all.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, Thanos treated as outside the pool}} Thanos is in the edited text.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 9/8/2018 1:46 AM}} Going the other way, seven minutes after the list appeared the same member named the swamp transition territory as being in the pool and asked why; it is on the August list, it is absent from the edited text, and the next evening the alliance's map-maker listed it among the entries he was taking out.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|8–9 Sep 2018, the swamp transition}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 8/6/2018 3:51 PM}}

The document that came down therefore differs from the one that was posted by at least three entries. One of the three was already accounted for: the 11 September edit had been reconstructed as the removal of Thesead, which was assigned to [[emorians|Emorians]] and struck off the pool by announcement that same day.{{cite:free-for-all-amendments-2018-2019-discord-private|the 11 Sep edit reconstructed}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|dwIcy, 9/11/2018}}

The reading-aloud turned into the only quantitative argument about territory value the alliance ever had. Over the following two hours the member who had started it worked through the list territory by territory, compiled the eight territories in the game whose total quest experience passed two million, found three of them in the pool, and proposed a rule to replace judgement: two million and above, and a territory goes in.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the audit and the proposed rule}} The same member then published a replacement list of fifteen, each entry annotated with its experience figure or with a note that it was a grinding site or a landmark, said the pool as it stood held twenty-two of which fewer than half were any good, and asked twice whether anybody had an argument against it.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the replacement list}} Twenty-two is the count the alliance had printed on its August declaration, and whether that was a count of the list as posted or the August total repeated went unrecorded.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 8/6/2018 3:51 PM}}

The answers were not about experience. One came from the member who had published the list: it would make their own assigned ground worthless. A guild leader said that no guild would surrender territories, and that two of the richest quest territories in the game sat outside the pool because the alliance's largest member guild could not be allowed to lose them.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the answer about the largest guild}} A third said making them common would be chaos.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the third answer}} The point about the largest guild is one member's account in conversation rather than a decision on record. It runs with what the man who drew the maps told the leadership six weeks later: that the holdings of the larger guilds could not really be cut at all.{{cite:federation-blocs-within-2018-discord-private|24 Oct 2018, the arithmetic of the veto}}

## Amended in a committee channel, twice in two days

On the evening of 9 September 2018 the alliance's map-maker posted a working pool of twelve territories to the territory assignment committee, with an explicit ledger of three additions and three removals and a reason for each.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|9 Sep 2018, the ledger}} Two of the removals were being reassigned to [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] on the ground that the guild deserved more and that the two territories were of low value; the third went to [[the-hive|The Hive]] to make up for swamp ground it was losing to Emorians.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|9 Sep 2018, the reasons given}} He asked the channel whether it seemed fair and was told it did. The next morning he posted a different set again — three territories in, one out, and a whole volcanic region taken out save for two — inside a negotiation conducted in units of guild experience.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, the second ledger}} Neither ledger was published.

The common ground was a compensation fund. It was what the map-maker had left to give when a guild came out of a redraw badly, and it was spent that way twice in one evening. On 21 September he was still discussing taking two further territories out of it.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|21 Sep 2018}} On the 25th a guild leader complained in the leadership channel that his guild had lost its free-for-all ground, naming a volcanic region and one further territory, and was answered that the guild had in fact gained one, that the territories were not his to lose, and that the one he named and one other had become free for all again.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|25 Sep 2018}}

## What it was for

The alliance gave three incompatible accounts of the pool's purpose inside a single conversation. On the evening of 9 September a member asked the alliance's high-level guilds to stop taking free-for-all ground off guilds far below their level merely to display wealth.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|9 Sep 2018, the plea}} He was told that the pool existed so that everyone had a chance to gain experience and activity was rewarded, and separately that it was chiefly a way of inflating a guild's territory count.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|9 Sep 2018, the two purposes given in answer}} The day before, the volcanic region had been described as made common for the sake of competition, and because ground outsiders attacked constantly was easier to leave unassigned than to defend.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the volcanic region}} A fourth had been given in June, when the Fox Viceroy said one purpose was to let a guild show it could handle more territory than it held.{{cite:free-for-all-territories-discord-private|19 Jun 2018, the fourth purpose}} None of the four was ever written down as a rule.

What was actually happening was said aloud by the guild doing it. Asked to give the smaller guilds a chance, a leader of [[thenolifes|TheNoLifes]] answered that his guild was consistently among the top three on the server because it had a chokehold on the free-for-all territories.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|9 Sep 2018, the chokehold}} Eight minutes later another member guild's territory tracker went into the same channel: eleven free-for-all territories with their holders and how long each had been held, of which that guild held five, one for close to three days, the rest split between four other member guilds and two guilds from outside the alliance.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|9 Sep 2018, the tracker}} The tracker was one guild's watch list rather than the pool — five of its eleven are not on the declaration published three days earlier — so it shows what was being treated as open on 9 September and not what the alliance had declared.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 9/8/2018 1:46 AM}}

## The last declaration

Then, for five weeks, nothing. The alliance announced no amendment to its free-for-all list between 11 September and 12 October 2018.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|no announced amendment, 11 Sep – 12 Oct 2018}} Just before midnight on 12 October its founder posted a new declaration to the channel outsiders could read, and to no other; no copy went to the members' announcements channel and no motion accompanied it in the voting channel.{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Drew1011, 10/12/2018 11:57 PM}}{{cite:federation-last-declaration-october-2018-discord-private|12 Oct 2018, one channel and no vote}}

What it said that night is gone. The method that recovered the September text was tried on it and found nothing: across the two days spanning the posting, nobody in any Federation channel mentioned the list, named a territory as inside or outside it, or referred to a new one having gone up.{{cite:federation-last-declaration-october-2018-discord-private|the search of the informing tier, 12–13 Oct 2018}} The text that came down is the version left by an edit made on 2 November, twenty days after posting and eight days before the alliance ceased to exist, and what that edit changed went unrecorded.{{cite:free-for-all-amendments-2018-2019-discord-private|the edit audit, 2 Nov 2018}}{{cite:federation-last-declaration-october-2018-discord-private|the edit of 2 Nov 2018, 16:16 UTC}}

The following night the founder told the leadership channel what the publication had been for. He had made the list public and had described it as being there for aspiring guilds; somebody had taken that to mean the Federation was inviting guilds it had never heard of to hold all of the free-for-all territories; he had said it was nothing of the kind, and the man had taken it badly.{{cite:federation-last-declaration-october-2018-discord-private|13 Oct 2018, why it was published}} That is the only statement anywhere about why the list was published, and it establishes nothing about what the list named. Eleven weeks earlier the same document had been treated as alliance-restricted, a member asking whether an outsider could be shown it at all.{{cite:free-for-all-territories-discord-private|26 Jul 2018, the request}} Setting the two beside each other is this article's.

As it now reads the October declaration names eighteen territories: eight in Gavel, one in the Ocean province and nine in Wynn.{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Drew1011, 10/12/2018 11:57 PM}} Against the September text it adds Thesead, Bandit's Toll and Battle Tower and drops Thanos.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 9/8/2018 1:46 AM}}{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Drew1011, 10/12/2018 11:57 PM}} None of those four movements was announced. Thesead had in fact been announced in the other direction on 11 September, when it was assigned to Emorians and taken out of the pool; how it came back was never said.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|dwIcy, 9/11/2018}}

The edited text was already wrong on the afternoon it was edited. Three days earlier the map team had agreed to throw the whole Ragni region open, the change went into [[federation-the-last-map-2018|the map carried on 2 November]], and from that morning members and leaders in three separate channels were taking ground there and calling it free for all — one of them twenty-five minutes before the declaration was rewritten, and others still doing it on the 6th.{{cite:federation-last-declaration-october-2018-discord-private|2–6 Nov 2018, the region treated as open}} The declaration, revised that same afternoon, names no territory in that region.{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Drew1011, 10/12/2018 11:57 PM}} Nothing further was published before the alliance dissolved on 10 November.{{cite:federation-last-declaration-october-2018-discord-private|no further publication, 12 Oct – 10 Nov 2018}}

## What the declarations cannot say

The published declarations are not a series of states of the map. They are occasional photographs of a pool that was being altered continuously in a committee channel, by a small number of people, for reasons that had more to do with settling grievances than with what a territory was worth.

That matters because a capture on free-for-all ground was not an act of war against whoever held it a minute earlier, so a capture count means one thing on that ground and another off it. For the alliance's last two months no declaration says which ground was which. The declaration of 8 September describes the pool as it stood on the 11th, and the pool was altered twice in committee within two days of publication and again afterwards without announcement, so it does not describe the map between 8 September and 12 October.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-september-2018-discord-private|the pool between 8 Sep and 12 Oct 2018}} The declaration of 12 October describes the pool as it stood on 2 November, so it does not describe the map between those two dates either.{{cite:federation-last-declaration-october-2018-discord-private|the pool between 12 Oct and 2 Nov 2018}} And on 10 November the alliance's declarations ended with the alliance.{{cite:federation-last-declaration-october-2018-discord-private|no further publication, 12 Oct – 10 Nov 2018}}

The allocations the pool sat beside are at [[federation-territory-allocations|Federation territory allocations]], the body that amended it at [[federation-territory-committee|the territory committee]], and the wider practice at [[territory-warfare|territory warfare]].

## The instalment and the objection

At about two in the morning on 4 March 2018 the alliance's founder typed into his serialised history the passage on how [[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]] came out of [[war-syndicate|War Syndicate]]: a quarrel over its owner moving to [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] and back, after which the guild left the alliance.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L856–863, composed 4 Mar 2018}} Later that day the leader of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] told the Federation's leadership channel that the account was false, and named that episode as the thing he objected to: the guild had been put out of War Syndicate rather than walking out of it.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|4 Mar 2018, the account called false}} A former officer of KingdomPhoenixes answered inside the minute, and contradicted him from the other side — the guild had left first, and the expulsion came afterwards, when there was nothing left to expel.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|4 Mar 2018, the former officer's version}} Neither gave a date, and neither returned to it.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|4 Mar 2018, no date from either side}}

## What the Federation was

The deeper disagreement was about what the alliance itself amounted to. In the same exchange of 4 March the leader of Titans Valor refused the alliance's account of its own origin: the [[coalition|Coalition]] had never destroyed War Syndicate, its guilds were all still there, and by admitting them the Coalition had put them back in power rather than beaten them.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|4 Mar 2018, the origin refused}} Other members answered that both predecessors were finished and that the Federation was a single alliance rather than two joined together.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|4 Mar 2018, the answer given him}}

He put the same position again on 23 March, in the discussion channel beside the serial, and was refused there too. The Federation was a temporary joining of two alliances, he said, neither of which had ended, his own among them.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|23 Mar 2018, the position restated}} Members answered that the earlier alliance had had its time, that the Federation was the two of them combined, and that the arrangement was not temporary.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|23 Mar 2018, the answer in that channel}}

The founder gave the opposite account of the same continuity on 12 July 2018, and it is the one the alliance ran on. The Coalition had ended when five guilds left it and the Federation was formed; the Federation was the Coalition under another name; nothing in the alliance's structure had been altered when the War Syndicate guilds were absorbed.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|12 Jul 2018, the founder's account}} He put the whole of it as one alliance joining the other and the result being renamed.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|12 Jul 2018, the summary he gave}} The five guilds he meant left on the night of 16 February 2018 and formed [[alliance-alliance|Alliance Alliance]].{{cite:coalition-fracture-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018}}

Both men held office while describing the alliance as two different things, and neither was overruled.

## Who founded it

Founding claims were made in passing and never tested. On 8 March 2018 a member of [[imperial|Imperial]] told the leadership channel that his guild had been among the alliance's first and had effectively founded it; within the same minute the founder said in the same channel that merging with the War Syndicate guilds had been his own idea.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|8 Mar 2018, the two claims in one minute}} Both remarks were made inside a joking argument about which guild was least useful, and neither carried a roster or a date.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|8 Mar 2018, the setting of the argument}} The question was asked in earnest four months later and produced two more answers inside a minute — [[federation-explaining-itself-2018|the Federation explaining itself]].

## Who ended HackForums' hold on the map

The credit for bringing down [[hackforums|HackForums]] was argued at length on 15 October 2018, and three incompatible explanations were given in one exchange.

The leader of Titans Valor said his guild and the other War Syndicate guilds had done it, while everybody else was inactive or inside a collapsing [[emperium-of-wynn|Emperium of Wynn]].{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|15 Oct 2018, the Titans Valor claim}} A leader of another guild answered that the rest had not stood by but had been unable to act, because dozens of subguilds were feeding the dominant guild territory, and that it fell on the day that practice was made against the rules.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|15 Oct 2018, the subguild explanation}} The founder added a third: a small number of very committed players had been holding off hundreds, under a system nobody else was willing to keep fighting.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|15 Oct 2018, the founder's explanation}} The exchange ended when the man who had raised it withdrew the subject.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|15 Oct 2018, the withdrawal}}

None of the three gave a date for the fall, and the only anchor any of them offered was a period of alliance history named by an abbreviation.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|15 Oct 2018, no date offered}} What the guild's own contemporaries said had made the position possible — abused subguilds, legal alt warring, no cooldown on a recapture, and wars that hardly worked — is at [[hackforums|HackForums]].{{cite:thread-263975-p02|post #31, 9 Mar 2020}} Setting the two beside each other is this article's.

Times here are the alliance's own clock, which ran eight hours behind UTC that winter.

## The list was the authority

[[the-federation|The Federation]] kept a list of allies and a list of enemies, both maintained by one officer and both amended by asking her in an open channel.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|the two lists and their keeper, February 2018}} Members treated them as settling the question they were about.

On 24 February 2018 a captain of a member guild asked the general channel whether the guild NYN was really an ally, because NYN had been telling people in his guild that it was and he could find nothing confirming it.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, the question}} A member directed him to the alliance's information channel; he answered that NYN was not to be found there, and two members told him that settled it.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, the answer}} One added that a guild claiming an alliance it did not have ought to be wiped off the map.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, the remark}} Nobody in the exchange proposed asking anyone senior.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, nobody dissents}} The alliance had voted only on issuing NYN an invitation, and was still voting on the guild the following month.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|25 Feb 2018, the invitation motion}}

## The lists that were not kept

No copy of either list survives. The information channel holds one message in its whole export, dated 22 September 2018, seven months after members were reading a list in it.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|the information channel, 17 Feb - 22 Sep 2018}} The companion welcome channel holds a single message of the same date.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|the welcome channel, 17 Feb - 22 Sep 2018}} Both had been created on 17 February, the morning after the alliance was founded.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|channel creations computed from Discord ids, 17 Feb 2018}}

The alliance was nonetheless using them. Two days before the NYN exchange a member asked the leaders' channel for the ally list so that he would not attack a friend by mistake, was directed by the founder to the welcome channel, and reported that it was not there either.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|22 Feb 2018, the request}} So the Federation had at least one such list in February 2018, it was consulted, it was found wanting at least once, and none of it was kept.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|the February content and the September message}} Whether it was deleted, replaced, or posted somewhere a member merely thought of as that channel was never established. Some list of member guilds was also being maintained that month, and where it lived was never established either.{{cite:federation-vote-procedure-february-2018-discord-private|28 Feb 2018}}

## Enemies entered on request

On 19 February a member asked the list-keeper to enter [[angels-of-eternal|Angels of Eternal]] among the allies and another sent her a link to work from; whether the entry was made went unrecorded.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018, the ally request}} On 28 February a member of the [[serpentem-empire|Serpentem Empire]] asked the same officer to enter TheWeirdoSquad on the list of enemy guilds, saying it had done enough to earn a place there.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|28 Feb 2018, the enemy request}} She asked what the guild's full name was, and two members supplied the grounds: that it had chased [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] and [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]] through the Molten Heights and interfered with the Serpentem Empire when it came to help.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|28 Feb 2018, the grounds given}}

## Clubs, not spades

On 22 February the officer who kept the lists asked for the alliance's symbol to be corrected, on the ground that the Federation was clubs and not spades.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|22 Feb 2018, the suit}} The guilds descended from the [[coalition|Coalition]] — [[hackforums|HackForums]] among them — were the ones using spades.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|22 Feb 2018, who kept spades}} Which display she meant was not stated. The convention was being enforced six days after the split that produced both sides of it.

## The truce nobody could confirm

What the lists could not record was a truce, and on 24 February that gap opened in public. Members spent the day disagreeing about the guild JAT: a leader asked in the morning whether there was an arrangement under which JAT would not attack alliance guilds and would help against the opposing bloc.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, the morning question}} Another said JAT was nominally on the alliance's side but skirmished with it and was probably playing both ways; a third argued it was clearing the alliance's enemies out of ground it meant to keep for itself.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, the two answers}}

That evening a member of Kingdom Foxes reported that a JAT representative had apologised for attacking, asked him to leave JAT's ocean holdings alone and offered him ten liquid emeralds for the damage, and that he had taken it.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, the payment}} The alliance's founder answered that no member was to agree a truce with any outside guild unless he or his co-leader had expressly allowed it.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, the instruction}} A member then said that one of the alliance's own officers had told him it was truced with JAT, and the founder replied that he knew of no such truce.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, the contradiction}}

Eight days after it was founded the alliance had a rule against private diplomacy and no way of telling whether it was at peace. Members had accused the same guild of duplicating currency the day before — see [[federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018|reading the enemy]]. The rule was not written down for another four months: it went into a shared rules document on 3 July 2018, after a secret pact between a member guild and an enemy guild was exposed.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Federation guild-leader vote, 1-3 Jul 2018}}

## Known unevenly

Membership was decided in one room and known unevenly in the others. On 20 February a member asked the war channel whether the guild Children of Eris was in the alliance; told it was not, another member called it an enemy, was corrected, and said he had thought it allied to [[house-of-sentinels|House of Sentinels]].{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, Children of Eris}} Within a minute the guild had been reclassified as a target on that basis alone and a member had set off to attack it.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, the reclassification}}

Eight days later a member of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] was refused help by another member on the ground that his guild was Titans Valor. He answered that he had left it and was now with [[illustratus|Illustratus]], and was told he still carried the old tag.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|28 Feb 2018, the tag}} Titans Valor had been a member since the 17th and Illustratus had been voted down on the 25th, so a member could still be turned away on the last day of the month over which of two guilds he belonged to.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|17 and 25 Feb 2018, the two motions}}

## The March ratifications

Both March 2018 allocations were drawn, put to the guild leaders in the voting channel and only then published. The first went to that channel at 19:57 UTC on 10 March, with an approve, disapprove or abstain vote opened ninety-seven seconds later, and was published at 22:11 the same evening.{{cite:federation-map-making-march-2018-discord-private|10 Mar 2018, the first ratification}} The second went up at 22:54 on 23 March with the same three options and was published at 20:14 on the 24th.{{cite:federation-map-making-march-2018-discord-private|23-24 Mar 2018, the second ratification}}

The second was carried by one vote, and the vote was asked for. At 19:55 UTC on 24 March the map-maker told the leadership channel that the motion needed one more yes or four more noes and named the six guilds that had not voted; the alliance's leader, who had said he meant to wait the full twenty-four hours, voted for the map at 19:57:51, and the map-maker declared the vote concluded ten seconds afterwards.{{cite:federation-map-making-march-2018-discord-private|24 Mar 2018, the last vote}} A minute later the leader told the channel that a two-thirds threshold is a hard test for a redistribution, because a map that reduces some guilds is voted on by the guilds it reduces.{{cite:federation-map-making-march-2018-discord-private|24 Mar 2018, the leader on the threshold}} The map-maker said they would rather not settle a redistribution by majority at all and would prefer unanimity; the leader answered that unanimity had happened once, in the [[coalition|Coalition]].{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|24 Mar 2018, the exchange on unanimity}}

A separate motion the day before moved the alliance's open ground. At 10:35 UTC on 23 March the leader of [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] proposed taking the free-for-all area out of the Light Forest and putting it into the Molten Heights, and a motion putting that region's eight territories into the pool in place of the Light Forest went to the voting channel four minutes afterwards.{{cite:federation-map-making-march-2018-discord-private|23 Mar 2018, the free-for-all swap proposed and put}} It carried sixteen to two with three abstentions as exported, and fifteen to one with two abstentions from eighteen accounts once the account sitting on all three options comes out — one guild against the swap rather than two. The motion itself was edited the following morning, thirty-one hours after it went up and after the voting had run, so the tally is against text that did not stay as the guilds had reacted to it; what changed was not kept.{{cite:federation-map-making-march-2018-discord-private|the swap motion's tally, corrected by account id}} The Light Forest went to [[imperial|Imperial]].{{cite:federation-map-making-march-2018-discord-private|the Light Forest to Imperial, 24 Mar 2018}} What the two March allocations then said is at [[federation-territory-allocations|the territory allocations]].

## The four June image maps

Four times between 16 and 30 June 2018 the alliance put its map to the guild leaders as five images and published the set once the vote carried.{{cite:federation-map-by-image-june-2018-discord-private|the four image ballots, 16–30 Jun 2018}} Each ballot opened within a minute of the images going up, and they carried thirteen to nothing with two abstentions on 16 June, fourteen to nothing on the 21st, eleven to nothing on the 25th and thirteen to one on the 30th.{{cite:federation-map-by-image-june-2018-discord-private|the four ballots and their tallies, 16–30 Jun 2018}} The announcements followed between three quarters of an hour and two and three quarter hours afterwards, the first at 07:53 on the export's clock with a role ping and no text.{{cite:federation-map-by-image-june-2018-discord-private|the four publications, 16–30 Jun 2018}} Four of the five images are named for the Light Realm, Gavel, the Ocean and Wynn each time; the fifth is unnamed, and the fifth is the one that matters.{{cite:federation-map-by-image-june-2018-discord-private|the composition of the four image sets}} Nobody voted against a Federation map in June until the last of the four, and then once.

**The sets are not a picture in place of a list. They are a picture with a list underneath it.** The unnamed fifth image is a legend: a table giving every member guild, the number of territories it held and the region it held them in, and beneath that a box stating the free-for-all pool in words.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|the legends beneath the June and July map sets}} The four region maps carry the same information a second way, each territory drawn as a bordered box with a coloured disc lettered for the guild holding it and pooled ground marked with a disc of its own.{{cite:federation-map-by-image-june-2018-discord-private|the markings used across the image sets}} So the map that replaced the list still records who held what, in a form nobody has yet counted. What may not be done is to count the discs by eye across four large images: on one of the four the only pool marker sits off the landmass in a box of its own and is almost certainly a key, and read as a territory it would add one to the pool.{{cite:federation-map-by-image-june-2018-discord-private|the marker off the landmass, and the caution against counting by eye}} Until the markers are read mechanically, the run of published territory counts ending on 4 June stays broken, and what the four maps leave is their majorities.{{cite:federation-map-by-image-june-2018-discord-private|the image sets of 16, 21, 25 and 30 Jun 2018}} Matching each published set to its ballot is this article's, by sequence. A rule voted through on 17 June let assigned territory fall into the free-for-all pool with no map drawn at all, and is at [[federation-free-for-all-territories|the free-for-all territories]].{{cite:federation-map-by-image-june-2018-discord-private|17 Jun 2018, the motion and its tally}} The twenty-one motions the channel carried that quarter are at [[federation-ballots-april-june-2018|the Federation's ballots in April–June 2018]].

## Five votes, five majorities

The results were seventeen in favour and two against on 13 July 2018, sixteen and none on the 18th, thirteen and one on the 25th, thirteen and none on 1 August, and sixteen and one on the 16th.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|poll results, 13 Jul – 16 Aug 2018}} Each map was a set of region images posted to [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s voting channel by the member who had drawn them, followed by a two-option poll put to the guild leaders.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|the five map polls, 13 Jul – 16 Aug 2018}} No further map went to that channel until 10 September, and somebody else drew that one.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|no map vote, 16 Aug – 10 Sep 2018}}

The alliance posted five images and tagged the guild leaders and the war role on 13 July, 18 July, 26 July, 1 August and 16 August.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 7/13/2018 6:00 PM, 7/18/2018 2:57 PM, 7/26/2018 2:11 PM and 8/1/2018 5:25 PM; 8/16/2018 4:02 PM}} On 18 July the poll opened at 14:50{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|18 Jul 2018, the poll time}} and the map went up at 14:57, seven minutes later, with the vote still open.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 7/18/2018 2:57 PM}} The other intervals ran about two hours on 13 July, five on 1 August and two and a quarter on 16 August; the map voted through on the night of 25 July was published at 14:11 the following afternoon.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|publication intervals, Jul – Aug 2018}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 7/26/2018 2:11 PM}}

## The count before the count

A draft reached the leaders long before the poll did. The map-maker posted drafts to the alliance's territory assignment committee channel roughly a dozen times between 5 July and 29 August 2018, asked the leaders there what they objected to, and revised in place.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|committee drafts, 5 Jul – 29 Aug 2018}} Each draft drew a show of reactions from the leaders who could see it, and the alliance treated that tally as the reading.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|the committee tallies treated as a reading}}

The private tallies carry the disagreement the public ones do not. They ran eleven to one on 18 July, nine to four on the 25th, six to two on 1 August, ten to eight on 14 August, twelve to one on the 16th, eight to four on 25 August and fourteen to one on the 29th.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|committee reaction tallies, 18 Jul – 29 Aug 2018}} The draft of 14 August, the only one on which the leaders were near to evenly divided, never reached the voting channel; no map was voted on there between 10 and 16 August, and a revised map went up on the 16th and passed sixteen to one.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|the 14 Aug draft and the 16 Aug vote}} That the map was withheld because of its tally rather than for some other reason is this article's reading; the tally, the gap and the revision are what was set down.

## Where the bargaining was done

The trade itself was made elsewhere. On 18 July the map-maker set down that she had asked a member guild about a region and been told it would sooner give up a different one, with [[imperial|Imperial]] taking three territories elsewhere in exchange.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|18 Jul 2018, the Imperial exchange}} On 1 August, in the interval between the draft going up and the poll opening, four guild leaders settled a swap of Gavel territories between [[the-hive|The Hive]] and [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] among themselves in the leadership channel; the map carrying it passed that afternoon, thirteen to nothing.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|1 Aug 2018, the Hive and IceBlue Team swap}}

The alliance set the procedure down only in October, when a map-maker described approaching each guild about to lose ground beforehand, so that it had no cause to call on its own grouping.{{cite:federation-blocs-within-2018-discord-private|24 Oct 2018, pre-consultation}} It had been in use since at least the first of these five votes.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|committee drafts, 5 Jul – 29 Aug 2018}} He added that it had failed exactly once, with [[emorians|Emorians]] and after a vote rather than before one; two Emorians episodes of September 2018 answer to that description and his words do not distinguish them, and both are described at [[federation-territory-committee|the territory committee]].{{cite:federation-blocs-within-2018-discord-private|24 Oct 2018, the one failure}}

## What a reaction was worth

A reaction was meant to stand for a guild and did not always. On the night of 11 July 2018 the member running that evening's vote reminded the leadership channel that the rule was one vote per guild, and that a co-leader's reaction would be taken off where the guild's leader had already voted.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|11 Jul 2018, one vote per guild}} Every total on a map poll is therefore an upper bound on the guilds behind it, and it needed policing by hand.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|reaction counts as an upper bound}} One check runs the other way: on 26 July a member reported the map vote then running to the leadership channel as thirteen to one, which is what that poll's reactions came to.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|26 Jul 2018, the reported total}}

## The cadence was disputed

On 31 July a member objected in the leadership channel that maps should not go out this often, and that five territories changing hands was not reason enough to redraw the allocation and tag the whole alliance for it; he wanted a fresh map kept for changes of some size.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|31 Jul 2018, the objection}} The alliance had published maps on 13, 18 and 26 July, and published another on 1 August.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 7/13/2018 6:00 PM, 7/18/2018 2:57 PM, 7/26/2018 2:11 PM and 8/1/2018 5:25 PM; 8/16/2018 4:02 PM}}

## Opened for six weeks

The committee channel was opened to every guild leader on the evening of 18 July 2018, the alliance's founder saying they had asked for more transparency. At first they could read it without writing in it, and the members already there joked about being on show.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|18 Jul 2018, the channel opened}} It was made private again on 31 August, and the founder gave his reason plainly: opening it had killed the channel, because nobody would say what he actually thought of the map.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the channel closed}}

On 26 September a guild leader asked to see the map being drawn.{{cite:federation-mapmakers-2018-discord-private|26 Sep 2018}} He was not granted it until 10 October.{{cite:federation-october-2018-discord-private|10 Oct 2018}} The map-making had been open to the leaders for six weeks that summer, and had been shut deliberately.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|18 Jul and 31 Aug 2018}}

## The last map

The map-maker asked to be relieved on 26 August 2018, saying she had made mistakes on the map then in force and was drained.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the request to stand down}} The last map she drew went to no vote in the voting channel at all: the committee's show of reactions on 29 August, fourteen to one, stood as the decision, and when a member asked in the committee where the voting emotes were he withdrew the question himself.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|29–30 Aug 2018, the last map}} The images went to the announcements channel on the morning of 30 August.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 8/30/2018 10:28 AM}}

That same morning a leader told the leadership channel that he would change his own vote on a pending guild application in order to close it, so that the map could go to a vote immediately afterwards.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, changing a vote to clear the queue}} No map vote followed. Her successors are at [[federation-territory-committee|the territory committee]], the allocations themselves at [[federation-territory-allocations|Federation territory allocations]].

## The map drawn in experience points

The committee channel had carried eight messages in the first week of September 2018 and carried more than four hundred across the 9th and 10th, while a member who had not drawn a map before drew one.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|the committee channel, 1–10 Sep 2018}} It went to the guild leaders in the voting channel at 13:08 on 10 September, and the motion's wording was amended a minute later, at the leaders' request, to say what the vote was for.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, the motion and its amendment}} Asked in the leadership channel why one province had been divided oddly, its author answered that nobody had wanted it, and then that this was his first attempt.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, the first attempt}} The images went to the announcements channel just before three that afternoon.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/10/2018 2:54 PM}}

The map was drawn against a single number: the total quest experience each guild's holdings would yield, which measured what a territory produced for whoever held it rather than what its holder had done to get it.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|the measure}} On the morning of 10 September the map-maker negotiated one guild's allocation directly in that currency, telling its representative that the arrangement would take the guild from roughly 8.7 million to roughly 5.3 million and put it about level with the alliance's largest guild.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, the negotiation}} He summarised the outcome the same way: most of the large guilds had lost territory, one guild had gained around ten, the smaller guilds all held ten to twelve, and one held eighteen because in his judgement it was fighting enough to warrant them.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, the summary}} His stated aim was to make the map as balanced as possible.

The figures were one member's. Presenting the map, he told the leaders that anyone wanting the reasoning behind the free-for-all decisions should ask him or another member, who could go into more detail.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, whom to ask}} The following morning he said the whole calculation ran on quest experience and on the assumption that most players did quests, that she was working on making it more accurate, and that the numbers were all hers.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|11 Sep 2018, the model}} Two days before the map she had audited the alliance's published free-for-all list against the same measure, in a different channel, and proposed replacing it on the strength of it.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|the audit of 8 Sep 2018}}

The alliance's founder stated a ceiling on guild size while the vote was running. Asked why one province had been split between three guilds, he answered that he had divided it because he did not want [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] to pass forty territories, and that he had always argued against any guild going over forty; he said it once, in passing, and the alliance neither voted on a limit nor published one.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, the forty-territory ceiling}} The table published that afternoon gives Kingdom of Foxes forty territories.{{cite:federation-allocation-september-2018-discord-private|the table of 10 Sep 2018}}

Before the vote the map-maker canvassed the member guilds and said so: he had gone round every single guild in private messages to be sure each was content with what it had been given, or at least to try, and nobody could use that as a complaint any more.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, the canvass}} He predicted that the free-for-all decisions would be the part of the map most questioned.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, the prediction}} Three objections were made and not answered: that the map had been drawn without everyone having been consulted, with a request for one or two further rounds on it; that a guild disagreed with what it had been given but had no standing to argue against a majority; and that a member supposed he would be ignored again.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, the objections}} The map carried.

Thirteen hours after it went to the vote, at ten past two on the morning of 11 September, the leader of [[emorians|Emorians]] told the leadership channel that he and his captains were unhappy with the map, that none of his guild's requests had been respected, and that his guild's already small experience yield had fallen sharply.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|11 Sep 2018, the Emorians complaint}} At 03:02 the map-maker put a motion to the voting channel saying that, being new to the work and this being his first map, he had left Emorians without a single half-decent territory and pushed its experience yield to nearly the bottom at 0.3 million; he proposed assigning it Thesead and taking that territory out of the free-for-all pool, which would raise the guild to about 3.0 million.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|11 Sep 2018, the correction motion}} In the leadership channel a quarter of an hour later he put 0.3 million at about the fourth or fifth worst in the alliance and 3.0 million at high-middling.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|11 Sep 2018, the figures in context}} The alliance announced the reassignment at 12:16 the same day.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|dwIcy, 9/11/2018}} The table published the previous afternoon had given Emorians forty-one territories, more than any other guild in the alliance{{cite:federation-allocation-september-2018-discord-private|the table of 10 Sep 2018}}; setting the count beside the yield is this article's, and only the yield had been used to draw the map.

The ground handed over had been made common on purpose. Asked why Thesead had been free for all at all, the member who kept the list answered that it had been put there to reduce [[fantasy|Fantasy]].{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|11 Sep 2018, why Thesead was open}} The map-maker agreed and explained: the region carried a great deal of experience in total and Fantasy sat in the middle of it, so that territory was the most efficient thing to take away, and making it common meant others got the benefit.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|11 Sep 2018, the reasoning}} Asked whether the point had not been to leave a rich area for guilds to fight over, he said that was part of it.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|11 Sep 2018, the contested-ground answer}} A guild leader objected that the alliance was now proposing to hand that same rich ground to somebody as assigned territory.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|11 Sep 2018, the objection to the grant}}

The objection to the measure itself came from the same leader. He did not trust the experience figures, he said, because his own guild had held one of the territories being called rich and had found it almost useless, and he would be watching and would come back and fight it if the numbers turned out to be far from what he had been told.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|11 Sep 2018, the objection to the measure}} Nobody answered him.

[[the-federation|The Federation]] lost four member guilds between 22 and 25 July 2018 and admitted two.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 7/22/2018 and 7/25/2018}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|7/7/2018}} Below that the guilds themselves were turning over, and the alliance's record of who represented them followed the changes rather than authorising them.{{cite:federation-member-guilds-july-2018-discord-private|July 2018}}

## Guilds changing hands

One changed owner on 6 July and the alliance learned of it because somebody asked for the roles to be corrected. In the advisory channel that morning a member asked to be contacted and another asked for roles to be updated; within four minutes the channel had established that the guild's leader was already gone.{{cite:federation-member-guilds-july-2018-discord-private|6 Jul 2018, the roles and the departure}}

A guild could also lose its seat in the alliance's leadership channel simply by having nobody willing to sit in it. On 14 July members of that channel established that a guild had had a representative and that he had left, and that a second person named as a possible replacement would not join either.{{cite:federation-member-guilds-july-2018-discord-private|14 Jul 2018, the empty seat}} The alliance's founder said the first had gone because he had objected to a territory map.{{cite:federation-member-guilds-july-2018-discord-private|14 Jul 2018, the reason given}} Whether the guild was represented at all after that went unrecorded.

On 22 July a guild leader asked the founder in the leadership channel to make another member of their guild a guild leader in their place and to remove them.{{cite:federation-member-guilds-july-2018-discord-private|22 Jul 2018, the resignation}} Other members asked why, and a colleague answered with a personal reason.{{cite:federation-member-guilds-july-2018-discord-private|22 Jul 2018, the answer}} Two member guilds left the alliance the same day, and nothing in the record connects the two events.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 7/22/2018}}

## Guilds ended, and guilds begun

On 7 July, in a leadership-channel exchange in which several members were comparing guilds they had disbanded, one described having ended LE Flowers [LEF]: another member had performed the disbandment, he said, but he had made him do it, and what remained of the guild — its membership and its command — had then passed to him.{{cite:federation-member-guilds-july-2018-discord-private|7 Jul 2018, the account of the disbandment}} That is his account of his own conduct, given in a conversation in which several people were claiming credit of the same kind, and nothing else records the coercion. The guild had held twenty territories in the [[coalition|Coalition]]'s final allocation five months earlier.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|allocation list, part 17, composed 11 Jul 2018}}

A new guild appearing on the map late in the month could not be identified by the alliance's own war channel until four members between them explained it. On 29 July a member asked who the guild called Roses was and why it was taking territories in the swamp.{{cite:federation-member-guilds-july-2018-discord-private|29 Jul 2018, the question}} He was told it belonged to a particular player, that it was essentially a splinter of SKY with an element of [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] [PUN] in it, and, by a fourth member, that it was the product of Paladins United and Angelic.{{cite:federation-member-guilds-july-2018-discord-private|29 Jul 2018, the answers}}

Two days later the alliance treated another new guild as a prospective member before anyone had spoken to it. Early on 31 July a member of the leadership channel said he expected a guild newly founded by somebody who had left [[lunatic|Lunatic]] to want to join the Federation, and posted its public statistics page.{{cite:federation-member-guilds-july-2018-discord-private|31 Jul 2018, the expectation}} Other members had not known the guild existed, and one confirmed the founder had left Lunatic.{{cite:federation-member-guilds-july-2018-discord-private|31 Jul 2018, the answers}}

## Sixty stacks of emeralds

What a guild was worth to the people running them was said outright in the middle of the month. On the morning of 10 July the leader of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] [ANO] said he had once offered to buy [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] outright for sixty stacks of emeralds, on the condition that the guild stayed under its own leadership, and that the offer had been refused.{{cite:federation-member-guilds-july-2018-discord-private|10 Jul 2018, the offer}} He said he had been dared into making it and described himself as long since wealthy, and in the same conversation offered forty stacks for another member's participation in something.{{cite:federation-member-guilds-july-2018-discord-private|10 Jul 2018, the dare and the second offer}} Another leader said he would sell a guild for that, and the Titans Valor leader pointed out that founding one cost five emeralds.{{cite:federation-member-guilds-july-2018-discord-private|10 Jul 2018, the price of founding one}}

Whether the offer was ever really made cannot be established: it is his account of himself, given in a conversation about how rich he was, minutes away from the messages in which he set out what he actually wanted from the alliance.{{cite:titans-valor-departure-july-2018-discord-private|10 Jul 2018, 06:35 to 07:28, Legio Praetoria}} He announced his guild's departure ten days later.{{cite:titans-valor-departure-july-2018-discord-private|20 Jul 2018, 14:34, the announcement}}

## A week in which everybody fought everybody

On the morning of 30 August 2018 [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s leadership channel was arguing about whether to readmit [[odysseia|Odysseia]], whose members had left Federation guilds, attacked most of the alliance, and were now applying to come back.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the readmission argument}} One leader wanted the application refused and the guild made to wait before reapplying; another asked what the offence was supposed to be — attacking too effectively — and whether the guild had not humbled itself enough to be allowed to ask.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the two positions}} Four minutes later that second leader proposed, as a joke, that the alliance simply hold a week in which everybody fought everybody.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the suggestion}} A third had suggested a few minutes earlier that three days a month be thrown open.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, three days a month}}

Within ten minutes a member had taken it seriously and designed it into a recurring season with territory as its prize: every month or two, the whole map thrown open for a week, or for three days, after which each guild's allocation would follow from how it had done.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the seasons proposal}} His argument was that under the assignment system members attacked nothing unless their own ground was under attack, and that opening the map would put everyone in the field at once.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the argument for it}} He accepted that guilds should be able to stand out of it, and named the flaw in that himself: the event failed if nobody joined.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the opt-out}} Objections came in the same minutes — that some guilds would be singled out, and that the rival coalition would use the window.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the objections}} One member said a wholly open map was what the rival alliance's founder had been demanding all along.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the remark about the rival alliance}}

The alliance had refused a territory system responsive to effort a week earlier, on 23 August, when a member set out a complete redrawing of the map from its war-activity graphs.{{cite:federation-territory-doctrine-2018-discord-private|23 Aug 2018}} Reading the two together is this article's.

## It came back out of a complaint

On the evening of 2 September a leader of Odysseia told the war channel that other guilds did help but had to be shouted at first, and then that most of them were indifferent; another member agreed and said he had not wanted to say it aloud.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|2 Sep 2018, the complaint}} Within a minute a member asked for a day on which every territory was open, and the proposal was live again.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|2 Sep 2018, the proposal revived}} It had arisen both times out of a complaint that the alliance's guilds would not turn out for one another; reading the two occasions together is this article's.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|30 Aug and 2 Sep 2018, the two occasions}} What the calls for help produced that month is at [[federation-war-effort-september-2018|the alliance's war effort]].

## The founder refused it

The same evening the alliance's founder told the war channel that there would be no such event.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|2 Sep 2018, the refusal}} Asked whether he would object even if a majority wanted it, he said he would: it was too destabilising, and it was the worst suggestion anyone had made.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|2 Sep 2018, against a majority}} Three minutes later he gave a different reason. It sounded enjoyable, he said, but it would finish with everyone quarrelling over it and half of the alliance's leaders disliking one another.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|2 Sep 2018, the founder's reason}}

## The arguments

The case against it was put on the morning of 3 September by a leader of Odysseia, a guild that had itself left the alliance and attacked it five weeks earlier.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the case against}} Leaving each other's assigned ground alone was what an alliance existed for, he said, and dropping it would help nothing.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, what an alliance was for}} The territories designated free for all were named for what they were, and the rest were not.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the free-for-all distinction}} The effect would be to make people dislike each other more, and anyone who wanted to attack the Federation should do the honest thing and leave it.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, leave rather than attack}} He proposed a games evening instead, on the ground that people who disliked each other might enjoy something together.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the alternative}} In the middle of the same argument he observed that a guild from outside the alliance was being allowed to accumulate twenty territories with nobody noticing or answering it; the guild went unnamed.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the twenty territories}}

The case for it was that the alliance already permitted this and had survived it. A guild leader answered that the event took nothing from the practice of attacking allies that the free-for-all system did not already allow.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the case for}} Assigned territory would return to its holders afterwards and should not be touched by the event at all, and it would last a day rather than a week.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the terms proposed}} The reason for doing it, he said, was that nobody had a reason against it.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, no reason against}} Asked directly what would be achieved by allies attacking each other, he said everyone needed to relax.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the answer}} What the free-for-all system already allowed is at [[territory-warfare|territory warfare]].

The design questions were answered before the vote. Asked what would stop guilds wiping a non-participant anyway, the channel answered: the rules, protected assigned territory, and a published list of the guilds staying out.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the safeguards}} The alliance's map-maker asked that any vote say plainly that the event lasted twenty-four hours and that a guild bought immunity by standing out of it.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the map-maker's request}}

He gave the practical objections as well. Clearing up afterwards grew worse the longer the event ran, and opened a wider window for an enemy strike.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the clean-up objection}} Guilds would settle old dislikes they could not otherwise act on.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, old dislikes}} After the first hour or two the largest guilds would hold the entire map while everyone else lost interest.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the largest guilds}} A member added that a fixed twenty-four hours fell unevenly across the playerbase, and that nobody could fight for a day without stopping.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the duration objection}}

## The motion announced its own result

At 09:12 on 3 September the motion went to the voting channel: which guilds would take part in a twenty-four hour period, beginning at a stated hour, in which the entire map was open.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the motion}} A guild choosing not to take part was exempt from attack, and so was its assigned territory.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the exemption}} Members reported the tally in the leadership channel as running against it from the first minute and standing at nothing to eleven a quarter of an hour later.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the reported tally}} One of them noted that some of the eleven were placeholders under the rule of one vote per guild.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the placeholders}} The alliance published no count of its own.

At 09:18, six and a half minutes after posting it, the motion's author edited it to add that since nobody wanted it they could all stop discussing it.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the amendment}} That line was not in front of the guild leaders while they were voting: the motion as it finally stood ended by announcing its own result.

## Afterwards

On the morning of 4 September the argument resumed in the alliance's general channel, among members who were not guild leaders and had no vote, and the losing side complained that leaders had voted no without reading the motion.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|4 Sep 2018, the general channel}} A member of the alliance's leadership said the topic should never have reached that channel, and set out what the leaders had in fact agreed: that two guilds wanting to arrange something of the kind between themselves were free to do so, but could not draw any other guild into it.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|4 Sep 2018, the settlement}} No vote establishing it was ever put. He told the participants to take it back to the leaders' channel or drop it.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|4 Sep 2018, the instruction}}

Others in the channel said the argument was creating tension and asked repeatedly for it to stop, and one member said he would mute the channel if it continued.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|4 Sep 2018, the members' response}}

Times here are the alliance's own clock, which ran eight hours behind UTC that winter.

## An expense nobody could account for

[[the-federation|The Federation]] could see two things about a guild it was fighting: what a territory was defended with, and who had taken what. From those two it reached, inside one week, two accusations of currency duplication, three conclusions that guilds had gone over to the other side, one decision to leave a guild alone, and a judgement that one of its principal opponents was running out of money.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|the week's inferences, 19-28 Feb 2018}}

The first accusation was arithmetic. On 21 February 2018 members costed a guild's territory defences at over twenty-two thousand emeralds each and asked where the money came from.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, the costing}} One said that if every territory were defended that way, bankrupting the guild would be straightforward, but that she could not see how it was affording it.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, the affordability}} Another answered that they were duplicating currency.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, the accusation}} The two then reasoned that a guild founded only a year earlier could not be living on accumulated wealth, and concluded that duplication was the answer.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, the inference from age}} No evidence beyond the expense was offered. The founding date the inference turned on was not settled either — two irreconcilable dates for that guild come out of different conversations.{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|the two founding dates}}

Two days later the same accusation was made about a different guild with a figure fifty times larger. On 23 February a war-leader of [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] told the war channel that the guild JAT had to be duplicating currency because there was no way it could afford a million emeralds a territory.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|23 Feb 2018, the million}} A member agreed, and a third began working out how many territories that implied across the canyon and the Sky Islands.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|23 Feb 2018, the agreement}} Members had said shortly before that the same guild had probably run out of funds or was about to.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|23 Feb 2018, the funds}} The day after that the alliance could not establish whether it had a truce with JAT at all — see [[federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018|its lists and truces]].{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, the contradiction}}

A third guild attracted the same reasoning on the same day, and here nobody proposed a mechanism. A war-leader reported that the guild Jaguars had defended Lava Lake with maximum-level creatures and every available boss.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|23 Feb 2018, the defence described}} The alliance's founder said he had no idea how they had that much money, and the war-leader said he did not either.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|23 Feb 2018, the two answers}} Lava Lake was on the free-for-all list the alliance had itself declared two days earlier, so it was ground any guild could take from any other, and a heavy defence of it says nothing about a guild's posture towards the Federation.{{cite:free-for-all-territories-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, the fourteen territories}}

## One member argued back

Exactly one member pushed back that week, and about a fourth guild. On 23 February, in the same channel, he said plainly that [[necronomicon|Necronomicon]] was not duplicating anything.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|23 Feb 2018, the contradiction}} He gave an ordinary explanation instead: some of its members had found valuable items and others spent all their time farming in one location.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|23 Feb 2018, the explanation offered}} Nobody argued with him. It is the only contradiction of any of the month's accusations, and it concerns a guild none of them were aimed at.

None of the accusations was ever settled. No finding by any moderator followed, and in none of the three cases was anything offered beyond the expense itself.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|the three cases, February 2018}}

## Allegiance read off the map

The alliance inferred which side a guild was on from what it captured, at least three times in one week. On 22 February a war-leader said he was starting to think the guild Yall Need God was allied with the opposing bloc.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|22 Feb 2018, Yall Need God}} On the 23rd two members said they thought another guild was friendly with two of the alliance's enemies.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|23 Feb 2018, the friendly guild}} A third said the guild Achte Shadow was working with them, on the evidence that it had put heavy defences on a Federation-held territory.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|23 Feb 2018, Achte Shadow}} On the 24th a member concluded that a guild fighting alongside three of the alliance's enemies in Corkus was a new recruit to their side.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, Corkus}} None was confirmed.

The same reasoning also produced a decision not to attack. On 19 February a member asked whether the guild DragonsArise was allied with the alliance's two principal enemies.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018, the question}} A member of the leadership said he knew nothing about the guild and that it should be left alone for now because they could not be sure.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018, the decision}} Another said it was a friend of [[house-of-sentinels|House of Sentinels]] and had belonged to a grouping called [[aurelion|Aurelion]].{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018, Aurelion}} That much was right: the guild had joined Aurelion on 27 February 2017, alongside House of Sentinels, which founded the grouping.{{cite:thread-135191|post #1, as last revised 29 Apr 2017}} Members treated the guild's attacks as an irritation rather than a threat.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018, the irritation}}

## What a capture could not tell them

The alliance read its opponents' finances the same way it read their loyalties. On 28 February members observed that [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] was holding several free-for-all territories and inferred that it was running out of money.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|28 Feb 2018, the inference}} One added that the guild had gone bankrupt some months earlier, without giving a date.{{cite:federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018-discord-private|28 Feb 2018, the undated bankruptcy}} That holding open ground indicates poverty was the members' inference and not a stated rule. The alliance gave a different account of what that ground was for in June, when the Fox Viceroy said one of its purposes was to let a guild show it could handle more territory than it held.{{cite:free-for-all-territories-discord-private|19 Jun 2018, the fourth purpose}}

Judging a guild's side from what it captured is unsafe on any ground the alliance had declared open, because a capture there was not an act of war against whoever held it a minute before — the practice is set out at [[territory-warfare|territory warfare]] and the pool itself at [[federation-free-for-all-territories|the Federation's free-for-all territories]]. The alliance had no list of that ground written out by name before 21 February 2018{{cite:free-for-all-territories-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, the fourteen territories}} and no capture record of its own to check anything against, and it reached its conclusions anyway. Setting the alliance's method against the difficulty of the inference is this article's; no participant said anything of the kind.

[[the-federation|The Federation]] transacted its admissions twice: once in a motion put to the guild leaders, and once in the conversations that came before it. August 2018 is the month in which somebody described the second kind aloud, in front of the leaders he was describing.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|25 Aug 2018, the account given}}

## The region came into play because its holder could not keep it

In the early hours of 13 August 2018, while the alliance's map-maker was canvassing the territory committee for changes, the leader of [[emorians|Emorians]] said that [[imperial|Imperial]] had decided to split Corkus and that his guild would like a share.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|13 Aug 2018, the split and the request}} A member answered that Imperial had gone back on the offer.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|13 Aug 2018, the offer withdrawn}} The alliance's founder said Imperial needed a smaller allocation, having lost over half of its main war force{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|13 Aug 2018, the state of Imperial}}, and that it had stayed afloat that night only because [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] had lent it about four members.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|13 Aug 2018, the loan of members}}

The case was argued the next evening and it turned on who could defend the ground. On 14 August the Emorians leader told the leadership channel that the purpose of splitting Corkus was to protect it from being targeted, and that the leaders had agreed [[ex-nihilo|Ex Nihilo]] was the wrong guild for it because that guild was itself under sustained attack.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|14 Aug 2018, the purpose and the objection to Ex Nihilo}} His own guild had been offered a share and would gladly give up its Rymek territories for it.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|14 Aug 2018, the exchange offered}} If the plan was for Ex Nihilo and Imperial to divide their fighters between Corkus and their own ground, he added, he could not see the point: Imperial wanted the split precisely because it was short of fighters, and the other guild was struggling to hold what it had.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|14 Aug 2018, the objection to the division of fighters}}

## A guild described by one region

On 19 August, three days before the readmission vote, members of the war channel said Corkus was the hardest ground on the map to hold because [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] cared about nothing else, that the guild had no interest in the Wynn province, and that it called itself after the region.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|19 Aug 2018, the war channel}} A member asked why, if Corkus was all it wanted, it did not simply rejoin the Federation and take the region that way, and was surprised to be told it had been a member before.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|19 Aug 2018, the question and the answer}}

The guild was readmitted on 22 August by fourteen votes to three with one abstention.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|22 Aug 2018, the motion}} The announcement went up at 16:06 on 23 August, one sentence long.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|23 Aug 2018, 16:06 on the export's clock, the announcement}} Just under two hours later the war channel met the news with jokes about who had joined whom.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|23 Aug 2018, the reaction in the war channel}} That evening a member of the general channel asked whether there would be a territory reset now, and two others answered that a new map would probably come within days.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|23 Aug 2018, the expectation of a map}}

## Terms nobody wrote down

On 25 August a leader of the readmitted guild told the leadership channel what he understood the arrangement to have been. Speaking, as he said, as a member rather than as a guild leader, he stated that from the beginning of the talks about joining, multiple guilds and their leaders had hinted that his guild would receive the southern part of Corkus — not all of it — almost immediately.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|25 Aug 2018, the expectation described}} On that understanding, he said, the guild had fought around the clock for its new allies, he personally for hours at a time.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|25 Aug 2018, what the guild did on the strength of it}} There had never been any question what his guild would ask for, and every guild leader knew it.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|25 Aug 2018, what the leaders knew}} Not receiving part of Corkus would damage his guild's morale and probably reduce its war count; as a guild leader he thought they had earned it; and with Ex Nihilo gone it had looked likely they would get it with little opposition.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|25 Aug 2018, the consequences put}} He would not name who had hinted it.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|25 Aug 2018, the refusal to name}} Nobody in the passage denied that somebody had.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|25 Aug 2018, no contradiction offered}} Earlier the same morning he had asked the channel whether his guild could fully defend the southern Corkus territories it was to receive.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|25 Aug 2018, the defence question}}

The answer came in the early hours of 26 August, and it was that terms of entry are terms of entry. The Emorians leader said he respected the guild's history with the Federation, but that a great deal had happened while it was away — Imperial's ownership of Corkus, and the decision lately taken to divide it.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, what had happened in the interval}} These were not unreasonable conditions for joining or rejoining an alliance, he said; any guild joins on such conditions, and the question was not about trust but about respect for what had been agreed in the guild's absence.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the answer}} The guild's leader had argued that it was not a new guild needing to earn trust, that it had left voluntarily when it went inactive so as not to be a burden, and that since rejoining it had warred almost continuously for its allies.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the guild's argument}} The doctrine of time the same exchange produced is at [[alliance-governance|alliance governance]].

## The map gave it the ocean

On 28 August, looking at the draft allocation, the guild's leader asked why his guild was getting only the small islands when five territories were spare.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|28 Aug 2018, the question about the islands}} The alliance's map-maker answered that the ocean seemed the right fit because the guild had held it before, that the five spare territories lay in the middle of the Wynn province, and that the islands were a staging point rather than a settled allocation.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|28 Aug 2018, the map-maker's answer}} The leader replied that the islands were not even the good ones{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|28 Aug 2018, the islands objected to}}, that his members had been unhappy when told, particularly since they had expected southern Corkus, and that one of them had stopped warring altogether until they got it — adding that he did not mean it to sound hostile.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|28 Aug 2018, what the members were told}} The map-maker said maps did not last long, and hoped progress would be made towards the guild holding Corkus again.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|28 Aug 2018, maps do not last}}

## The region went to a third guild

On the morning of 29 August a member asked the leadership channel why Emorians had Corkus at all, and said he would far rather it went to Imperial or to Paladins United.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, the question}} A guild leader agreed, while doubting Imperial could hold Corkus and the Light Forest at once and pointing out that it had gone badly the previous time.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, the doubt}} The Paladins United leader said Emorians should in that case have given up its Llevigar ground for Corkus{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, the counter-proposal}}, a member of the same guild said southern Corkus would be theirs next, and the following morning that guild's leader posted a slogan for it in the leadership channel.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|29-30 Aug 2018, the claim renewed and the slogan}}

The allocation published on 30 August 2018 was a set of images, and what the leaders said about the draft is the evidence that Corkus went to Emorians on it.{{cite:federation-south-corkus-august-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, the draft as discussed}} The published maps of that round are at [[federation-territory-allocations|the territory allocations]]. Six weeks later the alliance was arguing the same region again: on 11 October a leader proposed compensating Imperial for the loss of North Corkus so that Paladins United could be given the province entire, and nothing was transferred.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018, the Corkus proposal}}

The vote was public and the expectation was not, and when the two came apart the only account of the second is a member giving it in front of the people he was accusing. How the alliance's maps were drawn is at [[federation-territory-committee|the territory committee]], and the motion readmitting the guild at [[federation-ballots-august-2018|the Federation's ballots in August 2018]].

## Four maps in ten weeks

The alliance wrote its allocation out in words six times between 21 February and 4 June 2018, naming every member guild and its region; five of the six gave each guild a territory count as well.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|2/21, 3/10, 3/24, 4/22, 5/10 and 6/4/2018}} After that it kept publishing the allocation, in pictures, roughly weekly. Between 16 June and 1 August it went out at least eight more times as sets of four or five region images, and **each set carries a legend giving every member guild, its territory count and its region, with the free-for-all pool written out beneath**.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|the legends beneath the map sets, 16 Jun – 1 Aug 2018}} Every one went to the announcements channel and to the voting channel.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|both channels, each publication}} So the run of written allocations does not stop on 4 June, as this article once said; it changes format and continues, and the alliance's own count of what each guild held is recoverable for most weeks of that summer.

Two cautions govern any use of them. **A count in a legend is published fact; an absence from one is not.** The legend of 16 June lists twelve guilds and omits four that legends of the same month carry, so a guild missing from a map has not been shown to have been given nothing.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|twelve guilds listed on 16 Jun 2018}} And the figures in this article are taken from the legends, never from counting the coloured markers on the maps themselves: reading those by eye is unsafe at this scale, and one set carries a marker off the map that would inflate the pool if it were counted.{{cite:federation-map-by-image-june-2018-discord-private|the caution against counting the markers by eye}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|image sets of 6/16, 6/21, 6/25, 6/30, 7/13, 7/18, 7/26, 8/1 and 8/16/2018}}{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|the five map publications, 13 Jul – 16 Aug 2018}} The run of figures therefore stops at 4 June and picks up again only with the territory tables of 30 August and 10 September, which are images as well.{{cite:federation-allocation-september-2018-discord-private|the tables of 30 Aug and 10 Sep 2018}} Four of the six written allocations fall in the first ten weeks, and the founding settlement of 21 February gives regions without counts, so the run of figures begins with the second.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|2/21/2018, 3/10/2018, 3/24/2018 and 4/22/2018}} The votes behind the five image sets published from 13 July onward are at [[federation-map-votes-2018|the Federation's map votes]].

| Allocation | Guilds listed | Territories assigned | Free-for-all |
