The sixteen guilds come to 369 territories, and with the sixteen named as free for all on the same sheet that is 385 — the number of distinct territories that changed hands anywhere on the map between 30 March and 8 October 2018.{{cite:federation-allocation-september-2018-discord-private|the table of 10 Sep 2018}}{{cite:who-fought-2018-discord-private|385 distinct territories, 30 Mar – 8 Oct 2018}} The arithmetic is this article's, and the match is less than it looks: 8 October is the byte at which a truncated export stops rather than a date anything happened, so the second figure counts a slice of the year chosen by an accident of collection. Across 2018 as a whole 394 territories changed hands, nine of them never appearing on the sheet. What the sheet establishes is a partition of the ground actually being fought over rather than of the map entire.{{cite:territory-exchanges|distinct territories with at least one exchange, 3 Jan – 31 Dec 2018 UTC: 394, against 385 for 30 Mar – 8 Oct}} It is still a partition and not a list of the parts worth arguing over. The table's own caption gives the free-for-all count as seventeen, one more than it lists, and which of the two was wrong went unrecorded.{{cite:federation-allocation-september-2018-discord-private|the caption, 10 Sep 2018}}

Set against the table of 30 August, it shows the churn exactly. Two guilds leave the allocation and two enter: Lunatic and Metric go, Nethers Ascent and Odysseia arrive.{{cite:federation-allocation-september-2018-discord-private|the tables of 30 Aug and 10 Sep 2018}} The announcements channel carries all of it — Lunatic left on 31 August, Metric was removed on 2 September on the alliance's finding that it had joined only to spy and cause internal chaos, and Nethers Ascent joined on the 3rd.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|8/31/2018, 9/2/2018 and 9/3/2018}} The charge against Metric is the alliance's own.

## Two assignments that did not reach the map

The alliance's decisions did not always survive into the document that recorded them. On 1 September it told its whole membership that the leaders had voted to treat Lunatic's Savannah territories as Odysseia's rather than draw a new map, and on the 3rd, after Metric's removal, that Nethers Ascent would take the six Rymek territories Metric had held.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/1/2018 and 9/3/2018}}{{cite:federation-allocation-september-2018-discord-private|the two reassignments, 1 and 3 Sep 2018}} On the map published on the tenth, Savannah belongs to Nethers Ascent and Rymek to Arisen, and Odysseia holds Ragni and Nivla Forest.{{cite:federation-allocation-september-2018-discord-private|regions on the table of 10 Sep 2018}} Whether the two were reversed or simply overtaken by a fuller redraw was never said, and nothing was written about either after the announcement.

The redraw went out with a request for tolerance. Five minutes after the images, the alliance asked its members to remember that many guilds were adjusting to new areas, and that if an ally took one of theirs by mistake they should politely pardon it and reclaim the territory.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|9/10/2018 3:00 PM}} An alliance publishing a wholesale redraw expected its own members to attack each other by accident within the week.

The open ground moved with the assigned. The free-for-all list on the August table ran to twenty-two territories and the September one to sixteen; on 11 September, the day after the map, the alliance announced that Thesead had been assigned to Emorians and was no longer free for all, striking a territory off a sheet published the previous afternoon.{{cite:federation-allocation-september-2018-discord-private|free-for-all lists, 30 Aug and 10 Sep 2018}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|dwIcy, 9/11/2018}} The open ground and its declarations have their own page at [[federation-free-for-all-territories|the Federation's free-for-all territories]].

## The allocation nobody was told

The census of 10 September 2018 was the last allocation the alliance published. The map it carried on 2 November reached no announcement channel, and after 12 October it published no territory document of any kind.{{cite:federation-last-declaration-october-2018-discord-private|both announcement channels, 12 Oct – 10 Nov 2018}} That last map was drawn against a surplus rather than a shortage: on 30 October the leaders counted eleven member guilds where a month earlier there had been eighteen, four of the eleven already held more than forty territories, and about eighty stood unassigned.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, the count and the figures}} Rather than assign them the alliance threw a whole region open, and the change went into the map and into no document.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct – 2 Nov 2018, the Ragni decision}} It is at [[federation-the-last-map-2018|the Federation's last map]].

The **territory committee** drew [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s territory map through the alliance's last three months. It was never constituted as a body: it was a channel, a job, and whoever was doing it that week.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|September 2018}} That the alliance kept producing this institution informally and never made it an office is this article's reading.

## Created in the last week of March 2018

The channel was made at 19:35 UTC on 25 March 2018, six minutes after a new leaders' back-channel that replaced the separate Discord server the leaders had been using and that was retired the next morning.{{cite:federation-map-making-march-2018-discord-private|25 Mar 2018, the two channels}} It arrived the day after the second March allocation was published, and its first week went on the argument that map had left behind: which of the guilds that voted against it had a case, on which it settled on [[imperial|Imperial]] and [[the-hive|The Hive]]; whether territories the game had newly created were free for all; and which guilds were large enough to sit on the committee at all, including whether [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]]'s leader should.{{cite:federation-map-making-march-2018-discord-private|the committee's first week, 25 Mar 2018 onward}} Five days after the channel opened the alliance expelled his guild.{{cite:federation-buildcraftia-expulsion-march-2018-discord-private|30 Mar 2018}}

It was told at the outset that it could decide nothing. Within a day of the channel opening the alliance's leader told it that it could not make territory decisions on its own authority and that a vote would be needed.{{cite:federation-map-making-march-2018-discord-private|late March 2018, the limit put on the committee}} Between its creation and 10 April only four accounts posted in it.{{cite:federation-map-making-march-2018-discord-private|posting accounts, 25 Mar - 10 Apr 2018}}

## Where the April map was argued

The channel was nearly silent through the fortnight in which the alliance's next map was drawn, and the drafting went on in the leadership channel instead. On 5 April 2018 the leader of [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] told that channel the draft scattered island territories between guilds, and asked either that his guild keep Corkus or that the islands be gathered into clusters; the following morning he put the complaint at greater length, objecting that half of two island groups had gone to a single guild and that two guilds would have to cross the ocean to reach their own ground.{{cite:federation-allocation-counts-recounted-2018-discord-private|5–6 Apr 2018, the islands objection}} On 7 April the alliance's founder told the map-maker she could announce the map if she wished, and minutes later that adjustments might be needed.{{cite:federation-allocation-counts-recounted-2018-discord-private|7 Apr 2018}} On 16 April a member asked when the next assignment push would come and began relaying requests from the guilds — more free-for-all ground, a named territory for one guild, the Llevigar area for another.{{cite:federation-allocation-counts-recounted-2018-discord-private|16 Apr 2018, the requests relayed}} In the same discussion a member held that guilds should be assigned territory in places and quantities they could hold, and a member guild's representative asked not to be given much more because his guild's income would not support it.{{cite:federation-allocation-counts-recounted-2018-discord-private|16 Apr 2018, the capacity argument}} The leader of Paladins United proposed that north Corkus go to Angelic while his own guild kept south Corkus, and was asked directly whether his guild would be able to hold it.{{cite:federation-allocation-counts-recounted-2018-discord-private|16 Apr 2018, the Corkus proposal}}

The Corkus proposal was not adopted. The allocation published on 22 April gives Paladins United Dead Island and four territories, and no part of Corkus.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|4/22/2018 2:34 PM}}{{cite:federation-allocation-counts-recounted-2018-discord-private|the roll of 22 Apr 2018}}

The map was agreed about two hours before it was published, and the delay between the two was administrative. At 12:24 on 22 April an officer told the guild leaders it had practically been approved by everyone, correcting himself immediately to most; a member guild's leader asked to be told when it had been formally agreed so that he could inform his own members, and was told it would go up in the announcements channel as soon as somebody with the right access came online. The announcement went up at 14:34.{{cite:federation-allocation-counts-recounted-2018-discord-private|22 Apr 2018, the approval and the delay}} That dates the approval and not the contents: the announced text was edited five days afterwards, and those messages discuss the map's approval without naming a single territory.{{cite:federation-allocation-counts-recounted-2018-discord-private|the edit of 27 Apr 2018}}

A ballot on a map in the first week of April left nothing behind but the people mentioning it — one asking whether a leader had voted against it, one whether the map was official, one joking that the options had not been labelled — and no motion for it went to the voting channel.{{cite:federation-allocation-counts-recounted-2018-discord-private|early Apr 2018, the ballot referred to in passing}} What that vote was, and whether it decided anything, was never set down. The twenty-one motions the voting channel did carry that quarter are at [[federation-ballots-april-june-2018|the Federation's ballots in April–June 2018]].

## What was to replace it

The channel was closed to the general membership on 31 August 2018, and the conversation that closed it also produced a proposal for what should stand in its place. A member joked in the leadership channel that now that the committee channel was open the people in it had simply moved somewhere else, and the alliance's founder denied it.{{cite:federation-keeping-track-august-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the joke and the denial}} The map-maker said the committee had died the moment it was opened to every guild leader, because nobody wished to be blamed for what the map did.{{cite:federation-keeping-track-august-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the map-maker's diagnosis}} The founder agreed that opening it had been a mistake, that nobody was willing to say what he really thought about which guild should get what, and that the channel was therefore dead.{{cite:federation-keeping-track-august-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the founder's agreement}}

What the map-maker proposed was a return to a private committee that would post its map in the leadership channel for comment before any formal vote — the drafting closed, the review open.{{cite:federation-keeping-track-august-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the proposal}} The founder's answer was that transparency in the committee was worth nothing if it left the channel silent.{{cite:federation-keeping-track-august-2018-discord-private|31 Aug 2018, the answer}} The canvass of ten September, in which the map was put to every member guild privately and the replies reported to the leaders, is that proposal in operation, and joining the two is this article's.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, guild-by-guild canvass}} The six weeks the channel spent open, and the alliance's reasons for opening it, are at [[federation-map-votes-2018|the Federation's map votes]].

## A tool, a canvass and a way to say no

The alliance's first assignments, in February 2018, were map images with a typed list of guilds and regions.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|2/21/2018}} On 6 September 2018 a committee member announced that another member had written software for the job, letting them draw a map by selecting an area and reassigning it to a different guild, and the channel took it as a straightforward improvement.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|6 Sep 2018}}

Three days later the alliance acquired a way to reject a map. In an argument about the current allocation, a member proposed a vote to reject it, so that the alliance would know what its members thought.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|9 Sep 2018, rejection vote proposed}} She asked who was normally responsible for making the map, got no clear answer, and set the vote up herself.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|9 Sep 2018, no answer on responsibility}} Seven months in, the leadership channel could not name the office that produced its territory map. She left the argument in the next minute and told the others to work it out; the outcome of the vote went unrecorded.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|9 Sep 2018, withdrawal from the argument}}

The canvass came the following day. On 10 September the map coordinator posted a running list of the guilds he had put the map to, sorted into those that had approved it and those he was unsure of, naming eleven approvals.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, eleven approvals}} He then went guild by guild, asking each leader to confirm and negotiating specific changes.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, guild-by-guild canvass}}

## What a territory was worth

The allocation argument of 9 September ran on numbers. A member challenged why two guilds should gain experience at more than three times the rate of two others.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|9 Sep 2018, rate challenge}} The [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]]' Viceroy answered with a proportionality case — his guild fought more than twice as much and carried about a quarter of the alliance's wars — and cited a calculation that the alliance's experience gains matched the effort put in.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|9 Sep 2018, proportionality answer}} The counter was that much of that fighting had been on free-for-all ground, and the Viceroy answered that his guild fought the enemy alliance about twice as often as it fought for free-for-all territory.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|9 Sep 2018, free-for-all distinction}} Both sides treated a capture on declared open ground as earning nothing. The quarter is tested against the capture log at [[federation-war-effort-september-2018|the alliance's war effort that month]].

The next morning a member proposed measuring it properly: estimating a territory's value by drawing a random sample of player names from the server list with a randomiser and querying the game's interface for each, rather than counting by hand.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, sampling proposal}} The coordinator's concern was accuracy against the time it would take.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018, coordinator's objection}} Whether the study was ever carried out was never recorded.

## Neither of them stayed

On 16 September the coordinator asked to be removed from the committee channel. He had joined mainly to understand the thinking behind the map and how it was made, he said; he had largely worked that out, so there was nothing further for him to do there, and another member had enough data to take the work over.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|16 Sep 2018}} Both of the people who built the alliance's territory process were out of it inside a fortnight, in each case wanting no further part in the argument rather than disputing the work.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|9–16 Sep 2018}} Neither was replaced by an office. The four map-makers who followed are at [[alliance-governance|alliance governance]].

## Compensation and remedy

On 21 September two leaders brought the leadership channel an agreement between them that [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] should have a named region, one of them saying the guild deserved it, having been cut so heavily that the loss had to be made good.{{cite:federation-map-round-september-2018-discord-private|21 Sep 2018, the agreement}} The map-maker pointed out where the grant would leave that guild in the alliance's territorial order — first or second — and the supporter reconsidered in his next message.{{cite:federation-map-round-september-2018-discord-private|21 Sep 2018, the reconsideration}}

Five days later a guild leader gave the general form of it as he saw it: most leaders believed they deserved more and would give nothing up, and the only guild he could name that had given things up more than once was IceBlue Team, the guild they had just agreed to compensate.{{cite:federation-map-round-september-2018-discord-private|26 Sep 2018}} He said it in front of the leaders he was describing, and none of them contradicted him.

On 27 September the map-maker described a use of territory that appears nowhere else in the alliance's records. He had given [[emorians|Emorians]] a named eastern region as a last chance to raise its war activity so that it might recover its position on the map — not a reward and not an entitlement but a remedy.{{cite:federation-map-round-september-2018-discord-private|27 Sep 2018, the grant}} The guild's leader, he added, had then accused him of lying about what the region was worth.{{cite:federation-map-round-september-2018-discord-private|27 Sep 2018, the accusation}} In the same conversation he said the next map would potentially give the Corkus region in its entirety to [[paladins-united|Paladins United]], that compensating [[imperial|Imperial]] would be difficult, and declined to go into details on the ground that this was not the leaders' channel.{{cite:federation-map-round-september-2018-discord-private|27 Sep 2018, the next map}}

## Asking a guild before cutting it

A month later the man who had drawn the map set out the procedure that kept the blocs inside the alliance from vetoing one. When cuts were made, he or the map-makers of the time would approach each guild about to lose ground and ask beforehand whether it was content, so that it had no cause to call on its own grouping.{{cite:federation-blocs-within-2018-discord-private|24 Oct 2018, pre-consultation}} It had failed exactly once, he said, with Emorians, and after the vote rather than before it; the result was the argument the channel remembered.{{cite:federation-blocs-within-2018-discord-private|24 Oct 2018, the one failure}} He named the guild, dated the failure to after a vote, and said it later turned into a large argument in the same channel — so the sequence is his rather than this article's. On 10 September he canvassed every member guild privately before the map vote and said so in the leadership channel; thirteen hours later the guild's leader told that channel that none of its requests had been respected, and the map-maker corrected the allocation himself the next morning.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|10–11 Sep 2018, the canvass and the complaint}} On 25 September the same leader pressed him in the same channel, saying he had been told one thing about what a region was worth and given another, and posted a public apology the following morning.{{cite:federation-map-round-september-2018-discord-private|25 Sep 2018, the quarrel}}{{cite:federation-map-round-september-2018-discord-private|26 Sep 2018, the apology}} The failure is the first and the argument the second. The only reading here is that his 'later' separates the two rather than restating one.

He had also gone back through the voting record and was careful about what it proved. It was often impossible to say whether the guilds of a grouping had voted together or whether it was coincidence, he said, because [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]] almost always voted with whichever side won; of the four guilds in the grouping he examined, only Emorians ever broke from the other three.{{cite:federation-blocs-within-2018-discord-private|24 Oct 2018, the voting record}} That is a Federation map-maker's reading of how the [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]] and Valkyrie guilds voted, given in his own alliance's channel, and neither grouping answered it. What the veto did to the map is at [[alliance-governance|alliance governance]].

## The process, made public

Three hours before the September resignation, at 05:23 on the morning of the 26th, the alliance told its whole membership that anyone with concerns or suggestions about the map should speak up, leader or not, map team or not: "Everyone here has an equal voice, don't be afraid to use it to voice your concerns".{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Goden, 9/26/2018}}

It opened the input rather than the process. What a guild leader asked for in the leadership channel three hours later — in the conversation in which the map-maker resigned — was to see the map being drawn, and that was not granted until 10 October.{{cite:federation-allocation-september-2018-discord-private|26 Sep 2018}}{{cite:federation-mapmakers-2018-discord-private|26 Sep 2018}}

Sixteen days later it was made good in the leadership channel, and narrowed. The new map-maker introduced himself there on 10 October, saying he had been given access so that he could discuss the map with the leaders and that he meant to keep the process open.{{cite:federation-october-2018-discord-private|10 Oct 2018}} On the 14th a second member joined him and was told that once the two of them had finished the map between them it could be posted for the leaders to comment on.{{cite:federation-mapmakers-2018-discord-private|14 Oct 2018}} Neither held an office, because there was none.

## The map showed who had left

On 7 October a member asked in the leadership channel why guilds that left the alliance never lost its symbol from their territories, and named three that still carried it.{{cite:federation-october-2018-discord-private|7 Oct 2018, the question}} Another answered that it was because they usually died.{{cite:federation-october-2018-discord-private|7 Oct 2018, the answer}} At any moment the map carried the emblems of guilds that had gone, which makes an assignment map evidence of who belonged to the alliance when the emblem was set rather than of who belonged that day.

## Which guilds could still hold ground

By October the renegotiation had stopped being about which guilds deserved territory. On 11 October a leader argued that [[nethers-ascent|Nether's Ascent]] should have its holdings reduced, because it plainly could not manage its boundary and its rate of reclaiming had become very low, making it a hindrance on the map with enemies about.{{cite:federation-october-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018, Nether's Ascent}} He had spoken to the captain warring for the guild, who told him he did not play often and who declined an offer of help from another guild's captains, and two other leaders agreed.{{cite:federation-october-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018, the captain}} In the same conversation a leader said, expressly without personal animus, that [[aphelion|Aphelion]] should not remain in the alliance because as far as he could see it had only one capable defender; others answered that the guild had been trying and needed no change, and one proposed instead giving it an isolated territory that enemies would find awkward to attack.{{cite:federation-october-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018, Aphelion}} A leader argued against reducing [[the-divine-swords|the Divine Swords]].{{cite:federation-october-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018, the Divine Swords}} All four judgements are the Federation's own, and none of the guilds discussed answered them in that channel.

The alliance had spent seven months arguing about which guilds had earned their ground. By the middle of October it was asking which of them still had anybody left to hold it.

## The October round

The round opened with a departure and a complaint. Renegade was announced as having left the alliance at 21:11 UTC on 30 September 2018.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|30 Sep 2018}} On 4 October a leader asked the leadership channel when the next territory map was, explicitly because of that departure.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|4 Oct 2018}} On the 8th the same leader said some guilds were not pulling their weight reclaiming, that the alliance had a whole desert sitting as feed for the rival bloc, and that boundaries had constantly to be fought over because nobody reclaimed them regularly.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|8 Oct 2018, the complaint}} The answer given was that a map could not be made without causing tension.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|8 Oct 2018, the answer}}

On 11 October the whole leadership argued the map in one sitting, and the two questions were Corkus and compensation. A leader proposed that one of two territories the game had not yet added should go to [[imperial|Imperial]] as part of the compensation for its potentially losing North Corkus, on the reasoning that less would then have to be taken from other guilds if [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] were given the whole province; the map-maker said Gavel was being settled first, then Corkus and Wynn.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018, the Corkus proposal}} A member observed that Paladins United had controlled Corkus long before, in the era of the previous mega-alliance.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018, the older claim}} [[emorians|Emorians]] argued that its own holdings matched neither its numbers nor its warring effort and that two new territories would fix that.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018, the Emorians claim}} Later the same hour the largest question was compensating Imperial specifically, and the answers offered were a named territory and a quest seal.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018, the compensation}} All of it was proposal and argument. Nothing was transferred.

## The guild that was both

[[the-divine-swords|The Divine Swords]] was named in the same conversation as the alliance's most attacked guild and as the guild whose holdings should be cut. On 11 October the map-maker asked its leader directly whether the guild's warriors were burnt out and was told they were, mostly because it was the alliance's number one target; in the same minutes the committee agreed to expand its Gylia holding.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018, the Divine Swords}} Five days later a leader said its territories should be at least halved, and its leader answered that giving up eight was acceptable — almost half — but not the good ones.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|16 Oct 2018, the halving}} On the 17th the committee settled it: the guild was to get fewer territories but keep the ones that mattered, members arguing that it was wiped often, sat wiped, and did not join attacks on enemy ground.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|17 Oct 2018, the settlement}} Those last are other guilds' leaders characterising it, not findings of the alliance. In the same conversation the committee recorded that [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] was gaining slightly and that nothing was changing for Emorians until the two new territories arrived.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|17 Oct 2018, the rest of the map}}

Nineteen days later the alliance voted the guild out, and the ground the motion gave was the loss of its warriors.{{cite:federation-last-motions-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, the expulsion}} Reading this conversation as where that becomes legible is this article's.

## The pool was worth more than the members

On the evening of 19 October 2018 a member of [[the-hive|The Hive]] posted the only quantified answer the Federation ever produced to the question of what its territory was worth. It was a table of quest-experience income built from a sample of a few hundred players' completion rates, offered with the warning that it was not fully accurate but was better than assuming every quest was done once.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|19 Oct 2018, 21:18 UTC, the table}} The free-for-all pool stands on it at 3,478.4 million. The eight member guilds listed stand at 882 for Kingdom Foxes, 647.3 for The Hive, 603.8 for Imperial, 349.6 for [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]], 206.2 for Paladins United, 179.9 for Emorians, 174.2 for the Divine Swords and 129.2 for [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]] — 3,172.2 million between them.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|19 Oct 2018, the figures}} The ground the alliance had assigned to nobody out-earned every member guild in the table put together. Adding the eight is this article's arithmetic on his numbers, not a conclusion he drew; the table omits [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]], Arisen, [[nethers-ascent|Nethers Ascent]], [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] and [[aphelion|Aphelion]], so the comparison holds against the eight listed and not against the alliance entire.

Nobody in the channel said what it meant. Its author used it on himself: Imperial's half of Corkus, he worked out, was about forty per cent of that guild's income, and the territory his own guild had been refusing to give up was about nineteen per cent of its own, which changed his mind about surrendering it.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|19 Oct 2018, the two conclusions}} The alliance had drawn its September map against a single number as well; that measure and the argument about it are at [[federation-map-votes-2018|the Federation's map votes]].

## Settled between two people

Two days later a member guild's leader told the leadership channel that he and the map-maker had settled the next map privately between them: Kingdom Foxes would have all of Gavel, Imperial would have Corkus and [[odysseia|Odysseia]] would have all of Wynn.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|21 Oct 2018}} It was put to the room as an agreement already reached, and the room approved of it. That the alliance decided such things by a two-thirds vote of the guild leaders is what makes the exchange worth reporting, and saying so is this article's.

None of it happened. Odysseia left the alliance eight days later, and the map that eventually passed was drawn after the alliance had lost seven of its eighteen guilds — see [[federation-the-last-map-2018|the Federation's last map]].{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, the count}}

## The rule kept, and the room suspected

The alliance's own voting procedure was enforced in the middle of the negotiation. During the 11 October sitting a member urged that a motion had reached two thirds and could be accepted at once; two others answered that it had not yet run its twenty-four hours and that the rules should be kept to.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018, the twenty-four hours}} Both rules had been settled in March.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|the rule, March 2018}}

Four days later, after a guild withdrew its application, leaders in the same channel said openly that this implied the guild had learned the vote results, asked how it could have known, and asked whether what happened in the leaders' channel or the voting channel should not stay there.{{cite:federation-map-round-october-2018-discord-private|15 Oct 2018, the suspicion}} Nobody proved a leak. Three weeks later the same leaders declared their advisory channel full of spies; joining the two is this article's.{{cite:federation-two-rooms-2018-discord-private|early Nov 2018}}

{{map:2018-11-02|The map on the day the Federation's last allocation was carried}}

## Eleven guilds, eighty territories

At 21:26 UTC on 30 October 2018, minutes after the vote that removed [[the-divine-swords|The Divine Swords]], a member of [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s leadership channel wrote out the guilds still in the alliance and made them eleven; the map-maker confirmed the number and said there had been eighteen about a month earlier.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, 21:26 UTC, the count of eleven}} A leader of [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] answered that they were losing guilds far too fast, and in the same breath asked when the next map would be.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, the request for a map}}

Thirty-eight minutes later the map-maker told the guild leaders what the difficulty had become. The map was enormous, balancing it across eleven guilds was very hard, and the cap of fifty territories on a single guild would therefore probably be scrapped.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, 22:04 UTC, the cap}} No such limit had ever been published, and the alliance never wrote its territory rules down at all.{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|written rules, 2016–2019}} Four minutes on he gave the figures: four member guilds already held more than forty territories and several more than twenty, and seventy-four were unassigned — a number he corrected upward to eighty in the same breath.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, 22:08 UTC, the figures}} The following night he put the unassigned remainder in the province of Wynn alone at about thirty.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|31 Oct 2018, the Wynn remainder}}

Every map round before it had been an argument about scarcity, and about who could stop a cut. Six days earlier the man who had drawn the alliance's maps, by then only advising, had told the same channel that the holdings of the larger guilds could not really be rearranged at all: any grouping big enough to be worth cutting was big enough to vote the map down.{{cite:federation-blocs-within-2018-discord-private|24 Oct 2018, the arithmetic of the veto}} The round of 30 October had the opposite problem.

## The guilds would not take it

Offered ground, member guilds refused it. IceBlue Team's leader said her guild would rather stay low on territory count and was told it would end up with at least twenty-five whether she liked it or not.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, IceBlue Team asking to be kept low}} Another leader said he would remain in his province even if it took him past his own fifteen-territory soft cap, but asked not to be given one particular region.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, the fifteen-territory soft cap}} A member said that now they had too many territories people were refusing them, and asked what world this was.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, the remark}}

At 22:11 UTC the same IceBlue Team leader said she would accept an increase provided her guild kept its holdings in the Canyon.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, 22:11 UTC, the Canyon condition}} She had spent the preceding hour dividing that region three ways with two other member guilds, in a council the [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]] had opened in its own server that afternoon.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-founded-2018-discord-private|the Canyon division, 30 Oct 2018}}{{cite:federation-two-rooms-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, 21:42 and 22:11 UTC, the same leader in both rooms}}

## A region nobody could be given

The surplus was solved by taking ground out of assignment altogether. Late on 30 October, in the alliance's territory assignment committee, the map team agreed to throw the whole Ragni region open as free-for-all; a member observed that there was nobody to give it to, and the alliance's founder said he liked keeping an entire region open for new guilds and that it could stay open until there were enough members to allocate it.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, the committee decision}} At 02:17 UTC on 31 October the map-maker put it to the guild leaders — people had been pushing for a free-for-all region for some time, the alliance had a great deal of open ground in Wynn and trouble finding takers for it, and the region a departing member guild had given up would suit. He asked for their thoughts rather than for a vote.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|31 Oct 2018, 02:17 UTC, put to the leaders}}

The one argument against was that the alliance could not afford to fight itself. A guild leader said he disliked so large a free-for-all area, and that having lost a great many allies they should not be fighting each other so much.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, the objection}} Another answered that there were simply not the guilds to assign the whole province, and the map-maker said they were pushing for it because they had run out of ground guilds would willingly take.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, the answers to the objection}} In the same exchange a member guild's leader said the alliance had fewer allies than it used to, that this was good, and that it meant more territories to assign.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, fewer allies}} That was one guild's view of its own prospects, eleven days before the alliance ceased to exist.

The alliance's last published allocation, of 10 September 2018, gives Ragni to [[odysseia|Odysseia]], and Odysseia's departure was announced on 29 October.{{cite:federation-allocation-september-2018-discord-private|regions on the table of 10 Sep 2018}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Yoo Jin-ish, 10/29/2018 7:08 AM}} Identifying that guild as the departing member whose ground was thrown open is this article's reading.

The change was never published, and it took effect anyway. Captures on the territories named for Ragni ran at 307 in the eight days to 2 November and 1,059 in the eight days after it, across six territories in each window, with the number of distinct guilds taking one rising from thirty-eight to forty-seven.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures on territories named Ragni*, 25 Oct – 2 Nov against 2 – 10 Nov 2018, raw, UTC: 307 across 6 territories from 38 attackers, then 1,059 across 6 from 47}}

## Carried, and never announced

The map went to the voting channel at 02:12 UTC on 2 November 2018, a set of images under one line of motion.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|2 Nov 2018, 02:12 UTC, the motion}} Five minutes later its author presented it to the leadership, apologised for the delay and listed what it did to each guild's quest-experience yield: every member guild gained except [[imperial|Imperial]], whose yield was flat, and IceBlue Team, whose yield fell by roughly half, for which he gave the Air Temple as the reason.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|2 Nov 2018, 02:17 UTC, the per-guild yields}} The free-for-all pool's own yield rose from about eighteen million to about twenty-one.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|2 Nov 2018, the pool's yield}} Asked why the gains were so general, he answered that this was what came of a smaller alliance.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|2 Nov 2018, the answer}} The figures were one member's model of quest experience rather than a measure of what any territory produced, which is how the alliance had argued about territory since September.{{cite:federation-map-in-experience-points-2018-discord-private|10–11 Sep 2018, the model and whose figures it was}}

At 10:27 UTC the map-maker remarked that a map vote had at last come out flawless; at 14:17 a guild leader reported it a hundred per cent pass and asked for it to be announced.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|2 Nov 2018, 10:27 and 14:17 UTC, the result}} The reactions still attached to the motion are not unanimous: eleven approving and two opposing, and the mover sits on both options, which leaves ten to one once the placeholder is taken out — one opposing reaction, from an account that voted on nothing else.{{cite:federation-last-motions-2018-discord-private|the map motion as exported}} A participant's count on the day and a count of the reactions years later are two measurements rather than a contradiction, and both give a wide margin. The ten motions of the alliance's last five weeks are at [[federation-last-motions-2018|the Federation's last motions]]. What followed was a short confusion about whether he was permitted to post maps at all. He believed he needed a vote authorising him; the founder said he had never said any such thing, that a map with a majority could be posted, and that what wanted voting on was the map and not who announced it.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|2 Nov 2018, who may announce a map}}

Nothing was then posted. Neither of the alliance's announcement channels carries the map, an amendment to the free-for-all list, or any other territory document after 12 October 2018.{{cite:federation-last-declaration-october-2018-discord-private|both announcement channels, 12 Oct – 10 Nov 2018}} The only public trace of the round is an edit made at 16:16 UTC that afternoon to the free-for-all declaration the founder had posted to the public channel three weeks before, and only the edited text came down.{{cite:federation-last-declaration-october-2018-discord-private|the edit of 2 Nov 2018, 16:16 UTC}}{{cite:free-for-all-amendments-2018-2019-discord-private|the edit audit, 2 Nov 2018}} That declaration names no territory in the region the map had just thrown open.{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Drew1011, 10/12/2018 11:57 PM}} Which guild held what under the alliance's last map was never written down anywhere a member could read it.

## What the map governed

It governed eight days. Between 2 and 10 November 2018 there were 9,605 captures across 339 territories, of which 2,874 fell on ground the revised declaration named as open, leaving 6,731 that did not.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures 2 – 10 Nov 2018 UTC, filtered against the free-for-all declaration of 12 Oct as revised on 2 Nov: raw 9,605 across 339 territories, 2,874 on declared open ground, 6,731 remaining}}

The alliance dissolved on 10 November. That day carries 2,386 captures across 352 territories, the highest figure on both measures of any day in October or November 2018, and no free-for-all list governed it: the Federation's declarations ended with the Federation.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures on 10 Nov 2018 UTC, raw and unfiltered: 2,386 across 352 territories; highest daily figure on both measures, 1 Oct – 30 Nov 2018}}{{cite:federation-last-declaration-october-2018-discord-private|no further publication, 12 Oct – 10 Nov 2018}}

The votes that carried the alliance's earlier maps are at [[federation-map-votes-2018|the Federation's map votes]], the allocations themselves at [[federation-territory-allocations|Federation territory allocations]], the body that drew them at [[federation-territory-committee|the territory committee]], and the common ground at [[federation-free-for-all-territories|the Federation's free-for-all territories]]. The collapse is at [[the-federation-dies|the Federation dies]].

[[the-federation|The Federation]] held the whole territory map through July 2018 and spent the month being attacked on it.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|July 2018}} The change is legible in the ordinary traffic of its channels well before it reaches any decision the alliance took, and the internal politics of the same weeks are at [[the-federation-july-2018|the Federation in July 2018]].

## A new guild on the first of the month

On 1 July 2018 a member of the war channel reported that a warring player had left [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] [BCr] for a four-member guild called Faith, and that Faith had begun taking territory from a member guild alongside BuildCraftia's own attacks.{{cite:federation-rule-and-practice-july-2018-discord-private|1 Jul 2018, the report}} That is one member's commentary on the map over about ten minutes, not anything the alliance established.

The fighting is in the log. Between 13:12 and 16:12 UTC that day forty-four captures involved Faith across fifteen territories in the canyon and around Aldorei, twenty-two taken by it and twenty-two lost, all but three of them to and from [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]], and not one of them stood on the free-for-all list the alliance had declared on 4 June.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges involving Faith, 1 Jul 2018 UTC, free-for-all list of 4 Jun 2018 excluded: 44 raw across 15 territories, 0 on the list, 44 contested}} BuildCraftia made 102 contested captures of its own the same day.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures by BuildCraftia, 1 Jul 2018 UTC, free-for-all list of 4 Jun 2018 excluded: 102 contested of 107 raw}} The report and the fighting both fall on 1 July, the alliance's clock running seven hours behind the log's. The exchange at 16:12 is the last in which Faith appears at all, and its whole record runs from 15 June 2018 to that afternoon.{{cite:territory-exchanges|complete record of Faith, all dates UTC: 15 Jun – 1 Jul 2018, 99 captures}}

## Retaken within the quarter hour

By 3 July three of the alliance's smaller guilds were being taken back faster than they could recover. A member told the alliance-wide channel that [[ex-nihilo|Ex Nihilo]], [[the-hive|The Hive]] [THI] and Verinian Trials [ViT] were all being targeted.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|3 Jul 2018, the three guilds}} oSo was retaking their territories, he said, usually within fifteen minutes of their recapturing them.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|3 Jul 2018, the fifteen minutes}} Another member told him to get used to it and said they would not stop.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|3 Jul 2018, the answer}} In the same exchange he mentioned that the leader of the opposing side was still trying to get his own guild to change sides.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|3 Jul 2018, the approach}}

Four days later that had worked on somebody else. On the evening of 7 July a guild leader told the leadership channel that the opposing leader had bought one of his chiefs into [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] [BCr] for thirty liquid emeralds, and that the man was a close personal friend of his.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|7 Jul 2018, the bribe}}

## A plan to attack at one moment

On 3 July the leader of [[the-hive|The Hive]] [THI] suggested to the war channel that the allied guilds attack together at a single moment.{{cite:federation-rule-and-practice-july-2018-discord-private|3 Jul 2018, the suggestion}} A member of the leadership agreed and said the opposing side would not know how to respond, and a third observed that this was exactly what the enemy's leader did to them.{{cite:federation-rule-and-practice-july-2018-discord-private|3 Jul 2018, the answers}} Over the following twenty minutes they named Verinian Trials [ViT], [[dogsamongus|DogsAmongUs]] [DAU], [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]] [SDU] and [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] [ANO] as guilds that might be brought in, and discussed tagging the guild leaders to tell them.{{cite:federation-rule-and-practice-july-2018-discord-private|3 Jul 2018, the guilds named}} The obstacle they settled on was not strategy but availability: getting everybody online at the same time, which would need a coordination channel of its own.{{cite:federation-rule-and-practice-july-2018-discord-private|3 Jul 2018, the obstacle}} Whether it was ever attempted was never recorded.

## The warnings came at night

At 09:57 on 7 July a member reported in the war channel that the opposing leader was threatening [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] and that a strike should be expected, and said it would be bloody if the man was not bluffing.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|7 Jul 2018, 09:57, the warning}} Twelve hours later, at 22:07, the alliance's founder called for backup in the same channel because that guild was being attacked, and a member remarked on it happening in the small hours.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|7 Jul 2018, 22:07, the call for backup}}

Shortly before two on the morning of the 12th the founder told the leadership channel that Aincrad had joined the opposing alliance and that Spirit Rogues were planning to.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|12 Jul 2018, the defections reported}} On the 15th at 13:15 he told the same channel that the opposition meant to attack Kingdom Foxes that night, that the Federation held most of the Wynn province, and that the intention was to sweep it out of the province and take it over.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|15 Jul 2018, 13:15, the intention}} These times are the alliance's own clock, seven hours behind the capture log's.

What the alliance could not do was answer in one place. Through the morning of 10 July members of the war channel described being attacked by two guilds at once, then by [[hackforums|HackForums]] [Hax] as well.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|10 Jul 2018, the escalation}} By the end of it they were saying the whole opposing group had come in and that BuildCraftia had begun taking it seriously.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|10 Jul 2018, the whole group}} Members of other guilds offered to send fighters.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|10 Jul 2018, the offers of help}} Nothing in those messages describes a planned joint operation; what they describe is an escalation across a morning.

## Joining at the worst possible moment

The alliance told its newest member so the day after admitting it. On 8 July, while the war channel was working out which territories to hand Renegade [ReA], a member said that this was a terrible time to join the Federation and that any guild that did so became a prime target for the opposing alliance.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|8 Jul 2018, the warning to the new guild}} The new guild's leader answered that he had known it when he came in.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|8 Jul 2018, the answer}} His guild had been admitted the previous afternoon by eleven votes to three — [[federation-ballots-july-2018|the ballots of July 2018]].{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|7/7/2018}}

## Forty territories

The alliance kept a running count of how much of the map its enemies held, and the figures got worse through the month. At 21:04 on 18 July the founder told the leadership channel that almost forty territories belonged to enemies.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|18 Jul 2018, 21:04, almost forty}} At 18:09 on the 30th a member counted thirty-seven territories not held by the Federation and broke them down as seven to [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]] [HoF], seven to Achte Shadow and nine to Grim.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|30 Jul 2018, 18:09, the breakdown}} The three come to twenty-three, and the remaining fourteen were left unattributed.

The breakdown is exact. Replaying every exchange to 01:09 UTC on 31 July — which is 18:09 on the alliance's clock the day before — puts Hall of Fame on seven territories, Achte Shadow on seven and Grim on nine.{{cite:territory-exchanges|holdings replayed from every exchange at 31 Jul 2018 01:09 UTC: Hall of Fame 7, Achte Shadow 7, Grim 9, 386 territories held in all}} The seven hours between the two clocks are what make the two line up.

## Ten captures, six of them on open ground

Grim [GRI] had been refused admission on 11 July.{{cite:federation-ballot-record-july-2018-discord-private|11 Jul 2018, the Grim motion}} Between the 14th and the 16th it took ten territories.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures by Grim, 14–16 Jul 2018 UTC: 10 across 8 territories}} Six of them stood on ground the alliance's own free-for-all declaration of 4 June 2018 had thrown open to anybody, and the declaration was still in force: the map the alliance ratified on 13 July restates the pool in a legend beneath the picture, and it is the June list unchanged — the same twenty-one territories in the same order, down to the count in brackets at the end.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|the legend of the map ratified 13 Jul 2018}} The map re-posted on the 18th carries the same twenty-one again.{{cite:federation-map-votes-july-august-2018-discord-private|the legend of the map re-posted 18 Jul 2018}} That is worth stating because the alliance twice changed the pool by publishing a picture and writing nothing down, on 25 July and again on 5 August, so a July map is exactly where a silent change would sit — Temple of Legends and Herb Cave twice each, Jungle Lake and Detlas once — and they came off four different guilds.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures by Grim, 14–16 Jul 2018 UTC, free-for-all list of 4 Jun 2018: 6 of 10 on the list, from four defending guilds}} The other four were the City of Troms, Great Bridge Jungle, Jungle Mid and Jungle Lower, all four taken from Kingdom Foxes' assigned map.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures by Grim from Kingdom Foxes, 14–16 Jul 2018 UTC: City of Troms, Great Bridge Jungle, Jungle Mid, Jungle Lower}}

Read without that distinction the ten look like a rejected applicant making war on a whole alliance. Read with it they are four attacks and six ordinary contests, and the difference is the alliance's own published list — [[federation-free-for-all-territories|the free-for-all territories]].

## A treaty broken in a morning

[[house-of-sentinels|House of Sentinels]] [Snt] came back to life at the start of the month. On 5 July members of the war channel asked one another whether its revival was confirmed.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|5 Jul 2018, the revival noticed}} There were people in it now, they said, and put the number at six.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|5 Jul 2018, six members}} Its first capture in three months fell at 01:10 UTC on 6 July, which on the alliance's clock is the evening of the 5th.{{cite:territory-exchanges|first House of Sentinels exchange after 1 Apr 2018: Light Realm Mushrooms from Ex Nihilo, 6 Jul 2018 01:10 UTC}} The alliance made an arrangement with it two days later over Tree Island.{{cite:federation-carried-its-dead-2018-discord-private|7 Jul 2018, the Tree Island motion}}

On 30 July it found the arrangement broken. At 18:07 a member asked the founder whether a named figure was violating the agreement, informally called after the guild; the founder said yes, and another member said it had been violated earlier that morning.{{cite:federation-under-attack-july-2018-discord-private|30 Jul 2018, 18:07, the treaty}} The morning is in the log. Between 15:55 UTC that day and the message, House of Sentinels took fourteen territories across eleven pieces of ground, ten from Ex Nihilo and four from [[fantasy|Fantasy]], and not one of them stood on the free-for-all pool in either of the two states the pool was in that week.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures by House of Sentinels, 30 Jul 2018 15:55 UTC – 31 Jul 2018 01:07 UTC: 14 across 11 territories, Ex Nihilo 10 and Fantasy 4, none on the free-for-all list of 4 Jun 2018 nor on the amended pool recorded 27 Jul 2018}}

## The end of the month

In the last days of July the opposing bloc mounted a sustained assault on Kingdom Foxes, and the alliance thanked the guilds that had answered it after fourteen hours of being targeted.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Arianna, 12:23 AM, 7/30/2018}} That night is at [[the-federation-july-2018|the Federation in July 2018]]. Four member guilds had left the alliance in the eleven days before it.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 7/22/2018 and 7/25/2018}}

Times taken from the alliance's channels are its own clock, which ran eight hours behind UTC that winter. Territory-exchange figures are UTC and say so.

## Two clearings and a recovery

[[the-federation|The Federation]] was founded on the evening of 16 February 2018 and was fighting within two days — see [[the-federation-founding-night|the founding night]].{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018, 20:05-20:06}} Its war channel spent 20 February reporting the end of [[hackforums|HackForums]]. At 03:50 a member noted that the guild had dropped out of the game's top three.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, 03:50}} At 08:06 another said it was off the map, and a war-leader put the whole opposing bloc at ten territories between them.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, 08:06}} By 12:48 members were reporting HackForums attacking again with [[emorians|Emorians]] alongside it.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, 12:48}} At 13:37 a member reported it cleared a second time.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, 13:37}} On the morning of the 22nd the alliance's founder put it at nothing once more.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|22 Feb 2018}}

Territory exchanges give the same fortnight a second shape. HackForums held 96 territories at the start of 19 February 2018 and 108 by midday — it was gaining ground on the day the alliance believed it was breaking.{{cite:territory-exchanges|HackForums holdings reconstructed from the last capture on each territory, 19 Feb 2018 00:00 and 12:00 UTC}} It fell through the 20th, to 79 at 06:00 and 25 at noon, and reached zero at 18:00 UTC.{{cite:territory-exchanges|HackForums holdings, 20 Feb 2018 06:00, 12:00 and 18:00 UTC}} It held four territories again by midnight, none by 06:00 on the 21st, and fourteen by 06:00 on the 22nd, falling to one by that midday.{{cite:territory-exchanges|HackForums holdings, 21-22 Feb 2018 UTC}} The guild kept taking ground throughout: eight captures in the 21:00 hour of the 20th and eleven in the 04:00 hour of the 22nd.{{cite:territory-exchanges|HackForums captures by UTC hour, 20-22 Feb 2018}}

Setting the two records side by side is this article's, and the eight-hour offset is the whole of it: 12:48 in the channel is 20:48 UTC, which falls inside the hour the log has HackForums taking seven territories.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, 12:48}}{{cite:territory-exchanges|HackForums captures, 20 Feb 2018 20:00-22:00 UTC}} A page dating the end of HackForums as a territorial power to 19 February 2018 is dating it to the day the guild peaked. The campaign itself is at [[the-wipe-of-hackforums|the wipe of HackForums]].

## Ground was ranked, not defended alike

The alliance did not answer every loss. On the evening of 18 February a member reported that [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] had taken Detlas.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|18 Feb 2018, Detlas taken}} A war-leader answered that it should be left alone, and sent the effort to the swamp and to stopping Emorians retaking Llevigar instead.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|18 Feb 2018, the war-leader's direction}} That the ground was being ranked rather than held uniformly is this article's reading of the instruction, and nobody in the channel put it in those terms.

Corkus went in a day. At 13:01 on 19 February a war-leader of [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] told the channel that his guild and [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]] were sweeping the region together and that the others should work elsewhere.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018, 13:01}} By 18:29 members reported the region substantially taken, with Emorians, [[house-of-sentinels|House of Sentinels]] and Paladins United still contesting it.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018, 18:29}} At 22:32 the founder judged that Paladins United had stopped for the night and proposed finishing it then.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018, 22:32}} It did not hold. Before dawn on the 23rd a member of [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]] called the whole alliance back to Corkus, [[necronomicon|Necronomicon]] and House of Sentinels having taken it and gained more than a dozen territories doing so.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|23 Feb 2018, the call to Corkus}} The twelve Corkus territories carried 121 exchanges on 19 February and 165 on the 23rd.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges on the twelve Corkus territories, 18-24 Feb 2018 UTC, raw: no enumerated free-for-all list covers the month}}

[[iceblue-team|Ice Blue Team]] was in the line on the 19th, holding the Light Forest with Kingdom Foxes before both moved to Llevigar; it had been admitted by vote the day before.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018, the Light Forest}}{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|the Federation motion, 18 Feb 2018}} A second front opened in the ocean regions shortly after midnight on 24 February, and the alliance sequenced it: within forty minutes members had agreed to clear Necronomicon out first, with [[the-hive|The Hive]] assisting, and to take Paladins United afterwards.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, the ocean}}

## Desertion, not territory

Nobody in the war channel gave territory as the reason for the campaign. On 20 February members said that HackForums had abandoned them and gone over to their enemies.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, the desertion grievance}} On the same morning members called for House of Sentinels to be barred permanently from holding any ground at all.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, the proposed bar}} Both are about the walkout of 16 February rather than about ground; reading them together as one grievance is this article's.{{cite:coalition-fracture-2018-discord-private|Coalition general channel, 16 Feb 2018}} The proposed bar was one member's wish in a war channel and no motion on House of Sentinels went to the alliance's guild leaders that month.{{cite:federation-admission-votes-february-2018-discord-private|the thirteen motions, 17-28 Feb 2018}}

The refusal had been stated the day before. On the morning of 19 February a member listed ten guilds fighting under the Federation's banner and predicted that House of Sentinels would want to rejoin.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018, the ten guilds listed}} The leader of Hall of Fame said he would not allow it.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018, the refusal}} The members around him framed it as a penalty for disloyalty rather than a military judgement.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018, the framing}} It was one guild leader speaking in a war channel and not a decision of the alliance.

The opposing bloc's largest war party of the fortnight was reported on 18 February, when the founder told the channel that House of Sentinels had put fifteen people into a single war and called it a record.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|18 Feb 2018, the fifteen}} No comparable party was reported on the Federation's own side that fortnight.

## The collapses did not line up

The alliance's opponents came apart at different rates on the same morning. [[kangronomicon|Kangronomicon]] was down to five territories by the early hours of 19 February on a member's own count off the game's map, while the same member reported that House of Sentinels had by then retaken everything the alliance had taken from it.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018, 04:31, the count of five}} The count is exact. Kangronomicon held 44 territories at 06:00 UTC on 19 February and five at noon, and 04:31 on the alliance's clock is 12:31 UTC.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Kangronomicon holdings, 19 Feb 2018 06:00 and 12:00 UTC}} A stronger account of the same day, putting three opponents into collapse at once, is at [[the-federation|the Federation]].{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018}}

Fighting on 20 February ran across the whole front at once. Within one minute in the afternoon a member called for help for Hall of Fame, which was under attack by Paladins United and House of Sentinels together; a second reported attacking HackForums in the Molten Heights; a third reported attacking Paladins United; and a war-leader added that Kingdom Foxes was being hit at the same time.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018, the minute}}

## A guild two hours old

On 24 February members asked who MoneyzUnited was. At 12:00 a member answered that its owner had made the guild two hours earlier and suggested it was a feeder for Paladins United; the suspicion was repeated through the day and into the 25th, and Paladins United denied it in the alliance's hearing.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|24-25 Feb 2018, MoneyzUnited}} Nothing settled it either way. The guild's first capture falls at 18:37 UTC on 24 February and its last on 2 March, forty-seven in all.{{cite:territory-exchanges|MoneyzUnited, first and last capture and total, 24 Feb - 2 Mar 2018 UTC}} How the alliance reached conclusions of this kind that week is at [[federation-reading-the-enemy-february-2018|reading the enemy]].

## Nine regions in one day

By 28 February the fighting had spread across almost the whole map at once. Members of nine Federation guilds reported contesting ground in Corkus, the canyon, the Sky Islands, the Molten Heights, the Wynn province, the Light Forest, Llevigar, Ragni and Rodoroc on that single day, asking for reinforcement across guild lines throughout.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|28 Feb 2018, the nine regions}} Seven opposing guilds were named across those reports.{{cite:federation-war-fortnight-february-2018-discord-private|28 Feb 2018, the guilds named}} Five of them — HackForums, Paladins United, Emorians, Kangronomicon and House of Sentinels — are the six guilds that left the Coalition twelve days earlier less [[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]], which had returned; the other two named were Necronomicon and New York Nibbas.{{cite:coalition-fracture-2018-discord-private|the departing guilds, 16 Feb 2018}} Setting the day's reports against the walkout roll is this article's, and a member naming a guild in a war channel is not evidence that the guild was acting corporately.

Both rosters are assembled from many separate reports across one day rather than stated by anyone as a list. What the channel offers is not a record of who was allied with whom but a record of what the alliance found it plausible to believe about the guilds attacking it.

Goden rose from recruit to guild leader inside [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] across [[federation-era|the Federation era]].{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, 13 Nov 2018}}

Answering [[drew1011|Drew1011]]'s tribute on 13 November 2018, [[lego-dw|Lego_DW]] named "the absolute gods of war this alliance created" — himself, 21(n), Goden, _Antho74_, InnerJoker, 0x7cf and snowy — as the players who "really held the alliance together in terms of war power".{{cite:thread-237070|post #18, Lego_DW, 13 Nov 2018}} Of the seven, Goden is the only one recorded elsewhere.

He appears in the warring arguments of the period on both sides of the map's balance. In June 2016 he credited a guild outside the anti-[[hackforums|HackForums]] bloc with breaking its hold: "Nether's Ascent took over Hax recently... and they've been able to hold the map decently well."{{cite:thread-130386-p05|post #94, 21 Jun 2016}} Defending the Federation's dominance in April 2018, he cited [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] as proof that a small guild could still reach the top of the leaderboard — "that case of more members = #1 isn't always the case, as shown by how IBT took #1 with like four active warring members in total".{{cite:thread-224905|post #7 (Goden)}}

## History

### An unnamed coalition
By 18 December 2020 an unnamed group of anti-[[Artemis]] guilds had formed - among them [[Avicia]], [[Emorians]], [[Eden]], Ultra Violet, Bovemists, Diablo and The Dark Phoenix - together with [[Paladins United]] and [[Imperial]], the two surviving founders of Artemis itself.{{cite:thread-279337|post #1, 18 Dec 2020}} The coalition's coordinated war on the Artemis core began that same December, when territory exchanges jumped to 913 for the month after months of skirmishing.{{cite:territory-exchanges|territory_exchanges map-data analysis}} The [[goose-artemis-war|Goose-Artemis war]] would run for the rest of the coalition's life.

### Named by January 2021
The name Goose first appeared on the morning of 19 January 2021, in two [[the-forum-megalist|megalist]] posts made within half an hour of each other: 99loulou999 of The Dark Phoenix noting that 'TNR (TNI and TNA) are part of Goose', and Lev98bug of Eden asking 'Eden is in Goose if you could add that'.{{cite:thread-278112-p04|p4 #71 and #72, 19 Jan 2021}} The megalist's maintainer added both and replied, 'Just want to drop in and say that I'm a huge fan of the name.'{{cite:thread-278112-p04|p4 #73, 19 Jan 2021}} Who coined it, and when the coalition formally adopted it, was never written down.

Growth continued through the spring.{{cite:thread-278112-p06|p6 #112 and #113, 4 Mar 2021}} On 1 February 2021 [[urbymine]], a former Chief of Avicia, noted that 'BNU(IBT), SHY and TNL are all goose now' - [[ShadowFall]]'s defection from an Artemis leadership seat, alongside IceBlue Team and TheNoLifes.{{cite:thread-278112-p04|p4 #76, 1 Feb 2021}} By late March the future [[Khaos]] core ([[Aequitas]], Wheres The Finish, ChinaNumberOne, Eden, joined in April by Fuzzy Spiders) was aboard.{{cite:thread-289196|post #1, 30 Mar 2021}}{{cite:thread-289556|post #1, 6 Apr 2021}}

### The fanart roster
Goose was the only alliance whose membership was ever drawn. On 30 March and 6 April 2021 [[drasalia|Drasalia]], a member of ChinaNumberOne, posted two illustration threads personifying twelve member guilds: The Mage Legacy, Aequitas, ChinaNumberOne, Wheres The Finish, TheNoLifes and IceBlue Team in the first,{{cite:thread-289196|post #1, 30 Mar 2021}} Eden, Guardian of Wynn, Fuzzy Spiders, ShadowFall, The Dark Phoenix and Avicia in the second.{{cite:thread-289556|post #1, 6 Apr 2021}} The threads were explicitly partial and unofficial - 'this is just my doodles of what I think the Guilds would look like if they were people', with the second noting 'the GOOSE alliance still has more I need to finish'.{{cite:thread-289556|post #1, 6 Apr 2021}} Members of the drawn guilds replied approvingly, among them Lalatera and FrozenGalaxy of Aequitas, ZrBence of IceBlue Team, DeathLucifer of TheNoLifes and Eden's Lev98bug.{{cite:thread-289196|posts #8, #10, #12 and #17, 30 Mar – 7 Apr 2021}}

![Character art of six Goose member guilds from the second of Drasalia's illustration threads, 6 April 2021, each figure captioned with its guild tag: EDN, GsW, Cxz, SHY, TNI and AVO.](/images/chronicles/media/goose-902f9e79.webp){{cite:thread-289556|post #1, 6 Apr 2021}}

On 25 January 2021 Goose and Artemis jointly granted [[Profession Heaven]] neutral status as a public profession-XP guild.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} Goose took map control from Artemis and held it until 11 May 2021, when both alliances collapsed on the same day - [[the-great-collapse|The Great Collapse]] - splitting into [[Valhalla]] and Khaos.{{cite:community-guild-timeline}}

### What remains unknown
Goose's formation date, internal leadership and full roster were never recorded. Every recorded membership join is a 'by' date.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} Two guilds named in the December 2020 coalition list - 'Lily Pad' and 'HICH' - cannot be identified with any known guild.{{cite:thread-279337|post #1, 18 Dec 2020}}

## Membership
{{alliance:Goose}}

### Goose and Elysium

In the merger talks of 9–11 May 2021 the bloc's own negotiators used two names for their side in the same conversation, and used them for different things. **Goose** named the bloc and its guilds — negotiating with a number of Goose guilds, pulling guilds from Goose, splitting the moderator seats so many from Goose and so many from Artemis. **Elysium** named a body and a structure — the current Elysium system, Elysium having one guild leader and two congress seats, what Elysium as a body would make of somebody.{{cite:artemis-elysium-negotiation-may-2021-discord-private|9–11 May 2021}} One speaker spoke of inviting former Goose guilds into the alliance, which points at succession rather than two names for one thing.{{cite:artemis-elysium-negotiation-may-2021-discord-private|10 May 2021}}

Which of the two contained the other, or whether Elysium was the newer organisation drawn out of a wider Goose, was never set down. What the talks establish is only that both names were in use by the same people at the same time, meaning different things.

## Legacy
Goose's split seeded the next era: Valhalla drew on its broad membership while Khaos formed from its core, and the [[valhalla-khaos-war|war between them]] began the day both were founded. The joint protection of Profession Heaven that Goose co-established persisted under successor alliances until 2026.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}

On 9 March 2020, in VeeeKoooN's thread "The Artemis Alliance And Why It's Problematic To Me", GoulartJJ replied: "Hello, as the Shadow Fall leader and one of Artemis leaders I'd like to say that small guilds can grow very easily, alliance or not"{{cite:thread-263975-p02|p2 #36, 9 Mar 2020}}. No other first-person claim to an [[artemis|Artemis]] leadership seat was ever recorded.

The rest of the post set out how Artemis explained itself. On growth: ShadowFall "grew with FFA's, we were level 20ish by August of last year, we tried joining [[luna|Luna]] when we were leveling but got denied, that didn't stop us"{{cite:thread-263975-p02|p2 #36, 9 Mar 2020}}. On the map: "If you're not in a map controlling alliance, nothing stops you from attacking any territories in the map, some may reclaim it instantly, others would rather use a diplomatic approach"{{cite:thread-263975-p02|p2 #36, 9 Mar 2020}}. On the distribution of land: "Guilds who are with big claims are the guilds who have contributed the most to the alliance." On entry: "Guilds may also apply to Artemis by contacting one of the guild leaders, and possibly be accepted."{{cite:thread-263975-p02|p2 #36, 9 Mar 2020}}

Within a year ShadowFall had changed sides — [[urbymine]] reported on 1 February 2021 that "BNU(IBT), SHY and TNL are all goose now"{{cite:thread-278112-p04|p4 #76, 1 Feb 2021}}. GoulartJJ was later a member of [[imperial|Imperial]]{{cite:thread-263975-p02|p2 #36, author guild badge as captured (badge markup, not post text)}}.

Guilds of this period ran institutions as well as wars, and the two were administered by the same people in the same rooms.

## Public faces

A guild's recruitment thread on the game's forums was an official document rather than an advertisement. One council treated a member who held inconsistent ranks across the in-game guild, its Discord and its forum thread as a problem requiring resolution.{{cite:guild-community-and-institutions-discord-private|guild council, 2016–2017}} A handbook and a programme of events were markers of a properly run guild: a leader dividing her time between two of them described the second as having neither, and named the first as her primary.{{cite:guild-community-and-institutions-discord-private|guild leadership discussion}}

From the applicant's side the thread was the only door, and it was a door into a place he might never have been. A player who joined one of the large guilds of the first alliance era had never used the forums at all, and made an account for the sole purpose of submitting his application; it was his first post there.{{cite:holders-of-le-storytime-2018-discord-private|the forum application}} He had narrowed his choice to three guilds of the same alliance and took the smaller of them, on the ground that it still had room to grow{{cite:holders-of-le-storytime-2018-discord-private|choosing among three guilds of the alliance}}, and did not disband the guild he was leaving until the application had been answered, keeping it alive eight days as somewhere to return to.{{cite:holders-of-le-storytime-2018-discord-private|the eight-day fallback}}

Recruitment was a policy choice. In February 2017 one guild paused recruiting on its forum thread altogether, preferring the quality of the connections between the members it already had to further growth.{{cite:guild-community-and-institutions-discord-private|recruitment pause, February 2017}} Leadership also read what the wider community said about them: a forum thread criticising guild culture circulated in December 2016 and was discussed by one guild's leadership, who took it as making thinly veiled criticisms of guild behaviour in general.{{cite:guild-community-and-institutions-discord-private|leadership discussion, December 2016}}

## Community as an office

Community work was formally constituted. The [[emperium-of-wynn|Emperium of Wynn]] elected a Community Leader as one of three governing positions, alongside the war and economy portfolios{{cite:guild-community-and-institutions-discord-private|alliance offices, 2017}}, and the office ran events and policed members' conduct.{{cite:gdoc-1u97hsmpzu2lapij|The Emperial Truth, Tier 1, Community Leader}} Alliances distinguished war alliances from community alliances, and guilds moved between the categories: two guilds left a war alliance in June 2016 to form a small community alliance of their own, and a guild worn down by months of sustained targeting reclassified itself as a community guild in January 2019 while still intending to recover its territory.{{cite:guild-community-and-institutions-discord-private|war and community alliances, 2016–2019}}{{cite:aesir-pact-2018-discord-private|member guild reclassification, January 2019}}

Belonging came in gradations. Earlier alliances used auxiliary guilds that fought alongside them without full membership, and the [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]] later ran an explicit follower rank for a guild it had not yet decided whether to admit.{{cite:guild-community-and-institutions-discord-private|auxiliary and follower status}}{{cite:aesir-pact-2018-discord-private|follower status, 12–21 Nov 2018}}

## Ceremony and anniversary

Admissions were marked ceremonially. On 18 March 2016, nine days after [[ordo-malleus|Ordo Malleus]] collapsed, four guilds gathered at Llevigar for a ceremony at which [[holders-of-le|Holders of LE]] and HaHaUnited were announced as joining [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]] — an admission to an existing alliance rather than the founding of a new one, and preceded by Valkyrie's leader questioning the two of them to satisfy herself they were a good fit.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L466–473, second edition, retyped 26 Feb 2018}}{{cite:community-guild-timeline|18 Mar 2016}} The other two guilds present, [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] and [[imperial|Imperial]], had founded the alliance on 2 June 2015 and were in it already.{{cite:gdoc-1b8e1nh_ate9h69n|the Valkyrie Alliance, founded 2 June 2015}} The town is worth stating carefully. The instalment was carried in two guild channels, and the later one is a re-typing rather than a copy: the two name different towns in a sentence otherwise identical word for word.{{cite:storytime-two-editions-2018-discord-private|the ceremony sentence in both editions}} The telling copied out of Discord says Llevigar and gives the date as 18 March 2016, and this article follows it.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L473, composed 23 Jan 2018}} It is the second edition itself rather than an independent copy: its instalment headings carry the newer channel's evenings only, and its attachments all came from a single channel created at 19:41 UTC on 17 February 2018, the morning after the alliance that had commissioned the history collapsed.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|instalment headings and attachment manifest, channel 414506744228872193, id-encoded creation 17 Feb 2018 19:41 UTC}} The older channel had been open since 11 January.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|instalment channel opened 11 Jan 2018}} Chronology decides nothing either, then: the later typing is the one that was copied out, and it can only agree with itself.{{cite:storytime-two-editions-2018-discord-private|the two editions of the ceremony sentence}} Which town the guilds walked to went unsettled.

It is not the only such difference. Elsewhere in the same series a single word separates the two typings of one sentence, and that word reverses whether the people in a photograph belonged to the guild at the time.{{cite:storytime-two-editions-2018-discord-private|the photograph sentence in both editions}} Of six hundred and seventy-one instalments the two channels share, all but fifteen match word for word, and nine of those fifteen are rewordings rather than slips of spacing.{{cite:storytime-two-editions-2018-discord-private|671 shared instalments, 656 identical, 15 differing, 9 reworded}} The two editions are one man's words twice over rather than two witnesses, so neither can be produced as a check on the other; where they disagree both readings belong in the record, and [[guild-history-two-editions|the guild history's two editions]] sets out the rest of them.

Anniversaries were kept. Members marked one guild's founding anniversary and identified it as among the oldest in its alliance, and a leader separately marked the second anniversary of the day an earlier alliance had died.{{cite:guild-community-and-institutions-discord-private|anniversaries}} That instinct produced the period's [[guild-histories|most substantial written history]]. Guild leaders narrated long retrospective accounts in channels set aside for the purpose — dozens of events told in sequence with dates, and corrected as they went by other people who had been there.{{cite:guild-community-and-institutions-discord-private|retrospective channels}}

The [[coalition|Coalition]] built one into its rebuilt Discord in January 2018, "for leaders to tell their Wynn stories".{{cite:coalition-welcome|1/23/2018}}

## Infrastructure

The clearest sign of an alliance dying was not territorial. In January 2017 a guild's council recorded that the alliance it belonged to no longer had a shared Discord server and that its group chat had gone dead, leaving members with nowhere to coordinate — an obituary written entirely in terms of missing channels.{{cite:guild-community-and-institutions-discord-private|guild council, January 2017}} The Emperium of Wynn, the most heavily governed alliance of the period, ended the same way: its server was pruned to three people on 3 December 2017.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|3 Dec 2017}}

An emptied server did not always mean an emptied guild, because servers were also replaced outright and the old room left to whoever stayed in it. [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] announced on 8 March 2017 that it was remaking its Discord; the replacement went up on the 9th, members were invited across on the 9th and 10th, and those still in the first server were cleared out of it on 21 March.{{cite:kingdom-of-foxes-state-council-2016-2017-discord-private|8–21 Mar 2017}} The [[coalition|Coalition]] did the same under duress ten months later, its first server having been deleted by a member guild's former monarch and a replacement created forty seconds afterwards.{{cite:coalition-discord-deleted-january-2018-discord-private|12 Jan 2018, 04:19:40 UTC}} Both guilds carried on; only the rooms stopped.

Server access was a sanction in its own right. Guilds banned rivals' leaders from their alliance servers, and members recorded having been banned from a member guild's own server after criticising its leadership.{{cite:federation-at-its-height-2018-discord-private|Discord bans, August–September 2018}}{{cite:guild-community-and-institutions-discord-private|server access as sanction}}

## Availability

When people could play was a strategic variable. Territory was lost when a guild's war commander was unavailable to defend it, and rival guilds timed attacks to periods when a target's defenders were offline.{{cite:guild-community-and-institutions-discord-private|availability and defence}} One rival pushed deep into a [[coalition|Coalition]] member's territory overnight while the alliance's defenders were largely asleep, forcing an organised recapture the next day.{{cite:coalition-founding-2017-discord-private|overnight attack, early 2018}}

## The serialised history

The [[coalition|Coalition]] opened two channels in its rebuilt Discord server on 11 January 2018, one for instalments of a written history of the guilds and one for readers to argue with them; the welcome card gave the first as being "for leaders to tell their Wynn stories".{{cite:coalition-welcome|1/23/2018}} Its author was [[drew1011|Drew1011]], who described it on the forums as "a massive ongoing written history of the guild community from May 2015 onwards".{{cite:thread-251543|post #6, 27 Jun 2019}}

Both channels moved to [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s server five weeks later, and instalments continued at widening intervals until 1 February 2021.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|instalment and discussion channels, Jan 2018 – Feb 2021}} The discussion channels ran to more than four times the length of the instalment channels and were still in use for reminiscence in December 2024.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|channel volumes; discussion channel to Dec 2024}}

Asked on 12 July 2018 whether he worked from a draft, he answered that he wrote from memory, fixed his dates against his guild's own timeline document and went back through old logs, and that anything finer than that was memory.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|method, 12 Jul 2018}} Readers pressed him for dates in the same session and the answers were approximate: one guild placed somewhere in February, another only as earlier than the alliance, a third's decline a few weeks after a change of leadership.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|dating questions, 12 Jul 2018}}

## Two editions

The history was typed out twice. Its author filled the Coalition's channel over six evenings between 19 January and 16 February 2018, and from 22 February retyped it into the Federation's, message by message and in the same order, before the serial went on into new material.{{cite:storytime-two-editions-2018-discord-private|the reposting of 22 and 26 February and 4 March 2018}} Of the 671 instalments the two channels share, 656 match word for word; nine of the rest are rewordings, and three of those change what the history says.{{cite:storytime-two-editions-2018-discord-private|671 shared, 656 identical, 9 reworded, 3 altering meaning}} From the instalment of 26 February the direction reverses and the older channel takes the copy.{{cite:storytime-two-editions-2018-discord-private|651 of 741 duplicates appear first in the Coalition channel, 90 first in the Federation's}}

Two things follow. Counted without the duplicates the instalment channels hold about 1,066 distinct messages rather than the 1,834 they carry between them.{{cite:storytime-reposted-february-2018-discord-private|1,834 text-bearing messages, 768 duplicates, about 1,066 distinct}} And a telling dated from the second edition is dated five weeks late, which for a retrospective carrying no event date of its own leaves no reliable date at all: see [[guild-history-two-editions|the two editions]].{{cite:storytime-reposted-february-2018-discord-private|dating a copy to its appearance rather than its origin}}

## Two tests

Two passages can be checked against the alliances' own dated announcements.

The instalment of 20 June 2019, narrating [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]]'s expulsion from the Federation, breaks off to complain about the revisionism the episode had undergone and puts the vote on 30 March.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|instalment of 20 Jun 2019}} That is right: the motion went into the alliance's voting channel on the afternoon of 30 March 2018, the leaders channel was reacting six minutes later and the general channel had the outcome inside the hour.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|voting, leaders and general channels, 30 Mar 2018}} The opt-out for guilds unwilling to fight was in the text of the motion as posted.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|text of the motion, 30 Mar 2018}}

The final instalment, written on 1 February 2021, fails. It has [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]] and Verinian Trials plotting together and leaving the Federation on 25 June 2018.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|final instalment, 1 Feb 2021}} What the alliance announced on 25 June 2018 was that Verinian Trials had joined it, on the afternoon [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] and [[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]] left.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 6/25/2018}} Hall of Fame and Verinian Trials left together, having partnered with the opposing bloc, a month later to the day.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 7/25/2018}} Reading the error as two crises falling on the twenty-fifth of consecutive months and folding into one another is this article's. He had opened that instalment by writing that nearly three years had gone by, that his memory of anything but the significant events had gone, and that he would cover only the highlights.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|final instalment, 1 Feb 2021}}

## A second serialised history

It was not the only history of the period written down by somebody who had lived through it. Over two evenings in April 2018, on the 2nd and the 7th, a member of [[holders-of-le|Holders of LE]] posted 117 messages of his own history of the 2015-16 guild scene into a channel in his guild's server.{{cite:holders-of-le-storytime-2018-discord-private|2 and 7 Apr 2018, 117 messages}} It shares no text with either edition of the longer account: a separate narrative rather than a copy of one.{{cite:holders-of-le-storytime-2018-discord-private|no text shared with either edition}}

It is not a second witness to the first. Its author opened by placing himself beside the other narrator, who had kept organised records of the major guild events where he had only his own old game logs to search{{cite:holders-of-le-storytime-2018-discord-private|2 Apr 2018, the opening comparison}}, and began his second instalment by comparing the two accounts of the first great alliance.{{cite:holders-of-le-storytime-2018-discord-private|7 Apr 2018, the two versions compared}} He had read the other history before writing his. Where the two agree about politics he did not see himself, one of them is a reader of the other.

Those logs were the whole of his method, stated at the outset and returned to later.{{cite:holders-of-le-storytime-2018-discord-private|the stated method}} They were private and were never published. A log keeps the wording of exchanges memory alone would lose, and it also leaves no way of telling a date read off a log from a date supplied by the author. What the account is good for is set out at [[the-rank-and-file|the rank and file]].

## The timelines

The dates came from documents the guilds kept for themselves. A [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] timeline handbook was being argued over in the guild's private channel in December 2016 — whether it should list every council and its members, whether it should carry dates and council numbers beside the events, and how it should be laid out — with an old and a new version both in circulation.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|timeline handbook, December 2016}} They were revised after the fact: in August 2018 the historian noticed that the Federation had first been entered on the Fox timeline as the Second Coalition, its maintainer having wanted a Napoleonic reference and a numbered series of wars, and that she had since changed it.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|22 Aug 2018}}

He was not the only one writing. Two other members said in July 2018 that they were keeping histories of their own guilds in documents outside Discord.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|July 2018}} The Kingdom of Foxes' guild history sets its events against the guild's monarchs, viceroys and governments in a dated table, and its text runs from April 2014 to April 2021 — well past the last instalment of the serialised account.{{cite:gdoc-1kajulcrdc0cpf1j|Apr 2014 – Apr 2021}}

## Standing documents

Alliances also kept standing cards describing themselves, and those carry their dates worst of all: Discord holds only an edited message's final text, so a card's posting date need not be the date of the words on it.{{cite:standing-documents-as-retrospection-discord-private|edited announcement-tier messages}}

The Federation's alliance-information card was posted on 22 September 2018 and last edited on 1 March 2019, nearly four months after the alliance dissolved, and its standing text lists eleven member guilds and twenty-one enemies.{{cite:standing-documents-as-retrospection-discord-private|Federation alliance-information card}} Its roster can be dated from inside it, and to neither of those days: on 29 October 2018 a member of the alliance's territory committee pasted the guild list as thirteen, the card's eleven plus Nethers Ascent and [[the-divine-swords|TheDivineSwords]], and those two left on the 29th and the 30th.{{cite:standing-documents-as-retrospection-discord-private|territory committee, 29 Oct 2018}} The eleven are the Federation of the eleven days between 30 October and its collapse on 10 November 2018. An edit date fixes only that a card is no longer the document it was posted as.

The Coalition's card is the same kind of object. Posted on 11 January 2018 and last edited at six on the morning of 14 February, it lists fourteen member guilds — the number the alliance had on the night it broke two days later — and all six of the guilds that walked out are on it.{{cite:standing-documents-as-retrospection-discord-private|Coalition alliance-information card}} It is a good roster because it was revised while the membership was breaking up, not in spite of that.

The Federation's card was built on it: the two share an ornamented heading over the membership list, the same information heading, and the same short paragraph asking members not to spam the alliance's warring role.{{cite:standing-documents-as-retrospection-discord-private|the two cards compared}} Members said as much of the alliance itself: in August 2018 one leader called it a copy-paste of its predecessor down to the server rules, and its own leader agreed in the same exchange that nothing in the structure had changed.{{cite:standing-documents-as-retrospection-discord-private|22 Aug 2018}}

[[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]]' welcome channel held a rules card and a card of links, posted eleven seconds apart on 13 January 2018 and both revised the following June, and neither named an alliance.{{cite:standing-documents-as-retrospection-discord-private|Kingdom of Phoenixes welcome channel}} When the guild's members spent a morning that September asking one another which alliance they were in, there was no notice for them to be out of date about.{{cite:kingdom-phoenixes-september-2018-discord-private|14 Sep 2018}}

## An account that hardened

The [[coalition-civil-war|Coalition's collapse]] of 16 February 2018 was explained by the same participant repeatedly over seven months, and the explanation moved.

Three days afterwards he told the successor alliance's leaders channel that the guilds which walked out had wanted steadily more, listed the regions the largest of them held, put its allotment at sixty-nine territories and pasted the assignment document as support; about six minutes later a colleague objected that the figures counted allotted land only, left out the Wynn territories those guilds already held, and that they had come out of the settlement with less.{{cite:coalition-collapse-as-remembered-discord-private|leaders channel, 19 Feb 2018}} A rival attribution circulated by April, when a Coalition guild leader relayed into a Federation channel that BuildCraftia's leader was claiming to have brought the alliance down single-handed; two Federation leaders rejected it, and she said it was nowhere near the reason.{{cite:coalition-collapse-as-remembered-discord-private|Federation channel, 4 Apr 2018}}

By 30 August the correction had gone. Asked why the guilds had left, he answered that they had judged the rest of the alliance feeble and undeserving of what it held, and drew from that the figure that they had written off two-thirds of the alliance — which needs about fifteen member guilds and five departures.{{cite:coalition-collapse-as-remembered-discord-private|leaders channel, 30 Aug 2018}} The alliance's own membership card lists fourteen{{cite:standing-documents-as-retrospection-discord-private|Coalition alliance-information card}}, and six guilds left that night.{{cite:coalition-fracture-2018-discord-private|Coalition general channel, 16 Feb 2018}} That the two errors together are what turn a plain majority into two-thirds is this article's reading; his five is defensible for what he actually said, guilds that went over to the rival bloc, because one of the six left on its own grounds and was inside the successor alliance within a day.{{cite:coalition-collapse-as-remembered-discord-private|the count, 30 Aug 2018}}

The vote that preceded the walkout had acquired a single author by then, a woman said to have started it, and he agreed that she had.{{cite:coalition-collapse-as-remembered-discord-private|30 Aug 2018}} On 10 February 2018 one leader had argued against assigning the Wynn province at all, a second asked why it should not go to a vote, a third told the alliance's leader to open one, and he opened it about four minutes later.{{cite:coalition-collapse-as-remembered-discord-private|leaders channel, 10 Feb 2018}}

He then supplied the correction himself and did not use it, adding in the next message that on what he had heard the departing guilds had been discussing leaving before the vote was started at all.{{cite:coalition-collapse-as-remembered-discord-private|30 Aug 2018}} That is the part the dated announcements bear out: on the night of the walkout the guilds that stayed noticed that one of the departed had already been allotted an entire province in an alliance that had no name yet.{{cite:coalition-fracture-2018-discord-private|Coalition general channel, 16 Feb 2018}} By the end of that summer the episode had a settled name inside the alliance and was being used in passing as a shared reference in arguments about other things.{{cite:coalition-collapse-as-remembered-discord-private|late Aug – early Sep 2018}}

## It never reached the end

The narrative stopped in the summer of 2018 and closed by looking forward to the alliance's death without narrating it; the Federation had [[the-federation-dies|dissolved on 10 November 2018]].{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|final instalment, 1 Feb 2021}} The day after it appeared a reader asked in the discussion channel to be told how the Federation died, and nothing further went into the instalment channel.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|discussion channel, 2 Feb 2021}} A member marked the alliance's third anniversary on 10 November 2021, to the day.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|10 Nov 2021}}

The guild community's [[guild-histories|serialised history]] exists in two typings, and nothing inside either of them says that one is a copy of the other.{{cite:storytime-reposted-february-2018-discord-private|neither channel marks the second copy as a copy}}

## Six evenings, then three more

[[coalition|The Coalition]] opened a channel for instalments of a written history of the guilds on 11 January 2018, in the server it had rebuilt that week; the welcome card gave it as being "for leaders to tell their Wynn stories".{{cite:coalition-welcome|1/23/2018}}

Its author filled it over six evenings — 19 and 23 January, 15 and 16 February, then 27 February and 4 March, the last two after the alliance had ceased to exist.{{cite:storytime-two-editions-2018-discord-private|the six evenings and their message counts}}

The alliance broke on the night of 16 February 2018, and its successor's permanent channels were built the following morning.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018, 20:05}}{{cite:federation-origin-as-its-members-told-it-2018-discord-private|channel structure built the morning of 17 Feb 2018}} On the evening of 22 February the author began the history again in the new server — not with what came next, but with the opening instalment, message by message and in the same order.{{cite:storytime-reposted-february-2018-discord-private|22 Feb 2018, the first evening}} That evening carried 261 messages, of which 235 had stood in the older channel on 19 January; the passage describing the retirement of [[kangronomicon|Kangronomicon]]'s leader stands in one at 21:14 on 22 February and in the other at 20:10 on 19 January.{{cite:storytime-reposted-february-2018-discord-private|261 messages, 235 with 19 January originals; the Kangronomicon passage in both}} He finished the back catalogue on the 26th and went straight on into new material in the same sitting.{{cite:storytime-reposted-february-2018-discord-private|26 Feb 2018, 539 messages}}

Only the instalments were carried across. The two discussion channels beside them, of 992 messages and 7,803, share nothing longer than twenty-five characters.{{cite:storytime-reposted-february-2018-discord-private|the two discussion channels compared}}

## After 26 February the newer channel holds the original

Which channel holds the first typing changes partway through. Up to and including 16 February 2018 the Coalition's channel is the original; from the instalment of 26 February the Federation's is, and the older channel takes the copy.{{cite:storytime-two-editions-2018-discord-private|651 of 741 duplicates appear first in the Coalition channel, 90 first in the Federation's}} The 57 messages posted into the older channel on 27 February include 48 that had appeared in the newer one the evening before, and on 4 March an instalment reached the newer channel at two in the morning and the older one after lunch.{{cite:storytime-reposted-february-2018-discord-private|27 Feb and 4 Mar 2018}} For the last week of its life the older channel was receiving copies of instalments told in the newer one.

## Fifteen sentences came out differently

Six of the fifteen differ only in spacing.{{cite:storytime-two-editions-2018-discord-private|the six whitespace-only differences}} Nine are rewordings, and three of those change what the history says: a ceremony of March 2016 moves to a different town, a sentence about a photograph loses its negation and so admits into a guild the people it had excluded, and a passage about how long a group of leaders kept fighting drops its stated duration.{{cite:storytime-two-editions-2018-discord-private|the three sentences that alter meaning}} The rest are smaller: a founder's name given in full the second time, an interval before a guild's departure loosened from a definite few days to an indefinite soon afterwards, an elapsed month brought up to date, punctuation and tense.{{cite:storytime-two-editions-2018-discord-private|the remaining changes}}

The sentence that moves the ceremony describes 18 March 2016, nine days after [[ordo-malleus|Ordo Malleus]] collapsed, when four guilds gathered and an admission to [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]] was announced.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L473}} Two of the four were the ones joining, [[holders-of-le|Holders of LE]] and HaHaUnited{{cite:community-guild-timeline|18 Mar 2016}}{{cite:gdoc-1iacdk2t_3tzwo_a|section 1.1, guilds joining 18 March 2016}}; [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] and [[imperial|Imperial]] had founded the alliance on 2 June 2015 and were already in it.{{cite:gdoc-1b8e1nh_ate9h69n|the Valkyrie Alliance, founded 2 June 2015}} The second edition puts the ceremony at Llevigar.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L473}} The first edition names a different town, and that reading was never copied out.{{cite:storytime-two-editions-2018-discord-private|the ceremony sentence in both editions}}

## Only the second edition was copied out

Only one of the two editions left Discord, and it is the second. Its instalment headings run 22 and 26 February, 4 and 22 March, 20 May, 11 July and 30 August 2018, 20 June 2019 and 1 February 2021 — the newer channel's evenings, and none of the older channel's.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|instalment headings, 22 Feb 2018 – 1 Feb 2021}} Its sixty-two attachments all came from one channel, whose identifier encodes its creation at 19:41 UTC on 17 February 2018, the morning after the Coalition broke.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|attachment manifest: single channel 414506744228872193, id-encoded creation 17 Feb 2018 19:41 UTC}} The Coalition's channel had been open since 11 January.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|instalment channel opened 11 Jan 2018}}

Only the later reading of each of the fifteen travelled, then. The two editions are one man's words twice over rather than two witnesses, so neither can be produced as a check on the other, and chronology settles nothing either: nothing makes a reviser a corrector rather than a man misremembering five weeks on.

Those same attachment identifiers fix the clock the headings were written on. Eleven attachments timed between 05:01 and 05:32 UTC on 23 February 2018 sit under a heading of nine in the evening on the 22nd, eight hours behind; eight timed between 06:42 and 06:49 UTC on 21 May sit under a heading of 11:49 at night on the 20th, seven hours behind.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|attachment times against instalment headings, February and May 2018}} The export ran on a clock that kept daylight saving, and a flat offset is an hour out on every winter date.

## What a date on the second edition means

An instalment found in the newer channel and dated late February is a copy of a January or mid-February telling. That matters more here than it usually would, because these are retrospectives carrying no event date of their own: the date of the telling is the only date they have, and taken off the second edition it lands five weeks late, on a day when the alliance being described had already been replaced.{{cite:storytime-reposted-february-2018-discord-private|dating a copy to its appearance rather than its origin}} Six undated observations of the period were dated that way.{{cite:storytime-reposted-february-2018-discord-private|six observations whose tellings belong to 19 or 23 January or 16 February 2018}}

The duplication inflates the corpus as well. Counting messages that carry text, the two instalment channels hold 1,834 between them; 768 of those are the same message twice, leaving about 1,066 distinct.{{cite:storytime-reposted-february-2018-discord-private|1,834 text-bearing messages, 768 duplicates, about 1,066 distinct}} The largest deposit of pre-2018 guild detail is roughly two-fifths smaller than a channel-by-channel count makes it.

## What a serialised history will bear

A second serialised history of the same years shows the same weakness from the other side. Written in April 2018 by a member of [[holders-of-le|Holders of LE]], sharing no text with either edition of this one{{cite:holders-of-le-storytime-2018-discord-private|no text shared with either edition}}, it dates the central event of its own story twice. In its first instalment the author emptied his guild of its members on 28 November 2015 and spent a further eight days disbanding it{{cite:holders-of-le-storytime-2018-discord-private|first instalment, 2 Apr 2018, the disbanding}}; in the second, five days later, he gave the disbandment as 3 December 2016 and his admission to Holders of LE three days after that.{{cite:holders-of-le-storytime-2018-discord-private|second instalment, 7 Apr 2018, the disbanding}} The story running between the two is one continuous sequence, so both cannot be right, and nothing in the two evenings decides which is.

The year is the costlier of the two errors, because it moves the man between alliances: Holders of LE was a founding member of [[ordo-malleus|Ordo Malleus]] from 6 November 2015{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L169–178, composed 22 Feb 2018}} and had gone into hibernation on 1 December 2016, two days before the later date.{{cite:gdoc-1bbnalbfij8cjnog|Dec 2016}} Setting the two datings against the guild's own timeline is this article's reading and settles neither of them; both are given at [[the-rank-and-file|the rank and file]].

Taken with the rewordings above, the two failures measure the same thing from opposite ends. A serial typed out years afterwards, from memory or from a player's own game logs, carries detail that nothing else carries and cannot be trusted to the day. Neither of the period's two serialised histories can.

Where the two editions disagree and nothing settles it, both readings belong in the record. That a guild's own history came out twice, in two versions, is itself part of what happened to it.

Guild identity in the alliance era was unstable in ways that make the names alone an unreliable guide. A guild could be disbanded and rebuilt under the same name months later, sold to a stranger, run from a second account, or dissolved into a merger that inherited neither parent's name, and its members' quarrels usually travelled with the members.

## Disbandment and revival

The clearest documented case is the [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]], disbanded by its own leader in July 2016 after an internal dispute, whose former members immediately founded [[house-of-sentinels|House of Sentinels]].{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|July 2016}}{{cite:community-guild-timeline|Jul 2016}} Four months later the same leader proposed reviving it: the proposal was made on 21 November 2016, a placeholder guild was created and a recruitment thread posted, and the original name was then reacquired by negotiation from an outside player who was holding it on an alt account.{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|revival, 21 Nov 2016}} The placeholder was [[foxton-legacy|Foxton Legacy]], created on 24 November 2016 because the Fox name and tag had already been re-registered by outsiders.{{cite:community-guild-timeline|Nov 2016}}

## How guilds actually ended

The capture log records about a hundred and fifty-five thousand territory changes and not one guild's death, because guilds in this history almost never ended by being beaten.{{cite:territory-exchanges|territory_exchanges, full span}}

They were **disbanded from inside**. The [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]], the largest guild of its era, was disbanded by its own leader on 2 July 2016 after an internal dispute.{{cite:how-guilds-ended-discord-private|2 Jul 2016}} They were **deleted by accident**: [[house-of-sentinels|House of Sentinels]] was removed in March 2018 by an administrator inside it, six weeks after being one of [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s principal enemies, and a member noted the loss of some eight hundred from his war count and that this was the second of his former guilds to go that way.{{cite:how-guilds-ended-discord-private|March 2018}} They were **spent**: one guild afforded eleven level-sixty mercenaries for a war and ten level-forties for the next, lost, and stopped.{{cite:guild-finance-and-war-discord-private|15 Feb 2018}} Most often they were **absorbed** — Aphelion into [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]], Peace and Tranquility when its leadership moved on — which was neither defeat nor always decline, but a guild stopping while its people carried on somewhere better resourced.{{cite:how-guilds-ended-discord-private|2018}}

And they were **put to sleep**. On 16 January 2017 one leader told his council that he had placed his older guild, [[holders-of-le|Holders of LE]], into hibernation until the game's guild system was repaired, and had stopped advertising for it; it was neither disbanded nor handed on.{{cite:kingdom-of-foxes-state-council-2016-2017-discord-private|16 Jan 2017}} Waiting for the mechanics was a general reason rather than one man's: clearing out its inactive members in October 2017, the [[emperium-of-wynn|Emperium of Wynn]]'s leadership kept the guilds whose leaders were still present on the ground that they would be worth having if the guild system were fixed.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-membership-2017-discord-private|4 Oct 2017, the reasoning}}

And they were **re-founded**, which from outside looks like absorption and is not. LE Flowers was dissolved on 8 April 2018 and a new guild, [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]], created by the same leadership within eight hours, the officer announcing that they meant to begin again from nothing and build a roster that would play.{{cite:le-flowers-to-diamond-deities-2018-discord-private|8 Apr 2018}} Its alliance twice proposed a merger with an existing member instead and was refused both times, because the new guild already existed.{{cite:le-flowers-to-diamond-deities-2018-discord-private|8 Apr 2018, the merger proposals}}

The distinction was drawn at the time. The alliance's founder, listing his members, gave the count as "37 guilds. 40 if you count guilds such as LEF and DDT to be entirely different ones."{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, Drew1011, 13 Nov 2018}}

And they **ran out of people**. Team United [TUN], founded in the autumn of 2015, reached about eighteen members and then emptied; its founder put the failure down to having recruited only from players he already met in the game rather than advertising for members{{cite:holders-of-le-storytime-2018-discord-private|Team United and the reason for its failure}}, and observed that a player who plays at one time of day exhausts the pool of people he can meet.{{cite:holders-of-le-storytime-2018-discord-private|playing hours and the pool of recruits}} He had passed through two others in the four months before it — one he joined in August 2015 and left or was put out of{{cite:holders-of-le-storytime-2018-discord-private|the first guild, August 2015}}, and a second that decayed after its own founder stopped playing.{{cite:holders-of-le-storytime-2018-discord-private|the second guild, September 2015}} Nothing else was ever written down about the three of them; [[the-rank-and-file|the rank and file]] sets out how that lifecycle looked from inside.

And some endings did not happen. [[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]] announced its disbandment on 4 February 2018 and rejoined the alliance four days later, its territory having been held for it in the meantime by two other guilds.{{cite:coalition-last-fortnight-discord-private|4–8 Feb 2018}} Guilds announced their own endings more often than they reached them. Beyond the guild that declared disbandment and rejoined four days later, members recorded a leader announcing he would give his guild away, a leader offering to hand his position to another so the guild could continue, and a leader who stepped down while his guild went on under a new owner.{{cite:how-guilds-ended-discord-private|announced endings, 2016–2018}}

What ended the Kingdom of Foxes was not written down. The member who told the story three years later did not describe the dispute, having not been there and having only second-hand accounts of it.{{cite:how-guilds-ended-discord-private|2019}}

## Splinters and descent

Several later guilds are described as splinters of the Kingdom of Foxes, more than one of them founded or co-founded by the same person, and two later guilds were separately described by members as having come out of Fox.{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|guild descent discussions}} The descent was disputed as it was being stated: in the same conversation in which two guilds were called Fox's children, another member argued that Fox had itself descended from [[imperial|Imperial]].{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|guild descent discussions}} Nobody settled it. Hall of Fame was founded by a member who had left House of Sentinels, and went on to join the [[coalition|Coalition]].{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|Hall of Fame founding}}

## Subguilds

Subguilds were a standard structure rather than an improvisation. [[necronomicon|Necronomicon]] [Feq] was founded as a subguild of [[kangronomicon|Kangronomicon]] [Fuq] while Kangronomicon was among the most powerful guilds in the game, and [[holders-of-le|Holders of LE]] became a subguild of the same guild in August 2015 — one day after the two established relations, and after staging skirmishes to demonstrate its worth.{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|subguild arrangements, 2015}}{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L96–100, L111–114, composed 19 Jan 2018}} The practice is described further in [[territory-warfare|territory warfare]]. Subjection was still being proposed at the end of the period: the leader of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] said on 3 September 2019 that his guild had come close to becoming a subject guild of [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] about a year or more earlier, and gave no date for it.{{cite:undated-observations-verified-discord-private|3 Sep 2019}}

## Ownership

Guild ownership changed hands as property. Guilds were sold, and were transferred to alt accounts — in one case publicly, through a second identity presented as another person.{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|ownership transfers}}{{cite:espionage-and-infiltration-discord-private|alt-account transfers, 2015–2018}} Holders of LE passed through at least seven owners in three and a half years.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L104–107, composed 19 Jan 2018}}

## Names

Names moved independently of the organisations behind them. The alliance eventually known as [[war-syndicate|War Syndicate]] carried at least three in succession, beginning as The Alliance, becoming United Guilds, and ending as War Syndicate.{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|alliance renaming}} When it fell, its remnant Discord server was renamed rather than abandoned, becoming Illustratus — which subsequently appears as an alliance in its own right and later still as a guild inside [[the-federation|the Federation]], where Illustratus held Nemract in the first territory assignment of February 2018.{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|Illustratus}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018}} A separate grouping formed from the same remnants was tracked by its opponents under a nickname and treated as a second incarnation of the original.{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|successor groupings}}

Mergers ran the other way, producing guilds that inherited neither name. In August 2018 two Federation member guilds dissolved into a single new guild, which then had to apply to the alliance in its own right and met objections that its members were defectors reforming under a new name rather than leaving honestly.{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|merger and application, August 2018}}

One guild named its family after its own past, and the names look backwards rather than tracing a descent. On 21 September 2018 the leader of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] told [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s leadership channel that this was his guild's convention: one sister guild carried a name standing for what was left of the [[war-syndicate|War Syndicate]] alliance, and another was named for an alliance Titans Valor had once belonged to.{{cite:titans-valor-family-of-guilds-2018-discord-private|21 Sep 2018, the naming convention}} A further guild had broken off from the family in the hope that standing alone would improve its chances of admission to the Federation.{{cite:titans-valor-family-of-guilds-2018-discord-private|21 Sep 2018, the guild that split off}} That gives [[illustratus|Illustratus]] a fourth sense on top of the three above — a renamed server, an alliance, a guild, and a backward-looking name given to one of this leader's own guilds — and none of the four is evidence that the thing named was continuous with any other.

The alliance's succession of names was given a second time and came out shorter. On 12 July 2018 the Federation's founder named United Guilds as the first name of the alliance that became War Syndicate, in the course of arguing that War Syndicate had been the better of the two.{{cite:federation-history-contested-2018-discord-private|12 Jul 2018, United Guilds named as the first name}} He was describing that alliance and not his own, and his version leaves out the step recorded above; both are given because nothing settles which is right.

## Two founding dates

Contemporaries gave [[hackforums|HackForums]] two irreconcilable founding dates. One member cited the game's own API to place the guild on 24 December 2014; a separate conversation a year earlier had members putting its founding on 21 February 2017.{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|founding-date discussions}} The API date is the one the guild is otherwise dated from{{cite:community-guild-timeline|Dec 2014}}, and the gap may equally mark a refounding under the same name, which happened often enough in this period to be unremarkable.

## People

Individual careers ran through guilds faster than the guilds themselves changed. One member's account of his own path traces six guilds in about a year, including one he founded, and ends with him left behind when the others merged into an alliance.{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|member accounts}} Grudges travelled with people rather than with banners: a demoted captain who left one guild for another then used the new guild to attack his former guild's territory repeatedly.{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|guild disputes}}

The dominant territorial power of [[early-guild-era|the early guild era]], HackForums kept the same owner in every recovered revision of its recruitment thread from 2016 to 2023: "HackForums [Hax] Lvl. 92 - Created 12/24/2014 / Guild Owner: [[thedarksoul|TheDarkSoul]]"{{cite:thread-109319|post #1}}.

## History

### Ordo Malleus, expulsion and readmission (2015–2016)

HackForums was one of the seven founding guilds of [[ordo-malleus|Ordo Malleus]] on 6 November 2015, TheDarkSoul sitting in its leader chat{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L169–178, composed 19 Jan 2018, re-posted 22 Feb 2018}}, and it was assigned the desert after End of Everything was expelled from the alliance on 1 December 2015{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L238–239, composed 19 Jan 2018, re-posted 22 Feb 2018}}. It was later expelled and then readmitted. [[drew1011|Drew1011]] wrote in 2016 that "Hax was actually kicked out of Ordo"{{cite:thread-143392|post #7, Drew1011, 9 Aug 2016}}, and that "Hax was one of the original guilds, although we kicked them out, and then for some reason, let them back in"{{cite:thread-133666|post #6, Drew1011, 27 Jun 2016}}, giving no date or reason.

The expulsion came in early January 2016, immediately after TheDarkSoul was banned from the forums: Drew1011 raised the ban in the alliance's senior chat, Yamipanda of ToastedPandas moved a vote, and the vote was unanimous or nearly so. The grievances were repeated accidental attacks on allied guilds, and defending recaptured allied territory with full mob defences instead of the light defences the alliance's recapture tactic required.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L333–342, composed 19 Jan 2018, re-posted 26 Feb 2018}} HackForums is absent from Ordo Malleus' 20 February 2016 roster{{cite:thread-109350|post #11, Drew1011, 20 Feb 2016, quoting the opening post}}, and on 11 February 2016 its own opening post listed a different alliance entirely — "Alliances: Astrafterí-affinity"{{cite:thread-109319|post #13, ThePastryMaster, 11 Feb 2016, quoting the opening post}}. In late February 2016 Paladin A'renos re-invited the guild.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L435–436, composed 19 Jan 2018, re-posted 26 Feb 2018}}

### The rise to dominance (2016–2017)

Drew1011, writing in February 2018, gave no dates for the guild's rise: "even my timelines and knowledge of guild history has its limits, and I know no exact dates for any of this. All I know is that Hax began their reign sometime around the summer of 2016."{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L517–518, composed 23 Jan 2018, re-posted 26 Feb 2018}} By his account the guild's freedom of action grew when it moved into the Astrafteri Affinity, because "they no longer had a massive list of allies that they could not attack, and had much more freedom in wars"; when Astrafteri's guilds went inactive and the alliance died, "they stayed in an alliance with [[Libertas]], and divided all of Wynn amongst themselves and a few others such as Blacklisted"; and when those partners in turn went inactive, "Hax was left as the one truly active war guild. With nobody to challenge them, they expanded their horizons and engulfed all of Wynn and Gavel."{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L508–516, composed 23 Jan 2018, re-posted 26 Feb 2018}} He adds that the guild "would occasionally give territories to other guilds such as [[paladins-united|Paladins United]]"{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L517, composed 23 Jan 2018, re-posted 26 Feb 2018}}, and offers a judgement: that HackForums "intended to share the glory with a few allies such as Libertas" and expanded into the vacuum its allies left{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L519–523, composed 23 Jan 2018, re-posted 26 Feb 2018}}.

One element of his sequence is dated earlier than he places it: he puts the move to the Astrafteri Affinity after Ordo Malleus' collapse of 9 March 2016, but the guild's own thread listed Astrafteri as its alliance on 11 February 2016, a month before{{cite:thread-109319|post #13, ThePastryMaster, 11 Feb 2016, quoting the opening post}}. The Hax–Libertas–Blacklisted bloc was described repeatedly at the time. A June 2016 post described a "rule of two" with [[Libertas]]: "This enemy, known only has 'Hack Forums' quickly established their rule through military might... Soon after, another guild, Libertas, joined the surge. The two guilds crushed all opposition and still rule with an iron fist."{{cite:thread-133666|post #1, 26 Jun 2016}} A member of UltimateXeons wrote the next day that "our alliance with Hax and Lir (we are UXs) is gonna let the three guilds keep their territory... without anybody really taking it unless one of the guilds leaves or breaks the alliance"{{cite:thread-133666|post #13, 27 Jun 2016}}. "You do realize Hax has not been a one many army in ages?", a poster objected in August 2016. "They work with Lir and Bla in order to hold territories."{{cite:thread-143392|post #12, 9 Aug 2016}} Blacklisted [BLA] — founded 19 Jan 2016 under NeonRider and Naraka00 — stated the arrangement from the other side in its own recruitment thread, under a heading reading "ALLIANCES AND ALLIES": "We are allied with Libertas (Lir) and Hackforums (Hax)."{{cite:thread-131893|post #1, as revised 15 Sep 2017}} In March 2017 a defender of Hax asked its critics "are Lir, BLA, UXs somehow exempt?"{{cite:thread-179383|post #7, 1 Mar 2017}}.

On 8 June 2016 Hax held 94 territories{{cite:thread-130386|post #1, 8 Jun 2016}}. By 23 September 2016 HackForums held 146 of the map's ~332 territories, nearly two and a half times the next guild (Libertas, at 60), with Hax, Libertas and Blacklisted together on 228{{cite:pre2018-territory-snapshots|pre2018-territory-snapshots.json — Wayback Machine capture of the Wynncraft v1 API guild leaderboard, 23 Sep 2016}}. Its peak ran higher still: a May 2017 forum thread title asked why Hax had "MORE THAN 300 TERRITORIES"{{cite:thread-189768|post #1, thread title, Hide007, 22 May 2017}}, and in 2020 a player put the maximum "closer to 350", "when they owned the whole map"{{cite:thread-265297|post #18, 4 Apr 2020}}.

The bloc appears to have had no name while it existed. A contemporary corrected the UltimateXeons member by calling it "a mutual land agreement" rather than an alliance{{cite:thread-133666|post #16, 28 Jun 2016}}; a Libertas member who asked his own guild owner "the name of our alliance" in August 2016 received no answer anywhere in the thread{{cite:thread-119596-p08|post #152, 9 Aug 2016}}; and no alliance thread was ever posted for the bloc{{cite:forums-wynncraft-com-forums-guilds-72-prefix-id-82-order-post-date-direction-asc|complete census of Alliance-prefixed threads, page 1}}{{cite:forums-wynncraft-com-forums-guilds-72-page-2-prefix-id-82-order-post-date-directi|complete census of Alliance-prefixed threads, page 2}}. The label "Hax & Co" is retrospective: Drew1011 used it in August 2018{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L1086, L1097, composed 30 Aug 2018}}, and the earliest public use is a July 2019 post listing "Federation, Hax & Co, Coalition" among Wynn's mega-alliances{{cite:thread-252647|post #5, 21 Jul 2019}}.

The dominance was resented in public and never checked. Two anti-Hax alliances were posted within three weeks of each other in June 2016. The Banhammer, opened by Mr_Robin_Hood on 8 June, proposed a universal non-aggression bloc on the ground that "Hax currently owns 94 territories / They are too strong for a single guild to fight / and the only way for us to be able to beat them / is if all of the guilds unite!", and listed World of Crafters [WoC], The Wynn Guard [TwG] and MythicZodiac [MZS] as members{{cite:thread-130386|post #1, 8 Jun 2016}}. A Hax-side poster answered it the same day: "I will not stop till every inch of wynncraft is mine or my allies, my allies will attack you or be attacked if they revolt."{{cite:thread-130386|post #5, 8 Jun 2016}} The Separatists, a Star Wars-themed open-enrolment alliance founded by Tapu_Fini on 26 June around the guild The Anarchy, followed{{cite:thread-133666|post #1, 26 Jun 2016}}; its founder conceded that "even if we don't beat them, at least we can be an annoyance"{{cite:thread-133666|post #11, 27 Jun 2016}}. At the end of the month a Hax chief noted in passing that "right now there is 2 anti hax alliances"{{cite:thread-134638|post #13, 30 Jun 2016}}. Neither worked; an UltimateXeons member's verdict on the earlier attempts was "It didn't work, all those guilds no longer have any territory."{{cite:thread-133666|post #10, 27 Jun 2016}}

In March 2017 a thread titled "Haxforums Should Be Limited On Territory Influence" argued that Hax's "taking advantage of the broken guild system has clearly killed the guild system for everyone else"{{cite:thread-179383|post #1, 1 Mar 2017}} and that "they cheat the game with there control and need to be broken up"{{cite:thread-179383|post #5, 1 Mar 2017}}. The thread's respondents rejected a cap almost unanimously, Drew1011 among them: "Although I dislike a single guild or alliance having such a monopoly over the territories, I have never, and will never support the limiting of guild territories... Their success should not be limited just because other people are upset."{{cite:thread-179383|post #6, 1 Mar 2017}} His explanation of the position was that it was an artefact of a broken and unloved system rather than of force: "The reason that Hax is able to hold so much is because, to put it simply, nobody is trying to challenge them... When people begin trying again... it will become very difficult for Hax to maintain control of the 100+ territories they currently own."{{cite:thread-179383|post #6, 1 Mar 2017}} A 2020 retrospective listed the mechanics that had made it possible: "subguilds were heavily abused / there was no cd timer after you took a territory, you could instantly snipe it back / alt warring was legal / wars hardly worked at all so no one was motivated whatsoever"{{cite:thread-263975-p02|post #31, 9 Mar 2020}}

A market in territory grew up around the monopoly. When the guild Skaikrew offered on the forums in January 2017 to buy a territory from HackForums, a HackForums member answered publicly: "We are currently selling territories! Cheap price of 64le per land!"{{cite:thread-176003|posts #1 and #3, 27 Jan 2017}} Others in the thread told the buyer the purchase was worthless because the guild would simply retake the land, and one added that "guild wars are shit anyway, and very broken, so there's no real point anyways" — the period in which the war system barely functioned.{{cite:thread-176003|post #2, Reti, 27 Jan 2017}}

### The Coalition and the wipe (2017-2018)

By the end of 2017 HackForums was a member of the [[Coalition]], the alliance holding full map dominance that winter{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}. Drew1011, who recruited it, records that he approached TheDarkSoul personally in the autumn of 2017 and that the guild was revived for the purpose: TheDarkSoul "said that he was busy and did not know when he would have the time to revive Hax, but that when he did have the time, he would most certainly help us deal with War Syndicate."{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L878, composed 22 Mar 2018}} The guild reappeared on the map that winter.

On 17 Feb 2018 HackForums left the Coalition with four other guilds to form [[Alliance Alliance]], starting the [[coalition-civil-war|Coalition Civil War]]{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}; the community timeline gives the same five departures on the same date{{cite:community-guild-timeline|Feb 2018 entry}}, and Drew1011 dates the split to 16 February, within an hour of the announcement of a proposed division of the province of Wynn{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L1057–1059, composed 11 Jul 2018}}. Two days later the newly formed [[the-federation|Federation]] attacked and [[the-wipe-of-hackforums|wiped Hax to zero]]. The capture log has the guild at 108 territories by midday on 19 Feb 2018 and at none by the evening of the 20th{{cite:territory-exchanges|holdings reconstructed from the capture log, 19-22 Feb 2018 UTC}}; it held none on 22 Feb 2018{{cite:thread-219744|post #1, 22 Feb 2018}}.

The attack came at the end of a four-day campaign that had already taken Llevigar, the swamp and the forests; the decision was Snowythewolf's, of Hall of Fame. Drew1011 wrote: "At the very latest hours of the 19th, Snowy announced it was time to attack Hax. At the time they held 111 territoires. Less than 10 hours later, they had none."{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L1091, composed 30 Aug 2018}} His figure of under ten hours is shorter than the "under 12 hours" of the community timeline image{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, the community guild-timeline image (imgur seklYAQ)}}, and both are estimates. The thread of 22 February reporting the fall noted that "the guilds with the most territories have only 43"{{cite:thread-219744|post #1, 22 Feb 2018}}: guilds nobody was fighting had drifted to the top of the leaderboard in the confusion — Blacklisted among them, which "sat at #1 on the leaderboards for roughly a day"{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L1092, composed 30 Aug 2018}}. It was the end of Hax as a territorial power, and the agent was the Federation rather than the Coalition. A moderator closed the 22 February thread four minutes after it opened, as not relevant{{cite:thread-219744|post #3, 22 Feb 2018}}.

The fall is often placed in 2017 — a participant wrote in 2020 that it was "probably the biggest event that happened in 2017"{{cite:thread-265297|post #16, 4 Apr 2020}} — but it came in February 2018. What belongs to 2017 is the decline, from a reported peak near 350{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, the community guild-timeline image (imgur seklYAQ)}} to the roughly hundred territories Hax still held when the Federation came{{cite:territory-exchanges|holdings reconstructed from the capture log, 19-22 Feb 2018 UTC}}.

### After the fall

The guild endured. By 3 March 2019 it was back at 33 territories, at guild level 82 with the largest roster on the leaderboard (84 members){{cite:pre2018-territory-snapshots|pre2018-territory-snapshots.json — Wayback Machine capture of the Wynncraft v1 API guild leaderboard, 3 Mar 2019}}. In 2021 it joined [[Valhalla]] at its founding{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}, and it was a member of the community alliance [[Hestia]] by 6 Jun 2021, in a six-guild roster posted to the guild megalist{{cite:thread-278112-p07-wb20211128|post #140, 6 Jun 2021}}, remaining through September 2022{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}. Its thread today describes a guild that has given up the thing it was known for: "This is a community guild, we do not war."{{cite:thread-109319|post #1}} The guild still exists{{cite:wynncraft-api|Wynncraft API v3/guild, retrieved 2 Sep 2026}}.

## Money, and the pattern of readmission

The guild's power rested on a treasury it had not earned in the period it mattered. Members traced its reserves to an earlier era when a whole region of the map could not be attacked at all, letting income accumulate uninterrupted long after the conditions that produced it had gone; by March 2018 [[the-federation|the Federation]] was calculating its holdings at roughly thirty million emeralds and concluding that its bloc's combined treasuries outweighed the Federation's own.{{cite:hackforums-guild-profile-discord-private|March 2018}}{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|bank balances, March 2018}} That asymmetry is why the guild kept mattering after each defeat: a guild driven off the map with a full bank could return, and one with an empty bank could not.{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|territory income and defence costs, 2015–2019}} It had suffered an internal collapse at an earlier point. Reviewing it in July 2018, a member put that down to the guild's own people not feeling appreciated — the same explanation he gave in the same conversation for a comparable collapse in [[paladins-united|Paladins United]].{{cite:hackforums-guild-profile-discord-private|member's account, 18 Jul 2018}}

The expulsion from [[ordo-malleus|Ordo Malleus]] in January 2016 followed its leader's removal from the game's forums, which the alliance's leaders — already out of patience with the guild's conduct toward allies — took as the occasion; one of those leaders re-invited it within weeks.{{cite:hackforums-guild-profile-discord-private|early 2016}} The decision caused a secondary dispute inside the alliance, a senior member of another guild arguing publicly that she had not been told of it.{{cite:hackforums-guild-profile-discord-private|early 2016}}

It led the walkout that destroyed the [[coalition|Coalition]] on 16 February 2018, announcing its withdrawal first and criticising the alliance's voting system as weighted toward larger members at the expense of the guilds carrying the fighting; within hours it was attacking its former allies, and within two days the successor alliance had driven it from dozens of territories to none.{{cite:hackforums-guild-profile-discord-private|16–20 Feb 2018}}{{cite:coalition-fracture-2018-discord-private|Coalition general channel, 16 Feb 2018}} In August 2018 four enemy guilds were found to be capturing territories and handing them to it undefended, inflating its holdings without its having to fight; a game moderator ruled the practice not technically against the rules, and the Federation answered with a full mobilisation that cut the holdings back sharply.{{cite:hackforums-guild-profile-discord-private|August 2018}}{{cite:federation-at-its-height-2018-discord-private|August 2018}}

It remained a standing target and was readmitted anyway. The [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]] organised a coordinated multi-guild invasion campaign against it in November 2018, and guilds of a separate alliance declared joint war on it the same month, framing it as an end to its raids on their territory.{{cite:hackforums-guild-profile-discord-private|November 2018}}

The Aesir campaign ran six days. Its leader licensed the pact's guilds on 13 November to attack HackForums wherever and whenever they liked, on the grounds that it was trying to take the entire map and had threatened the pact more than once; on the fourteenth two member guilds scheduled a joint attack twenty-one hours in advance and a third offered to surrender its Molten income for a while to keep the harassment going.{{cite:aesir-and-hackforums-november-2018-discord-private|13–14 Nov 2018}} On the morning of the sixteenth the guild carrying the attacks told the pact's council that it had made its own deal; the pact's leader overruled him, forbade any member guild making terms with HackForums, [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]], Wrath of Poseidon or Achte Shadow without the council, and thirty-six minutes later announced an alliance-wide truce with HackForums.{{cite:aesir-and-hackforums-november-2018-discord-private|06:44 and 07:22 on 16 Nov 2018, export clock}} By the twenty-third the pact was reporting the truce broken, and on the twenty-seventh it placed HackForums on its list of guilds not to be touched, beside [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]], [[imperial|Imperial]], [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] and [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]].{{cite:aesir-and-hackforums-november-2018-discord-private|23 and 27 Nov 2018}} The grounds for the campaign, and the report that the peace had been broken, are the pact's account of the guild. In September 2019 [[terra|Terra]] voted it in eleven to ten, as declared at the close, over explicit internal objection that it had a history of betraying the alliances it joined.{{cite:terra-ballot-record-september-2019-discord-private|readmission motion of 17 Sep 2019 and the reactions still on it at export}}{{cite:hackforums-guild-profile-discord-private|September 2019}}

That was the second ballot, and the two were put under different rules. The first went up on 10 September and lost, nine in favour to nineteen against on the exported tally.{{cite:terra-ballot-record-september-2019-discord-private|the motion of 10 Sep 2019 and its tally}} The readmission motion of the 17th set a simple majority and a day's clock, where the territory map put to the same channel five days earlier had been set to close on two-thirds — a bar eleven of twenty-one would not have cleared.{{cite:terra-ballot-record-september-2019-discord-private|the thresholds stated on the motions of 12 and 17 Sep 2019}} That motion was edited the following day, struck through and marked closed, so its wording is the wording that stood after the close and not the wording the leaders read.{{cite:terra-ballot-record-september-2019-discord-private|the edit of 18 Sep 2019}} Two days after the first vote members were describing it as having lost badly; in that discussion Terra's ambassador argued that the guild had a legacy and would spill blood if it were offered nothing, and set out the counteroffer being prepared — a region nobody had asked for in the map submissions, in place of the area the guild wanted — while others objected that it was a distant ally of the alliance Terra had just beaten and that most people did not want it in.{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|12 Sep 2019}} The revote opened on the 17th on the ground that the outstanding questions of land, military relations and claims had been settled.{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|17 Sep 2019}} The case put for admission was deterrence rather than merit.

Admission was bought with a different piece of ground. The guild's return had been reported to [[terra|Terra]]'s leadership on 8 September as a threat: the ambassador said HackForums was reviving and that he had been warned it meant to attack the jungle, its old claims, to make a statement of return to the defeated alliance.{{cite:terra-hackforums-admission-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 8 Sep 2019}} [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]]'s leader answered in the same channel that the guild would not get the jungle because it had done nothing, that the alliance would reward the guilds that had contributed first and guilds like it could have the leftovers.{{cite:terra-hackforums-admission-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 8 Sep 2019, the doctrine of reward}} When [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] attacked HackForums on 10 September, [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]]'s leader instructed the war channel not to defend it, the guild not being officially in the alliance.{{cite:terra-hackforums-admission-2019-discord-private|war channel, 10 Sep 2019}} At 02:15 on the 14th the same leader told the leadership channel that the negotiation had succeeded: the alliance had offered the Llevigar plains area, the guild had dropped its insistence on the jungle and accepted, it had assembled a war team and could hold territory, and it wished to apply again.{{cite:terra-hackforums-admission-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 02:15 on 14 Sep 2019}} On that basis he proposed a fresh admission vote.{{cite:terra-hackforums-admission-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 14 Sep 2019, the proposal}}

The alliance's standing arrangement with the guild had never been put to a ballot. On the day of the vote the ambassador told the voting-discussion channel that Terra had an active peace deal with HackForums, that breaking it would make Terra the betrayer and that anyone who broke it risked removal from the Discord.{{cite:terra-hackforums-admission-2019-discord-private|voting-discussion channel, 17 Sep 2019}} Two members answered that the deal had never gone to a vote and should not have been made without one; he replied that it had been made to stop members warring the guild and that nobody had opposed it once it was announced.{{cite:terra-hackforums-admission-2019-discord-private|voting-discussion channel, 17 Sep 2019, the objection and the answer}} The opposition's argument was about who the guild's members had been: that the membership of an earlier hostile guild had merged into it, that those members had threatened people, and that most of the support for admission was really support for them.{{cite:terra-hackforums-admission-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 17 Sep 2019, the case against}}

Three weeks after admission the guild's officer told the leadership channel that some of its members were talking about attacking other member guilds on the ground that those guilds were useless, and that he was holding it back.{{cite:terra-hackforums-admission-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 13 Oct 2019}} Terra's discord-management lead called it treason and said the guild would be out if it attacked allies{{cite:terra-hackforums-admission-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 13 Oct 2019, the warning}}, and twenty minutes later pinned a notice that threats of treason would be dealt with urgently and that further instances would have consequences for the guild responsible.{{cite:terra-hackforums-admission-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 13 Oct 2019, the pinned notice}} On 28 October its leader demanded a second vote on its own membership, arguing that the guilds warring hardest were not the ones getting expansions, that his guild kept a monthly war count and had passed seventy-five territories more than once, and that the first vote had not properly gone through.{{cite:terra-hackforums-admission-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 28 Oct 2019}} The war count and the figure of seventy-five are the guild's own claims about itself. The ambassador answered that a majority vote had admitted them and that the earlier vote had been made hastily and without proper precautions, and both sides were told to take it to direct messages.{{cite:terra-hackforums-admission-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 28 Oct 2019, the answer}}

Its claims were being fought over before it was a member at all. On the afternoon of 8 September one Terra leader told the alliance's war channel that HackForums was trying to claim the jungle and was fair game; within the same minute another told the channel not to attack it and that its claims would be discussed with it, and repeated the instruction as the argument went on.{{cite:terra-terms-of-surrender-2019-discord-private|8 Sep 2019}}

By the autumn it was one of Terra's two largest generators of experience, and it used that. Asked on 17 October whether it would let some of its ground go to guilds that needed it, it agreed; eleven days later one of its officers told the leadership channel that he had his guild leader's permission to say that until the guild received the claims it wanted it would stop assisting, and that if it had to wait for the next map revision it would wait.{{cite:terra-october-2019-discord-private|17 and 28 Oct 2019}} Terra's ambassador called it going on strike, said it would damage the guild's chances and complained that no senior member had raised the matter civilly; the officer answered that it was the only way they would get anywhere, and that he had tried.{{cite:terra-october-2019-discord-private|28 Oct 2019}} It left that alliance during a raid in November 2019, declaring neutrality and citing other member guilds' failure to defend it, and by December the alliance that had previously driven it off the map was granting it land again.{{cite:hackforums-guild-profile-discord-private|November–December 2019}}

## After the wipe, measured

The scale of what the [[the-federation|Federation]] took from it in February 2018 shows in what came after. Across the six months from 30 March 2018 the guild made 5,846 captures and suffered 5,849, and its holdings peaked at twenty-four territories — a guild that had held 146 territories in September 2016 and reportedly near 350 at its 2017 peak.{{cite:who-fought-2018-discord-private|HackForums: 5,846 captures made, 5,849 suffered, peak 24, 30 Mar – 8 Oct 2018}}{{cite:pre2018-territory-snapshots|23 Sep 2016}}

## The founding date

Contemporaries gave the guild two irreconcilable founding dates. One member cited the game's own API to place it on 24 December 2014, the date the guild is otherwise dated from; a separate conversation a year earlier had members putting its founding on 21 February 2017.{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|founding-date discussions}}{{cite:community-guild-timeline|Dec 2014}} Both were stated in good faith, and the gap may equally mark a refounding under the same name, which was common enough in this period to pass without comment.{{cite:guild-lineage-and-succession-discord-private|founding-date discussions}} The pattern is described at [[guild-lineage|guild lineage and succession]].

## Alliances

- [[ordo-malleus|Ordo Malleus]] — founder guild, 6 Nov 2015; expelled early Jan 2016 and readmitted late Feb 2016{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L169–178, L333–342, L435–436, composed 19 Jan 2018}}{{cite:thread-109350|post #11, 20 Feb 2016}}{{cite:thread-143392|post #7, 9 Aug 2016}}
