## Legacy
Alongside [[Hestia]] and the [[Empire of Nemract]], Niflheim was one of the few community alliances of the era whose membership is known{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}; beneath the war-alliance churn of 2020–2021 ran a quieter, more stable social layer.

## Formation

On 24 August 2018 the leadership and active members of [[ex-nihilo|Ex Nihilo]] and of [[lunatic|Lunatic]] left both guilds to found Odysseia, and [[the-federation|the Federation]] declared Ex Nihilo out of the alliance the same day.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|24 Aug 2018}}{{cite:lunatic-mox-guild-profile-discord-private|24 Aug 2018}} A participant described the mechanics of the Lunatic side without decoration: nearly every active member was moved across the moment the new guild existed, and the outgoing leader was left with what remained.{{cite:lunatic-mox-guild-profile-discord-private|24 Aug 2018}}

It was admitted to the alliance only after argument, members contending that guilds of either parent's lineage should be treated with caution — and members of the new guild were themselves calling it a second incarnation of one of them within the week.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|August 2018}}{{cite:lunatic-mox-guild-profile-discord-private|24 Aug 2018}}

Members of the Federation read it as something else again. Within an hour of the guild appearing, one of them said in the war channel that this was Vestige uniting into a single guild — Vestige being a bloc inside the alliance that its own members had never defined the same way twice — while others asked whether the new guild was a splinter of Lunatic and when Ex Nihilo's leader had joined it.{{cite:federation-vestige-2018-discord-private|24 Aug 2018}} Three weeks later a guild leader told the leadership channel flatly that Vestige had become the new guild, and nobody in the room disagreed.{{cite:federation-vestige-2018-discord-private|14 Sep 2018}} The bloc and the two July descriptions of it are at [[alliance-governance|alliance governance]].

## Leaving and betraying

By the end of August 2018 the alliance had three guilds on its books that had left it and taken its territory, and had dealt with each differently: [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] voted back in on the 22nd, [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] expelled in March and still the principal enemy, and Odysseia admitted a week after attacking most of the alliance.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|22 Aug – 3 Sep 2018, the three cases}}

On 30 August, with the admission vote running, a leader argued that the guild should be turned down on its first attempt as a matter of course — not for anything it had done since, but because attacking guilds for entertainment and then expecting to be admitted straight away was arrogance. He proposed a procedure rather than a refusal: refuse the first application, watch whether the good behaviour held, and admit it on a second vote.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the proposal}} Others answered that a first-time refusal would be an injustice, and that the guild would still have been in the alliance if it had discussed the merger beforehand.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|30 Aug 2018, the answers}} The motion putting it was never recorded. Leaders counted down the vote's last forty-six minutes on the morning of 31 August, and one of them then gave the result as ten to four.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|30–31 Aug 2018, the vote and the result reported at the time}} The alliance made no announcement of the admission, listing the guild a day later among the week's changes in its weekly bulletin.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/1/2018}}

The question the admission left open was argued out on the morning of 3 September. A leader of Odysseia said the Federation had handled his guild badly and had given it far more abuse than it had given back.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the complaint}} A member guild's leader agreed, apologised for the abuse, and said that any guild should be free to leave without social consequences and that being an enemy of the Federation afterwards was nobody's business but its own.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the concession}} She had compared his guild to BuildCraftia, she said, not to insult it but because its conduct had not matched its stated attitude, and the point was that a guild could not turn on its allies and expect a welcome back.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the comparison}}

He said the comparison was simply wrong, and gave his test: BuildCraftia had still been a member of the alliance when it attacked, had never given formal notice of leaving, and had refused to stop when it was asked.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, his test}} Hers was that his own guild had given no notice either — the two guilds it had emptied were handed on to nobody, no one was told beforehand, and the rest of the alliance was left to clear up. It came down to communication, she said, exactly as the earlier case had.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, her test}} He answered that they had announced something. Neither withdrew, and within four minutes the channel had moved on to the argument about opening the map.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the end of it}}

The two tests are not the same kind of test, and separating them is this article's: his turns on status and notice and would exculpate any guild that resigned before attacking, hers on whether the alliance was told in time to adjust and would condemn both. The alliance never voted on the question and never wrote anything down.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|25 Aug – 3 Sep 2018, no rule agreed}} What it had instead was a precedent, given in one line earlier that morning that nobody picked up: the guild had been let off because nothing of the kind had happened before.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the precedent}}

## Two months

In September 2018 a former member who had been pushed out of the guild joined an enemy guild and leaked its bank information, which the alliance recorded — noting that the information was already known, which made the leak a gesture rather than a loss.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|September 2018}}

At the end of October the guild announced that it was leaving the Federation and quitting the game.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|late October 2018}} Within days the alliance found that the announcement had been the smaller half of it: the guild had quietly placed former members into other Federation guilds, and had been concealing members holding officer rank in guilds outside the alliance while sitting inside it.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|late October 2018}}{{cite:federation-collapse-2018-discord-private|Federation leadership channels, 29–30 Oct 2018}} It then joined the guilds attacking Federation territory.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|late October 2018}}

Its departure was one of the five that emptied the alliance in the fortnight before [[the-federation-dies|it dissolved]] on 10 November 2018.{{cite:federation-collapse-2018-discord-private|Federation leadership channels, 27–30 Oct 2018}}

## One membership, four names

Between June and October 2018 substantially the same players held four guild identities in succession — Hall of Fame's departing captains into Ex Nihilo, Ex Nihilo absorbing Verinian Trials, and Ex Nihilo and Lunatic combining into Odysseia — and the Federation admitted them at every stage and argued about it each time.{{cite:ex-nihilo-odysseia-discord-private|June–October 2018}} A guild that leaves an alliance is a loss it can count. A guild that leaves while its people are already inside the alliance's other guilds was something the Federation had no rule for.

Paladins United [PUN], created on 2 Jun 2017 by EHTYSCYTHE,{{cite:wynncraft-api|Wynncraft API v3/guild, retrieved 2 Sep 2026}}{{cite:thread-246617|post #1, opened 5 Apr 2019, last edited 19 Sep 2022}} belonged to more alliances than nearly any other guild. Its forum thread was opened in April 2019 by Carrie, one of the guild's leading figures through the 2019–2020 alliance wars; Vholtz_ owned the guild at that date, and by 2022 ownership had passed to Auxiliary_ — when is not recorded.{{cite:thread-246617|post #1, opened 5 Apr 2019, last edited 19 Sep 2022}}

## History

### The Coalition and the Federation (2017-2018)

By late 2017 Paladins United was in the [[Coalition]]. On 17 Feb 2018 it left with four other guilds to co-found [[Alliance Alliance]], starting the [[coalition-civil-war|Coalition Civil War]]; when the war ended on 20 Mar 2018 the AA guilds were absorbed into [[the-federation|the Federation]], where PUN served two stints (to June, and from August until [[the-federation-dies|the collapse of 10 Nov 2018]]).{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, the community guild-timeline image (imgur seklYAQ)}}

Paladins United was among the guilds that withdrew from the [[coalition|Coalition]] on the evening of 16 February 2018, leaving within minutes of HackForums; the alliance's own channels count six withdrawals that evening.{{cite:coalition-fracture-2018-discord-private|Coalition general channel, 16 Feb 2018}}

### Leaving and returning (2017–2018)

The guild had collapsed once before the period the surviving channels cover, at a cost in territory. Reviewing it in July 2018, a member put the collapse down to the guild's own people not feeling appreciated — the same explanation he gave in the same conversation for a comparable collapse in [[hackforums|HackForums]].{{cite:paladins-united-guild-profile-discord-private|member's account, 18 Jul 2018}} By September 2017 it had a new leader, had lost most of its previous membership and was being described as largely inactive, with another guild noting it had taken advantage of the situation.{{cite:paladins-united-guild-profile-discord-private|September 2017}} Leadership changed hands again in January 2018, the outgoing leader staying on as co-leader, and the new leader was admitted to the [[coalition|Coalition]]'s private leadership channel later that month.{{cite:paladins-united-guild-profile-discord-private|January 2018}} It enforced alliance loyalty on its own members, expelling one in January 2018 after finding that they held roles in a rival alliance's Discord server.{{cite:paladins-united-guild-profile-discord-private|January 2018}}

Its leader had argued the substantive case before the vote that broke the alliance. In February 2018 he objected to assigning all Wynn-province territory to member guilds rather than leaving it open to whoever claimed it, on the grounds that assignment stifled competition.{{cite:paladins-united-guild-profile-discord-private|February 2018}}{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|assignment debates, 2018}} He lost the argument, the alliance voted for assignment, and a fortnight later the guild left with five others. Whether the second followed from the first was never recorded; the order of events is, and the argument was made in public before any of it happened.

The alliance decided to admit it minutes after refusing to make peace with it. On 5 March 2018 the Federation reduced [[hackforums|HackForums]] to no territory at all, and one minute after the report of it a member turned the alliance onto Paladins United, the last enemy standing — a reflex rather than an order; its leadership then argued whether to destroy the guild or to let its leader keep the contested Corkus region on condition it stayed neutral. The peace was put to a vote and failed.

The argument behind that failure was set out in full. The founder brought the offer to the leaders saying he was not personally a fan of it but that it had to be discussed either way; one leader said he did not really want it and that they could suppress the guild anyway, and another refused outright on the grounds that the terms would leave it far too well placed if it ever turned on the alliance.{{cite:federation-corkus-campaign-march-2018-discord-private|5 Mar 2018, the discussion}} The founder then remembered that a deal of exactly that shape had already destroyed the guild once by stopping it from growing, to the point that when a major game update arrived only one member was warring for it; a leader answered that it had come back when it was given the chance, and that the proposal amounted to the arrangement the [[war-syndicate|War Syndicate]] had held with it. The founder said he had changed his vote, and the same leader observed that there was now no way to reach two thirds.{{cite:federation-corkus-campaign-march-2018-discord-private|5 Mar 2018, the vote}} Whether he offered that history for the deal or against it, he never said. A peace needed a supermajority and did not get one, so the alliance's default was war.

A member had reported the day before that [[hackforums|HackForums]] was no longer the largest territory holder and that Paladins United held eighty-eight territories.{{cite:federation-corkus-campaign-march-2018-discord-private|4 Mar 2018}} That is the size of the guild the alliance had just declined to settle with, and it is a member's count rather than a published figure. In the minutes after it failed, the alliance's leader opened a second vote — on admitting Paladins United outright, in exchange for the same region.{{cite:federation-march-2018-discord-private|5 Mar 2018}} No one in the channel remarked on it. The alliance would not concede Corkus to an enemy and would concede it to a member, and the difference between those two things was a vote taken the same evening.

The readmission a fortnight after the walkout is the best-documented negotiation of the period, and its terms were specific: share the contested Corkus region rather than hold it outright, keep only a minority of it, repair relations with the guild it had fought hardest, admit that guild's observers to its own Discord, and accept council oversight until trust had been rebuilt.{{cite:paladins-united-guild-profile-discord-private|readmission terms, late February 2018}} The alliance voted on 7 March 2018 and carried it under the two-thirds threshold on thirteen votes in favour, two guilds abstaining, and welcomed the guild back the following day, formally ending the war between them.{{cite:paladins-united-guild-profile-discord-private|readmission vote, 7–8 Mar 2018}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|the admission announcement}} The alliance's founder gave a different figure when he announced the result to the leadership channel on the morning of the 8th: fourteen in favour and two abstentions, which he said met two thirds.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|8 Mar 2018, the announcement}} The motion itself, posted to the voting channel on the evening of the 7th, carries fifteen in favour, two against and two abstaining, with nothing to take off it, and the two against went unmentioned in either account.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|multi-option reactions across the fourteen March motions}} The count given in the channel that morning was the contemporaneous one and is the better evidence of the vote as cast.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|the Paladins United motion's tally, 7 Mar 2018}} The result stands on any of the three readings: fourteen was the threshold on the founder's own rule and his own count of twenty-two member guilds, and eleven if the two against are counted back into the total.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|9 Mar 2018, the rule and the electorate}} The motion also carries an edit made twenty-three minutes after it was posted, so its final wording is not certainly the wording the guilds reacted to.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|the edit stamp, 7 Mar 2018}} It had been opened ninety seconds after the alliance closed a failed vote on another applicant, with the same three options on it.{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|7 Mar 2018, the two motions}} Its recorded reasoning was the guild's fighting strength, its low toxicity, and an acceptance that it had left with legitimate grievances.{{cite:paladins-united-guild-profile-discord-private|readmission vote, 7–8 Mar 2018}} Within a day members were telling one another not to attack it, and arguing with it about its reluctance to fight the side it had just stopped belonging to. Within a week it held forty-three territories.{{cite:federation-march-2018-discord-private|8–14 Mar 2018}}

The case became the alliance's general precedent: a guild that leaves and later returns does not reclaim its former territory, because the holdings will have been reassigned in its absence.{{cite:paladins-united-guild-profile-discord-private|readmission precedent, 2018}}{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|admission terms, 2016–2019}} A further readmission vote in August 2018 was contested on the grounds that the original terms should still bind, the alliance's leader arguing they had already been satisfied while another member raised the territorial balance a guild of its size would create.{{cite:paladins-united-guild-profile-discord-private|August 2018}} Corkus itself never stopped being the argument: members at one point counted how many times the region had changed hands between the same two guilds.{{cite:paladins-united-guild-profile-discord-private|Corkus disputes}}{{cite:federation-at-its-height-2018-discord-private|territory disputes, August 2018}}

The motion put to the guild leaders on 22 August argued in the guild's favour that it had left voluntarily rather than in anger, and that in all the time it had been attacking Federation ground it had never made common cause with either of the alliance's enemies.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|22 Aug 2018, the motion}} It carried by fourteen votes to three with one abstention.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|22 Aug 2018, the tally recorded on the motion}} The announcement the next afternoon said the guild had joined the Federation, and was corrected within the hour: "Rejoined*".{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire and Deleted User, 8/23/2018}}

The precedent was contested again four days later. In the early hours of 26 August a member of the guild objected in the leadership channel to a member guild's leader who had been arguing that a guild returning from outside owed the alliance an interval before it could claim what it used to hold. His was not a new guild that had to earn trust, he said: it had been in the Federation before, it had left voluntarily while going inactive rather than inconvenience anyone, and it had warred almost continuously for its allies since coming back.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the objection}} She answered that a good deal had happened while it was away, including the assignment of the ground it wanted to another member and a decision to divide it, and that those things could not be undone because an old member had returned.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the answer}} The question was not trust but respect for what had been settled in the guild's absence. No guild in the alliance had a right to demand territory, she said, only to argue its case from its dedication and standing, and the Federation owed his guild nothing at that moment.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the principle stated}} He answered that his guild had earned it even in the short time since returning.{{cite:federation-leaving-and-attacking-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the reply}}

Five hours later the alliance's founder proposed a mandatory waiting period for refused applicants. He was prompted by a different guild entirely, and nobody in either conversation connected them.{{cite:federation-admissions-rush-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the proposal and its stated reason}} That the alliance spent one morning working in two rooms on the same idea — that a guild coming back from outside owes an interval — is this article's observation.

The captures were logged as they happened, and the count can be given. Corkus is twelve territories rather than one, and between 6 April and 7 October 2018 they passed directly between Paladins United and [[imperial|Imperial]] 1,665 times — 946 of those captures by Paladins United and 719 by Imperial.{{cite:corkus-contested-2018-discord-private|the twelve Corkus territories}}{{cite:corkus-contested-2018-discord-private|direct transfers between Paladins United and Imperial, 6 Apr – 7 Oct 2018: 1,665 (946 to PUN, 719 to Imperial)}} It was not steady attrition: about nine in ten of those transfers fell in August 2018, and on 2 August alone the two guilds took Corkus territory from each other 191 times — twelve territories changing hands roughly sixteen times over in a day.{{cite:corkus-contested-2018-discord-private|monthly distribution and the peak day, Aug 2018: 1,493 of 1,665; 191 on 2 Aug}}

Its departure of 25 June 2018 came in the middle of [[the-federation-june-crisis|the Federation's June crisis]], twenty minutes after the leadership channel had turned to removing inactive members: its leader announced the guild was leaving on the grounds that it did not contribute and had become inactive, and repeated it in a second channel two minutes later.{{cite:federation-25-june-2018-discord-private|25 Jun 2018}}

### Leaving by its own criterion (June 2018)

On 25 June 2018 the Federation's leadership was working through which inactive guilds should be removed from the alliance. Paladins United's leader said that on that subject his own guild was leaving too, because it did not contribute. Members confirmed the same day that it was no longer a member, and the channel records no argument, no vote and no recrimination.{{cite:paladins-united-guild-profile-discord-private|25 Jun 2018}} A guild leader heard a criterion proposed for expelling others, agreed with it, applied it to himself and withdrew.

Its holdings had been falling throughout. The alliance's own published maps give it 22 territories on 10 March 2018, 19 on 24 March — by then Corkus alone — and 4 on 22 April: four fifths gone in six weeks, the sharpest decline in the allocation series.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|3/10/2018, 3/24/2018 and 4/22/2018}} It still held those 4 on 10 May and none at all on 4 June, three weeks before its leader told the leadership channel that the guild did not contribute.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|5/10/2018 and 6/4/2018}} When readmission was argued in August the question was put as one about the alliance rather than the guild — whether a member that goes quiet is an enemy when it comes back — and answered with the observation that it had three members.{{cite:paladins-united-guild-profile-discord-private|August 2018}}

### The Corkus settlement of September 2019

At about a quarter to five in the morning on 8 September 2019 a [[terra|Terra]] leader told the war channel to leave Paladins United alone in Corkus, that its island holdings were open without restriction, and that terms were being discussed.{{cite:terra-terms-of-surrender-2019-discord-private|8 Sep 2019}} A ceasefire here was not a state between two powers but an instruction to the people doing the fighting, drawn across a map by province. One of them said he did not agree and asked why anybody would consent to it, and was told it was politics and that having the guild on side would be useful.{{cite:terra-terms-of-surrender-2019-discord-private|8 Sep 2019}}

The settlement gave up the ocean, the swamp and the Light Isles and kept Corkus.{{cite:terra-victory-september-2019-discord-private|8 Sep 2019}} [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]]'s leader objected that every alliance did this for the guild, that it felt entitled, and that this was why it was so highly levelled, tracing the pattern to the War Syndicate era.{{cite:terra-victory-september-2019-discord-private|September 2019}} He had been in the room in March 2018 when [[the-federation|the Federation]] voted down a peace that would have let the same guild keep the same region, and then admitted it and granted the region anyway.

### 2019: Terra to Artemis

In the [[the-alliance-wars-of-2019|alliance wars of 2019]] Paladins United appears to have fought with [[Terra]] against [[Luna]], staying until 30 Oct 2019 — the day it co-founded [[Artemis]] with [[Imperial]] and Caeruleum Order.{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, the community guild-timeline image (imgur seklYAQ)}} It was in [[Valkyrie]] by 14 Sep 2019,{{cite:wattpad-com-784189853-inside-myself-recent-wynncraft-guild-history|Leonhart_Arkada, 14 Sep 2019}} and in the community alliance [[Hestia]] from around mid-2020;{{cite:thread-278112|post #10, 2 Dec 2020}} its thread listed both until 19 Sep 2022.{{cite:thread-246617|post #1, opened 5 Apr 2019, last edited 19 Sep 2022}}

### The Valkyrie join-date problem

Whether 14 September 2019 marks a join or only a first mention is unclear. [[arkada|Arkada]], writing that day, opens: "The Valkyrie alliance, consisting of Kingdom Foxes, Imperial, Paladins United, and Emorians, have existed for longer than I've played Wynncraft."{{cite:wattpad-com-784189853-inside-myself-recent-wynncraft-guild-history|Leonhart_Arkada, 14 Sep 2019}} He wrote as a hostile observer describing an established, long-standing bloc of four guilds — yet Paladins United and Imperial are recorded entering Valkyrie only in September 2019, after [[Emorians]] had left it in the revolt of late August 2019, so that on those dates the four guilds were never simultaneously members.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} The dates probably mark first mentions rather than joins: Valkyrie's threads had gone quiet after 2017 and no membership list was published between then and the 2020s.{{cite:thread-159734|posts #2 and #7, 15 Oct 2016}} Carrie, writing in March 2020 as "the leader of PUN", placed the guild "in Valk, in a large alliance".{{cite:thread-263975-p03|p3 #41, 9 Mar 2020}}

### Side-switch and the blocs (2020-2023)

By December 2020 PUN had drifted from Artemis to the opposing coalition — it appears in the 18 Dec 2020 anti-Artemis list that became [[Goose]]{{cite:thread-279337|post #1, 18 Dec 2020}} — and stayed until [[the-great-collapse|the Great Collapse]] of 11 May 2021, then joined [[Valhalla]] at its founding and followed it into [[Vanir]].{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} At the start of Season 8 (autumn 2022) it joined the renamed 'Smtn else' — later [[smtn elf]] — alongside [[The Simple Ones]] and TerraLune, remaining until the alliance's quiet end in mid-2023.{{cite:titantimes-github-io-vol6|vol. 6, 12 Oct 2022}}

