## Legacy
smtn elf was the last of the public super-alliances, and its unannounced death is the [[private-diplomacy-era|private-diplomacy era]] in miniature: no roster, no dissolution notice, just silence between two season posts. Its collapse set up the [[Avocado Aquarium]] pact and the wars that followed.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}

## History

### Creation
SOL was created in the post-collapse chaos following [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s fall of 10 Nov 2018{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, the community guild-timeline image (imgur seklYAQ)}}, around December 2018.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}

### The war with [[Luna]]
On 14 Feb 2019, Luna was formed "as an alliance to counter SOL," and the two blocs fought through the spring as part of [[the-alliance-wars-of-2019|the Alliance Wars of 2019]].{{cite:community-guild-timeline}} SOL was still a current bloc in June 2019 — "the current SOL and Luna guilds"{{cite:thread-251543|post #1, Pikacal, 26 Jun 2019}} — and then disappeared as Luna consolidated the map in July. No dissolution was ever recorded.

### Roster
No member list of SOL was ever published. Its six guilds appear to have been [[DiamondDeities]], Achte Shadow, Wrath Of Poseidon, Beyond the Scene, Spectral Cabbage and, from January 2019, Ecliptic Awakening: all fought Luna's founders from Luna's founding onward — Beyond the Scene alone accounting for nearly 4,000 territory exchanges — while showing near-zero fighting among themselves.{{cite:territory-exchanges|territory_exchanges map-data analysis}}{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} DiamondDeities' membership is consistent with the Treaty of Rodoroc — its peace with [[Kingdom Foxes]], effective 2 Mar 2019{{cite:community-guild-timeline}} — and with a late-2018 arrangement to share Molten Heights with Fox.{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, the community guild-timeline image (imgur seklYAQ)}}

## Membership
{{alliance:SOL}}

## Legacy
SOL bridged the Federation's fall and the alliance wars that consumed 2019: it inherited the map's chaos, provoked Luna into existence, and dissolved into obscurity without a recorded ending. Several of its members resurfaced in the autumn blocs — DiamondDeities and Achte Shadow both appear in [[Terra]].{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}

**Tapu_Fini** founded [[the-separatists|The Separatists]] on 26 June 2016, the second of the two anti-[[hackforums|HackForums]] alliances of that month.{{cite:forums-wynncraft-com-forums-guilds-72-prefix-id-82-order-post-date-direction-asc|thread listing: The Separatists, Tapu_Fini, 26 Jun 2016}} At the time he posted under the name Supermariowifi and led The Anarchy, the alliance's founding guild; his forum profile still lists The Anarchy as his guild.{{cite:thread-133666|post #12, Drew1011, 27 Jun 2016, quoting post #11}}{{cite:thread-133666|post #1, 26 Jun 2016}}{{cite:thread-133666|forum profile guild field in the page markup, captured 3 Sep 2026}}

His opening post is the earliest surviving written description of the bloc that held the 2016 map, and casts it as a duumvirate: "This enemy, known only has 'Hack Forums' quickly established their rule through military might... Soon after, another guild, [[libertas|Libertas]], joined the surge. The two guilds crushed all opposition and still rule with an iron fist."{{cite:thread-133666|post #1, 26 Jun 2016}} The whole thread is written in Star Wars references, which he confirmed when a reader objected that separatists forming an alliance made no sense: "Ever seen Star Wars? In star wars the separatists were an alliance of systems that wanted to leave the republic."{{cite:thread-133666|post #11, 27 Jun 2016}}

He was candid about the odds — "Luck will definitely be needed to take down hax lol"{{cite:thread-133666|post #4, 26 Jun 2016}} and "even if we don't beat them, at least we can be an annoyance"{{cite:thread-133666|post #11, 27 Jun 2016}} — and, challenged on whether he had ever attacked the bloc at all, gave the only account of the alliance in action: "I mean lir and hax. And we have. Usually around Ragni. Katoa ranch we tend to attack a lot."{{cite:thread-133666-p02|post #23, 28 Jun 2016}} His last recorded posts, on 1 July 2016, argue for the guild-repair update over new content: "I'd rather have usable content then lots of content."{{cite:thread-133666-p02|post #34, 1 Jul 2016}}

## History

### Formation
Terra formed on 30 Aug 2019 in opposition to [[Luna]], amid the Emorians–Avicia revolt that had broken out the day before{{cite:community-guild-timeline}}; several of its guilds were already fighting Luna from 25 Aug.{{cite:territory-exchanges|territory_exchanges map-data analysis}}

Terra was a merger rather than a fresh founding. After Luna took the map, [[arkada|Arkada]]'s guild [[Titans Valor]] joined **The Ascendancy**, "a small and weak band of guilds that resisted Luna through unsuccessful attacks"; as more guilds tired of Luna's reign, "small alliances similar to The Ascendancy merged together, forming a larger and more organized counter-alliance called Terra", Arkada wrote.{{cite:wattpad-com-784189853-inside-myself-recent-wynncraft-guild-history|“ASSAULTS ON BCR” and “A RAY OF HOPE”, 14 Sep 2019}} By the time of the decisive raid the merged bloc fielded "nearly 15 guilds"{{cite:wattpad-com-784189853-inside-myself-recent-wynncraft-guild-history|“THE WEEKEND RAID”, 14 Sep 2019}}, more than the twelve otherwise recorded{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}; the roster was never published. The Ascendancy outlived Terra as a distinct grouping: its departure collapsed the [[Council of Canyon Kingdoms]] on 23 Nov 2019.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}

What set it off was constitutional rather than military. [[emorians|Emorians]] left [[luna|Luna]] without first consulting the wider [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]] alliance, which its fellow members said breached a rule requiring unanimous agreement before such a move, and Valkyrie had no means of responding because one of its members held permanent and unremovable status under its own rules.{{cite:terra-alliance-2019-discord-private|August 2019}} The coalition that formed in answer assembled quickly and in the open, coordinated by a small group of leaders and drawing in around a dozen guilds within days, its members describing what they were building as a counter mega-alliance.{{cite:terra-alliance-2019-discord-private|founding, late August 2019}} One departing guild asked for its leadership roles to be hidden in the new coalition's Discord, out of concern that the alliance it was leaving had spies inside it.{{cite:terra-alliance-2019-discord-private|August 2019}} Told from the other end six weeks afterwards, the founding had two acts rather than one: a leader of Emorians said in the war channel on 28 October that his guild had left Luna without telling Valkyrie first and had been kicked from Valkyrie for it{{cite:terra-accounting-for-the-war-2019-discord-private|war channel, 16:55 on 28 Oct 2019}}, and that [[the-simple-ones|The Simple Ones]], ThisIsMurica and [[avicia|Avicia]] came with it.{{cite:terra-accounting-for-the-war-2019-discord-private|war channel, 28 Oct 2019, the three guilds}}

### The Luna–Terra war
The war for the map ran 7–19 Sep 2019 and was fought across the whole of it: 12,516 territory exchanges over 364 territories between 6 and 16 Sep alone.{{cite:territory-exchanges|6–16 Sep 2019, UTC; no free-for-all list was in force at the war's start, Terra's being published on 14 September}} The fighting was spread rather than concentrated, which is what separates a war from ordinary churn — the forty busiest territories took 37% of the exchanges in that window against 67% in the last ten days of July, when Luna held everything.{{cite:territory-exchanges|top-40 territory share, 6–16 Sep against 20–30 Jul 2019, UTC}} [[The Simple Ones]] defected from Luna to Terra on 8 Sep, and Terra took map control the same day; [[Kingdom Foxes]] left Luna on the 18th and Luna collapsed.{{cite:community-guild-timeline}} The decisive action was a weekend raid intended only to take [[Imperial]]'s land, which instead "ended in nearly six hours and resulted in the complete wipe of all Luna guilds except Kingdom Foxes", Arkada wrote{{cite:wattpad-com-784189853-inside-myself-recent-wynncraft-guild-history|“THE WEEKEND RAID”, 14 Sep 2019}}, followed on 14 Sep by a Luna counter-raid that Terra repelled.{{cite:wattpad-com-784189853-inside-myself-recent-wynncraft-guild-history|“LUNA'S RETALIATION”, 14 Sep 2019}}

{{map:2019-09-08|The map as Terra took control}}

What triggered it was procedural rather than military, and its members said so by comparison: one guild had left an allied alliance without the consultation its constitution required, where [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]]' own earlier departure had been notified in advance and was held up as how it should be done.{{cite:foxes-imperial-2019-discord-private|August 2019}} The guilds angered by the mismatch left and founded the new alliance.

The coalition's founding purpose was executed in a single operation: a coordinated multi-guild raid on 7 September 2019 wiped its principal target and much of the wider Luna alliance's territory within roughly twelve to sixteen hours, and by the following day Luna's core guilds had been pushed entirely off the map.{{cite:terra-alliance-2019-discord-private|the raid of 7 Sep 2019}} Two things surfaced during the raid itself: [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] negotiated a separate non-aggression deal mid-operation, giving up specific territory claims in exchange for being left alone, and another guild revealed that it had been passing information to the coalition from inside the opposing side.{{cite:terra-alliance-2019-discord-private|the raid of 7 Sep 2019}}{{cite:kingdom-of-foxes-guild-profile-discord-private|7 Sep 2019}}

### Dispersal and fall
A week after victory, on 15 Sep 2019, Terra's core — Kingdom Foxes, [[DiamondDeities]], Titans Valor, The Simple Ones, [[Fantasy]], Phantom Hearts and Empire of Sindria — founded the Council of Canyon Kingdoms.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} [[Paladins United]] departed at [[Artemis]]'s founding on 30 Oct.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} Terra itself fell on 2 Nov 2019, seven weeks after taking the map; on 10 Nov its fragments launched one final raid on the newborn Artemis.{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, the community guild-timeline image (imgur seklYAQ)}}

The alliance had begun losing members within eleven days of its victory. On 18 September two guilds left together to go neutral, giving as their reason a dislike of what the alliance had become now that it was the dominant bloc, and by the 24th its own members were debating openly whether the coalition still had a purpose — one arguing that its only plan had ever been to demonstrate that the dominant alliance could be beaten.{{cite:terra-alliance-2019-discord-private|September 2019}} Further guilds left through October and November, several of them to a neutral canyon grouping that had originally been created as a joke.{{cite:terra-alliance-2019-discord-private|autumn 2019}}

October took four of them by four different routes. On the 2nd TheNoLifes went into hibernation until the game's next major update{{cite:terra-october-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 2 Oct 2019}}, and My Wynn Academia announced its departure on the ground of its own inactivity.{{cite:terra-october-2019-discord-private|2 Oct 2019}} Asked on the 17th what had become of X Hunters, Terra's ambassador said the guild had simply fallen apart internally, and that its lands had been a hindrance because they were basically never in their owner's hands.{{cite:terra-october-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 17 Oct 2019}} Asked on the 20th why [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] was attacking [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]], he answered that there had been no official announcement but that DiamondDeities' leader had told him directly the guild was merging its members into Titans Valor.{{cite:terra-october-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 20 Oct 2019}}

Redistribution was by request rather than by rule, and it worked once. During a map update on 17 October the ambassador reported that the alliance's two largest generators of experience — [[emorians|Emorians]] and [[hackforums|HackForums]] — had been asked whether they would let some of their ground go to guilds that needed it, and that both had agreed.{{cite:terra-october-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 17 Oct 2019}} Within a few messages the channel was proposing what else could be taken from one of them.{{cite:terra-october-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 17 Oct 2019}}

Eleven days later that guild withheld its fighting. A HackForums officer told the leadership channel he had his guild leader's permission to say that until the guild received the claims it wanted it would stop assisting, and that it would wait for the next map revision if it had to.{{cite:terra-october-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 28 Oct 2019}} The ambassador called it going on strike, said it would damage the guild's chances, and complained that no senior member had raised the matter civilly{{cite:terra-october-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 28 Oct 2019}}; the officer answered that it was the only way they would get anywhere, and that he had tried.{{cite:terra-october-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 28 Oct 2019}}

The alliance had admitted [[hackforums|HackForums]] on 17 September 2019, eleven to ten as the diplomat declared it closed at the twenty-four hours, over explicit internal objection that the guild had a history of betraying the alliances it joined.{{cite:terra-ballot-record-september-2019-discord-private|readmission motion of 17 Sep 2019 and the reactions still on it at export}} The exported tally reads twelve to eight: the yes side matches the declared count once the seed comes out, and the no side is two lower, so two opposing reactions came off in the years afterwards.{{cite:terra-ballot-record-september-2019-discord-private|declared count and exported tally compared}}{{cite:hackforums-guild-profile-discord-private|September 2019}} A motion of the 10th had refused it nine to nineteen; the readmission was put under a stated simple majority, where the map of five days earlier had been set to close on two-thirds.{{cite:terra-ballot-record-september-2019-discord-private|the motions of 10, 12 and 17 Sep 2019 and the thresholds stated on them}}

Its membership came apart during the Artemis raid itself. Two member guilds, HackForums among them, declared neutrality mid-battle on 2 November, each citing the alliance's failure to defend itself, and Terra lost two significant territories to the attackers; one of those declarations was made without the declaring guild's own leader knowing, and he attempted to reverse it afterwards.{{cite:terra-alliance-2019-discord-private|2 Nov 2019}} On 10 November the alliance's founding guild left to join [[artemis|Artemis]] — the alliance it had been founded to oppose, under its new name — on the same day as another member.{{cite:terra-alliance-2019-discord-private|10 Nov 2019}} A proposal was made that month to rebrand Terra back to the name of its predecessor alliance, on the argument that it had been beset by leadership conflict since its founding.{{cite:terra-alliance-2019-discord-private|November 2019}}

The alliance was formally dissolved and its Discord archived on 30 December 2019, four months after it was founded, after its co-manager proposed disbanding on the grounds that the remaining active guilds were ready to leave anyway.{{cite:terra-alliance-2019-discord-private|30 Dec 2019}} In a closing retrospective the alliance's ambassador described Terra as having formed somewhat accidentally, held dominant control of the map for a period after defeating its rival, and then lost members steadily until nothing was left.{{cite:terra-alliance-2019-discord-private|closing retrospective, December 2019}}

## Before the raid

The campaign itself had been written out and put to the guild leaders on 2 September, and is at [[terra-the-war-plan-2019|Terra's written war plan]].{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|2 Sep 2019, the plan posted to the voting channel}} Its first rules were made before the raid. Four days out the alliance ruled that nothing would be allocated until the war was won. On 3 September 2019 a member guild pressed the leadership channel repeatedly for a large share of the areas it had asked for, and the ambassador answered that quarrelling over land was more than the alliance could bear, that everyone would gain from the operation, and that negotiation came afterwards.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 3 Sep 2019}} A leader then told the channel that claims could be argued once Luna had been destroyed, and pinned the message so that it stood as the alliance's position.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 3 Sep 2019}} [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]]'s leader reached the same rule independently that day, telling the channel that ground not yet won could not be assigned{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 3 Sep 2019}}, and TheNoLifes' leader argued for it against his own claim to some two dozen territories in the Sky Islands.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 3 Sep 2019}} The rule lasted until the morning after the victory, when the leadership was registering territorial preferences while two guilds were still being negotiated with{{cite:terra-victory-september-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 8 Sep 2019}} and a draft split of the conquered ground had been assembled by a few leaders ([[the-terra-settlement|the Terra settlement]]).{{cite:terra-victory-september-2019-discord-private|September 2019}}

The first command rule came out of an unauthorised attack. On 2 September an attack was launched from a member guild against [[the-simple-ones|The Simple Ones]], which the coalition was treating as a possible ally; the war planner demanded to know who was responsible and was told that it had been a joke arranged in a voice call with the target's own side, and, separately, that they had been building their war count.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|2 Sep 2019}} He answered that a possible ally's claims were not where a war count is built and that the alliance might have lost the war over it.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|2 Sep 2019}} Members observed that the opposing alliance did not recognise the coalition as an alliance at all, taking it for one man's project or a faction of [[emorians|Emorians]], so there was no way to disown the attack.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|2 Sep 2019}} On 4 September [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]]' leader, who was directing the war planning, told the leadership channel that from then on he was to be told before anyone made a significant move against an enemy's claims.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 4 Sep 2019}} Nobody in the later message refers back to the earlier one; reading the rule as an answer to the attack is this article's.

Admission on the eve of the raid was refused for its duration and replaced with a battlefield test. On 6 September a member reported that Mystic Woods wished to join and asked whether he could at least give them access to the general channel; the ambassador answered that guilds should not be brought in without a vote or a consultation, and gave his reason from institutional memory — during the [[confederation|Confederation]] a novice guild had been recruited and had turned out to belong to somebody on the enemy's side.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|general channel, 6 Sep 2019}} A member argued that the applicant should stay outside until the raid was over, the plans being live.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|general channel, 6 Sep 2019}} TheNoLifes' leader proposed the terms adopted: the guild would be asked, as the raid opened, to attack [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]], and if it could distract Fox even slightly it would be let in.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|general channel, 6 Sep 2019}} Mystic Woods took no territory from Kingdom Foxes during the raid, though an attack that is repelled moves none; Kingdom Foxes had taken four from it late on the 6th.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges between Mystic Woods and Kingdom Foxes, 6–9 Sep 2019, UTC; no free-for-all list was published for the window, so nothing is netted out}} On the evening of the 7th it took forty-six territories from [[paladins-united|Paladins United]], a Luna guild.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures with Mystic Woods as attacker and Paladins United as defender, 7 Sep 2019 from 17:55 UTC; same window, nothing netted out}} Its admission carried in the voting channel on 7 September, the day the raid opened.{{cite:terra-ballot-record-september-2019-discord-private|the admission motions of September 2019}}

One member reserved an enemy guild out of the war on the strength of a peace of its own. On 3 September Titans Valor's leader told Terra's leadership that his guild had drained [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] heavily, logging some two thousand wars against it over a short period without defending any, and the war planner thanked him for it as useful preparation.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 3 Sep 2019}} He then said that Titans Valor was barred for the time being from attacking BuildCraftia, the two having lately come to a resolution ending a large war on terms his guild was satisfied with.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 3 Sep 2019}} He added that he doubted [[imperial|Imperial]] could be driven off the map at all, that he was well disposed towards Kingdom Foxes, and that he was not hostile to Paladins United.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 3 Sep 2019}} He was in an alliance whose stated purpose was to drive Imperial off the map. His guild had made the same move in [[the-federation|the Federation]] in April 2018, declaring neutrality on its Senate's behalf while remaining a nominal member.{{cite:titans-valor-guild-profile-discord-private|April 2018}}

An approach from the enemy's side was refused as bait. On 30 August a member of the planning channel reported that the leader of a guild still counted as [[luna|Luna]]'s had sent members over and had let him watch the Luna leaders' chat over a screen share.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|planning channel, 30 Aug 2019}} The channel's answer was that the man belonged to Luna, was assisting both sides at once and was unpredictable, and that the plans should be withheld from him even if his members were accepted.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|planning channel, 30 Aug 2019}} The member who had been given the access agreed, said it might be a trap, reported that there had been nothing of value in it, and asked why he in particular had been approached.{{cite:terra-before-the-raid-2019-discord-private|planning channel, 30 Aug 2019}}

## Organization

Terra wrote down more procedure in a fortnight than [[the-federation|the Federation]] adopted in eight months. It formalised territorial claims by having each member guild's leadership submit the areas it wanted through a shared form, as the first step in reconciling overlapping requests before anything went to a vote.{{cite:terra-institutions-2019-discord-private|September 2019}} A body its members called the Divine Council settled territorial questions between full votes, approving at least one interim arrangement that let a guild keep a claim until a competing claim elsewhere was resolved.{{cite:terra-institutions-2019-discord-private|autumn 2019}} It had an expulsion procedure and used it. The war planner warned the leadership on 12 September that a further unexplained strike against an ally or a named protected target would bring a motion, that it would be the fifth such incident, and that the alliance had been issuing warnings instead.{{cite:terra-ballot-record-september-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 12 Sep 2019}} The motion went up the following evening, giving toxicity and direct attacks on allies as its grounds, and carried nineteen to four on the exported tally — eighteen to three once the reactions the mover placed on both options are set aside.{{cite:terra-ballot-record-september-2019-discord-private|the expulsion motion of 13 Sep 2019 and its tally}} Early on the 14th the alliance's discord-management lead reported the vote successful and ready to act on, and by late morning another leader was speaking of Azeron [Zer] as already removed.{{cite:terra-ballot-record-september-2019-discord-private|14 Sep 2019, the managers' channel and the leadership channel}} Neither announcement channel carried a notice of it.{{cite:terra-ballot-record-september-2019-discord-private|no expulsion notice in either announcement channel, 13–15 Sep 2019}}

Intelligence work was an ordinary function and was discussed in the open. In September 2019 the leadership obtained and worked out enemy communications showing the opposing alliance preparing a large coordinated raid timed for daytime{{cite:terra-institutions-2019-discord-private|September 2019}}, and told guild leaders to ready their war teams quietly rather than let on what they knew.{{cite:terra-institutions-2019-discord-private|September 2019}} On 13 September a member guild's leader told the leaders' channel that he had written a genuine application to join [[imperial|Imperial]] in order to see that guild's community from the inside, and asked what they thought{{cite:terra-institutions-2019-discord-private|leaders' channel, 13 Sep 2019}}; the first question back was who would run his own guild if it succeeded.{{cite:terra-institutions-2019-discord-private|leaders' channel, 13 Sep 2019}}

The name was chosen for its meaning. A jocular proposal on 9 September to adopt one in the pattern of the [[confederation|Confederation]]'s{{cite:terra-institutions-2019-discord-private|9 Sep 2019}} drew a serious answer: a leader said he preferred Terra, because it meant earth and he wanted a name that carried a second meaning.{{cite:terra-institutions-2019-discord-private|9 Sep 2019}}

How the alliance gave orders, what happened when they were disobeyed, and the graded alarm it built in October are treated in [[terra-command-and-discipline|command and discipline in Terra]].

## Why the raid worked

Three days afterwards, Titans Valor's leader told the leadership channel that [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] had honourably stopped aiding Imperial, and that this was part of why the coalition had got such quick results.{{cite:terra-institutions-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 10 Sep 2019}} Another leader corrected him: Fox had stopped because its entire war team had gone on strike, its leader having said that anybody in the guild who helped Imperial would be doing it alone.{{cite:terra-institutions-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 10 Sep 2019}} Members who had not been at the raid asked to hear the story.{{cite:terra-institutions-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 10 Sep 2019}}

The largest guild in the war made its decisive contribution by a refusal of its own fighters that its allies' leadership had not known about. Its leaders had suspended help to their allies once before, in July 2018, as a decision taken at the top.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|July 2018}}

## The settlement

What Terra did with the map it had won is treated in [[the-terra-settlement|the Terra settlement]].

## Membership
Terra's founding roster was never recorded{{cite:community-guild-timeline}}; only The Simple Ones' membership is directly documented — it joined hours after leaving Luna. Eleven of the twelve guilds recorded as members attacked Luna's guilds between 133 and 704 times in the fortnight from the alliance's formation to the eve of its first free-for-all declaration; the twelfth, Phantom Hearts, attacked them 92 times.{{cite:territory-exchanges|attacks by each guild recorded as a Terra member on Luna's guilds, 30 Aug – 13 Sep 2019 UTC, The Simple Ones excluded from the defending side as the guild that crossed over: TheNoLifes 704, IceBlue Team 508, Lunatic 445, Paladins United 412, Achte Shadow 296, FinalFront 289, Titans Valor 214, Fantasy 206, BlueStoneGroup 199, The Simple Ones 167, DiamondDeities 133, Phantom Hearts 92. No alliance had a free-for-all list in force between the Federation's declaration of 12 Oct 2018 and Terra's of 14 Sep 2019, so for this window the raw count is also the contested count}}

{{alliance:Terra}}

## Legacy
Terra ended the last period of single-alliance map control in 2019 and seeded what came next: its core formed the Council of Canyon Kingdoms, and Paladins United left it to co-found Artemis, the alliance that would anchor the following era.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}

[[terra|Terra]] wrote down a claims process and a voting procedure within a fortnight of taking the map ([[the-terra-settlement|the Terra settlement]]), but it had no written code of membership, and when a member guild asked in September what rule stood in the way of what it wanted the answer it got was procedure.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 14 Sep 2019}}

## Argument at the top

The alliance's leadership was quarrelling before the raid it had been formed to mount. On 4 September 2019 a dispute in the leadership channel over war strategy turned into an accusation that the ambassador had spent two years saying how easily the alliances he faced could be beaten, treating every good day of warring as a great victory and underestimating his enemies throughout.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 4 Sep 2019}} He told his accuser to stop and left the Discord.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 4 Sep 2019, the walkout}} Within three minutes the war planner was asking for him to be brought back, saying leaders could not be allowed to leave over a debate and that they debated to improve the plan rather than to insult each other{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 4 Sep 2019, the war planner's appeal}}; about seven minutes later he told the channel that the ambassador was not leaving the alliance and was clearing his head.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 4 Sep 2019, the return}}

## The leak

The alliance's draft territory map reached the enemy on the day it was drawn. On 8 September a member told the leadership channel that a captain in [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] had obtained it, and posted the link he had been sent.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 8 Sep 2019}} The ambassador replied that the map was still very tentative and the alliance might not claim as much as it had drawn{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 8 Sep 2019, the tentative map}}, that Azeron's leader had put it in his own guild's announcement channel and the enemy had probably taken it from there, and that one more security breach would be that leader's last chance.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 8 Sep 2019, the route and the warning}} Terra would now face propaganda that it meant to control the whole map, he added.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 8 Sep 2019, the propaganda}} That same morning he had objected in the same channel to a territory map existing at all, on the ground that the alliance had not held the map from the defeated alliance for a week.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 8 Sep 2019, the objection to a map}}

A second leak was punished in six minutes. On 14 September at 10:11 a member told the leadership channel that a named account was passing material into the enemy alliance's chat and that it belonged to ThisIsMurica; at 10:14 the Discord owner said what had gone out was the accurate map in another format, and another leader judged that the leak would have no effect.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 10:11-10:14 on 14 Sep 2019}} At 10:17 the owner said the account was already banned.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 10:17 on 14 Sep 2019}} The guild's leader was spoken to directly about it at 10:55.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 10:55 on 14 Sep 2019}}

## The expulsion

The case against Azeron was made in the voting-discussion channel on the evening its motion opened. At 17:32 on 13 September the Discord owner muted the guild's leader after repeated argument over the new territory allocation, saying he had been told four times to take it to private messages and had only escalated, and that he could return when calm; the leader left the Discord instead.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|voting-discussion channel, 17:32 on 13 Sep 2019}} At 19:55 the ambassador set out three grounds for a kick: the walkout after a temporary mute together with threats of violent action against Emor Sanguinis, X Hunters and [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]]; the leader's general conduct; and the suspected leaking.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|voting-discussion channel, 19:55 on 13 Sep 2019}} He added that the guild's land was being reserved for Spectral Cabbage, which was due to be admitted{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|voting-discussion channel, 13 Sep 2019, the reserved land}}, and that the leader had threatened treason rather than submit a map through the alliance's mapmaking process.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|voting-discussion channel, 13 Sep 2019, the threat of treason}} The ground was allocated before the motion closed.

The day after the vote ThisIsMurica's leader asked the leadership channel repeatedly to invite the expelled leader back and give him his own guild's roles.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 14 Sep 2019, the request}} IceBlue Team's leader answered that the alliance had removed the man's guild because of him, that he had threatened Terra guilds including IceBlue Team with attack unless given the territory he wanted, and that the Discord managers would discuss the case first.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 14 Sep 2019, the refusal}} The requesting leader's answer was that there was no rule against it.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 14 Sep 2019, no rule against it}}

The expelled leader then fought the alliance for at least a fortnight. On 22 September a member reported him among the guilds attacking, and IceBlue Team's leader confirmed the expulsion{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|war channel, 22 Sep 2019}}; on the 27th the ambassador identified him warring alongside BuildCraftia against Achte Shadow in Corkus.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|27 Sep 2019}}

## An hour for claiming

The reclaim of the conquered map was run to a clock, and membership was made to depend on it. On 14 September the war planner told the leadership channel that claiming had not started, that a member guild's early claiming counted as a direct attack, and that a guild allied to it was not permitted to feed it territory.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 14 Sep 2019, the order}} At 07:09 he told that guild's leader that disobeying would put his guild's standing within the alliance at risk and that there was to be no claiming until noon Eastern.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 07:09 on 14 Sep 2019}} The leader disputed his authority to speak to his guild's politics at all{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 14 Sep 2019, the challenge}}, a member of that guild argued that a fixed hour was unfair to its European members{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 14 Sep 2019, the objection about time zones}}, and IceBlue Team's leader told the channel nine minutes later to take complaints to the Discord managers rather than act alone.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 14 Sep 2019, the intervention}} At 07:37 he said the assigned territory could be claimed in ninety minutes and that the finalised map and time would be posted within ten{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 07:37 on 14 Sep 2019}}; at 09:00 local a member counted the channel down from ten seconds.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 09:00 on 14 Sep 2019}} The map was still being finalised ninety minutes before the hour it took effect.

Mutual aid was made conditional in public the same day. At 10:55, with Corkus under attack, the ambassador called for all hands in the war channel and named a member guild with seven members online; its leader answered sarcastically that they did not help.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|war channel, 10:55 on 14 Sep 2019}} The ambassador replied that a guild which would not give help should not ask for it when its own claims were attacked, and that reclaiming could wait because the alliance was in a crisis.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|war channel, 14 Sep 2019, the condition}} The guild's leader said they were reclaiming and had been disrupted repeatedly by the game.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|war channel, 14 Sep 2019, the answer}} Six hours later the same leader recorded that his guild had put a four-man war team on the enemy, and IceBlue Team's leader thanked him for it.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|war channel, evening of 14 Sep 2019}}

## A graded alarm

The only instrument any of these alliances had for summoning its members was a Discord mention, and by late September Terra had several hands on it. On the evening of 26 September two allied guilds spent about ten minutes arguing over which had priority on a single war tag.{{cite:terra-war-tag-escalation-2019-discord-private|26 Sep 2019, the dispute}} At 17:01 the ambassador told the advisors' channel that the moderators were at that moment setting up a tag urgency system to decide which guild had priority when more than one wanted the same target, and that the squabble had been excessive.{{cite:terra-war-tag-escalation-2019-discord-private|advisors' channel, 17:01 on 26 Sep 2019}} At 15:01 the following day, in the middle of a defence, he said he would complete the tiered scheme himself and that it was happening that day.{{cite:terra-war-tag-escalation-2019-discord-private|15:01 on 27 Sep 2019}}

What came out of it had two levels, a lower advisory and a higher warning, and a number attached to the second.{{cite:terra-war-tag-escalation-2019-discord-private|war channel, late Sep - Oct 2019}} On 4 October at 15:38 a member asked whether the higher alarm should be pulled and answered himself two minutes later: the enemy was not at twenty but at seventeen.{{cite:terra-war-tag-escalation-2019-discord-private|war channel, 15:38-15:40 on 4 Oct 2019}} On 8 October the ambassador used the advisory level at 13:20, when an enemy guild appeared in Corkus in strength, and the warning level at 13:35, with the enemy alliance past twenty territories and Corkus going badly.{{cite:terra-war-tag-escalation-2019-discord-private|war channel, 13:20 and 13:35 on 8 Oct 2019}} Those times are the export's clock of UTC-07:00. At 20:20 UTC that day [[imperial|Imperial]] and [[paladins-united|Paladins United]], the two enemy guilds still holding ground, held seventeen territories between them, and at 20:35 UTC twenty; by 22:00 UTC they held two.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Imperial and Paladins United holdings from the last exchange on each territory at 20:20, 20:35 and 22:00 on 8 Oct 2019, UTC; holdings include the free-for-all ground declared 14 Sep 2019 as amended, whose names are held outside this repository}} Identifying the alliance's count with those two guilds is this article's; the roster behind it was never written down, and the count of 4 October cannot be reproduced from the log.

The cost of a general alarm was understood by the people who pulled it. Opening the counter-raid at 04:38 on 28 September, a member addressed the whole membership to say that the defeated alliance was raiding heavily and would probably continue through the day, that all hands were needed, and that he was sorry to set off everyone sensitive to mentions.{{cite:terra-war-tag-escalation-2019-discord-private|04:38 on 28 Sep 2019}}

## Resignations

The alliance closed down a member's territorial demand by moderation rather than by decision. Through 15 September [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]]'s leader pressed the leadership channel for his guild and IceBlue Team to take the Corkus region jointly, offering other regions in compensation{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 15 Sep 2019}}; Achte Shadow's leader said he would vote against any such motion, that Corkus was poor ground for training new players and that taking it would bring unnecessary conflict into the alliance.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 15 Sep 2019, the objection}} The ambassador told him twice to take it out of the public channels, said that begging, pleading and threatening for a territory was the surest way never to be given it in a map change{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 15 Sep 2019, the allocation principle}}, and announced that any further complaint about Corkus after that day would be deleted.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 15 Sep 2019, the deletion notice}}

On 21 October at 11:49 MasterWynnLords' leader told the leadership channel that another member was now leader of his guild, that its Discord had been passed to him, and that he was leaving both his guild and Terra's Discord, the alliance's discord-management lead having caused him more stress than everything else in the game and the server combined.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 11:49 on 21 Oct 2019}} The lead answered that the resigning leader caused most of the disruption himself.{{cite:terra-command-and-discipline-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 21 Oct 2019, the answer}}

[[Terra]] formed on 30 Aug 2019 in opposition to [[Luna]] and took the map from it on 8 Sep, after The Simple Ones' defection ([[Luna-Terra war]]){{cite:community-guild-timeline|30 Aug and 8 Sep 2019 entries}}. Its own end began almost immediately.

## The dispersal

Victory scattered the bloc in stages. On 15 Sep 2019 its core — [[Kingdom Foxes]], [[DiamondDeities]], [[Titans Valor]], [[The Simple Ones]], [[Fantasy]], Phantom Hearts and Empire of Sindria — left to found the [[Council of Canyon Kingdoms]]{{cite:community-guild-timeline|15 Sep 2019 entry}}. Paladins United departed on 30 Oct to co-found [[Artemis]]{{cite:community-guild-timeline|30 Oct 2019 entry}}. What remained was a rump: chronicle stints place Lunatic, IceBlue Team, Achte Shadow and FinalFront in Terra to the start of November{{cite:territory-exchanges|the rump roster's activity windows, autumn 2019}}.

The community guild-timeline records the end simply: "November 2nd 2019 Terra 'falls'" — the quotation marks are in the original{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, the community guild-timeline image}}, and no fuller dissolution statement survives.

## Five hours on 2 November

Terra was not defeated. It was attacked, and came apart from the inside while the attack was going on.

The warning went to the guild leaders at nine minutes past eight in the morning of 2 November 2019, on the export's clock of UTC-07:00: a large raid was coming from [[luna|Luna]] and guilds should prepare.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 2 Nov 2019}} The instruction attached to it was about a neutral rather than about the attackers — if [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] attacked any member even once outside free-for-all ground, the leadership was to be told at once.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 2 Nov 2019}} Six weeks earlier Terra had agreed complete and enduring neutrality with that guild in both directions.{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|17 Sep 2019}} The test was workable because the alliance kept a live list of open ground, eighteen territories declared on 14 September and maintained into December.{{cite:terra-alliance-announcements-discord-private|14 Sep – 11 Dec 2019}}

Within half an hour it was academic. Fox had left the neutral canyon coalition before attacking, which put its canyon holdings — some forty territories, by the alliance's own count — outside any restraint, and Terra's discord-management lead confirmed it to the guild leaders and added that they ought to have destroyed the guild when they had the chance.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 2 Nov 2019}} A leader answered that the alliance's survival mattered more than the ground on offer.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 2 Nov 2019}} [[lunatic|Lunatic]] left the canyon bloc about eighty minutes later.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 2 Nov 2019}}

Three member guilds left inside five hours. At 14:28 [[shadowfall|ShadowFall]]'s leader told the guild leaders that he did not see every guild contributing, that the alliance was growing weaker by the day, that this was not somewhere his guild wished to remain, and that it was neutral with immediate effect; he apologised for deciding it during a raid.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|guild-leaders channel, 2 Nov 2019}} At 14:56 [[avicia|Avicia]]'s founder said his guild was weighing its options and would be neutral for the raid's duration.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|guild-leaders channel, 2 Nov 2019}} At about 15:11 a leader asked whether [[hackforums|HackForums]] had gone, and by that evening it was confirmed that it had.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|2 Nov 2019}} Each gave the same reason: the alliance could not defend itself.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|2 Nov 2019}} At about 14:55, between the first two, a leader looking for help ran through what was left aloud and found two leaders who would not be moved and [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]] too weak.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|2 Nov 2019}}

What left with HackForums was smaller than the alliance believed. A member of [[emorians|Emorians]] told the channel that evening that the guild had taken more than fifty territories out with it, comparing the departure to those of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] and DiamondDeities earlier in the autumn.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|2 Nov 2019}} HackForums held 67 territories at the start of 1 November and 18 at the end of the 2nd; two days later it held 52.{{cite:territory-exchanges|HackForums holdings reconstructed from the last exchange on each territory at 00:00 on 1, 3 and 5 Nov 2019, UTC; holdings counts include the eighteen free-for-all territories declared 14 Sep 2019, whose names are held outside this repository}} It was stripped during the raid and rebuilt afterwards rather than leaving intact. The same day took Emorians from 36 territories to four and [[imperial|Imperial]] from three to sixteen.{{cite:territory-exchanges|same reconstruction, 1 and 3 Nov 2019, UTC}}

What came the other way was stranger. [[blacklisted|Blacklisted]] had been on Terra's enemy list until 11 October, when the alliance voted through a neutrality deal with it on the argument that it removed the strongest enemy the alliance had left.{{cite:terra-october-2019-discord-private|voting channel, 10 and 11 Oct 2019}} Three weeks later it was in the field for Terra, in a grouping of four reported to the alliance's war advisory on the morning of the raid as assisting against Fox and Luna.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|war advisory, 2 Nov 2019}} Two of the other three cannot be identified from what the alliance wrote down.

## The tidying up

A member guild left on 3 November and the ambassador said it had never done anything for them.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|3 Nov 2019}} Challenged on the remark the next day, he said the guild had never fought for the alliance, that one of its people had been a spy while inside, and that in his view it had been a security risk.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|4 Nov 2019}} Asked why it had then been allowed to remain, the discord-management lead answered that the decision had been his: he had kept the guild in place as one more block against Kingdom Foxes dragging Terra into a war with the canyon coalition, that he would sooner leave a guild in place than expel a canyon guild over a dislike, and preferred to retain a guild until it had been shown to be a problem.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|4 Nov 2019}} Membership, on that reasoning, was a buffer rather than a reward for contribution. The spy allegation was made after the guild had gone and was never tested.

By late November the neutral bloc that had structured the autumn no longer existed. On 10 November the ambassador told the alliance that ShadowFall had ended its neutrality with [[artemis|Artemis]] and was not joining an alliance.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|10 Nov 2019}} On 27 November, explaining why he no longer cared what became of DiamondDeities' remaining ground, he said that as soon as the canyon coalition had been wound up DiamondDeities had tried to get into Artemis, and that several people thought it unfit to hold that ground in any case.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|27 Nov 2019}} The same evening the leadership was arguing whether Titans Valor had any standing to declare further free-for-all territory while it held ground the alliance thought should be released back to it.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|27 Nov 2019}}

What occupied the ground through those months is treated in [[the-map-refilled|The map refilled]].

## The last raid

On 10 Nov 2019 Terra's fragments launched one final raid on the newborn Artemis, eleven days old. It was the bloc's last recorded act{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, the community guild-timeline image}}; its guilds then dispersed into the winter's realignments.

{{map:2019-11-02|The map when Terra fell}}

## A campaign written down before it was fought

Late on 2 September 2019 the coalition's war planner, the leader of [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]], posted a written plan to the guild leaders in [[terra|Terra]]'s voting channel, described it as a rough version but at least written down, asked the alliance to vote on it and invited suggestions.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|2 Sep 2019, the plan posted to the voting channel}} It set out opening attacks in preparation, the wipe of [[imperial|Imperial]] on the coming Saturday, and Imperial's claim held until the opposing alliance's overall leader came with a proposal — pushing on until Imperial's allies intervened with offers of their own.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|2 Sep 2019, the opening phases}} A ceasefire would then return part of Imperial's claim in exchange for immunity from attack anywhere on the map, with the rest divided evenly so that no member guild would feel short-changed.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|2 Sep 2019, the ceasefire clause}} Truces with [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] and [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] would bar attacks on each other's claims, after which the remaining guilds of the opposing alliance would be eliminated one at a time, on the reasoning that its collapse would follow from the quarrels that produced.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|2 Sep 2019, the truces and the sequence}}

The end state was written as plainly as the operation: alliance with Fox and Paladins United, no alliance with Imperial but the ceasefire kept at the smallest claim the coalition could give it, and decent claims for the two new friends chosen precisely so that they would have something to lose and stay loyal.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|2 Sep 2019, the end state}} The document is written throughout in the first person singular, and taking it as the alliance's and one guild leader's at once is this article's. Two leadership-channel remarks the following day called parts of it ambitious and insisted that the date and the scheme as first drawn be kept to, which shows it was treated as the operative document rather than that every leader agreed with it.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|3 Sep 2019, the leadership channel on the plan}}

## Ratified twenty-one to one

The exported tally is twenty-one in favour and one against, and the single mark against was the mover's own, on the option opposite the one his plan asked for.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|the ratification tally, 2 Sep 2019}} Nothing comes off it. What comes off a tally of this period is an account sitting on every option a motion offered, which was a placeholder holding the marks so that others could react; a mark on one option is a vote, whoever placed it, and the mover of a motion is a voter.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|placeholder accounts and movers' marks, March 2018}} An exported tally is in any case the reactions still attached to a message years afterwards rather than the vote as cast, so the direction and the margin carry and the exact figure does not.{{cite:terra-ballot-record-september-2019-discord-private|exported tallies against declared counts, September 2019}}

## The vote that nearly cancelled the raid

On the morning of 6 September a leader opened a three-option motion in the voting channel because the plan's author would be away the next day: attack as planned on the 7th, postpone to the Sunday, or postpone a week.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|6 Sep 2019, the three options}} The leadership channel turned on the motion rather than on the question. One member said flatly that the attack had to happen the next day or he was leaving.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|6 Sep 2019, the ultimatum}} Terra's ambassador argued that the alliance could not postpone, that a postponement had been one of the failures that brought down the predecessor alliance's Discord, that a member guild would probably be lost if it happened again, and that starting a vote capable of moving an event the whole alliance had planned for, without consulting anyone first, was not one person's decision to take.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|6 Sep 2019, the ambassador's objection}} The alliance's discord-management lead and another leader objected to the motion's drafting, saying that a mover should stay neutral or give a point for and against each option.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|6 Sep 2019, the drafting objection}}

The count moved while the argument ran. A leader reported that the standing tally now favoured keeping the attack; the ambassador answered that it had not favoured it before the discussion started.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|6 Sep 2019, the tally reported during the argument}} The exported tally is ten for attacking as planned, nine for postponing a week and four for the Sunday, with one account holding a reaction on all three options; taking that account out of all three leaves nine, eight and three.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|the tally of 6 Sep 2019 and the account on all three options}} The contemporaneous report is the better evidence of where the vote stood at any moment, and the exported figures are the end state.{{cite:terra-ballot-record-september-2019-discord-private|exported tallies against declared counts, September 2019}}

The war planner closed the argument by saying the vote would be nullified, and the problem it had been called about — that nobody would be commanding the opening hours — was solved instead by another leader offering to command them and hand over.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|6 Sep 2019, the nullification and the handover}} No formal closure or annulment was ever entered against the motion; what shows it was void is the war planner saying it would be and the raid going ahead on the original date.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|the motion's want of a recorded closure}} The command arrangements made the same day are at [[terra-command-and-discipline|command and discipline in Terra]].

## Eight people and a truce

The plan's first diplomatic clause was executed during the raid, and not by the guild leaders who had ratified it. Late on 7 September 2019, with the attack under way, the Discord managers polled themselves in their own channel on a non-aggression agreement with Kingdom Foxes.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|7 Sep 2019, the managers' poll}} The count was taken by asking the managers one by one and reached five of eight in about four minutes.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|7 Sep 2019, five of eight in four minutes}} The figure of eight managers is the mover's own, given in the channel at the time.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|7 Sep 2019, the mover's count of the managers}} When Terra's ambassador raised the question of safeguards against a mistake, the mover answered that they could not agree to putting it to the guild leaders because that would take too long; reading the refusal as a refusal to route the agreement through the leaders as well is this article's, the exchange being compressed.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|7 Sep 2019, the safeguards question and the answer}}

The agreement was announced to the alliance nine minutes after the count closed: a two-day non-aggression to let Fox consider leaving the opposing alliance and making a deal, stating openly that it had been agreed by majority vote in the managers' channel, and that attacking Fox would mean expulsion.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|7 Sep 2019, the announcement}} A second announcement a minute later set out the terms on both sides: Terra and its subguilds to leave every Fox claim alone, Fox not to aid Imperial with its main guild or any subguild, and neither side's members to move into other guilds in order to get round the agreement.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|7 Sep 2019, the terms}}

That last provision was in force ten days before the treaty that repeated it. The reciprocal ban on sending members into subguilds or other guilds in order to attack the other side's claims was in the announced terms of 7 September, before the long-term neutrality treaty with Fox of the 17th carried the same clause.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|the anti-bypass clause, 7 and 17 Sep 2019}}{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|the treaty of 17 Sep 2019}} Between the two the truce was extended: on the morning of 10 September a leader told the guild leaders it now ran until further notice.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|10 Sep 2019, the extension}}

## Half of the settlement

The Fox half of the plan ran to schedule — truce during the raid, extended on the 10th, made permanent on the 17th — but as neutrality rather than the alliance the plan had envisaged.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|the Fox settlement, 7–17 Sep 2019}}{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|the terms of the treaty of 17 Sep 2019}} The Paladins United half began the same way: early on 8 September the war planner told the war channel to leave that guild's Corkus territory alone while a possible ceasefire was discussed, and it kept Corkus.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|8 Sep 2019, the Corkus instruction}} On 10 September the alliance told its members that it was no longer at non-aggression with Paladins United, that the guild had refused every offer including one asking only for two named territories to be made free-for-all, and that it was now to be treated as an enemy.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|10 Sep 2019, the announcement of enmity}}

The Imperial clause was never executed. No ceasefire with Imperial was announced in Terra's announcement channels, and none was proposed in the leadership or war channels for the rest of September.{{cite:terra-written-plan-and-the-raid-2019-discord-private|no Imperial ceasefire, September 2019}} What the alliance did settle instead is at [[the-terra-settlement|the Terra settlement]].

Wynncraft's provinces are divided into territories that guilds capture and hold. Territory warfare has existed since [[the-guild-update|the Guild Update]] of December 2014{{cite:wynncraft-wiki-gg-wiki-guilds|Official Wynncraft Wiki, 'Guilds'}}, in two mechanical epochs.

## The two war systems

In the original system (2014–2021), wars spawned waves of mobs on dedicated war servers and territories generated XP for their holders{{cite:thread-277744|post #1, 'Guilds & Wars' sections, 23 Nov 2020}}. Version 1.20 'Gavel Reborn' (20 January 2021){{cite:thread-281822|post #1, 20 Jan 2021}} rebuilt it: attacking now costs guild emeralds and is resolved by defeating the territory's war tower; territories produce resources moved along trade routes, can be taxed (including a separate ally tax), and support a headquarters whose strength scales with connected holdings{{cite:thread-277744|post #1, 'Guilds & Wars' sections, 23 Nov 2020}}{{cite:wynncraft-wiki-gg-wiki-guild-war|Economy and Guild Headquarters sections; ally tax rate in the attack-cost formula}}. Patch 1.20.3 (5 July 2021) added [[guild-seasons|guild seasons]] — Season Rating earned by holding and attacking territories, with resets and rewards every season{{cite:thread-292974|Wynncraft forums thread 292974, '1.20.3 Full Changelog', 24 Jun 2021}}{{cite:wynncraft-wiki-gg-wiki-guild-seasons|Season Rating formulas and season reset}} — which turned holding land into a scheduled competition.

![In-game screenshot of a standard guild war arena, the instanced combat space a player enters when attacking a territory's war tower under the post-1.20 system.](/images/chronicles/media/territory-warfare-c603561e.webp)

## The territory economy, 2015–2019

Under the original system a guild's holdings and its treasury were the same quantity measured at different moments: territories generated experience and currency, and defending them cost currency.{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|territory income and defence costs, 2015–2019}} Ground was not interchangeable — one alliance leader objected to any allocation scheme that handed out a particular high-value area at all, because its experience yield dwarfed everything around it.{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|territory values, 2018}}

Because defence cost money, wars were won by bankrupting the enemy rather than by beating them in the field. A written strategy document circulated inside [[the-federation|the Federation]] in February 2018 proposed concentrating the alliance's attacks on one enemy guild to drain its bank rather than spreading pressure across the front{{cite:coalition-fracture-2018-discord-private|strategy document, 18 Feb 2018}}, and a campaign in March 2019 was scored by its own participants in emeralds removed from the target — roughly a million over four days.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|war-record estimate, 27 Mar 2019}} Guilds died this way: participants recorded guilds going bankrupt after successive wars on shrinking defence budgets, one of them giving up the fight and becoming the first of its grouping knocked out.{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|bankruptcies, 2018}}

Bank balances were therefore intelligence. Federation members in March 2018 calculated that a single rival guild held roughly thirty million emeralds and that the opposing bloc's combined treasuries outweighed their own.{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|bank balances and the leak, March 2018}} Wealth was also inherited: two guilds' outsized reserves were traced to an earlier period when a whole region could not be attacked at all, letting income accumulate untouched long enough to matter for years afterwards.{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|wealth disparities}}

The mechanics intervened directly at both ends of the period. The release of the Gavel province on 21 December 2015 locked every territory against attack for a fortnight, freezing ownership at the moment of release and handing a durable advantage to whichever guild happened to hold the best ground when the lock came down.{{cite:ordo-malleus-and-the-first-alliances-discord-private|Gavel release, 21 Dec 2015}}{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|Gavel release, December 2015}} A game update on 1–2 March 2018 rewrote guild warfare and broke it in the process. New defensive units could chain-heal one another, making some territories effectively impossible to take, and high-level defenders became much cheaper; members reported territories that could not be captured at all and money spent on defenders that never spawned.{{cite:war-update-march-2018-discord-private|1–2 Mar 2018}}{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|the March 2018 update}} It fell hardest on small guilds, whose ability to defend anything or take ground alone it crippled, and the opposing bloc launched a broad offensive on the day it landed, fighting from behind the new defences.{{cite:war-update-march-2018-discord-private|March 2018}}{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 17, composed 30 Aug 2018}} The changes had been consulted on and tried out first. On 4 February 2018 the developer taking suggestions on them did so in the [[coalition|Coalition]]'s leadership channel, was told that most of the game's active guild owners were already in that room, and was offered the alliance's own guild-leader rank while he was there.{{cite:war-system-test-february-2018-discord-private|4 Feb 2018}} [[war-system-test-february-2018|A public test on 8 February]] registered captures in the live game almost at once and set off a dispute inside the alliance over whether its members could take one another's ground during it.{{cite:war-system-test-february-2018-discord-private|8 Feb 2018}}

The developer who wrote that update held membership in a guild that was fighting under it, on the opposing side to the alliance whose channels record the period — a fact nobody at the time disputed.{{cite:war-update-march-2018-discord-private|March 2018}} His answer to the objection was to make his membership rotate: he announced that he would move between guilds to demonstrate impartiality, and left the guild he was in for one of the alliance's own, spending about a week in each.{{cite:war-update-march-2018-discord-private|March 2018}} Members disagreed at the time about why he had left the first guild, and nothing settles it; the rotation is the reading he gave himself. Unusually expensive defences were treated as evidence of cheating: two guilds were accused in January 2018 of funding defences with duplicated currency, one of them because it was defending nearly every territory with top-tier mobs.{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|accusations, January 2018}}

Two tactics followed from the economics. Concentrated attack — several guilds striking one target the moment it took territory — was a recognised manoeuvre with its own name among those on the receiving end, and was the mechanism behind [[alovilatious-treaty|the multi-guild targeting arrangements of early 2018]].{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|concentrated attack, 2018}} And guilds that stopped fighting lost their holdings, sometimes by explicit policy: one guild's leadership stripped territory from around three allied guilds for not warring actively, and the Federation later ran a standing rule opening the assigned land of any guild below a territory threshold to attack by its own allies.{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|inactivity and territory, 2018}}{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|the thirty-territory rule, 2018}} Whether territory should be assigned by the alliance at all, or left open to whoever claimed it first, was the central policy question of the era; it is described at [[alliance-governance|alliance governance]].

## Reading the exchange record

The territory_exchanges log has run continuously since 3 January 2018{{cite:territory-exchanges|continuous log from 3 Jan 2018}}. Raw exchange volume is not evidence of war: two phenomena generate enormous exchange counts in peacetime.

- **FFA hotspots** — clusters that churn perpetually regardless of politics, such as Detlas, the Hive, Lava Lake, Factory Entrance and the Emerald Trail, joined in 2020–21 by the Silent Expanse{{cite:chronicle-map-methodology|FFA hotspot list: Detlas, Hive, Lava Lake, Factory Entrance, Emerald Trail, Silent Expanse}}. Fighting concentrated on these territories is noise, not campaign{{cite:wynncraft-wiki-gg-wiki-guild-war|Glossary: 'free for alls are not officially recognised'}}.
- **Ally territory-trading** — from the 1.20 era, allied guilds mass-trade territories for XP and SR, producing near-symmetric exchange counts (Avicia and Emorians once traded roughly 5,000 captures each way while allied){{cite:chronicle-map-methodology|ally territory-trading: Avicia-Emorians ~5k each way, 76% on FFA/Silent Expanse, near-perfect symmetry}}.

![Annotated territory map of the Jungle FFA introduced in guild Season 17, published in Titan Times vol. 13 (22 March 2024): map squares labelled with the holding guild's tag - TAq, ANO, BFS, AVO - and numbered in attack order.](/images/chronicles/media/territory-warfare-1c6832fa.webp){{cite:titantimes-github-io-vol13|vol. 13, 22 Mar 2024}}

The feed those figures were first read off had one author. Of the 155,531 messages in the capture channel, 155,530 were one bot account's and one was a human post.{{cite:capture-feed-provenance-2018-discord-private|the capture channel's authorship, 155,530 of 155,531 messages in the truncated copy}} That account also posted in [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s own general and help-and-trade channels under the same account id — an identification by id, not by display name, which a bot can change and two bots can share.{{cite:capture-feed-provenance-2018-discord-private|the account-id join between the capture channel and the Federation's channels}} On the evening of 23 July 2018 the alliance's founder asked its Discord managers to shut three Wynncraft bots out of the private channels, and a minute later gave his reason: that bot belonged to a member of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] and had been used to spy on the [[coalition|Coalition]].{{cite:capture-feed-provenance-2018-discord-private|23 Jul 2018, the request and the reason given}} It is his account, and nothing established that any spying happened; Titans Valor had left the alliance the day before, and whether that bore on his raising it went unrecorded.{{cite:capture-feed-provenance-2018-discord-private|23 Jul 2018, the guild named and its departure}}

Less follows from that than it appears. A transition log records ground changing hands on a map anybody could watch, so it carried nothing privileged and there was no evident reason to falsify it.{{cite:capture-feed-provenance-2018-discord-private|what the single authorship does and does not bear on}} The figures on this page come from territory_exchanges, a separate table of 3.57 million rows running from 3 January 2018 and collected by a different route.{{cite:territory-exchanges|3.57M rows from 3 Jan 2018}} The two are not the same record, and neither is complete. Counted hour by hour over the period they share, 136 of the table's rows fall before the feed posted anything at all and are no disagreement; across the rest, the table holds 814 exchanges the feed does not and the feed holds 185 the table does not.{{cite:territory-exchanges|30 Mar – 8 Oct 2018 UTC, hourly: 155,521 in the table against 154,756 in the feed}} So about a thousand exchanges sit in one record and not the other, which the difference between the two totals — 765 — conceals by netting the two directions against each other.

Each record can be tested against itself, without reference to the other. Every transition names the guild that lost the ground and the guild that took it, so for any one territory the entries form a chain: the loser of each should be the taker of the one before. Where it is not, a transition happened that was never written down.{{cite:capture-feed-provenance-2018-discord-private|the chain test and what it measures}} Both records break. The feed breaks 256 times in 155,498 entries and the table 278 times in 156,605 — 0.165 per cent against 0.178.{{cite:territory-exchanges|chain breaks by month, 30 Mar – 8 Oct 2018}}{{cite:capture-feed-provenance-2018-discord-private|chain breaks in the capture feed}} Both figures are floors: two losses in a row that return a territory to the guild that held it before are invisible to the test, so neither is the error rate of anything.

They fail at different times, which matters more than the rates. The table breaks most in July and August, at 0.26 and 0.22 per cent, and steadies through the autumn; the feed is at 0.06 per cent in May and 0.29 in September, worsening until it stops altogether in October.{{cite:territory-exchanges|chain breaks by month, 30 Mar – 8 Oct 2018}}{{cite:capture-feed-provenance-2018-discord-private|chain breaks by month in the capture feed}} So neither can be used to check the other in a month when its own rate is high, and a figure from either is worth least exactly where that record is weakest. An earlier version of this passage said the two agreed row for row; that had been checked over a fortnight and does not survive six months. No figure anywhere in this corpus should be read as confirmed by the agreement of two sources when it rests on one, and a difference between two totals should not be read as the amount they disagree by. What remains is that the instrument had a keeper, the keeper belonged to a guild it was recording, and nobody kept a second log; treating that last as worth saying is this article's.{{cite:capture-feed-provenance-2018-discord-private|the single author of the capture record}}

### What six months of war did to the map

Every capture in the game between 30 March and 8 October 2018 was logged as it happened, and the figures below count all of them. That window belongs to the count, not to the log: the log had been running since 3 January 2018 and 394 distinct territories changed hands somewhere in the year, 291,637 times in all.{{cite:territory-exchanges|distinct territories with at least one exchange and total exchanges, 3 Jan – 31 Dec 2018 UTC: 394 territories, 291,637 exchanges}} Both of its edges are artefacts. The count begins on 30 March because that is where a bot started writing, and it ends on 8 October at the byte where the file recording it was cut off mid-structure, the export that produced it having failed partway through.{{cite:who-fought-2018-discord-private|the truncated capture feed, 30 Mar – 8 Oct 2018}} Nothing happened on either date, and the war does not stop at the end of the window. Inside the window the war ran over 385 territories, and they changed hands 155,498 times: an average of 404 captures per territory in a little over six months, with a mean of under ten hours between one capture of a territory and the next.{{cite:who-fought-2018-discord-private|captures, 30 Mar – 8 Oct 2018: 155,498 across 385 territories; mean interval 0.41 days}}

Almost nothing came of it, and the balance of captures is not what shows it. A capture made and a capture suffered are one event counted from its two ends, so over a window of months the two totals cannot differ by more than the map holds: a guild's entire net movement is bounded by the four hundred-odd squares in play, against captures running into five figures. Across the whole log the two figures diverge by a median of 0.50 per cent over thirty-day windows and 0.27 per cent over ninety-day windows, whoever is winning.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures made against captures suffered, ten guilds, all windows 2018–2026 UTC: median divergence 3.05% at one day, 1.03% at seven, 0.50% at thirty, 0.27% at ninety}} What the six months did leave is the distance between the most ground a guild ever held at once and the little it needed to take to get there.{{cite:who-fought-2018-discord-private|captures made and peak holdings by guild, 30 Mar – 8 Oct 2018}}

| Guild | Captures made | Peak held |
|---|---|---|
| [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] | 19,062 | 145 |
| [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] | 11,270 | 26 |
| [[imperial|Imperial]] | 7,960 | 84 |
| [[emorians|Emorians]] | 7,448 | 80 |
| [[lunatic|Lunatic]] | 5,998 | 64 |
| [[hackforums|HackForums]] | 5,846 | 24 |
| [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]] | 4,859 | 85 |
| [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] | 4,722 | 38 |
| [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]] | 1,943 | 50 |

The pattern holds at every scale. August 2018 was the heaviest month of the war — 41,920 captures, more than a quarter of the seven-month total, in four weeks — and the [[the-federation|Federation]]'s fourteen member guilds ended it seventeen territories ahead between all of them.{{cite:federation-august-2018-discord-private|August 2018: 41,920 captures; Federation guilds +23,624 / −23,607}}

Ground taken was ground that came back, usually within a day. The alliances made and broken over territory in this period, the guilds that went bankrupt defending it and the treasuries emptied to hold it are all described elsewhere on this wiki; the map they were fighting over ended roughly where it began.

### Two kinds of strong

Capture volume and territory held are close to unrelated, so a guild called strong in the channels of the period may mean either.{{cite:who-fought-2018-discord-private|captures against peak holdings, 30 Mar – 8 Oct 2018}} Kingdom Foxes did the fighting: 19,062 captures, about one in eight of every capture recorded in the game and some seventy per cent more than the next guild, peaking at 145 territories.{{cite:who-fought-2018-discord-private|Kingdom Foxes: 19,062 of 155,498 captures (12.3%), peak 145}} [[fantasy|Fantasy]] held ground instead, reaching a peak of 101 territories on 4,051 captures — barely a fifth as much fighting.{{cite:who-fought-2018-discord-private|Fantasy: 4,051 captures, peak 101}} Imperial and Sins of Seedia peaked within five territories of each other having done very different amounts of work to get there.{{cite:who-fought-2018-discord-private|Imperial 7,960 captures peak 84; Sins of Seedia 4,859 captures peak 85}}

Three cautions travel with these figures. A capture was not always a fight: territories could be handed over by arrangement, undefended, and in August 2018 four guilds were found doing exactly that for the benefit of a fifth, while inside the alliance members argued in the same week that leaving ground undefended for an ally to retake was legitimate.{{cite:federation-august-2018-discord-private|August 2018}} The practice was called out because it was exceptional, but a capture total for any guild engaged in it overstates the fighting behind it. Peak holdings are running totals from 30 March 2018, the first day of this count rather than of the log, so holdings a guild had built up earlier in the year do not enter them and its early peaks are floors; 37,916 exchanges across 384 territories had already been logged before that date.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges and distinct territories before 30 Mar 2018 UTC: 37,916 across 384 territories}} And the two logs end on different dates: captures run to 8 October 2018, while the log of which players fought under which guild stops on 30 July, so a guild's player count and its capture count describe different periods.{{cite:who-fought-2018-discord-private|the two halves of the record: captures to 8 Oct 2018, player-guild attribution to 30 Jul 2018}}

### The most fought-over ground

Lava Lake changed hands 8,592 times in the period — more than one capture an hour sustained across six months, and better than one in twenty of all the fighting anywhere in the game. Detlas follows at 5,201 and the Temple of Legends at 3,399, while some canyon territories moved fewer than a dozen times in the whole period.{{cite:who-fought-2018-discord-private|most-captured territories, 30 Mar – 8 Oct 2018: Lava Lake 8,592, Detlas 5,201, Temple of Legends 3,399}} Members of the period describe the Molten Heights, where Lava Lake sits, as the most valuable ground in the game for the experience it produced.{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|territory values, 2018}}

### Farming captures

One pattern in the capture figures went unremarked at the time. Between July and October 2018 a guild called HardcoreGamerzThree made 5,188 captures — 302 in July, 1,782 in August, 2,441 in September and 663 in October — while its own holdings never exceeded sixteen territories, taking them overwhelmingly from [[the-federation|Federation]] guilds and 1,207 from Kingdom Foxes alone.{{cite:who-fought-2018-discord-private|HardcoreGamerzThree: 5,188 captures Jul–Oct 2018, peak holding 16}} Capturing constantly while holding almost nothing is the signature of a guild pursuing the act of capture rather than the ground, and it happened as the Federation came apart, against the guilds coming apart, in the months its leadership channels were full of members accusing each other of failing to defend anything. That reading is an inference from the shape of the figures; the figures themselves are exact.

### What a contested region looked like

Territory names describe clusters rather than single squares, and the difference decides how a region could be fought over. Corkus was twelve territories in 2018 — a city, a castle, a mine, forests north and south, a mountain, countryside, outskirts, an abandoned tower, docks and a sea port — which is why two guilds could each be described as holding it, and why sharing it was a term guilds could negotiate.{{cite:corkus-contested-2018-discord-private|the twelve Corkus territories}} Between 30 March and 8 October 2018 Corkus land changed hands 5,927 times, more than half of it between [[imperial|Imperial]] and [[paladins-united|Paladins United]], who took it from one another 1,665 times.{{cite:corkus-contested-2018-discord-private|Corkus captures, 30 Mar – 8 Oct 2018: 5,927 total; 1,665 between Imperial and PUN}} The fighting came in bursts rather than steadily: about nine in ten of those transfers fell in August 2018, and on 2 August the two guilds took Corkus territory from each other 191 times in a single day.{{cite:corkus-contested-2018-discord-private|monthly distribution and the peak day, Aug 2018: 1,493 of 1,665; 191 on 2 Aug}}

## Dispersion, stated as doctrine

Asked in the war channel on 3 March 2018 to concentrate on defeating [[house-of-sentinels|House of Sentinels]], [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s founder refused: not one guild only, he said — pick a region and hold it.{{cite:federation-corkus-campaign-march-2018-discord-private|3 Mar 2018}} Pressed that evening on why his own guild was not joining in, he gave the reasoning. All of them going for the same target would only make the work harder; the object was to keep every guild in the enemy alliance busy so that none could go to another's aid, and his own guild was tying one of them up to that end.{{cite:federation-corkus-campaign-march-2018-discord-private|3 Mar 2018}}

That is not working together, the man arguing with him said. He was told he had it wrong.{{cite:federation-corkus-campaign-march-2018-discord-private|3 Mar 2018}} It is the clearest statement of military doctrine anywhere in this record, and it was stated twice and defended under challenge rather than assumed.

## Wars were won by outspending

Defence quality was bought, so a guild's bank was its life expectancy. Explaining on 15 February 2018 what had become of a guild that had been attacking them, a Kingdom of Foxes war leader said it was completely broke, that it had afforded eleven level-sixty mercenaries for one war and ten level-forties for the next, that it lost that one, and that it gave up.{{cite:guild-finance-and-war-discord-private|15 Feb 2018}} There is no fighting in the account.

Territory was accordingly income. On 21 January 2018 the [[coalition|Coalition]]'s leader described a member guild left near broke after one of its chiefs emptied its bank, and the alliance granting it the Jungle territory to refinance it — a territory as a bailout.{{cite:guild-finance-and-war-discord-private|21 Jan 2018}} An expensive defence was in turn a claim about a guild's finances, which is why a guild defending better than expected drew suspicion about where its money came from rather than about how it had fought.{{cite:guild-finance-and-war-discord-private|2017–2018}}

The same arithmetic runs elsewhere: guilds had a market price, [[the-federation|the Federation]] planned to bankrupt [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] by forcing it to defend, and the [[emperium-of-wynn|Emperium]] alone tried taxing its members.{{cite:subguilds-discord-private|24 Jan 2018}}

## Free-for-all territories as an institution

FFA territories were declared, not merely observed, and the alliances tracked them as a category of their own. The [[coalition|Coalition]] announced a tool on 17 January 2018 that sorted every territory on the map into three classes — assigned to a member guild, truced, or free-for-all — and its allocation announcements drew the free-for-all ground in black.{{cite:coalition-announcements|1/17/2018}}{{cite:coalition-announcements|allocation map legend, January 2018}} That three-way split is why a raw capture count cannot be read as evidence of hostility: churn on free-for-all ground was the alliance's own policy, not a breach of it.

The declared ground was wider than the handful of perpetual hotspots, and it moved. The Federation published seven enumerated lists between 10 March and 8 September 2018, running from twelve territories to twenty-three, and a map before them on 21 February.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|FFA declarations, 2/21/2018 to 9/8/2018}} Composition changed as well as size: four territories were free-for-all in September that had not been in April, and ten had been in April and were not in September.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|4/22/2018 and 9/8/2018}} The log itself stops from time to time, and a guild missing during one of those runs has not been shown to be missing from the map. There are twenty such runs since January 2018, fourteen of three days or more, the longest twenty-eight days across April and May 2019 — so anything inferred about that spring from capture volume is standing on a hole.{{cite:territory-exchanges|days with no rows, Jan 2018 – Sep 2026}} None of the fourteen falls in 2018, and the only run of any length that year is 1–2 January, where the log had not yet started. The distinction matters because an absence can be real: [[fantasy]] does not appear in the record for eighteen days after leaving [[the-federation|the Federation]] in September 2018, and the map recorded about twelve hundred exchanges a day throughout.{{cite:territory-exchanges|no exchanges involving Fantasy, 23 Sep – 11 Oct 2018}}

The declared list is also, in effect, the top of the contested table: the five most fought-over territories of 2018 — Lava Lake, Detlas, Temple of Legends, Hive and the Emerald Trail — were all on it.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges per territory, 1 Jan – 31 Dec 2018 UTC, unfiltered: Lava Lake 15,584, Detlas 10,440, Temple of Legends 6,363, Hive 5,208, Emerald Trail 4,841; a ranking of contested ground, so no free-for-all subtraction applies}} A ranking of contested ground measures how open a territory was before it measures anything about a war. The gap between the two instruments is wide enough to reverse a reading. In the week from 8 September 2018, 36 per cent of every capture on the map fell on ground the alliance had declared open that day.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures on the sixteen territories of the declaration of 8 Sep 2018, as a share of all captures, 8–15 Sep 2018 UTC: 2,946 of 8,158}} No share can be given for the week before it: the declaration then in force went up on 30 August as a map image, its territories never written out.

The [[the-federation|Federation]]'s founding allocation drew the same distinction on its face. Announced to the alliance on the evening of 21 February 2018, it gave each of twenty-two guilds a named region and closed with a single line — that the free-for-all territories were the ones in black.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}} Assigned ground was named in text; common ground was shown by colour and never listed there, which is why a list of it has to come from somewhere else.

A draft circulated the day before had carried a third category as well, four areas held unassigned for guilds that might join later. The announced version dropped it, and the ground went to guilds already in the alliance.{{cite:federation-first-map-2018-discord-private|draft and announced maps compared, 20–21 Feb 2018}} From 21 February 2018 onward there were two categories, not three, and that is what every later capture figure has to be read against.

The pool changed shape as often as the map did, and from March 2018 the alliance published it with each new allocation. On 10 March it named twelve free-for-all territories and, separately, seven more as available for the future and free-for-all in the meantime — which is why a member asking two days later how many there were could be told nineteen. A fortnight after that the two categories were merged into a single list of twenty-one, and by 22 April it ran to twenty-two, still as one list.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|map announcements, 3/10/2018, 3/24/2018 and 4/22/2018}} The Federation published it twice more that year, at twenty-two on 6 August and sixteen on 8 September.{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Viaire, 6 Aug and 8 Sep 2018}} The institution outlived the alliance that ran it. [[terra|Terra]] published a list of eighteen on 14 September 2019, a week after winning the map, and kept it up: Avos Temple confirmed open on 30 September, Raider's Base Lower released on 10 October after diplomacy with TheNoLifes, a further change in November, and Mine Base Plains back to free-for-all on 11 December when [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] let it go.{{cite:terra-alliance-announcements-discord-private|14 Sep – 11 Dec 2019}} Between the two lists the ground is readable only off single-territory settlements. The [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] war with [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]] ended on 10 January 2019 with Lava Lake declared free-for-all{{cite:gdoc-1kajulcrdc0cpf1j|10 Jan 2019}}, and the second on 24 February with Molten Reach declared free-for-all alongside a split of Molten Heights.{{cite:gdoc-1kajulcrdc0cpf1j|24 Feb 2019}} Both were still open ground fourteen months later.{{cite:thread-263975|post #10, Bart, 9 Mar 2020}}

A third list is a player's rather than an alliance's, and it is half again the size of anything the Federation published: thirty-one territories, posted to the forums on 9 March 2020 in an argument about whether small guilds had anywhere to war.{{cite:thread-263975|post #10, Bart, 9 Mar 2020}} Sixteen of them are Silent Expanse territories that did not exist when the Federation published its last list, which is why the 2020 list cannot be read backwards onto 2018 or 2019 — the pool did not merely grow, half of it was new map.{{cite:territory-exchanges|the sixteen Silent Expanse names on the March 2020 list appear in no exchange of September 2019}}

The future-territory category is the moving part. It appears in February, goes, returns on 10 March, and is gone again by the 24th — so a count of the free-for-all pool is only meaningful with a date attached, and two contemporaries could give different numbers for the same week without either being wrong.

A figure computed against the wrong month's list is therefore wrong by a knowable margin. Where this article and others give territory-exchange figures for a war, they count captures outside the list in force at the time.

The list in force could also be wrong, because a list was not the only thing that changed the pool. On 25 July 2018 the Federation's map-maker took Factory Entrance and Avos Temple out of the twenty-one declared on 4 June and put Mine Base Plains in, her reason for Factory Entrance being that the alliance had held it about nine times in ten and it had therefore failed as contested ground.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-2018-discord-private|25 Jul 2018, the substitution}} A member asking the war channel two days later which territories had changed was told that Guild Hall had come in as well, and put the pool at twenty-one both before the change and after it.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-list-july-2018-discord-private|27 Jul 2018, the four territories named}} Guild Hall was on no Federation declaration, and had not changed hands since 7 July when it changed hands four times inside an hour on the 27th.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges on Guild Hall, 1–31 Jul 2018 UTC: none 7–26 Jul, four between 14:35 and 15:28 on 27 Jul}} The fuller account is at [[federation-free-for-all-territories|the Federation's free-for-all territories]]. The map carrying the change passed the alliance's vote that evening, thirteen to one, and went to the announcements channel the next afternoon as five images with no list attached.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-2018-discord-private|25 Jul 2018, the vote}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 7/26/2018 2:11 PM}} It took effect on 26 July, the day the map was published rather than the day the vote passed, and the two territories that had gone could be seen only on the image.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-2018-discord-private|26 Jul 2018, the amended pool}} The map of 1 August took Mine Base Plains out as well and passed thirteen to nothing, over objections in the leadership channel that once open ground could be assigned every guild would ask for a share of it.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-2018-discord-private|1 Aug 2018, the objections and the vote}}

Two of the three came back four days later by announcement, with no map: "Factory Entrance and Mine Base Plains are now FFA again."{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 8/5/2018 8:13 PM}} Avos Temple did not, and the full list of twenty-two published the following afternoon leaves it off.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Viaire, 8/6/2018 3:51 PM}}

That one word is the whole of the removal's public trace: the pool was cut by a map that named nothing and restored by an announcement that drew nothing, and neither instrument was reliably the record.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-2018-discord-private|the removal by map and the restoration by announcement, Jul – Aug 2018}} The list of 4 June is therefore wrong for the fortnight from 26 July to 5 August 2018, and a figure filtered against it for that window is wrong with it.{{cite:federation-free-for-all-pool-2018-discord-private|the pool from 26 Jul to 5 Aug 2018}}

A contemporaneous report shows how badly the distinction can mislead when it is ignored. On 28 February 2018, in the heaviest fighting of the Federation's first fortnight, a member gave the leadership channel a summary of what the enemy held: one guild had the Emerald Trail, Detlas and Cinfras, another had Lava Lake and the Temple of Legends.{{cite:federation-first-counterattack-2018-discord-private|28 Feb 2018}} All five were on the alliance's own free-for-all list, published a week earlier.{{cite:federation-first-map-2018-discord-private|free-for-all list, 21 Feb 2018}} At the height of a war, the enemy holdings a member thought worth reporting were entirely ground his own alliance had declared open to everyone. Assigned territory was changing hands in the same days; this report is simply not evidence of it.

The distinction was not only the leadership's. In September 2018, arguing over who deserved which territory, [[the-federation|Federation]] members on both sides of the dispute treated fighting on free-for-all ground and fighting the enemy as different activities, and only the second as earning an allocation. One member challenged why two guilds should gain experience three times faster than two others; the reply set out a case from proportionality with a calculation behind it; the challenger's counter was that much of the fighting being counted had happened on free-for-all ground.{{cite:federation-territory-committee-2018-discord-private|September 2018}} By the next morning he was designing a random sample of players to measure what a territory was actually worth.

That is the ordinary membership using the category rather than the leadership defining it, and it is the strongest evidence that a capture count which ignores the distinction is not measuring what the people involved thought they were doing.

The alliance's own war-activity figures carried a selection problem its members discovered late. In August 2018 guilds noticed they were missing from the graphs everyone had been arguing from.{{cite:federation-territory-doctrine-2018-discord-private|August 2018}} The member who kept them explained that he had begun the series after the [[coalition-civil-war|Coalition Civil War]] with only the guilds then fighting, and had rarely added anyone since.{{cite:federation-territory-doctrine-2018-discord-private|war-activity graphs, August 2018}} Any argument of the form *the numbers show this guild does little* rested on a sample that excluded guilds by the accident of when they started — and the alliance went on arguing from it after being told.

The alliance also said what the ground was for. On 19 June 2018 the Kingdom of Foxes' Viceroy told the Federation's leadership that one purpose of the free-for-all territories was to let a guild show it could handle more land than it currently held.{{cite:federation-map-disputes-march-2018-discord-private|19 Jun 2018}} Churn there was not tolerated so much as required: it was how a guild qualified for an allocation.

Guilds were told what came first and no rule was ever made of it. Late on 11 July 2018 a member argued in [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s leadership channel that no guild should attack free-for-all territory while it did not hold all of its own assigned ground, and named one that had been doing so while member guilds were under attack.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|11 Jul 2018, the proposal}} The Kingdom of Foxes' Viceroy gave a precedent for the practice, a guild that had taken open ground without holding its assignment because it needed the income.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|11 Jul 2018, the precedent}} Another leader answered that a reminder would serve instead of a rule, and none was adopted.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|11 Jul 2018, the answer}}

The list itself was alliance business. On 26 July 2018 a member asked whether it could be given to somebody outside the alliance, and asked separately for a copy of it.{{cite:free-for-all-territories-discord-private|26 Jul 2018}} Open ground was not undefended ground: members described free-for-all territory as heavily held{{cite:free-for-all-territories-discord-private|2018}}, and in April 2018 the Fox Viceroy described a member guild reacting angrily at being asked not to attack allied free-for-all territories while an invasion was being repelled.{{cite:free-for-all-territories-discord-private|April 2018}}
 [[war-syndicate|War Syndicate]]'s charter of 2017 exempted twelve places from the territory cap it imposed and opened them to everyone, alliance members included: Lava Lake, the Hive, Qira's Battle Room, the whole Light Realm, the Guild Hall, Cinfras and Cinfras Entrance, the Temple of Legends, Bob's Tomb, the Tower of Ascension, Factory Entrance and Detlas.{{cite:gdoc-1kdasl6eq7y_zssy|War Syndicate charter, free-for-all territories}} [[the-federation|The Federation]] kept the practice and published its own list three times in 2018, on 6 August, 8 September and 12 October, growing it from twenty-two entries to a Gavel–Ocean–Wynn schedule of eighteen.{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Viaire, 6 Aug and 8 Sep 2018; Drew1011, 12 Oct 2018}} Most of the modern hotspots were already on both lists.

## Sub-guilding

The device followed from how everything was counted. Territory was allocated by guild, votes were cast by guild, and a guild could hold only so much and capture only so fast — so people made more guilds. It predates every alliance that later argued about it: one guild was taken in as a subguild in 2015 after proving itself in fights, and another was founded outright as a subguild of the strongest guild of its day, for no reason except that the alliance could then hold more of the map.{{cite:subguilds-discord-private|2015}}

Its ethics were settled in a Coalition war channel on 11 February 2018 in under a minute. A leader asked whether it was even fair that the other side was fighting with two guilds that were essentially one; she was told the second was a subguild made to war more; the member who had asked answered that if it was a subguild he thought it fair. She then set out what it bought them — a doubled rate of capture.{{cite:subguilds-discord-private|11 Feb 2018}} Neither treats the advantage as an objection to the practice; on this article's reading of the exchange, it is the reason for it.

The only constraint on any of it came from outside the community. On 24 January 2018 a Coalition leader reported that she had agreed conditions about alternate accounts and alternate guilds with a member of the game's own staff — not with the alliance, and not by a vote — and pinned it. The alliance's leader's immediate response was to hope aloud that he would not have to disband two guilds he kept on alternate accounts but did not war with; another leader mentioned that she had kept guilds simply because she liked their names.{{cite:subguilds-discord-private|24 Jan 2018}} The distinction between storing a guild and warring with it was his own, drawn in the moment.

The alliances knew the practice was against the game's rules, and had it in writing from April 2018. On the 10th a [[the-federation|Federation]] officer posted the rule into the leadership channel — no holding territory through an alternate guild or a subguild — and the founder pinned it.{{cite:subguild-rule-and-the-games-staff-2018-discord-private|10 Apr 2018}} The rule as posted did not turn on whether the second guild sat on an alternate account, and reached any guild used to take ground that a principal guild was then allowed to take from it in turn.

Three days later the same officer put four cases to a member of the game's staff, and the founder published and pinned the answer.{{cite:subguild-rule-and-the-games-staff-2018-discord-private|13 Apr 2018}} Permitted: a guild going after ground an allied guild had lost, meaning to hand it back, and the ally doing the same in return. Not permitted: a guild forming a second guild out of its own members, taking a province with it and handing the ground to itself; or replacing an allied guild's leadership with its own members and doing likewise.{{cite:subguild-rule-and-the-games-staff-2018-discord-private|13 Apr 2018}}

The line ran between cooperation and control — a formulation this article supplies, since the ruling does not put it that way and the four cases do not otherwise divide legibly. Two guilds recapturing for each other was lawful, and that is most of what the war record contains. A guild acting through a guild it controlled was not, and that is the device every alliance used, complained of in its enemies, and could never define in its own ranks.

The complaint used a different vocabulary from the practice. [[the-federation|Federation]] members recorded in 2018 that a guild attacking them was effectively a subguild run by an opposing leader, and that another was a proxy of the principal enemy guild{{cite:subguilds-discord-private|2018}}; the enemy was described in the same period as fighting alongside two apparent subguilds of its own.{{cite:subguilds-discord-private|enemy subguilds, 2018}} Nothing in those descriptions marks the arrangements out from the alliance's own. A subguild on your side was structure and a subguild on theirs was deception; the contrast is this article's and nobody in the record draws it.

Having pinned the ruling, the alliance never used it. It goes uncited in the expulsion of [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] four days earlier, in the [[libertas|Libertas]] exchanges of August, and in the argument that November over which of two guild accounts a member was.{{cite:subguild-rule-and-the-games-staff-2018-discord-private|April–November 2018}} Nobody who had it available reached for it, including the man who had pinned it.

The alliance also denied in public that the rule reached it. Answering an accusation of feeding on the game's forums on 26 May 2018, a [[the-federation|Federation]] member set out the rule{{cite:thread-226798|post #1, quoting rule 18}} and stated that all the alliance's guilds were equal and that none of them were alternate or subguilds.{{cite:thread-226798|post #1, the denial}} The post had been put before the alliance's guild leaders and checked by them before it went out.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|26 May 2018, the draft}} A Wynncraft administrator closed the thread four days later without changing the war rules.{{cite:thread-226798-p08|post #143, jpresent, 30 May 2018}} What the leadership channel was saying about subguilds in the same months is set beside that denial at [[the-feeding-accusation|the feeding accusation]].

It was named once more, on 5 August 2018, in a run of nostalgia jokes in the general channel: somebody said he missed rule 18.{{cite:subguild-rule-and-the-games-staff-2018-discord-private|5 Aug 2018}} Whether the rule had by then been renumbered, withdrawn or neither is not established, and the joke settles nothing — but a rule in materially the same terms was quoted from the server rules on a public forum a year later, on 22 August 2019.{{cite:thread-254047|post #5, Mapleric, 22 Aug 2019, quoting the server rules}}

The relations between the alliance and the people who wrote those rules were closer than the ruling suggests. On 26 July 2018 a member of the game's staff was in the Federation's general channel socially, asking whether he could join a guild and then whether that guild was not an alternate guild; the channel answered in jokes, one member saying the alliance was that guild's subguild and every guild was.{{cite:subguild-rule-and-the-games-staff-2018-discord-private|26 Jul 2018}}

The terms on which subguilds were tolerated were stated once, informally, and in defence of a particular guild. Told in August 2018 that subguilds were not allowed, [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s founder said that such guilds had been permitted before now, provided they left allies alone and held no assigned territory.{{cite:federation-libertas-2018-discord-private|12 Aug 2018}} That is the whole rule, and one person gave it in an argument.

It was also looser than the rule the alliance already had. The ruling it had obtained from the game's staff in April permitted allies to recapture ground for each other and forbade a guild raising a second guild out of its own members to hand territory to itself; the August test asked only that a subguild leave allies alone and hold no assigned ground, which says nothing about who the second guild's members were or who controlled it.{{cite:subguild-rule-and-the-games-staff-2018-discord-private|13 Apr 2018}}{{cite:federation-libertas-2018-discord-private|12 Aug 2018}} The same office pinned the first and gave the second, four months apart in the same channel. Setting the two beside each other is this article's; nobody in the exchange mentioned the April ruling.

It was immediately challenged and the challenge went nowhere. They are attacking allies, a member said; they attacked [[lunatic|Lunatic]], said another, though that had been a misunderstanding. The founder answered that apart from that he had only seen them take free-for-all ground and enemies, and asked when they had attacked an ally; a third member said he had not seen it either, and the exchange ended.{{cite:federation-libertas-2018-discord-private|12 Aug 2018}}

Six months later he was on the other side of it. Asked in the war channel on 25 July 2018 whether [[libertas|Libertas]] was an ally or a target, [[drew1011|Drew1011]] answered that it could be attacked, that he owned it, and that he had been using it to help war.{{cite:subguilds-discord-private|25 Jul 2018}} He added that he was a [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] member who owned two further guilds through alternate accounts, and another member told him it was not allowed.{{cite:subguilds-discord-private|war channel, 25 Jul 2018}} Nothing follows in the record.

The casualness is the point. It came in answer to a routine targeting question, in an ordinary channel, from the person who had drawn the distinction himself — and neither he nor anyone else treated the disclosure as an event.

A guild could raise a second guild to hold or feed it territory. The practice runs through the record — the Foxen Royal Navy under the Kingdom of Foxes in 2014, the Emperium of Wynn's Buddy System, [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]]'s Illustratus [Ius] in 2018 — and a 2019 forum exchange gives the working definition: "Subguilding is a war-related technique involving the use of secondary guilds to increase the offensive or defensive capability of a main guild."{{cite:thread-254047|post #4, Qzphs, 22 Aug 2019}} It was also against the server rules by then. The rule quoted in the same exchange reads: "Do not use alternate guilds or sub-guilds to maintain territory. An alternate guild doesn't have to be owned by a person's alternate account. This applies to any guild being used to capture territories which are then allowed to be captured by a main guild."{{cite:thread-254047|post #5, Mapleric, 22 Aug 2019, quoting the server rules}}

By 2019 a second guild was somewhere to keep land as well as a way of taking it. On 11 September 2019, during a joint push on Corkus, [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] withdrew territories its subguild Pirates United was holding; no reason was recorded.{{cite:subguilds-discord-private|11 Sep 2019}} In the same month [[terra|Terra]] members identified Kolibri as a closely affiliated subguild of [[imperial|Imperial]], fighting in that region outside any formal raid.{{cite:subguilds-discord-private|September 2019}} The device had by then become political as well: a guild and four or five subordinate guilds spied, negotiated and withdrew as one, inside an alliance that counted its votes by the guild.{{cite:subguilds-discord-private|21 Sep 2019}} That reading is set out at [[alliance-governance|alliance governance]] and is this article's.

A real war instead shows spread and asymmetry: sustained fighting across 100+ territories with a low share on FFA hotspots. Territory-exchange figures in these articles therefore count captures outside the FFA hotspots, and bloc rosters inferred from co-belligerence are inference, never recorded fact{{cite:chronicle-map-methodology|war criteria: real wars span 100+ territories with low FFA concentration}}. In the [[goose-artemis-war|Goose]] roster episode, a map-inferred roster initially contradicted the written record{{cite:chronicle-map-methodology|Goose roster 'ENTIRELY MAP-INFERRED' note}}.

## The proposal

The succession came out of the [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]]'s warriors' channel on the afternoon of 22 February 2019, was carried into the council a minute later, and drew agreement from exactly two people, one of them a chief of [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] who belonged to no member guild; the exchange is set out at [[the-aesir-oligarchy|The Aesir oligarchy]].{{cite:aesir-council-after-the-concession-2019-discord-private|22 Feb 2019, the proposal and the assent}} The alliance's leader, one of the two people its own information card named for [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]], had last posted in any of its channels on 2 January.{{cite:aesir-council-after-the-concession-2019-discord-private|2 Jan 2019}}

## The ballot

The vote opened in the alliance's voting channel at 20:49 on 22 February 2019, addressed to the whole server rather than to the council, and asked who should revive the alliance.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the ballot, 20:49:50 on 22 Feb 2019, export clock}} It offered two options, each keyed to a numbered emoji: the leader of [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]], who was posting it, and a chief of [[fantasy|Fantasy]] who had stood forward in the council three hours earlier.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the two options, 22 Feb 2019}} Four minutes later the same account added that voting would close in twenty-four hours, or sooner if one side led by enough.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the closing conditions, 20:53 on 22 Feb 2019, export clock}} The result was afterwards reported to the membership as a decision of the oligarchy.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the announcement, 15:03 on 23 Feb 2019, export clock}} The franchise used and the franchise claimed were not the same.

## The close

At 11:11 on 23 February the organiser of the ballot posted that it would end that afternoon for reasons that could not be disclosed, gave the closing time in a third time zone and described it as four hours off; it was three hours and fifty minutes off.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the notice, 11:11:07 on 23 Feb 2019, export clock}} The close came at 15:01, five hours and forty-eight minutes before the twenty-four-hour deadline the ballot had set.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the close, 15:01:12 on 23 Feb 2019 export clock, against the stated deadline of 04:49:50 UTC on 24 Feb}} The four hours was the notice given, not the earliness.

The announced result was five votes to four.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the count, 23 Feb 2019}} That is a margin of one, and the ballot's second closing condition — one side leading by enough — was never given a number.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the closing conditions and the announced count}}

## Who voted

The vote was taken by emoji reaction, so the ballot itself lasted.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the reaction record, 22–23 Feb 2019}} Nine distinct accounts appear on it and ten votes were cast, one account having reacted to both options; five reactions stand against each option.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|nine accounts, ten reactions, five against each option}} A reaction can be added to a Discord message or taken off it at any time after the message goes up, so a tally read years later is not the tally at the close: the five to four the organiser announced on the day is the count as cast.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the announced count beside the reaction tallies}} Double-reacting was a feature of the instrument rather than a peculiarity of this vote — the same channel's two other reaction ballots, an example vote of 6 June 2018 and the truce amendment of 15 December 2018, each carry one as well.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the voting channel's three reaction ballots, Jun 2018 – Feb 2019}}

## The leaders who voted

The alliance's information card named ten leaders, two from each of its five member guilds.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|the alliance-information card, text of 1 Dec 2018 at the latest}} At least four of the ten voted, and those four divided evenly: both of the card's DiamondDeities representatives for their own guild's leader, and the single representatives of [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] and [[thenolifes|TheNoLifes]] for the challenger.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the four card-listed voters, 22–23 Feb 2019}} Neither of the two the card named for Sins of Seedia voted, so the guild whose leader's absence had prompted the election took no part in choosing a successor; neither did either of the two it named for Fantasy, and the challenger, a chief of that guild, was not among the ten.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the card-listed leaders who did not vote}}

Five of the nine voters could not be matched to a card entry, and six of the card's ten names could not be matched to any account, so the turnout among the alliance's named leaders may have been higher than four.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the unmatched voters and the unmatched card names}} The card was three months old by the day of the ballot, and the alliance had outgrown it: on 31 January 2019 a member of Fantasy mentioned in passing that the guild had three people in the oligarchy, where the card gave every member guild two.{{cite:aesir-council-after-the-concession-2019-discord-private|31 Jan 2019}}

Reading the ballot against the card is this article's. On the leaders it can place, the winner had two of four, and the votes that carried the election came from outside the leadership the alliance had itself named.

## The winner's margin

Of the five reactions standing against the DiamondDeities option, one was the candidate's own and one that of the guild's other card-listed representative.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the winner's five reactions}} A third belonged to the council member who ran the alliance's negotiations and kept its territorial map, and who was on no list of its leaders — the same member who had struck [[the-canyon-concession|its Canyon claims]] off that map on 18 January 2019.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the winner's five reactions, placed}}{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|18 Jan 2019, 06:51, export clock}} A fourth belonged to the chief of Kingdom Foxes who was in no Aesir guild and had been sitting in the council since New Year, and who had held the office of viceroy in the [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]] alliance until standing down on 7 February 2019.{{cite:aesir-council-after-the-concession-2019-discord-private|1 Jan and 7 Feb 2019}} The fifth belonged to the account that had also reacted to the other option and that never posted a message in any channel of the alliance's server.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the double-reacting account}} On the challenger's side one reaction was the challenger's own, two were card-listed leaders of member guilds, one was the same double-reacting account, and one belonged to a member whose whole presence in the alliance's rooms was eight messages between 11 November and 5 December 2018.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the challenger's five reactions, placed}} Two of the nine could not be placed in any member guild at all.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the two unplaced accounts}}

## The new server

The successor server existed before the count. At 11:42 on 23 February, three and a half hours before the close, the DiamondDeities leader told the council that winning would mean building a new Discord server, because the departed leader would never give up ownership of the existing one, and a council member agreed that it needed doing.{{cite:aesir-after-the-election-2019-discord-private|23 Feb 2019, 11:42, export clock}} Sixteen minutes later came the admission that it had already been built, with roles, channels and bots.{{cite:aesir-after-the-election-2019-discord-private|23 Feb 2019, 11:58, export clock}} The invitation went to the council at 12:40, together with an undertaking to hand the new server over to the other candidate if the vote went the other way.{{cite:aesir-after-the-election-2019-discord-private|23 Feb 2019, 12:40, the invitation and the undertaking}} At 15:03, two minutes after the close, the whole membership was told that the oligarchy had chosen a leader to revive the alliance and asked to move across; the same invitation followed two seconds later.{{cite:aesir-after-the-election-2019-discord-private|23 Feb 2019, 15:03, the announcement and the invitation}}

## The objection

At 10:19 on 26 February 2019 the leader of TheNoLifes wrote in the voting channel that the election had been illegitimate, because a decision of that kind belonged to the guild leaders alone and because an unlimited number of chiefs could be put into the oligarchy to inflate a vote.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|26 Feb 2019, 10:19, export clock}} That leader had voted, and had voted for the challenger.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the objector's reaction}} The message carries no reaction, it is the last in the channel, and it is the last message from that account in any Aesir room.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|26 Feb 2019 and after, the alliance's seven channels}} In the eighty-two days between it and 18 May 2019 those seven channels carried fifty messages between them, forty of the fifty in the general chat, and none of them answered it.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|26 Feb – 18 May 2019, the alliance's seven channels}} The same complaint had been made in the same room in November 2018 and went unanswered then too; the two are joined at [[the-aesir-oligarchy|The Aesir oligarchy]].{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-membership-and-election-2019-discord-private|23 Nov 2018 and 26 Feb 2019}}

## The building empties

The announcements channel carried fifty-two messages in its life, and its last two were the announcement and the invitation of 15:03 on 23 February; neither carries an edit or a reaction.{{cite:aesir-after-the-election-2019-discord-private|the announcements channel, whole life}} The alliance's last call to arms, of 27 November 2018, carries four reactions and its two messages of 28 December eight each.{{cite:aesir-after-the-election-2019-discord-private|reactions on the announcements channel, Nov–Dec 2018}} The channel was silent for the five years that followed.{{cite:aesir-after-the-election-2019-discord-private|the announcements channel after 23 Feb 2019}}

The council's last message before the move was the invitation of 12:40, and it carried nothing at all for the next eighty-four days.{{cite:aesir-after-the-election-2019-discord-private|the council channel, 23 Feb – 18 May 2019}} The warriors' channel — where [[the-canyon-concession|the Canyon had been given up]] on the last morning of 2018{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|31 Dec 2018}} and where the election had been suggested on 22 February — fell quiet at 19:19 on 22 February, before the vote even opened, and produced seven more messages in its life before June, six of them on 6 March.{{cite:aesir-after-the-election-2019-discord-private|the warriors' channel, Feb–Jun 2019}}

The general chat filled instead. Within ninety minutes of the announcement a member asked whether the people left behind now owned Aesir; two hours later another said the room was for jokes now; twenty minutes after that a third greeted it as a successor to [[the-federation|the Federation]] before there was one — three members in one evening describing the place as no longer an alliance.{{cite:aesir-after-the-election-2019-discord-private|23 Feb 2019, the general channel}}

On 6 March a member proposed reorganising the old server by opening its channels up, as had been done to the Federation's after that alliance dissolved, and was answered that the departed leader still held ownership of it.{{cite:aesir-after-the-election-2019-discord-private|6 Mar 2019, the general channel}} Forty-seven minutes earlier, in the warriors' channel, members had observed that the newly elected leader held the server's manage-server permission.{{cite:aesir-after-the-election-2019-discord-private|6 Mar 2019, the warriors' channel}} Ownership of a Discord server and administrative permission on it are different things, and the alliance's members were working with both accounts of the position on one afternoon. Nobody joined the two exchanges at the time; joining them is this article's.

On 18 May 2019 the leader of IceBlue Team came back to the dead council to ask how the other Aesir was getting on, and nobody answered.{{cite:aesir-after-the-election-2019-discord-private|18 May 2019; the questioner placed from the alliance-information card, a text of 1 Dec 2018 at the latest}} The channel then carried nothing for four years. The general chat went on carrying traffic into 2020, 2021 and as late as 2024, and the council was opened twice more, in 2023 and 2024, by people asking when the alliance would be revived.{{cite:aesir-after-the-election-2019-discord-private|the old server, 2019–2024}}

## Afterwards

The vote did not end the alliance. Six days later the chief of Kingdom Foxes who had assented to holding it founded [[the-consortium|the Consortium]], whose founding circle was the Aesir leadership and whose membership card of 24 March 2019 named all five Aesir guilds.{{cite:aesir-into-the-consortium-march-2019-discord-private|1–24 Mar 2019}} The pact itself went on acting through March: on 22 March a Consortium member said it had removed the Fantasy chief who had stood against the winner the day before, a ballot of its own was being chased that same day, and it was last proposed as a body a guild could rejoin on the 27th.{{cite:aesir-into-the-consortium-march-2019-discord-private|21–27 Mar 2019}} That month is set out at [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]].

## Who was in the room

The pact created a leadership channel in its own server on the afternoon of 30 October 2018, addressed to a role its author said he had just given the alliance's most trusted members, and told them the planning would be done there.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-founded-2018-discord-private|channel creation and first message, 30 Oct 2018}} Every guild in it was still a Federation member, and stayed one for eleven days.{{cite:aesir-membership-record-2018-discord-private|membership, October–November 2018}} One member asked that the arrangement be permanent; a quarter of an hour later another asked that it be kept very small.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-founded-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018}}

Who belonged was disputed inside the channel itself, and answered three different ways in five days. On the morning of 23 November 2018 a leader of [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]] noticed a member he had not expected and said he had asked for a narrower group; the member left.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-membership-and-election-2019-discord-private|23 Nov 2018, the morning}} Nearly three hours later another member asked whether the room was not supposed to be for trusted people. The DiamondDeities leader answered that it was the leaders' chat and for leaders only, and added that if there were a genuinely leaders-only channel he would let all his own guild's chiefs into this one.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-membership-and-election-2019-discord-private|23 Nov 2018, the afternoon}} A third member pointed to the pact's warriors' channel, which was open to captains and above, and said it served much the same purpose: the council planned a coordinated attack and the warriors' channel was where it was announced.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-membership-and-election-2019-discord-private|23 Nov 2018, the warriors' channel}} Four days later the leader of [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] asked whether each guild was allowed three people in the channel, and was told he thought so.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-membership-and-election-2019-discord-private|27 Nov 2018}} Trusted people, leaders only, three a guild: none of the three answers bound anybody.

## The rule of 16 November

At 06:44 on 16 November 2018 the pact's leader ruled that no truce with [[hackforums|HackForums]], [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]], Wrath of Poseidon or Achte Shadow might be made without the council's approval, and posted the rule to the announcements channel two minutes later.{{cite:aesir-and-hackforums-november-2018-discord-private|the rule, 06:44 and 06:46 on 16 Nov 2018, export clock}} It answered a member guild that had halted an alliance campaign by agreeing its own peace, and thirty-six minutes after it went up the same leader announced an alliance-wide truce with the guild in question; the episode is told at [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]].{{cite:aesir-and-hackforums-november-2018-discord-private|16 Nov 2018}} The rule outlasted that peace. It was the mechanism by which the alliance took the making of friends and enemies away from its member guilds.

## The lists of 27 November

The pact's two standing lists were made in one conversation on the morning of 27 November 2018 and pinned that night. A council member proposed that alongside a list of enemies there should be a second list of guilds to be left alone, because a member who got into trouble on that ground could not be rescued; the pact's leader answered with his own guild's position — four guilds he treated as enemies, three he was truced with, and one region he did not attack out of respect — and then stated that the alliance as a whole was truced with HackForums and [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]].{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|27 Nov 2018, the morning}} Guilds were added on request over the following hour: one member asked for a guild to go on the wipe list and was told it had been added, and the leader asked the same for Titans Valor on the protected side, adding that any former [[confederation|Confederation]] guild without a treaty was open to being wiped.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|27 Nov 2018, the additions}} At 22:08 the same member restated both lists with one further name on the protected side and asked whether everyone agreed; three people answered, none dissented, and the message was pinned at 01:25 the next morning.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|27–28 Nov 2018, the restatement and the pin}}

Before the lists existed, enmity was personal and ad hoc. On 22 November a council member asked whether the alliance had any standing truce with [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] and was told it did not as a group, though individual guilds probably did.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|22 Nov 2018}} On the 23rd and again on the 25th the same member announced additions to the alliance's enemies on his own reasoning, and neither was put to anyone.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|23 and 25 Nov 2018}} The lists did not settle it either. On 1 December the pact's leader proposed adding to the enemies a guild the pinned list had carried there for four days.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|1 Dec 2018}}

What the protected list recorded was one guild's obligations extended to everybody. Over the night of 24 and 25 November members of [[thenolifes|TheNoLifes]], DiamondDeities and [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]] had coordinated an attack on Kingdom Foxes in the council channel, counting its defenders, calling for more people and moving from one region into its lands; one member said he was going to wipe it whatever anyone else thought.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|24–25 Nov 2018}} On the 27th Kingdom Foxes went onto the do-not-touch list, and the reason given in the same conversation was that the pact's leader was personally truced with it.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|27 Nov 2018, the protected list}}

## The card

The lists reached the pact's information channel on 5 December 2018 as a single card: six enemies, eight guilds under truce, and a set of notes.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|the card, posted 08:35 on 5 Dec 2018, export clock}} The notes restated the rule of 16 November and widened it, requiring that any proposal to truce, any event idea and any proposed method of warring be brought to the oligarchy first.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|the card's notes}} They then defined the oligarchy — the only definition the alliance ever wrote down — as trusted people drawn from each member guild, admission being by application to the pact's leader in person.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|the definition}} Membership was a favour rather than an office. The notes closed with a residual rule: a guild on neither list might be attacked freely, being neither enemy nor ally of the alliance.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|the residual clause}}

That last provision is not a free-for-all list and should not be read as one. The [[the-federation|Federation]]'s declarations named territories and were the thing everybody's captures were measured against; the Aesir card names guilds and leaves territory out of it.{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Drew1011, 10/12/2018}}{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|the residual clause}} Setting the two instruments side by side is this article's comparison.

The card cannot date its own contents. Its last recorded edit was made at 05:02 on 1 January 2019, and it had been changed at least once before that: twenty-nine minutes after it went up a member told its author that BuildCraftia had been put on the enemy list and the truce list at once, and by that afternoon the author answered that it had been fixed.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|5 Dec 2018 and 1 Jan 2019, the edits}} Two entries in the surviving text cannot have stood on 5 December. [[blacklisted|Blacklisted]], which the card places under truce, does not appear in the pact's channels before 16 December, was being swept off Aesir land on the 19th, and agreed a ceasefire only at 08:42 on 31 December, sixteen and a half hours before the recorded edit; Spectral Cabbage, which the card names an enemy, is first mentioned on 10 December.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|Blacklisted and Spectral Cabbage, Dec 2018}} The contents are undated between 5 December 2018 and 1 January 2019 except where an entry can be fixed to a message.

The pact's other standing document has the same defect. The alliance-information card was posted at 23:42 on 6 November 2018 and its last recorded edit is of 18:08 on 1 December; its surviving text lists five member guilds, where on 6 November the pact had three.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|the alliance-information card, 6 Nov and 1 Dec 2018}}{{cite:aesir-membership-record-2018-discord-private|membership, Oct–Nov 2018}} An edit date fixes only that the surviving text is not the posting date's, and it carries the last edit alone.

## Two occasions on which it acted

The oligarchy's approval was recorded twice. On 15 December 2018 the council member who kept the lists put it to the pact's voting channel that Titans Valor had changed its territorial claims, that this amounted to a change in the terms of their truce, and asked whether the alliance agreed.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|15 Dec 2018}} That was one of only two votes the channel carried in the whole life of the server.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|voting channel, whole life of the server}}

The second occasion was [[the-canyon-concession|the ceasefire of 31 December 2018]], under which part of the Canyon passed to Blacklisted. It was settled in the warriors' channel rather than the council; the approval the rule required consisted of a leader of DiamondDeities giving it four times over and then asking what he had approved; and the pact's leader said nothing in it.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|31 Dec 2018, the approval}}

## The proposal to dismantle it

At 20:24 on 8 January 2019 the list-keeper reported to the council that Sins of Seedia had broken the alliance-wide truce with HackForums, and posted the announcement of 16 November as the thing being broken.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|8 Jan 2019, 20:24, export clock}} A leader of [[fantasy|Fantasy]] answered that the deal should be treated as never having been made and that the alliance should return to arranging matters with each guild separately rather than collectively; his own guild had not attacked HackForums and did not intend to, a narrower bilateral arrangement would serve better, and restricting which guilds the other three members might attack served no purpose.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|8 Jan 2019, the reply}} The list-keeper objected that one has to honour one's own undertakings.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|8 Jan 2019, the objection}} Nothing was resolved and the exchange ends there. Whether the rule was in fact abandoned was never written down.

## The election

The succession came out of the warriors' channel. At 14:13 on 22 February 2019 a warrior who led no guild said the guild leaders ought simply to elect a replacement head for the alliance; a leader of DiamondDeities agreed, said he would take it to the council, and put it there a minute later, asking whether anyone agreed.{{cite:aesir-council-after-the-concession-2019-discord-private|22 Feb 2019, 14:13 and 14:14, export clock}} One member answered with a thumbs up, and six minutes after that the visiting chief of Kingdom Foxes, who belonged to no member guild, said he agreed. That was the whole recorded assent to holding the election at all.{{cite:aesir-council-after-the-concession-2019-discord-private|22 Feb 2019, the assent}}

The same leader put himself forward and waited an hour and a half for a second candidate; opened the vote in the pact's voting channel at 20:49, addressed to everyone and framed as choosing someone to revive the alliance; and said four minutes later that it would close in twenty-four hours, or earlier if one side led by enough.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-membership-and-election-2019-discord-private|22 Feb 2019, the ballot}} The next morning the organiser gave four hours' notice that the vote would end early, for reasons that could not be disclosed, and closed it at 15:01 on 23 February — five hours and forty-eight minutes before the twenty-four-hour deadline the ballot had set.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the notice of 11:11 and the close of 15:01, 23 Feb 2019, export clock, against the stated deadline of 04:49:50 UTC on 24 Feb}} The announced result was five votes to four, a margin of one against a second closing condition — one side leading by enough — that was never given a number.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the closing conditions and the announced count}} Two minutes later he told the whole alliance in its announcements channel that the oligarchy had decided to re-elect a leader and had chosen him, and asked the membership to join a different Discord server.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-membership-and-election-2019-discord-private|23 Feb 2019, the announcement}} He had told the council that morning that a new server was needed because the pact's leader would never hand over ownership of the existing one, and that he had already built it.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-membership-and-election-2019-discord-private|23 Feb 2019, the new server}}

On 26 February the leader of TheNoLifes wrote in the voting channel that the election was illegitimate, because a decision of that kind belonged to the guild leaders alone and because an unlimited number of chiefs could be put into the oligarchy.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-membership-and-election-2019-discord-private|26 Feb 2019}} It is the last message in the channel and the last word on the pact's constitution.

The objection had a dated basis three weeks earlier. On 31 January 2019 a member of Fantasy had mentioned in passing that his guild had three people in the oligarchy and one guild leader, where the pact's own card gave every member guild two.{{cite:aesir-council-after-the-concession-2019-discord-private|31 Jan 2019}}{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|the alliance-information card, text of 1 Dec 2018 at the latest}} Who voted was written into the ballot itself, which was taken by emoji reaction: nine accounts, of which at least four were among the ten leaders the card named, and those four divided two against two.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the reaction record against the card's leader list, 22-23 Feb 2019}} The ballot had been put to the whole server rather than to the oligarchy, and the result was reported to the membership as the oligarchy's decision.{{cite:aesir-election-ballot-february-2019-discord-private|the ballot's address, 22 Feb 2019, and the announcement of 23 Feb}} It is set out at [[the-aesir-election|The Aesir election]]. The complaint itself was the one raised in the same channel in November 2018 and never answered.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-membership-and-election-2019-discord-private|23 Nov 2018 and 26 Feb 2019}} Joining the two is this article's.

A member returned to the abandoned council channel on 18 May 2019 to ask how the other Aesir was getting on.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-membership-and-election-2019-discord-private|18 May 2019}} It was the last message of substance the channel carried, and it went quiet for four years afterwards.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-membership-and-election-2019-discord-private|council channel after 18 May 2019}}

The Aquarium [TAq] is a Wynncraft guild created on 22 Mar 2020,{{cite:api-wynncraft-com-v3-guild-the-20aquarium|creation date, retrieved 2 Sep 2026}} in the middle of the [[goose-artemis-war|Goose-Artemis war]]; its first territory exchange came the same day, seventy-two minutes after the guild was made.{{cite:territory-exchanges|first exchange with The Aquarium on either side, 22 Mar 2020 12:23:18 UTC, against the guild's creation stamp of 11:11:17 UTC that morning; a first-appearance check, so no free-for-all subtraction applies}} Its earliest alignment was never written down; it appears to have first fought on the anti-[[Artemis]] side of that war.{{cite:territory-exchanges|territory_exchanges map-data analysis}}

## History

### The Valhalla years (2021)

On 11 May 2021, the day of [[the-great-collapse|the Great Collapse]], The Aquarium was one of the thirteen guilds that founded [[Valhalla]], which took map control within days. When Valhalla reorganized as [[Vanir]] on 28 Nov 2021, The Aquarium was among the ten founding guilds; Vanir collapsed twenty days later.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} Through the following winter it fought in the [[valhalla-khaos-war|war against Khaos]], which peaked in January 2022 and faded by mid-July — the run-up to [[the-realignment-era|the realignment]].{{cite:territory-exchanges|territory_exchanges map-data analysis}}

### Cucumber Company to the Avocado split (2022-2024)

For guild Season 5 (spring 2022) The Aquarium joined [[Cucumber Company]],{{cite:titantimes-github-io-vol1|vol. 1, 8 May 2022}} appearing in the 14-guild roster posted to the [[the-forum-megalist|forum megalist]] on 26 Jun 2022 — the last public alliance roster ever —{{cite:thread-278112-p08|p8 #160, 26 Jun 2022}} and carried through the alliance's renames into [[smtn elf]].{{cite:titantimes-github-io-vol6|vol. 6, 12 Oct 2022}} In June 2023 it and [[Avicia]] walked out, rejecting pressure to shrink their claims,{{cite:thread-308780|Avicia guild season post, 10 Jun 2023}} and as the two-guild [[Avocado Aquarium]] pact — Avicia called TAq "our unwavering ally" —{{cite:thread-310286|Avicia guild season post, 19 Aug 2023}} the pair waged [[the-avocado-split|a sniping and claim-taking campaign]] against their former allies into early 2024,{{cite:territory-exchanges|territory_exchanges map-data analysis}} running shared infrastructure including the joint subguild Avoquarium [HQs] and the subguild [[ShadowFall]].{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}

### The guild in 2024

Its recruitment thread, as it stood in March 2024, describes a guild of 150 members at level 120 — the highest-level guild on the server — that had taken first or second place on the season leaderboard for a year running, ran monthly events built by its own build team with custom dungeons, items and creatures, and asked only a Discord account and a level 80 class of applicants. Recruitment ran entirely off the forums: applicants were told to find an online member through the guild's stats page for a Discord invitation and apply in a channel there, because "this forum thread isn't consistently monitored".{{cite:thread-269835|post #1, opening post as last edited 8 Mar 2024}} Applications kept arriving on the thread regardless, and went unanswered.{{cite:thread-269835-p15|p15 #291–292, Aug–Sep 2024}}

### The Idiot Co war (2024-2025)

From 29 Apr 2024 The Aquarium fought [[Idiot Co]] in [[idiot-co-aquarium-war|the biggest war since 2021]] — a weekly peak of 5,045 territory exchanges — through mid-July 2024, with a second round from November 2024 into January 2025, spanning the Rekindled World map rework of 10 Aug 2024.{{cite:territory-exchanges|territory_exchanges map-data analysis}}

## Alliances

- [[Valhalla]] — 11 May 2021 – 28 Nov 2021 (founding member)
- [[Vanir]] — 28 Nov 2021 – 18 Dec 2021 (founding member)
- [[Cucumber Company]] — spring 2022 (Season 5) – Sep 2022{{cite:titantimes-github-io-vol1|vol. 1, 8 May 2022}}
- [[smtn elf]] — Sep 2022 – Jun 2023 (departed mid-June){{cite:territory-exchanges|territory_exchanges map-data analysis}}
- [[Avocado Aquarium]] — Jun 2023 – ~Feb 2024

**The Banhammer**'s thread — carrying the guild subforum's Alliance prefix and titled "The Banhammer (anti-hax)" — was posted on 8 June 2016 by Mr_Robin_Hood{{cite:forums-wynncraft-com-forums-guilds-72-prefix-id-82-order-post-date-direction-asc|thread listing: Mr_Robin_Hood, 8 Jun 2016}}, and proposed one universal non-aggression pact so that smaller guilds could hold land against a guild then holding 94 territories.{{cite:thread-130386|post #1, opened 8 Jun 2016, last revised 11 Jun 2016}} It belongs to [[early-guild-era|the early guild era]].

## History

The opening post set out both the grievance and the mechanism: Hax held 94 territories and was too strong for any single guild, so the guilds should unite in one alliance whose members would not attack each other, letting weaker guilds hold land.{{cite:thread-130386|post #1, opened 8 Jun 2016, last revised 11 Jun 2016}} Guild owners were asked to open a forum conversation with Mr_Robin_Hood and would then be "added to a special alliance conversation" — the alliance's only recorded organ.{{cite:thread-130386|post #1, opened 8 Jun 2016, last revised 11 Jun 2016}} An attached poll ran from "yes hax is ruining the guild experience for me" to "no you are just salty and mad"; its results were visible only to voters and were never published.{{cite:thread-130386|thread poll options, from the page markup}} The thread was also renamed on the evening it was posted; its earlier title is not known.{{cite:thread-130386|post #16, 8 Jun 2016}}

The response from the [[hackforums|HackForums]] side was immediate. "Wtf is this NATO you cant take down mother russia," Shawarma replied the same afternoon. "I will not stop till every inch of wynncraft is mine or my allies, my allies will attack you or be attacked if they revolt."{{cite:thread-130386|post #5, 8 Jun 2016}} Defenders argued the thread was simple resentment: "viva hax and screw all the haters. If you cant get and hold land, dont hate on those who can", wrote qqcora, who described the rival Astrafterí Affinity alliance as "the alliance i created" and said it "has over 10 guilds, and every single one of them would back hax up".{{cite:thread-130386-p02|post #23, qqcora, 8 Jun 2016; and post #30, quoting qqcora, 9 Jun 2016}}{{cite:forums-wynncraft-com-forums-guilds-72-prefix-id-82-order-post-date-direction-asc|thread listing: Astrafterí Affinity, LordMuttonChops, 3 Apr 2016}} Mr_Robin_Hood answered that the alliance was not aimed at the guild's members: "it was never intended to hate on HAX i know they are a hard working and honourfull guild. its just that i think the smaller guilds could really benifit from an alliance because of how strong hax is right now."{{cite:thread-130386-p02|post #26, 9 Jun 2016}} Cybersoap707, who had founded the Morsus Mihi alliance a month earlier{{cite:forums-wynncraft-com-forums-guilds-72-prefix-id-82-order-post-date-direction-asc|thread listing: Morsus Mihi, Cybersoap707, 3 May 2016}}, offered the only coordination scheme anyone proposed: "If we get big enough, I think I might calculate and distribute territory areas equally to each guild in which they target any Hax Territories."{{cite:thread-130386|post #17, 8 Jun 2016}}

### The mid-June reversal

Within ten days the thread's participants were reporting that HackForums had collapsed on the map. "As of now Hax has ONE territory," wrote WhettoWen, a member of the Hax-allied guild UltimateXeons, on 16 June{{cite:thread-130386-p03|post #60, 16 Jun 2016}}, and on 18 June: "Hax is dead. They own no territory."{{cite:thread-130386-p04|post #77, 18 Jun 2016}} Buttless, a chief of World of Crafters, congratulated "everyone who participated in the alliance".{{cite:thread-130386-p04|post #63, 16 Jun 2016}}

The cause was disputed at the time. Cybersoap707 credited two member guilds — "All the Credit goes to Mythic Zodiac and The Crafters, when my guild was ready to attack we encountered a glitch that prevented us from attacking, it was fixed recently when Hax was already destroyed" — then narrowed the claim next day: "When I said destroyed i meant that Hax was basically put out of power. They're still a very powerful guild and in the top 5, but don't have over 90 territories anymore."{{cite:thread-130386-p04|post #78, 18 Jun 2016}}{{cite:thread-130386-p05|post #81, 19 Jun 2016}} Goden credited a guild outside the alliance: "Nether's Ascent took over Hax recently... and they've been able to hold the map decently well."{{cite:thread-130386-p05|post #94, 21 Jun 2016}} Yuno F Gasai of [[imperial|Imperial]] dismissed the episode: "So you took over for a couple of days, try to hold it forever now."{{cite:thread-130386-p04|post #79, 19 Jun 2016}} By 23 September 2016 HackForums held 146 of the map's roughly 332 territories, more than at any point in June.{{cite:pre2018-territory-snapshots|pre2018-territory-snapshots.json — Wayback Machine capture of the Wynncraft v1 API guild leaderboard, 23 Sep 2016}}

## Membership

The opening post listed three member guilds — World of Crafters [WoC], The Wynn Guard [TwG] and MythicZodiac [MZS]{{cite:thread-130386|post #1, opened 8 Jun 2016, last revised 11 Jun 2016}} — of which only WoC and TwG appear in the version of the post quoted on 8 June, so MythicZodiac joined during the thread's first days; the same early version carried a line, later removed, asking Hax members not to reply.{{cite:thread-130386-p02|post #18, quoting the opening post, 8 Jun 2016}} On 20 June a poster added the tag "DragonSlayers [WkD]" beneath a quotation of the roster, but the opening post was never revised to include it.{{cite:thread-130386-p05|post #92, 20 Jun 2016}} No further roster was published, and no leader other than Mr_Robin_Hood was ever named. Territory exchanges were not recorded until January 2018, long after the alliance's time.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}

## Legacy

The thread went quiet after 29 June 2016, and no dissolution was ever announced.{{cite:thread-130386-p06|post #101, 29 Jun 2016}} The next day a [[hackforums|HackForums]] chief remarked in a different thread that "right now there is 2 anti hax alliances"{{cite:thread-134638|post #13, 30 Jun 2016}}; the Banhammer and [[the-separatists|The Separatists]], posted eighteen days apart, appear to be the two. The community's verdict on such efforts was already settled before either began: "It didn't work, all those guilds no longer have any territory", an UltimateXeons member wrote of the previous attempts.{{cite:thread-133666|post #10, 27 Jun 2016}}

## The doctrine

On 11 November 2018, the day after [[the-federation-dies|the Federation came apart]], an Aesir leader told the whole alliance that the object was to hold the land richest in experience rather than to dominate the map, and that the alliance wanted other guilds to prosper elsewhere.{{cite:after-the-federation-2018-discord-private|11 Nov 2018}} The ground in question was the Canyon of the Lost, divided among the three guilds the previous day, each taking named clusters along a three-way split begun in council eleven days earlier.{{cite:aesir-pact-2018-discord-private|territory division, 10 Nov 2018}}{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-founded-2018-discord-private|the Canyon division, 30 Oct 2018}} On the morning of 11 November the pact's guilds held seventeen of the region's eighteen territories, ten of them [[fantasy|Fantasy]]'s, which had rejoined the pact the day before; the eighteenth, Canyon High Path, was held by [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] and stayed with it throughout.{{cite:territory-exchanges|holder of each of the eighteen territories named Canyon …, taken from the last exchange on each before 11 Nov 2018 08:00 UTC: Fantasy 10, DiamondDeities 7, Kingdom Foxes 1}}{{cite:aesir-membership-record-2018-discord-private|10 Nov 2018}}

## No declared open ground

Alliances published lists of territories open to anyone. Through this winter there was none in force. The Federation's declaration of 12 October 2018 was the last free-for-all list it issued before it dissolved on 10 November{{cite:free-for-all-amendments-2018-2019-discord-private|the declaration of 12 Oct 2018}}{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Drew1011, 10/12/2018}}, and nothing replaced it until [[terra|Terra]] declared eighteen territories on 14 September 2019.{{cite:terra-alliance-announcements-discord-private|14 Sep 2019}} No Canyon territory appears on the October list.{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Drew1011, 10/12/2018}}

The Aesir Pact published no such list at any point in its life.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|free-for-all mentions across the pact's channels, whole life of the server}} Free-for-alls in its channels were bilateral, unwritten and confined to single territories, and a draft treaty with [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]] that would have opened six named territories between the two alliances failed on the morning it was put, 28 December 2018.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|16 Nov and 28 Dec 2018}} On 22 January 2019 a member-guild leader asked the council what free-for-alls were, and nothing in the channel answers him.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|22 Jan 2019}}

What the pact published instead named guilds rather than ground: two lists, six enemies and eight guilds under truce, closed by a residual rule that any guild on neither might be attacked freely.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|the card's lists and residual clause, text of 1 Jan 2019}} That is a different instrument from a free-for-all list, and is set out at [[the-aesir-oligarchy|The Aesir oligarchy]]. The figures below are therefore raw counts, no ground having been declared open.

## The December campaign

[[blacklisted|Blacklisted]] first appears in the alliance's channels on 16 December 2018.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|16 Dec 2018}} From the 19th members reported that it and [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] were attacking them in concert, and the leader of [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] offered to hand two of his guild's experience territories to other Aesir guilds so that Blacklisted should not gain by taking them.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|19 Dec 2018}} On the 21st he said Blacklisted was the first guild to have kept his guild off the map for any length of time; the alliance's negotiator answered that the attacks were a response to Aesir's own.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|21 Dec 2018}}

The eighteen Canyon territories changed hands 1,047 times between 16 and 31 December, 402 of those captures made by Aesir guilds.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges on the eighteen Canyon-named territories, 16–31 Dec 2018 UTC, raw: 1,047 total, 402 by Fantasy, Sins of Seedia, IceBlue Team, DiamondDeities or TheNoLifes}} The heaviest days were 20 December, with 138 exchanges, and 29 December, with 161.{{cite:territory-exchanges|daily exchange counts on the same eighteen territories, Dec 2018 UTC}}

## Olympus

The alliance that took the Canyon from Aesir was one Aesir had itself been invited to join. On 25 December 2018 a leader of DiamondDeities told the council that her guild had joined a second alliance, Olympus, in order to bring Valkyrie down; that she had not wanted to join and had stayed loyal to Aesir; and that it felt like a betrayal of Aesir for not helping the two guilds under attack.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|25 Dec 2018}} Her account of Olympus's membership named both of the guilds then attacking Aesir, two guilds on Aesir's enemy list, two on its truce list and one Aesir member guild besides her own.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|25 Dec 2018, the membership list}}

Four days later the council member who ran the negotiations told the alliance's warriors that because Aesir had been invited into Olympus it was obliged not to war its allies, and that those allies included BuildCraftia and Blacklisted.{{cite:aesir-olympus-two-accounts-2019-discord-private|29 Dec 2018, 12:48, export clock}} Asked what the benefit of joining was, he answered in terms of the enemy losing more.{{cite:aesir-olympus-two-accounts-2019-discord-private|29 Dec 2018}} On the 30th he told the council he had repeatedly asked inside Olympus for the Canyon to be released until Valkyrie was brought down.{{cite:aesir-olympus-two-accounts-2019-discord-private|30 Dec 2018}}

A leader of [[thenolifes|TheNoLifes]], one of the guilds Aesir's own members had named as an Olympus member, described it differently. On 3 January 2019 he said Olympus was not a real alliance in his eyes, not the way Valkyrie or Aesir were; that he had been reading its server and found only argument; that he could not point to anything at its head; and that if it was anything it was a truce pact.{{cite:aesir-olympus-two-accounts-2019-discord-private|3 Jan 2019, 23:08, export clock}} Four days before that he had said he and one other member of his guild were in the Olympus Discord server, that he had spoken in it once, and that he had joined by following an invitation posted in the Aesir server.{{cite:aesir-olympus-two-accounts-2019-discord-private|30 Dec 2018}} Neither account was answered in the council.{{cite:aesir-olympus-two-accounts-2019-discord-private|Dec 2018 – Jan 2019}} Reading the two together is this article's: membership of Olympus, in these conversations, rested on presence in a Discord server, so a guild could be counted into it without having agreed to anything.{{cite:aesir-olympus-two-accounts-2019-discord-private|30 Dec 2018, the member list}}

Nothing the alliance published mentions Olympus. The name appears in neither its announcements channel, its information channel nor its voting channel at any date, and four of the five outside guilds its own members named as Olympus stand on the truce list of its published card, the fifth on the enemy list.{{cite:aesir-olympus-two-accounts-2019-discord-private|the three announcement-tier channels, and the card as it stood after its edit of 1 Jan 2019}} DiamondDeities told the council on 1 January 2019 that it belonged to three alliances at once — Aesir, Olympus and [[sol|SOL]] — and that it did not work with Olympus much.{{cite:aesir-olympus-two-accounts-2019-discord-private|1 Jan 2019}} It left Olympus on 5 January, and the name does not appear in the alliance's channels after the 6th.{{cite:aesir-olympus-two-accounts-2019-discord-private|5–6 Jan 2019}}

## The offer of 27 December

At 00:35 on 27 December 2018 the negotiator put a written proposal to the council.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|27 Dec 2018, 00:35, export clock}} BuildCraftia and Blacklisted would not release the Canyon unless they gained by it, so Aesir should take part in the attacks on Valkyrie in exchange for the region's release; DiamondDeities could then help reclaim it, and the other side could concentrate its forces instead of dividing them against two Aesir guilds. If no such offer were made, Aesir would forfeit the region and fight on without end, and he himself could give no help, because he could not attack his own allies.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|27 Dec 2018, the proposal}}

Fifty-two minutes later the leader of IceBlue Team refused outright. He would not take part in an attack on Valkyrie, he was on good terms with several of that alliance's people, turning on them would be indefensible, he would rather lose his homeland, and his guild wished to stay neutral.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|27 Dec 2018, 01:27, export clock}} He was told that Valkyrie would never help him reclaim.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|27 Dec 2018, the answer}}

That evening the leader of TheNoLifes told the council he was worried the pact would splinter and be absorbed into Valkyrie and Olympus, IceBlue Team going one way and DiamondDeities the other.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|27 Dec 2018, the evening}} The leader of IceBlue Team answered that he was worried about the same thing and that his guild would stay as long as it could.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|27 Dec 2018, the reply}}

## Three hours and eleven minutes

The deal was made four days later in the alliance's warriors' channel, which was open to captains and above, rather than in the council.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|31 Dec 2018}}{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-membership-and-election-2019-discord-private|the warriors' channel and who was in it, 23 Nov 2018}} At 08:10 the negotiator reported the other side's terms: Blacklisted would release the Canyon if [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]] and IceBlue Team helped against Valkyrie, and a guild need not join Olympus but must attack when told to.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|31 Dec 2018, 08:10, export clock}} The leader of IceBlue Team restated that his guild wished to stay neutral and was told that Blacklisted would then keep warring him.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|31 Dec 2018, the refusal}} He offered instead to give Blacklisted a defined block of his own claims and undertook never to touch that guild's Ragni holdings, saying his experience territories were held by an ally and he did not want them back.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|31 Dec 2018, the counter-offer}}

He then asked for the approval of the other Aesir leaders. A leader of DiamondDeities gave it, four times over, and then asked what he had approved.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|31 Dec 2018, the approval}} The alliance's rule, announced to the whole membership on 16 November 2018 and restated on its published card, was that no truce with an enemy might be made without the oligarchy's approval.{{cite:aesir-and-hackforums-november-2018-discord-private|the rule reposted, 06:46 on 16 Nov 2018, export clock}}{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|the card's notes, text of 1 Jan 2019}} The alliance's leader said nothing in it.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|31 Dec 2018}}

At 08:42 the negotiator reported that Blacklisted had agreed, and that this would pressure BuildCraftia into agreeing too.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|31 Dec 2018, 08:42, export clock}} At 11:18 he told the warriors not to sweep BuildCraftia out of the Desert and the Savannah because that had been agreed, and at 11:21 he announced a ceasefire with Olympus under which BuildCraftia kept its land in the Desert and the Savannah and Blacklisted kept Ragni and part of IceBlue Team's Canyon.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|31 Dec 2018, 11:18 and 11:21, export clock}} Asked whether that meant Blacklisted would hold a named Canyon territory, he said part of it, that it was the best he could negotiate, and that IceBlue Team could negotiate the ground back by agreeing to attack Valkyrie — but that its leader had said no.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|31 Dec 2018, the closing exchange}} Three hours and eleven minutes separate the first of those messages from the last.

The handover itself is dated to the minute. Those times are the export tool's clock, which ran eight hours behind UTC in winter, putting the announcement at 19:21 UTC.{{cite:aesir-canyon-conceded-december-2018-discord-private|export clock, UTC-08:00 in winter}} Between 18:54 and 19:19 UTC four Canyon territories passed from IceBlue Team to Blacklisted — Canyon Valley South, Canyon Walk Way, Canyon Mountain East and Canyon Of The Lost — the last of them under three minutes before the announcement went out.{{cite:territory-exchanges|four exchanges, attacker Blacklisted and defender IceBlue Team, 31 Dec 2018 18:54:22–19:18:44 UTC}} Joining the two records is this article's; nobody in the conversation names a territory.

## Forty-eight hours

At 05:07 on 2 January 2019 the leader of IceBlue Team reported in the council that [[imperial|Imperial]] had taken Blacklisted's Canyon territories, and at 08:52 that Imperial wanted a piece of Aesir's as well, which he refused because the demand also reached into Sins of Seedia's land.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|2 Jan 2019, 05:07 and 08:52, export clock}} Imperial was a Valkyrie guild and no party to the 31 December ceasefire.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|2 Jan 2019}} It also stood on the truce list of the alliance's own card, in the text that card carried after its edit of 1 January 2019.{{cite:aesir-enemy-and-truce-card-2018-discord-private|the truce list, text of 1 Jan 2019}}

Three of the four territories conceded on the 31st passed from Blacklisted to Imperial between 11:16 and 11:43 UTC that morning, about an hour and a half before the report reached the council.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Canyon Valley South, Canyon Walk Way and Canyon Mountain East, attacker Imperial and defender Blacklisted, 2 Jan 2019 11:16:22–11:43:00 UTC}} The fourth had been taken back by Sins of Seedia on New Year's Day and returned to Blacklisted that evening.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Canyon Of The Lost, 1 Jan 2019 09:57:06 UTC and 2 Jan 2019 19:16:09 UTC}}

Between 08:55 and 09:03 the negotiator set out where each member guild stood: Fantasy neutral, IceBlue Team neutral, Sins of Seedia fighting HackForums rather than assisting anyone, and nobody warring Imperial.{{cite:aesir-council-after-the-concession-2019-discord-private|2 Jan 2019, 08:55–09:03, export clock}} At 09:02 a DiamondDeities member asked whether the alliance was not supposed to be defending the Canyon, and nothing in the channel answers him.{{cite:aesir-council-after-the-concession-2019-discord-private|2 Jan 2019, 09:02, export clock}} At 11:15 the account that had issued every alliance-wide instruction since 6 November 2018 wrote for Sins of Seedia rather than for the alliance: the guild was tired of being pulled towards Valkyrie and Olympus, would be neither's, and the rest could take a side if they wished.{{cite:aesir-council-after-the-concession-2019-discord-private|2 Jan 2019, 11:15, export clock}} Within three minutes Fantasy and IceBlue Team had each restated that they were staying neutral.{{cite:aesir-council-after-the-concession-2019-discord-private|2 Jan 2019, 11:18, export clock}} That is the last day the account posted in any of the pact's channels; when a leadership election was opened seven weeks later, the reason given to the alliance was that the leader had gone from the game.{{cite:aesir-council-after-the-concession-2019-discord-private|2 Jan and 22 Feb 2019}}

The alliance's guilds took no Canyon territory at all on 2 or 3 January, while the eighteen changed hands 109 times.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges on the eighteen Canyon-named territories, 2–3 Jan 2019 UTC: 109, none by an Aesir member guild}} From 2 to 18 January they took ten out of 218.{{cite:territory-exchanges|same measure, 2–18 Jan 2019 UTC: 218 exchanges, 10 by an Aesir member guild}}

## The council of 8 January

At 15:53 on 8 January 2019 a leader of DiamondDeities asked the council whether Aesir was still allowed to own the Canyon, or simply had to accept that it was Imperial's.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|8 Jan 2019, 15:53, export clock}} A leader of Fantasy answered that there was peace and no reason to disturb it; the visiting chief of Kingdom Foxes argued that letting a guild expand unchallenged only makes it harder to deal with later.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|8 Jan 2019, the exchange}} The Fantasy leader answered that attacking would achieve nothing in any case, because IceBlue Team had that same day reclassified itself as a community guild, Sins of Seedia was off attacking the Jungle, and Imperial would fight through subsidiary guilds.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|8 Jan 2019, the reasons}} There was nothing to do but wait to be attacked, he concluded, and then proposed that the alliance, being in his description a community alliance, should host an event.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|8 Jan 2019, the event proposal}}

That evening the same Fantasy leader proposed dismantling the rule that had made Aesir an alliance rather than five guilds, arguing that its truce with HackForums should be treated as never having been made and that members should go back to arranging matters guild by guild.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|8 Jan 2019, 20:24, export clock}} Nothing was resolved; the exchange is at [[the-aesir-oligarchy|The Aesir oligarchy]].

## Removed from the claims

At 05:36 on 18 January 2019 the council member who kept the alliance's territorial map asked whether they were giving up the Canyon, and said that if so he would formally remove DiamondDeities' four Canyon territories from its claims.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|18 Jan 2019, 05:36, export clock}} A leader of Fantasy answered that his guild would hold what it currently held there; asked what about the rest, nobody answered him.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|18 Jan 2019, the question}} At 06:51 he removed the four territories and marked the region lost.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|18 Jan 2019, 06:51, export clock}} Eight minutes later the leader of IceBlue Team said that accepting the deal with Imperial had been a mistake; the terms of that deal were never written down.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|18 Jan 2019, 06:59, export clock}} Later the same day a leader of DiamondDeities said they would have held the territories had anyone else held theirs.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|18 Jan 2019, later}}

No vote was taken and nothing went to the alliance's voting channel.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|18 Jan 2019}} By that morning the alliance's guilds held none of the eighteen territories, and since 6 January had held any of them at the start of only one day.{{cite:territory-exchanges|holder of each of the eighteen Canyon-named territories at 00:00 UTC daily, 6–18 Jan 2019: an Aesir guild holds one on 15 Jan and none on any other day; at 18 Jan 08:00 UTC the region stood Imperial 9, Blacklisted 7, Kingdom Foxes 1, BuildCraftia 1}} The deletion recorded a position the map had reached twelve days earlier.

Five weeks later, on 22 February, the leader of IceBlue Team asked in the warriors' channel whether he should attack what he by then called his guild's old Canyon claims, and was encouraged to; he replied that he had no reason to attack Kingdom Foxes' ground but did have a reason to attack Imperial.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|22 Feb 2019}}

## Never announced

The alliance's announcements channel carried its last instruction to the whole membership on 27 November 2018, a call to help [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] against Exorcism.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|announcements channel, 27 Nov 2018}} In the eighty-eight days from then to 23 February 2019 the channel carried seven messages: two greetings on 28 December, three on 2 January which are a member guild's advertisement for its own award ceremony and the alliance leader's emoji beneath it, and two on 23 February announcing a new leader and inviting the membership to a different Discord server.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|announcements channel, 27 Nov 2018 – 23 Feb 2019}} The voting channel carried ten messages in its entire life, of which only two were votes actually put: a change in the terms of the truce with Titans Valor on 15 December 2018, and the leadership election of 22 February 2019.{{cite:aesir-canyon-abandoned-january-2019-discord-private|voting channel, whole life of the server}} The concession was announced to the warriors; the loss of the region was announced to nobody.

## The deletion

At 04:19 UTC on 12 January 2018 a member of [[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]] asked in his guild's channel why he had just been thrown out of the [[coalition|Coalition]]'s Discord; a second asked the same, and the guild's sitting leader answered that the server had been deleted and that they were making it again.{{cite:coalition-discord-deleted-january-2018-discord-private|12 Jan 2018, 04:19:04 UTC}} The replacement Coalition server was created at 04:19:40 UTC and its general channel opened seven seconds later; every other channel of it began within the following hour.{{cite:coalition-discord-deleted-january-2018-discord-private|server creation 04:19:40 UTC, first general-channel message 04:19:47 UTC, 12 Jan 2018}}

The times are universal; the guild's channel stamped its own eight hours behind them that month, which puts the whole exchange on the evening of 11 January — the date the alliance's founder gave four months afterwards.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|L1022, part 17, composed 11 Jul 2018}}

In the hours before it the same former monarch had removed about fifteen of the guild's members in the game, and others were asking one another in the channel what was happening.{{cite:coalition-discord-deleted-january-2018-discord-private|11 Jan 2018, the removals}} The guild's announcement channel carried the removals that day and its leadership spent it inviting the members back.{{cite:kingdomphoenixes-announcements|1/11/2018}}

## The reason, and an invitation to attack

A minute after the deletion the guild's former monarch told the channel that she had done it, and gave as her reason a member of the guild's own leadership.{{cite:coalition-discord-deleted-january-2018-discord-private|12 Jan 2018, 04:20 UTC}} In her next message she addressed a member of another guild directly and told him this was a good moment to attack the Coalition, because she had deleted its Discord for that purpose.{{cite:coalition-discord-deleted-january-2018-discord-private|12 Jan 2018, the invitation}} Whether he took it up went unrecorded.

## Reversed within the hour

Fifty-six minutes after the deletion she apologised in the same channel, said she had removed the members earlier while angry about something that had happened among the guild's leaders, asked those affected to seek reinstatement through the guild's parliament, and said they would have no further trouble from her.{{cite:coalition-discord-deleted-january-2018-discord-private|12 Jan 2018, 05:16 UTC}} The sitting leader recorded shortly afterwards that everyone removed had been invited back.{{cite:coalition-discord-deleted-january-2018-discord-private|12 Jan 2018, the reinvitations}}

The following afternoon members set the affair out for those who had missed it as a change of leadership: the former leader had wanted the position back, the sitting leader had refused, and the alliance's server had gone.{{cite:coalition-discord-deleted-january-2018-discord-private|13 Jan 2018, the account given in the channel}} The Coalition's founder was in the channel while they did it, and corrected one detail, that the guild itself had not been deleted; he called the destruction of his alliance's Discord a mislaying.{{cite:coalition-discord-deleted-january-2018-discord-private|13 Jan 2018, the founder's correction}} Ten days later members were told in the same channel to stop discussing the incident. What both alliances did about who could read their channels the following month is at [[alliance-channel-security-february-2018|alliance channel security]].{{cite:kingdomphoenixes-announcements|1/21/2018}}

## What went with the server

Every channel of the replacement began that night, and nothing the alliance had written in its first three months went into it.{{cite:coalition-discord-deleted-january-2018-discord-private|the replacement's channels, 12 Jan 2018}} That was the whole of the campaign that made the alliance: it had been founded in October 2017{{cite:gavel-invasion-2017-discord-private|28 Feb 2018}}, and by the end of Christmas Day it held all 349 territories on the map.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 15, composed 20 May 2018}}

What the replacement carried instead was an information card, put up at 21:33 that evening on the same clock, 05:33 UTC on the 12th.{{cite:coalition-information|1/11/2018 9:33 PM}} Sixty-eight minutes earlier the alliance's leader had pasted its text into the general channel of a server then minutes old; the material had been saved as soon as the first one was threatened.{{cite:coalition-standing-documents-january-2018-discord-private|11 Jan 2018, 20:25 and 21:33}} That text named fifteen member guilds and eleven enemies.{{cite:coalition-standing-documents-january-2018-discord-private|the card as posted, 11 Jan 2018}} The card was kept current in place and last edited on 14 February 2018, so the fourteen members and fourteen enemies it now carries belong to the alliance's last week rather than to its first night — three of the guilds on its member list were admitted on 16 January, 1 February and 12 February.{{cite:coalition-information|1/11/2018, revised 14 Feb 2018}}{{cite:coalition-announcements|1/16/2018, 2/1/2018 and 2/12/2018}} Five days after the rebuild [[fantasy|Fantasy]], a member on the January text and an enemy on the February one, opened the week of fighting its participants named [[the-trench-war|the Trench War]].{{cite:territory-exchanges|first Fantasy–Kingdom Foxes exchange, 16 Jan 2018 01:43 UTC}}{{cite:coalition-standing-documents-january-2018-discord-private|20 Jan 2018, the enemy list}} The alliance broke apart on 16 February 2018.{{cite:coalition-fracture-2018-discord-private|Coalition guild-leaders channel, 16 Feb 2018}}

## Formation

The alliance was organised in the second week of March 2019 against the [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]] bloc, which warred that spring as [[luna|Luna]].{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} Its organiser founded it because he judged the alliance already opposing Valkyrie to be badly run, and too closely held by a few of its leaders to be useful.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|founding discussion, March 2019}} Asked directly what the new alliance was for, its members answered that it existed to oppose Valkyrie, and gave no other purpose.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|alliance general channel, March 2019}}

Recruitment was fast: at least a dozen member guilds joined in the first three weeks, and new ones were still being admitted days before the end.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|admissions, 11 March – 2 April 2019}} On 23 March 2019 the alliance published a formal roster naming seven enemy guilds and eleven allied ones.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|published roster, 23 Mar 2019}}

## Out of the Aesir Pact

The Consortium's organiser was a chief of [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] who had held the office of viceroy in the Valkyrie alliance until standing down on 7 February 2019, and who had been visiting the [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]]'s council since 1 January, and was one of only two members of that council to agree to [[the-aesir-election|its leadership election]] of 22 February.{{cite:aesir-into-the-consortium-march-2019-discord-private|the organiser's offices, 1 Jan - 22 Feb 2019}} The invitation to the new alliance's leaders channel went out at 17:09 on 1 March 2019, six days after that election, and its channels were created the same night.{{cite:aesir-into-the-consortium-march-2019-discord-private|1 Mar 2019, 17:09 export clock; the channels created 2 Mar UTC}} Asked on 3 March how an alliance was started, the organiser answered that one found a second guild to found it alongside, and that the only guild in mind was [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]] — the guild whose leader had won the Aesir election eight days before.{{cite:aesir-into-the-consortium-march-2019-discord-private|3 Mar 2019}}

The founding circle was the Aesir Pact's. By 10 March [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] was being invited as well, and on the evening of the 13th the question whether to bring in [[thenolifes|TheNoLifes]] was put to exactly three people: IceBlue Team's leader and both of the representatives the Aesir card named for DiamondDeities, one of them by then the elected head of the Aesir Pact.{{cite:aesir-into-the-consortium-march-2019-discord-private|10-13 Mar 2019; the three placed from the Aesir alliance-information card, a text of 1 Dec 2018 at the latest}} TheNoLifes was in by the following midday, and on 14 March the organiser told a prospective member that the Aesir guilds had been approached first because of the trust built between them over the Federation's nine months, and that the difficulty was recruiting further, Aesir alone not being enough to bring [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]] down.{{cite:aesir-into-the-consortium-march-2019-discord-private|14 Mar 2019}}

The alliance's information channel carried a membership card from 07:38 on 24 March 2019 naming twelve member guilds, all five of Aesir's among them.{{cite:aesir-into-the-consortium-march-2019-discord-private|the membership card, 07:38 on 24 Mar 2019, export clock}} Its last edit was made at 15:48 on 29 March, so its contents are undated between the two dates; one entry can be fixed to the edit, because on the evening of the 27th a member told the alliance's leader that the card would need updating now that [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]] had joined.{{cite:aesir-into-the-consortium-march-2019-discord-private|the card's edit of 29 Mar 2019 and the exchange of 27 Mar}}

Sins of Seedia had been described in its own alliance's rooms on 23 February as gone, its war team dissolved.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-membership-and-election-2019-discord-private|23 Feb 2019}} Its application, relayed into the Consortium's voting channel at 03:06 on 27 March by the alliance's leader, came from a new leader of the guild writing under a revival tag, and gave as its reason that the whole of the original Aesir membership was already inside — the applicant's own characterisation, though the Consortium's card bears it out.{{cite:aesir-into-the-consortium-march-2019-discord-private|the application, 03:06 on 27 Mar 2019, export clock}} [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]], which the Aesir Pact had carried since November 2018 as a friend rather than a member, applied in the same period and was rejected on 25 March, with the possibility of readmission later.{{cite:aesir-into-the-consortium-march-2019-discord-private|25 Mar 2019}}

[[fantasy|Fantasy]] was the Aesir guild whose condition was in doubt through the month. On 22 March members of the Consortium's leaders channel could not agree whether it was still active, and a worry that it might split off into a guild friendly to Valkyrie was contradicted in the same minute.{{cite:aesir-into-the-consortium-march-2019-discord-private|22 Mar 2019, Fantasy's condition}} Fantasy nevertheless stands on the membership card of 24 March, and its card-listed leader was one of the two people addressed on 27 March about Sins of Seedia's return to Aesir.{{cite:aesir-into-the-consortium-march-2019-discord-private|24 and 27 Mar 2019}}

## The campaign

War priorities were set out explicitly. Valkyrie was the primary target; two other standing conflicts were set aside, one leader describing them as personal grudges that would collapse of their own accord once Valkyrie fell.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|war priorities, March 2019}} The first coordinated offensive opened on 23 March against the weakest guild of the opposing alliance, the plan being to take Valkyrie's guilds one at a time and work up to the strongest.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|offensive of 23 Mar 2019}} [[imperial|Imperial]] was the principal enemy, named on the published roster alongside six other guilds, and the alliance called all its members to war on it at once.{{cite:foxes-imperial-2019-discord-private|March 2019}}

The roster bound nobody for long. [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] was on the same enemies list and was exempted from it within days, members being told not to attack it while it was helping to reclaim a territory from Imperial — a guild formally listed as an enemy and protected for its usefulness against the alliance's main target in the same week.{{cite:foxes-imperial-2019-discord-private|March 2019}} By the 27th its members put the campaign at roughly a million emeralds drained from the principal target — the measure of a war fought against treasuries rather than armies.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|war-record estimate, 27 Mar 2019}}{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|campaigns measured in emeralds drained, 2018–2019}}

The alliance changed hands on the day the offensive opened. Its organiser stepped back from directing it after a dispute with the guild he was attacking, handing responsibility to a leader who recorded misgivings about accepting it.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|change of leadership, 23 Mar 2019}} Two days later private discussion from the leadership channel reached a rival alliance's leader, and the channel was declared compromised.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|leak, 25 Mar 2019}} The same day a guild's application was rejected unanimously once it emerged that the applicant had helped Valkyrie during the Consortium's own weekend offensive.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|rejected application, 25 Mar 2019}}

## Dissolution

One founding guild stopped functioning during the campaign after its leader stepped away, and the disengagement spread outward from the guilds that had worked with it.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|internal assessment, late March 2019}} Departures began on 26 March, when a member guild declared neutrality and left, and continued on 1 April with a founding guild that withdrew to pursue neutrality rather than further attacks.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|departures, 26 Mar and 1 Apr 2019}} On 30 March the alliance's leader concluded that Imperial could not be beaten by the means available, given its treasury and its active fighting force, and proposed several months of rebuilding before another attempt.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|leadership assessment, 30 Mar 2019}}{{cite:foxes-imperial-2019-discord-private|late March 2019}} The campaign had been measured in money rather than ground throughout: a member circulated an estimate, derived from a stats database rather than from observation, that Imperial had been drained of roughly a million emeralds.{{cite:foxes-imperial-2019-discord-private|March 2019}} Asked that week whether the Consortium yet counted as a mega alliance, a leader answered that it did not, because about half its member guilds were not strong enough, but that it was approaching the threshold.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|leadership exchange, late March 2019}}

On 2 April 2019 the alliance voted in a new class of membership — a Distant Ally status giving the leaders of three affiliated guilds a formal role and access to its war-discussion channel — in the same hours that its members were arguing about whether it had already been dissolved.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|Distant Ally vote and dissolution argument, 2 Apr 2019}} Leadership had passed through three hands in three weeks and nobody would take it further: a longtime member stated that the serving leader was giving up and winding the alliance down, while a representative of a guild admitted days earlier argued it could still be salvaged.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 2 Apr 2019}} No later activity of the alliance was ever recorded.

## Legacy

The Consortium is the shortest complete alliance life cycle of the [[the-alliance-wars-of-2019|alliance wars of 2019]]: three weeks from organisation to dissolution, against a bloc that outlasted it by years.{{cite:consortium-2019-discord-private|March–April 2019}}{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} Its last institutional act, creating a rank of membership on the day it broke up, has a close parallel in the [[emperium-of-wynn|Emperium of Wynn]], which published a five-tier rewrite of its power structure while its council sat unfilled.{{cite:emperium-of-wynn-constitution-2017-discord-private|constitutional rewrite, late September 2017}}

## The record

The guild's founding date was never recorded, no disbandment was ever recorded, and nothing places it after October 2018.{{cite:divine-swords-guild-profile-discord-private|the silence in the record}} Its name is also a persistent source of confusion: Divine Souls and The Divine Swords are different guilds whose tags differ by a letter, and by August 2018 the older name had passed back to the parent.{{cite:divine-swords-guild-profile-discord-private|naming, 2017–2018}}

## History

### A subguild, and a leader who did not fight
The guild began beneath another. Members recount that Divine Souls had grown full and its leader created The Divine Swords under it, putting a member other than its most active fighters in charge of the new guild from the start.{{cite:divine-swords-guild-profile-discord-private|founding}} That arrangement shaped everything after it: its council later chose a leader over the two members who were doing most of its fighting, and one of them was still saying so a year afterwards.{{cite:divine-swords-guild-profile-discord-private|leadership disputes, 2017–2018}}

The tag was a visual pun on the parent's. Asked in the war channel on 6 August 2018 why theirs ended in a Q, a member answered that the letter had been chosen because its shape was the closest thing to the parent's O.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|6 Aug 2018, the tag}} The two names were given in the same exchange: the parent Divine Souls, the guild itself The Divine Swords.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|6 Aug 2018, the two names}} A second member gave the reason for the subguild three weeks later and independently, in a leadership-channel conversation on 29 August 2018 in which several guild leaders were recalling why their guilds had been founded at all: the parent had filled up, and one of its members had thought a recruitment guild would be a good idea.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|29 Aug 2018, the overflow}} Neither telling dates the founding.

The parent guild was disbanded around mid-2017 after its leadership proved unable to remove a disruptive member, and the person who disbanded it became the effective owner of the guild that survived it; leaders separately said that the parent's old leader had spied for one alliance against another before the end.{{cite:divine-swords-guild-profile-discord-private|accounts given in 2018}} Both of those come from people who were party to them, one of them the person who did the disbanding.

### Six leaders in a year
Its leadership was contested from the beginning and never settled. A new in-game leader took the role in mid-June 2018 with a co-leader installed alongside him; by early August the guild had three leaders at once, its previous leader having stepped down and handed ownership to another member while a third became co-leader; and days after that, one of the two members who had done most of its fighting stated that he was no longer a leader at all.{{cite:divine-swords-guild-profile-discord-private|June–August 2018}} Members put it at its fifth or sixth leader that month, and the sequence runs through at least six holders in a single year.{{cite:divine-swords-guild-profile-discord-private|August 2018}} In September [[the-federation|the Federation]] was querying how one person had come to hold two of its leadership positions at once.{{cite:divine-swords-guild-profile-discord-private|September 2018}}

One of the earlier changes had been a ballot, and two of the people who voted in it described how. The member who lost it said on 6 August 2018 that the outgoing leader had not picked him, that only two members had voted for him, and put the result at about three to six.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|6 Aug 2018, the result}} One voter said he had had no idea whom to pick and had gone with whichever option already had more marks on it.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|6 Aug 2018, the first voter}} The other checked the voting channel, found he had apparently voted for the losing candidate, and allowed that he might have added his mark afterwards; the losing candidate answered that it had definitely been afterwards.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|6 Aug 2018, the second voter}} What a count read off the marks left on a ballot measures is at [[alliance-governance|alliance governance]].

### A preferred target
It held territory in the Federation's assignments through 2018, including a transfer from another member in February made so that guilds further south could move their holdings northward.{{cite:divine-swords-guild-profile-discord-private|February 2018}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018}} It was also attacked more than most: seven guilds hit its desert holdings simultaneously at the end of May 2018 and took essentially all of them, it was described as the rival alliance's most persistent target and reported being wiped several times a day, and in August a single player from a splinter guild wiped its assigned territories overnight while none of its members were online.{{cite:divine-swords-guild-profile-discord-private|May–August 2018}} On 5 August 2018 the alliance was told what the pattern was. A member reported in the war channel that the guild's territory was under attack and named [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]]; at 18:17 he tagged the war role to say they were under attack from Hall of Fame, [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] and two smaller guilds.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|5 Aug 2018, the war channel}} Eighteen minutes later he asked that the alliance take on Hall of Fame rather than the small guilds, which they could handle themselves.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|5 Aug 2018, which attacker}} Asked why an enemy would trouble to respect the alliance's assignments, he answered that they took the alliance guild by guild, and that this guild was first.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|5 Aug 2018, guild by guild}} On 6 August it lost twenty-three territories off the free-for-all list to six guilds, eight to Hall of Fame and five to BuildCraftia, and over the week that followed twenty-six different guilds took contested ground from it.{{cite:territory-exchanges|The Divine Swords contested losses by attacker, 6 Aug 2018 and 6–12 Aug 2018 UTC, free-for-all list of 6 Aug 2018 excluded: 23 to six guilds, then 26 distinct attackers. The 5 August figures are not filterable, the list then in force having been amended twice since publication}}

In the first six days of September 2018 it lost 342 territories to twenty-two different attacking guilds, and took 342 back.{{cite:territory-exchanges|The Divine Swords captures and losses, 1–6 Sep 2018 UTC, raw — the declaration in force was the map-only one of 30 August 2018, whose territory names were never written out, so no free-for-all ground can be subtracted}} [[the-federation|The Federation]]'s war channel recorded it being wiped at the start of that month, twice inside two days, and a member who asked why an attacking guild suddenly held twenty territories was told it was because it had wiped this guild and another member.{{cite:federation-nobody-came-september-2018-discord-private|3–4 Sep 2018}}

### The last guild standing

By the end of August the guild had inherited the position of the two that walked out of the alliance on 24 August. Late on the 26th a member said in the war channel that his guild was now the rival coalition's main target, and another answered that they would go on knocking guilds down until they reached one big enough to stop them.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the main target}} Its attacker that week was not the enemy alliance but a guild newly formed out of the Federation's own members: on the morning of the 26th a member told the leadership channel that somebody should tell its leader to stop attacking Federation guilds if he wanted to be let in.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the complaint}} A guild leader answered that the new guild did not want to attack free-for-all territory, because it was dull and yielded no experience.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|26 Aug 2018, the reason given}} Two days later a recruit of a fortnight asked to leave, was let go, and was in that guild by the afternoon, attacking the one he had left.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|28 Aug 2018, the recruit}}

### Planning its own collapse
In late May 2018, while still a going concern, its members discussed openly that the guild might not survive the summer, and proposed that other guilds be allowed to reserve its members for recruitment in advance.{{cite:divine-swords-guild-profile-discord-private|late May 2018}} No other guild in this period is known to have arranged the orderly distribution of its own people before it died.

It advertised once more at the end. On 1 October 2018 a member asked publicly in the Federation's leadership channel whether anyone wanted to merge with the guild, and another member answered that he did.{{cite:federation-october-2018-discord-private|1 Oct 2018}} No merger followed in the record. In the map discussion of 11 October a leader argued against reducing the guild's holdings, while arguing for reductions elsewhere.{{cite:federation-october-2018-discord-private|11 Oct 2018}}

By late June alliance members were calling it dead, its remaining fighters having moved into another member guild, with an attempt at revival under way that nobody rated; by early July it held no assigned territory at all.{{cite:divine-swords-guild-profile-discord-private|June–July 2018}} When the revival took hold is not settled: the alliance had the attempt under way in late June, while a member's arithmetic on 9 October 2018 put it about two months earlier, which would be the beginning of August.{{cite:divine-swords-origins-and-siege-2018-discord-private|9 Oct 2018, about two months}} Its people did not disperse so much as relocate together: a member of the guild they joined claimed in July 2018 that his own guild had effectively been taken over, putting roughly eighty per cent of its active fighting force at former Divine Swords people.{{cite:divine-swords-guild-profile-discord-private|July 2018}}{{cite:sins-of-seedia-guild-profile-discord-private|mid-to-late 2018}}

### The expulsion
The Federation expelled the guild at the end of October 2018, on discovering that it was being controlled through the alt account of somebody no longer affiliated with the alliance, its principal fighters having defected to guilds outside it; a leaders' vote produced a majority to remove it, and several of its own members left in the same period saying they no longer felt welcome.{{cite:divine-swords-guild-profile-discord-private|30 Oct 2018}}{{cite:federation-collapse-2018-discord-private|Federation leadership channels, 29–30 Oct 2018}} It was one of the departures that emptied the alliance in the fortnight before [[the-federation-dies|it dissolved]].

A guild can survive being attacked, and this one was attacked more than most. What it could never answer was who it belonged to.

The **Federation** (tag **Fed**) was the dominant war alliance of 2018 on Wynncraft — the largest alliance the game had yet seen, holding the entire territory map for eight months and twenty-five days before collapsing in a single day on 10 Nov 2018. Founded and unofficially led by [[Drew1011]], it grew to 37–40 member guilds and gave its name to the [[federation-era|Federation era]].{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, Drew1011, 13 Nov 2018}}

## History

### Formation
The Federation was born from the fracture of the [[Coalition]]. On 16 Feb 2018 the Coalition published a guild-by-guild division of the province of Wynn; within an hour five guilds left and formed [[Alliance Alliance]] with two others, and held the map by nightfall{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 17, composed 11 Jul 2018}}. The Coalition remnant merged with the remains of War Syndicate — the power the Coalition had wiped the previous Christmas — into a new alliance, founded and led by Drew1011.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} Members dated the founding the same way from the other end: in mid-October 2018 they marked the alliance's eight-month anniversary, putting its beginning in mid-February without reference to any account written at the time.{{cite:coalition-last-fortnight-discord-private|mid-Oct 2018}}

![The Discord @everyone announcement of 16 February 2018 setting out the Coalition's guild-by-guild division of Wynn, from HackForums' allocation down to Immortalish's single region. The division assigned every region to a guild and left the free-for-all territories undecided; the alliance's earlier maps had drawn free-for-all ground in black. Its publication triggered the split that produced the Federation.](/images/chronicles/storytime/58-52bb6b02a8a9f0906e04907a12ab07bd.webp){{cite:drew1011-storytime|announcement of 16 Feb 2018, part 17}}

Drew1011 wrote that the merger was his own proposal, that he first messaged BuildCraftia's co-leader without response, and that [[simplyinfinity|simplyinfinity_]] of Sins of Seedia was the first to accept{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 17, composed 11 Jul 2018}}{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, Drew1011, 13 Nov 2018}}. In his farewell address of February 2019 he added that he had acted without authority: "with no permission from the Crown I immediately contacted our former enemies, the alliance we had conquered months before in December. They agreed to form a new alliance, one most of you are now familiar with: The Federation."{{cite:gdoc-16kljroe67z_p80r|"Drew's Farewell Address", 7 Feb 2019}} [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]], on the other side of the merger, describes the same event as the Coalition's remaining half combining with "the old remains of War Syndicate, Illustratus".{{cite:gdoc-1fev2xrzlfbeo2ll|Feb 2018}} He gives the name a mundane origin: "The name 'Federation' began as a joke, considering Coalition had also been named just by being a synonym for alliance, I joked we should do it again with the name Federation, but people liked it and it became our name", and says the bloc "copied the exact same structure that Coalition had"{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 17, composed 30 Aug 2018}}.



The joke about the name is corroborated from inside the week it happened. Two members recorded the naming independently on 17 February 2018, four hours apart and in different channels: one had searched for synonyms of the word alliance, the other for synonyms of the word coalition — the name of the alliance they had just lost.{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|17 Feb 2018}} The largest alliance in the period took its name from a thesaurus entry for its own predecessor.

Its members had another name for it, and used it on the alliance's third day. On the morning of 21 February 2018, minutes after a leader posted the free-for-all list into the guild leaders' channel, a member remarked that this had become the second Coalition.{{cite:federation-origin-as-its-members-told-it-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, the guild leaders' channel}} The founder did not argue: that, he said, was exactly what the [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] timeline's keeper meant to enter it as — the Second Coalition.{{cite:federation-origin-as-its-members-told-it-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, the founder's answer}} The member replied that it was essentially an identical copy of the old alliance without [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] and its associates, and another added that it had [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]] instead.{{cite:federation-origin-as-its-members-told-it-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018, the identical copy}} The entry did not last: by August 2018 the timeline's keeper had taken it out again.{{cite:storytime-as-a-source-discord-private|22 Aug 2018}}

The alliance dated itself the same way from the far side. On 14 May 2018, publishing per-guild war statistics, its founder told a member who asked what period they covered that they were every war fought after 16 February — the night [[hackforums|HackForums]] and the others left the Coalition and the night the merger talks began.{{cite:federation-origin-as-its-members-told-it-2018-discord-private|14 May 2018, the war channel}} Two days later he told the guild leaders that it was three months to the day since both.{{cite:federation-origin-as-its-members-told-it-2018-discord-private|16 May 2018}} On 16 August members marked the half-birthday and asked for the message to be pinned.{{cite:federation-origin-as-its-members-told-it-2018-discord-private|16 Aug 2018}} Each of the three is a retelling of that night rather than a record of it, so none of them fixes the date; what they establish is that the alliance never doubted it. The naming fell on the morning of 17 February, while the alliance's permanent channels were being made. The founding itself had come the evening before: the Discord server was created at five past eight on 16 February, fifty-two minutes after the first guild announced in the Coalition's general channel that it was leaving.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|server creation computed from the Discord id, 16 Feb 2018}} The merger was announced inside it as a completed fact within its first minute, and nothing was put to a vote — see [[the-federation-founding-night|the founding night]].{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018, 20:05-20:06}}

The alliance was formed, named, staffed and at war inside six days, in that order. Its first act was to absorb its predecessor's standing rival, a bloc Coalition members had spent months defining themselves against.{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|February 2018}} It admitted three guilds in three days with the fighting still running: [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] voted through eight to two, [[fantasy|Fantasy]] admitted on terms that required its leader's other guild to take part, and [[dogsamongus|DogsAmongUs]] simply joining while already helping with the attacks.{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|17–20 Feb 2018}} The force fighting under its banner was larger than its membership — members named ten guilds fighting for it on 19 February, several of them never voted in.{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018}}

That day broke three of the opposing guilds: [[kangronomicon|Kangronomicon]] fought to total territorial collapse across the canyon and Gavel, [[house-of-sentinels|House of Sentinels]] was pushed out of its Gavel holdings entirely, and [[emorians|Emorians]] emptied its treasury during the fighting and could no longer pay for defence at all.{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018}} By the evening the alliance had moved on to [[hackforums|HackForums]], the largest of its enemies, whose count was already falling. Its leader claimed mid-fight that the war had already surpassed the largest anyone could remember — the winning side's estimate, made before any record existed to check it against.{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018}}

The capture log measures the day from outside the alliance. These figures are unfiltered, and have to be: the alliance drafted its first territory allocation on 20 February and published it on the 21st, so on the 19th there was no Federation map and no declared free-for-all ground to leave out of the count.{{cite:federation-first-map-2018-discord-private|first allocation, 20–21 Feb 2018}} The heaviest attackers of 19 February were [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] with 185 captures, House of Sentinels with 183 and HackForums with 178; the heaviest losses fell on House of Sentinels with 198, Kingdom Foxes with 190, [[necronomicon|Necronomicon]] with 173 and HackForums with 172.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures and losses by guild, 19 Feb 2018 UTC, unfiltered}} Of the three the alliance counted as broken, only Kangronomicon breaks in the log: it took 89 territories on 17 February and 73 on the 18th, then 59, 16, and none at all on the 21st. Emorians took 30 on the 19th and lost 28 — a net gain on the day it was said to be finished, which fits the alliance's own account, since what failed there was the treasury rather than the position on the map.{{cite:territory-exchanges|daily captures and losses outside declared free-for-all territories, Kangronomicon and Emorians, 17–23 Feb 2018}} HackForums finished the 19th almost even, then lost 182 territories against 83 taken on the 20th — [[the-wipe-of-hackforums|the day it went to zero]].{{cite:territory-exchanges|daily captures and losses, HackForums, 15–25 Feb 2018}}

[[blacklisted|Blacklisted]] spent the week alongside the alliance rather than in it. Asked on 19 February whether the guild was with them, a member answered that it was only truced.{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|19 Feb 2018}} At four minutes past noon the next day someone called for it to be attacked; within a minute a member reported three territories taken off it, another established that it had never been a member, and a third stated that the truce was the only reason it held as many territories as it did.{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018}} That last was said at the moment his own side stopped honouring the truce, and it is the alliance's explanation of a rival's success rather than an account of it.

The capture log gives the same morning its scale, and this fighting was almost all off the free-for-all list rather than the churn those territories generate anyway. Blacklisted appears in the log for four days only, 18 to 21 February, taking 98 territories outside the free-for-all list and losing 97; the 20th was its worst day, 49 taken against 73 lost, with [[imperial|Imperial]] taking sixteen, [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] eleven and [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]] ten. Its own captures came off both sides of the war: 49 from House of Sentinels, which was fighting the alliance, and 35 from IceBlue Team, [[fantasy|Fantasy]] and Kingdom Foxes, which were in it.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures and losses by day and opposing guild outside declared free-for-all territories, Blacklisted, 18–21 Feb 2018}}

**On 21 February — five days in, the war barely over and the membership not yet settled — DogsAmongUs was already pressing to be assigned Ragni, a region the leadership had left unallocated, calling the omission a mistake given what the guild had contributed.**{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|21 Feb 2018}}

It spent that day failing to hold the place. DogsAmongUs took Ragni from Kangronomicon at 00:12 and Ragni East Suburbs six minutes later, lost Ragni to Eagle Talon at 03:29 and took it back four minutes after that, then lost Ragni Main Entrance in the evening; before that morning it had held nothing in the region at all. It was its largest day of the founding week: 46 captures across 38 territories, counted whole.{{cite:territory-exchanges|DogsAmongUs captures, 21 Feb 2018 UTC, unfiltered}} No free-for-all ground can be taken out of them — the alliance's first list went up at 9:12 that evening on its own clock, which is 05:12 the next morning in the log's, so the fighting came first and the list that would have governed it did not yet exist.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}} The alliance settled it that evening: the founding allocation announced at 9:12 PM assigned DogsAmongUs the Jungle and Ragni.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}} The argument was made and won inside a day.

The alliance had meant to keep something in hand. A draft of the map circulated on 20 February left four areas unassigned for guilds that might join later, among them the ground around Ragni.{{cite:federation-first-map-2018-discord-private|draft map, 20 Feb 2018}} The version announced the following evening carried no reserve at all, and at least eight allocations had moved in between.{{cite:federation-first-map-2018-discord-private|draft and announced maps compared, 20–21 Feb 2018}} Both reserved areas whose fate can be followed went to guilds already inside: Ragni to DogsAmongUs, the Detlas suburbs to Kasai Shinrai.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}} An alliance five days old had tried to hold ground for members it did not yet have, and could not hold it for twenty-four hours. What it asked the alliance for was the ground the map would not let it keep.

That is the argument the alliance was still having in June, in September, and on the morning it dissolved: who deserves which ground, measured against what they did in the war. It arrived before the alliance had finished admitting its members.

Even its genealogy was disputed among participants: Lego_DW described the Coa–WS merger in 2020 as "a creation of fed"{{cite:thread-265297|post #16, Lego_DW, 4 Apr 2020}}, while Slayne posted a rival claim on the tribute thread on 14 Nov 2018 — "In a way, you could say that I created Fed because I (along with some others) created Double AA. Take that drew! Owait. Im also drew."{{cite:thread-237070-p03|post #45, Slayne, 14 Nov 2018}} The claim is causal rather than direct: "Double AA" is Alliance Alliance, whose split from the Coalition triggered the merger that produced the Federation.

The alliance also ran its own entertainments. A tournament document circulated to its guild leaders set an alliance-wide player-versus-player bracket in which each guild entered a single fighter and guilds bid bounties on them, with "ONLY the 12 guilds with the highest bounty" admitted to the draw and the bounties going to the guilds' banks. Spectators were confined to the balcony of the Llevigar bank, mythic items and skill-point potions were barred, everyone fought on level 75 healing and level 50 mana potions, and poison builds were "banned upon request of the Federation leaders".{{cite:gdoc-12gia5l_mo0yig84|Federation alliance tournament document}}

### Governance
The alliance ran on a Discord server built by AurumKitsune of KingdomPhoenixes, thanked in Drew1011's tribute "for setting up the discord and coming up with things such as the 2/3 majority voting rule that contributed greatly to its success".{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, Drew1011, 13 Nov 2018}}

Decisions were taken by formal vote, one vote per member guild leader, and the same mechanism carried admissions, expulsions, territory maps and rule changes alike.{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|voting practice, 2016–2019}} The two-thirds threshold was not there from the start: the alliance raised it from a simple majority on 19–20 February 2018, immediately after an admission vote was closed and announced at nine votes in favour and another guild leader publicly disputed that it should have carried.{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|threshold change, 19–20 Feb 2018}} Six weeks later the same rule was the alliance's central political grievance, and the argument over it escalated into [[buildcraftia|the longest war it fought]].{{cite:buildcraftia-war-2018-discord-private|Federation leadership channels, March 2018}}

Two standing rules shaped everything else. Assigned territory was tied to contribution, with the best ground going to the guilds carrying the fighting{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|allocation and contribution, 2018}}, and the assigned holdings of any member guild that fell below thirty territories were opened to attack by its own allies — a rule against hoarding land one could not defend, which in practice made a guild losing a war a target for its own side.{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|the thirty-territory rule, 2018}} On 1 July 2018, after a secret non-aggression pact between a member guild and an enemy guild was exposed, the guild leaders voted to bar member guilds from holding war-related pacts with any guild outside the alliance, and wrote the ban into a shared rules document two days later alongside a prohibition on leaking war information.{{cite:pasta-treaties-2018-discord-private|Federation guild-leader vote, 1–3 Jul 2018}} The alliance's rules are described further at [[alliance-governance|alliance governance]]. Its two-thirds arithmetic was disputed inside its own leadership through March 2018, and the marks a guild leader reacted to had to be placed by somebody before anyone could vote at all: how that worked, and what every March tally has to be corrected for, is at [[federation-ballots-march-2018|the Federation's ballots in March 2018]].{{cite:federation-ballot-seeding-march-2018-discord-private|7–13 Mar 2018, the threshold and the marks}} The rule against taking an ally's ground was stated in the war channel rather than written down, and the month's five cases are at [[federation-attacking-allies-march-2018|attacking an ally]].{{cite:federation-attacking-allies-march-2018-discord-private|6 Mar 2018, the rule stated}}

### The first month
The Federation was at war from its first days, against the bloc that had walked out of its predecessor, with the fighting concentrated on one contested region — and within days the cost of defending that ground had collapsed the treasury of one of its own member guilds.{{cite:federation-first-month-2018-discord-private|Federation channels, late February 2018}} It was losing members while still forming: one guild announced it was leaving and would fight its former allies, then joined the opposing coalition, and another left without explanation and began attacking a member's territory.{{cite:federation-first-month-2018-discord-private|departures, late February – March 2018}} Its leaders often could not establish why a guild had gone. Three separate discussions of departures in this period record, respectively, that no reason was given, that it might have been friction with a subguild, and that nobody knew.{{cite:federation-first-month-2018-discord-private|departures, late February – March 2018}}

The administrative machinery ran anyway, and inconsistently. A vote to admit [[illustratus|Illustratus]] failed three to seven on 25 February 2018 — the day after the alliance's leader discovered it already held alliance territories and Discord roles without ever having been admitted — and the guild was inside the alliance a fortnight later, having declared itself independent of its parent guild in the interval and carried a second vote whose counting rule was announced along with its result.{{cite:federation-first-month-2018-discord-private|admission votes, late February 2018}}{{cite:titans-valor-guild-profile-discord-private|24 Feb – 17 Mar 2018}} Another guild was invited by sixteen votes to one, partly on the reasoning that it had helped fight a common enemy in the past.{{cite:federation-first-month-2018-discord-private|admission votes, late February 2018}} The alliance negotiated a temporary truce with one of the guilds attacking it purely so that its members could move forces to another front.{{cite:federation-first-month-2018-discord-private|truce, early 2018}} By late February it comprised roughly twenty member guilds and its leadership was compiling per-guild war statistics counting the wars each had fought since the split that created it — a document that was simultaneously a war report and an argument about who deserved territory.{{cite:federation-first-month-2018-discord-private|war statistics, late February 2018}} The founder posted and pinned it in the leaders' channel on 26 February 2018, giving for each member guild the number of wars it had fought since [[hackforums|HackForums]] changed sides and that guild's share of the alliance's total as a percentage, with individual guilds running from twenty-four wars to more than eight hundred.{{cite:coalition-explained-by-its-members-february-2018-discord-private|26 Feb 2018, the table}} Nobody stated the total, but four of the listed guilds give counts and percentages that each imply a figure between 11,111 and 11,123 — about eleven thousand one hundred wars for the ten days from 16 to 26 February.{{cite:coalition-explained-by-its-members-february-2018-discord-private|26 Feb 2018, the counts and percentages}} The thirteen motions the leaders' channel carried in the same fortnight are at [[federation-admissions-february-2018|admissions in February 2018]], and the ally and enemy lists it kept at [[federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018|its lists and truces]].

Members also refer to the alliance holding debating sessions they called parliament. One guild leader recorded having been demoted for insubordination after arguing with the alliance's leader in one, and a second member reported a demotion at about the same time; both accounts come from the people demoted.{{cite:federation-first-month-2018-discord-private|member accounts, March 2018}} Amid the fighting a leader recorded regret at having lost contact with the leaders of the guilds that had left — people who had been allies a fortnight earlier.{{cite:federation-first-month-2018-discord-private|leadership channels, March 2018}}

### The first battle, 17-19 February 2018
The war opened with an attack by Snowythewolf, a chief of Hall of Fame, on [[emorians|Emorians]]' Llevigar territories, chosen because she had heard the guild's leaders discussing a low bank. Drew1011 joined the attack on the night of the 17th and stopped at about 3 AM on the 18th with Llevigar still held; Emorians was defending at the full 1,000-mob cost of roughly 2,500 emeralds a defence despite being near bankruptcy. By noon on the 18th Llevigar had flipped and Emorians was, in Drew1011's words, "almost totally bankrupt".{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 17, composed 30 Aug 2018}}

Drew1011 wrote that he then coordinated the alliance's war effort personally for about fifteen hours, and that the Federation had "ballooned into at LEAST 22 guilds, fighting 7 of the strongest guilds Wynncraft had to offer". [[viaire|Viaire]] of [[fantasy|Fantasy]] defected from Alliance Alliance mid-battle on 18 February in exchange for keeping his canyon territories, after which "Fantasy immediately began targeting Hax".{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 17, composed 30 Aug 2018}} 

The alliance had voted Fantasy in that same day, on terms requiring its leader's other guild to take part, and gave it the mid canyon in the allocation of the 21st.{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|18 Feb 2018}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}} The closing date of 19–20 Mar 2018 that [[fantasy|Fantasy]] carries for Alliance Alliance is the one all seven of that bloc's guilds carry.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} Drew1011 credited [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] with holding the night shift of 18–19 February and taking the Light Forest and Aldorei while the rest of the alliance slept.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 17, composed 30 Aug 2018}}

The alliance’s first roster was posted in the Coalition’s own channel on 17 February 2018, the day after the split, under the words "Hey people these are our new allies": Sins of Seedia, BuildCraftia, [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]], LE Flowers, PaT, TheDivineSwords, [[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]], [[holders-of-le|Holders of LE]], [[imperial|Imperial]], DeathReapers and Immortalish.{{cite:coalition-announcements|2/17/2018}} Three of the eleven — Sins of Seedia, BuildCraftia and TheDivineSwords — had been named as enemies of the Coalition three days before, on a card posted in January but last revised on 14 February and kept only in its revised form.{{cite:coalition-information|posted 1/11/2018 9:33 PM, last revised 14 Feb 2018}} Six of the twenty-two guilds in the settlement of the 21st stood on the same list — see [[federation-founding-membership-2018|the Federation's founding membership]].{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}}

### The wipe of HackForums
Participants on both sides remembered the reconquest as taking three days. Drew1011 wrote "Within three days, we reconquered the provinces"{{cite:gdoc-16kljroe67z_p80r|"Drew's Farewell Address", 7 Feb 2019}}, and Titans Valor's history has the guilds that had left the Coalition "officially wiped out by the Federation after claiming all the map" on 19 February{{cite:gdoc-1fev2xrzlfbeo2ll|Feb 2018}}. Both are two days ahead of the map itself, which the Federation held at 35 per cent on the 19th and 93 per cent on the 21st; the 19th was the day of the HackForums wipe.

Three days after forming, the Federation [[the-wipe-of-hackforums|wiped [[HackForums]] off the map]]: the capture log has Hax at 108 territories by midday on 19 Feb 2018 and none by the evening of the 20th{{cite:territory-exchanges|holdings reconstructed from the capture log, 19-22 Feb 2018 UTC}} — the end of Hax as a territorial power, a year after its 2017 peak of roughly 350 territories.{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, the community guild-timeline image (imgur seklYAQ)}}

After the Federation had taken Llevigar, the swamp, the Dark Forest and the Light Forest, "at the very latest hours of the 19th, Snowy announced it was time to attack Hax. At the time they held 111 territoires. Less than 10 hours later, they had none."{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 17, composed 30 Aug 2018}} Accounts differ on the duration — under ten hours or under twelve. A leaderboard of 22 Feb 2018 shows the map's largest holder with only 43 territories{{cite:thread-219744|post #1, 22 Feb 2018}}: guilds such as Blacklisted "managed to rise to the top of the leaderboards out of pure chance. They were not Fed so AA did not attack them, and they were not AA so Fed did not attack them", and so topped the table for about a day{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 17, composed 30 Aug 2018}}.

### When the map was won
The Federation took the map on 21 February 2018, four days into the war, holding 356 of 383 territories, or 93%, against Alliance Alliance's 24{{cite:territory-exchanges|daily ownership reconstruction, 5 Feb – 10 Apr 2018}}{{cite:territory-exchanges|356 of 383 territories, 21 Feb 2018}}. Drew1011's account agrees — "Three days after the Civil War had begun, Federation had won the map"{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 17, composed 30 Aug 2018}} — as does the arithmetic of his tribute, whose "8 months and 25 days" of map control counts back from the collapse of 10 November 2018 to mid-February 2018{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, Drew1011, 13 Nov 2018}}.

Map control broke once. Federation holdings fell to 164 territories on 2 March and to 43, or 11%, on 3 March, with Alliance Alliance back at 308, recovering to 114 on 4 March and to over 320 from 6 March{{cite:territory-exchanges|holdings of each alliance’s member guilds, sampled every 20 minutes from the capture log, 2-6 Mar 2018 UTC; the Federation’s daily ranges were 131-375, 16-129, 85-182, 189-333 and 326-359}} — the interruption the tribute describes as "aside from ~3 days in March"{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, Drew1011, 13 Nov 2018}}.

The community timeline instead dates the Federation's map control to 19–20 March{{cite:community-guild-timeline}}. Those are the days Alliance Alliance fell from 72 territories to one and the Federation reached 380 of 383 territories, or 99%{{cite:territory-exchanges|Alliance Alliance from 72 territories to one, 19–20 Mar 2018}} — the elimination of the opposing bloc and the end of the war, a month after the map changed hands.

### Map dominance
The Federation took full map control on 21 Feb 2018{{cite:territory-exchanges|356 of 383 territories, 21 Feb 2018}} and eliminated the Alliance Alliance guilds as a territorial force on 19–20 Mar 2018, ending the [[coalition-civil-war|Coalition Civil War]] and absorbing them.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} It held the entire map for 8 months and 25 days, with a roughly three-day interruption in March.{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, Drew1011, 13 Nov 2018}} On 16 Apr 2018 all fifteen territory holders on the map were Federation guilds, together holding 384 territories.{{cite:pre2018-territory-snapshots|Wayback Machine guild-leaderboard API capture, 16 Apr 2018 (pre-2018 territory snapshots)}}

{{map:2018-04-16|The Federation's map at its height}}

The March interruption began with a game update that rewrote guild warfare on 1–2 March 2018: new defensive units could chain-heal one another, some territories became effectively impossible to take, and small guilds lost the ability to defend anything or take ground alone.{{cite:war-update-march-2018-discord-private|1–2 Mar 2018}} The alliance's largest guild was attacked by four guilds at once across two fronts in the days that followed, and the alliance organised a coordinated counter-effort against six named opponents.{{cite:war-update-march-2018-discord-private|early March 2018}} The mechanics of the update, and the conflict of interest members raised about it, are described at [[territory-warfare|territory warfare]].
 Drew1011 writes that "beginning almost as soon as the clock struck midnight and March 1st began, Hax & Co. began attacking Gavel. We quickly discovered that there had been a mob update and that they were using broken defenses that the vast majority of players were unable to overcome". Tntdestroyd coordinated the first two days of the counter-effort, concentrating it on House of Sentinels alone, and handed coordination to Drew1011 on 3 March; "by the 5th, the entire map once again belonged to Federation". [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] was admitted at [[slayne|Slayne]]'s request on 7 March, and House of Sentinels left Alliance Alliance on 12 March and returned to neutrality, at which point Drew1011 considered the war over.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 17, composed 30 Aug 2018}} His tribute thanks "@Tntdestroyd, for fighting hard to reclaim the map back in March".{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, Drew1011, 13 Nov 2018}} The trough of the relapse fell on 3 March, and the Federation was back near 300 territories from 6 March — a day later than the "by the 5th" of his account{{cite:territory-exchanges|Federation holdings, 2–6 Mar 2018}}.

Replying to the tribute on 13 Nov 2018, the member Lego_DW asked recognition for "the absolute gods of war this alliance created" — himself, 21(n), Goden, _Antho74_, InnerJoker, 0x7cf and snowy — who "along with too many others to credit, were the ones that really held the alliance together in terms of war power".{{cite:thread-237070|post #18, Lego_DW, 13 Nov 2018}} Of the seven, only Goden, a [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] officer, is otherwise known.

Membership churned heavily — [[Titans Valor]] alone served three separate stints.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} Within the bloc, [[Fantasy]], Sins of Seedia and IceBlue Team formed the [[Aesir Pact]] on 25 Mar 2018{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}, a pact of sister guilds dedicated to Gavel.{{cite:thread-227373|post #1, opening post (lotuss/simplyinfinity_)}} Fantasy stayed in the alliance until 22 September 2018 and then [[fantasy-turns-on-the-federation|fought it through October]], rejoining and leaving again on the same night of the 29th.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|9/22/2018 and 10/29/2018}}

### Internal conflict, spring 2018
Drew1011 records an unstable spring: BuildCraftia's seizure of Serpentem Empire's share of the swamp without penalty; a dispute over the Sky Islands; an attack by BuildCraftia on Kingdom Foxes territories while a vote on their status was still open, and a retaliation by Fox, Imperial, Paladins United and The Hive that he says he called off himself the following morning; and a **vote to expel BuildCraftia**, which its leader pre-empted by leaving.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 18, composed 20 Jun 2019}} He also names an alliance that guild's leader attempted to build from inside the Federation, "Centurion"; a thread post of 25 April 2018 has Tntdestroyd writing "We have already began forming what it is your looking to do now, join us if you like"{{cite:thread-224905|post #3, 25 Apr 2018}}. Whether that guild was expelled or left of its own accord remained a live dispute between the two men into 2019.

The vote fell on 30 March 2018, and the [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]]' guild history gives the same date.{{cite:gdoc-1kajulcrdc0cpf1j|30 March 2018 entry}} It carried an opt-out for member guilds unwilling to fight [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]]'s leader personally.{{cite:buildcraftia-war-2018-discord-private|expulsion vote, 30 Mar 2018}}

The whole of it took forty-six minutes. A member raised expulsion in the leaders' back-channel at 19:14 UTC, the motion went to the voting channel at 19:52, and it was declared passed at 20:00.{{cite:federation-buildcraftia-expulsion-march-2018-discord-private|19:14 to 20:00 UTC, 30 Mar 2018}} That was eight minutes into a vote the alliance's own rule gave twenty-four hours, and the mover was told that everybody had voted and that a motion which had passed and been announced was passed.{{cite:federation-buildcraftia-expulsion-march-2018-discord-private|30 Mar 2018, the twenty-four-hour rule set aside}} The count declared at 20:00 was ten abstentions, ten in favour and one against, and the leader who made it called the one against a placeholder.{{cite:federation-buildcraftia-expulsion-march-2018-discord-private|the count reported at 20:00 UTC, 30 Mar 2018}} The exported tally gives thirteen, one and eleven, and twelve, none and ten once the account sitting on every option comes out: no guild voted to keep BuildCraftia in, and on a threshold that took abstentions out of the total the ten abstaining guilds were close to half the alliance.{{cite:federation-buildcraftia-expulsion-march-2018-discord-private|exported and corrected tallies, and the abstention arithmetic, 30 Mar 2018}} The alliance's leader reached the leadership channel at 19:58 and asked whether they were expelling the guild; he had been asleep for the debate.{{cite:federation-buildcraftia-expulsion-march-2018-discord-private|19:58 UTC, 30 Mar 2018}}

### August 2018: maximum exertion

August 2018 was the heaviest month of fighting in the whole recorded war — 41,920 territory captures, more than a quarter of every capture logged in seven months, in four weeks.{{cite:federation-august-2018-discord-private|captures in August 2018: 41,920 of 155,498}} It moved almost nothing. Across that month the alliance's fourteen member guilds took 23,624 territories and lost 23,607, a net of seventeen between all of them; no member guild moved by more than forty-two in either direction, and no guild outside the alliance gained more than fourteen.{{cite:federation-august-2018-discord-private|Federation member guilds, August 2018: +23,624 / −23,607, net +17}}

More than a quarter of that month's fighting was on ground the alliance had itself declared open, over the stretch of it that can be measured: 8,986 of the 33,099 captures between 6 and 29 August fell on the list published on 6 August, leaving 24,113 that were contested at all.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures 6–30 Aug 2018 UTC filtered against the free-for-all declaration of 6 Aug 2018: 33,099 raw, 8,986 on declared open ground, 24,113 contested}} The days either side are a harder question, and only one of them has an answer. The pool between 1 and 5 August was published after all, in the place the alliance had been publishing it since June: written out beneath the map of 1 August, twenty territories.{{cite:federation-allocation-legends-2018-discord-private|the legend of the map published 1 Aug 2018}} Against it, 1,656 of the first five days' 6,091 captures were on open ground and 4,435 contested.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures 1–6 Aug 2018 UTC filtered against the pool stated on the map of 1 Aug 2018: 6,091 raw, 1,656 on open ground, 4,435 contested}}

The list before it is the one worth pausing on. On 26 July the alliance changed the pool and the count stayed at twenty-one: Avos Temple and Factory Entrance out, Mine Base Plains and Swamp Dark Forest Transition Mid in, with the total printed correctly in brackets beside both versions.{{cite:federation-allocation-legends-2018-discord-private|the legends of 18 and 26 Jul 2018}} So the single number a reader would check to see whether anything had moved is identical on either side of a real amendment, and the pool can only be compared name by name. An earlier version of this passage reconstructed the August list instead of reading it, arrived at the right size with the wrong contents, and satisfied a check built to reach that size — which is what a checksum arranged around its own answer is worth. The last two days have no answer: the declaration of 30 August was published as a map with no list, so the 2,662 captures after it stand raw.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures 30 Aug – 1 Sep 2018 UTC: 2,662}} August was still the heaviest month in the record after the subtraction, and it still moved nothing.

A guild's territory count had no stable answer, and members noticed. On 16 August a stats bot reported the alliance's largest guild holding a hundred territories and ranked first on the server; later the same day a member counting the map by eye made it barely a third of that. Replaying every capture logged in those twenty-four hours gives the reason: the guild's holdings moved between forty-eight and a hundred and twelve across 1,701 transfers, a territory changing hands somewhere roughly every fifty seconds.{{cite:federation-august-2018-discord-private|16 Aug 2018: holdings range 48–112 across 1,701 transfers}} The body adjudicating who held what was, on members' own account that week, one person drawing the map while the others commented; a full remap was finalised on that basis two days later.{{cite:federation-august-2018-discord-private|territory committee, mid-August 2018}}

From inside, it did not look like a height. The alliance's largest guild's leader said mid-month that his guild effectively carried its war effort, and that the one time they had stopped helping, the alliance could not hold the rival coalition off.{{cite:federation-august-2018-discord-private|August 2018}} The rival's method was concentration — attacking one Federation guild at a time and pinning it while the rest went untouched — which is how an alliance is dismantled without losing a general engagement.{{cite:federation-august-2018-discord-private|August 2018}} One member guild's leader estimated in late August that five to seven of his roughly forty members were still fighting, and called the guild at serious risk.{{cite:federation-august-2018-discord-private|late August 2018}} Anything the alliance decided that month was decided in front of a record: August 2018 carries 194,681 messages across twenty-four channels, 55,907 of them in the ten channels where alliance business was transacted.{{cite:federation-august-2018-discord-private|August 2018: 194,681 messages, 24 channels; 55,907 in 10 governance channels}}

### Administration

By the late summer of 2018 the alliance ran to around twenty member guilds and its leadership channels are occupied overwhelmingly by membership and territory administration rather than by fighting.{{cite:federation-at-its-height-2018-discord-private|Federation leadership channels, August–September 2018}} Guild composition churned continuously: within the first week of August alone one member guild ceased to exist, another's active members split off into a newly formed guild, a third flipped to the rival alliance, and a fourth was reported to be merging with an enemy guild — an announcement made at an in-game karaoke event rather than through alliance channels.{{cite:federation-at-its-height-2018-discord-private|August 2018}}

Admission standards were real and were used. Two guilds were refused entry within a short period, another was admitted only on its third application and only after undertaking to stop attacking members, and a small low-level guild's application was debated explicitly in terms of its size, its win rate and how likely it was to be targeted if admitted.{{cite:federation-at-its-height-2018-discord-private|admissions, August–September 2018}} The churn made basic facts hard to establish: one member guild had three leaders at once in August 2018 after an ownership handover, another had a member publicly uncertain whether he was still a leader, and the alliance separately disagreed about who currently led one of its principal rivals.{{cite:federation-at-its-height-2018-discord-private|August 2018}} Guilds moved between the alliance, its rival and neutrality fast enough that its own enemy and ally lists needed constant revision.{{cite:federation-at-its-height-2018-discord-private|August–September 2018}}

Territory disputes between allied guilds were persistent and specific — a long-running argument over a single named region between two member guilds, with members counting the number of times it had changed hands, and a separate multi-day dispute over one guild drawing far more experience income than any other from its assigned holdings.{{cite:federation-at-its-height-2018-discord-private|territory disputes, August 2018}} The sharpest episode of the period was a dispute about what counted as cheating. In early August four enemy guilds were found to be capturing territories and handing them over undefended to [[hackforums|HackForums]], inflating its holdings without its having to fight; a moderator ruled the practice not technically against the rules, and the alliance answered with a full mobilisation that cut its holdings back sharply.{{cite:federation-at-its-height-2018-discord-private|August 2018}}{{cite:hackforums-guild-profile-discord-private|August 2018}} Federation members had been arguing the same mechanic four days earlier, from the friendly side: a member guild's representative held that it was legitimate to leave a captured territory undefended so that an allied guild could take it the instant the retake cooldown expired.{{cite:federation-at-its-height-2018-discord-private|August 2018}}{{cite:federation-august-2018-discord-private|August 2018}} Nobody in the alliance connected the two. Underneath it the old asymmetry held: one older guild with a bank of roughly thirteen million emeralds took a string of territories from a member guild in quick succession, and there was no rule against that either.{{cite:federation-at-its-height-2018-discord-private|August 2018}}{{cite:territory-economy-and-warfare-discord-private|wealth and war, 2018}}

Discord access became a political instrument in its own right. A Federation leader banned the leader of a rival-aligned guild from the alliance's server for repeatedly attempting to poach members, and Federation members separately recorded having been banned from a member guild's own server after criticising its leadership.{{cite:federation-at-its-height-2018-discord-private|Discord bans, August–September 2018}}

### Admission and administration

The alliance kept an announcements channel in its Discord, and it doubles as a record of how the bloc was run. Guilds applying were told in September 2018 to message one of three Discord managers — Drew1011, Catherine or simplyinfinity_ — or any member guild's leader, to write a paragraph on why the guild was ready, and to expect their war record to be weighed: "if you have only done 50 wars, you probably are not ready to apply yet."{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Deleted User, 20 Sep 2018}} The alliance ran a Discord bot tracking war statistics and territory assignments, whose database was wiped on 29 September 2018 and restored the next day without the data.{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Gas, 29–30 Sep 2018}}

For three weeks it also had a newsletter, and the reason it was started is the point of it. On 1 September 2018 a member posted the first alliance-wide weekly update to the announcements channel and explained that a good many people in the alliance did not keep up with what was going on, that he had lately begun writing weekly updates for his own guild after the person who used to do them left, and that another member had suggested he do one at alliance level as well.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/1/2018}} A guild leader answered in the leadership channel that he would have written them there himself had he not been beaten to it.{{cite:federation-weekly-update-2018-discord-private|1 Sep 2018}} Six months in, an alliance of about twenty guilds was discovering that most of its members did not know what it was doing.

The second issue went out on 9 September and there was no third.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/9/2018 1:49 PM}} On the 24th a guild leader remarked in the leadership channel, joking, that there had been no briefings for a fortnight and proposed that the author be shamed for it; the author said he would do one when he got home and, pressed on whether his own guild's had stopped too, that he had been busy.{{cite:federation-weekly-update-2018-discord-private|24 Sep 2018}} None followed for the rest of the alliance's life.{{cite:federation-weekly-update-2018-discord-private|September–November 2018}} Like the territory committee and the map-maker's role, the bulletin was one member's habit rather than an office the alliance made, and it lasted a fortnight.

The channel also shows how far the bloc reached. The Wynncraft administrator jpresent posted directly into it — a server-wide appeal against inter-guild hostility on 27 July 2018 and two client-mod bans, and on 10 August 2018 an @everyone message telling the alliance to attend the Kingdom of Foxes' beach party, an event the Foxes' own history records for the same day.{{cite:federation-public-announcements|jpresent, 27 Jul, 10 Aug and 4 Sep 2018}}{{cite:gdoc-1kajulcrdc0cpf1j|Aug 2018}}

### More members than map (May–July 2018)

The alliance had no written constitution. In May 2018 a member proposed drafting a formal charter, noting that it had run for a long time on rules announced informally and never set down.{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|May 2018}}

Its central problem was arithmetic rather than constitutional. At 26 July 2018, with 383 of the map's 385 territories in known hands, three member guilds held 188 of them between them — [[emorians|Emorians]] and [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] on 64 each and [[imperial|Imperial]] on 60.{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|running totals at 26 Jul 2018: Emorians 64, Kingdom Foxes 64, Imperial 60 of 383 held}} Roughly half the map sat with three members of an alliance of about twenty, and its leadership named the problem in those terms, debating redistribution in mid-June because so much rested on so few.{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|June 2018}}

The redistribution produced the opposite of a settlement. Three member guilds came out of a single June revision with no assigned territory at all{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|June 2018}}; one of them argued at length with the largest guild's leader about being left with nothing, and a smaller member had already complained in May that the alliance treated its small guilds unfairly.{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|May–June 2018}} The published maps show four: between the allocation of 10 May 2018 and that of 4 June, Constellations, [[holders-of-le|Holders of LE]], [[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]] and [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] each went from a named region and a count to nothing at all.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|5/10/2018 and 6/4/2018}} The two figures need not be of the same revision: the alliance issued four further maps that month as images rather than lists.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|6/16, 6/21, 6/25 and 6/30/2018}} A policy favouring the smaller members passed in June after several days of argument, over the largest guild's strong objection that it would destabilise the alliance.{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|June 2018}}

By the end of July the members had stopped waiting for the alliance to decide. One guild proposed handing several of its own assigned territories to a member that held none, carried the proposal by a vote among themselves and acted on it — and another leader objected that the assignments were not the guilds' to redistribute.{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|July 2018}} He was right, which is the point: the alliance's machinery had stopped producing answers its members would accept, so they settled the territory question privately instead.

The alliance's own infrastructure failed once in the middle of it. On 25 June 2018 its Discord administrator, herself a member guild's leader, deleted every role on the alliance's server; by that afternoon her guild faced an expulsion vote and she had left, two other leaders had announced departures, and a replacement guild had been voted in — see [[the-federation-june-crisis|the June crisis]].{{cite:federation-25-june-2018-discord-private|25 Jun 2018}} Four more member guilds left the following month, and the guild doing a quarter of the alliance's fighting stopped fighting for the others — see [[the-federation-july-2018|the Federation in July 2018]].{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 7/22/2018 and 7/25/2018}}

Underneath the argument the membership was hollowing out. Across the same three months [[house-of-sentinels|House of Sentinels]] disbanded outright, another guild announced it would stop warring and told its remaining officers to find other guilds, a founder-era guild was described as effectively dead with its territory gone, and one leader admitted in channel that he could no longer play or lead and had nobody to hand his guild to.{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|May–July 2018}} The alliance went on admitting new members throughout.

### Decline (September–November 2018)

By September 2018 the alliance was winning and ungovernable at once. One of its own members argued in public that the Federation had become too dominant and was spoiling the game's competitive balance for the guilds outside it.{{cite:federation-decline-2018-discord-private|September 2018}} From 30 August to 4 September its leaders argued across four channels over a twenty-four hour internal purge — a period in which the whole map would be open and allied guilds could attack one another freely.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|30 Aug – 4 Sep 2018, the proposal}} The alliance's founder refused it outright on the evening of 2 September, telling the war channel that it would finish with everyone quarrelling and half of the alliance's leaders disliking one another.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|2 Sep 2018, the founder's reason}} It went to a vote on 3 September and members reported it losing from the first minute, at nothing to eleven a quarter of an hour in.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|3 Sep 2018, the reported tally}} Members noted that the rival alliance was reportedly already doing something similar.{{cite:federation-decline-2018-discord-private|internal purge proposal, early September 2018}} What the leaders settled instead was that two guilds could arrange such a thing between themselves without drawing a third into it.{{cite:federation-purge-proposal-2018-discord-private|4 Sep 2018, the settlement}} The episode is at [[federation-purge-proposal-2018|the purge proposal]].

Readmission was understood as poaching. On the afternoon of 29 October the alliance's founder asked the leadership channel whether they could please continue the trend of [[fantasy|Fantasy]]'s leader betraying his own alliance to join theirs — while the same channel was saying that Fantasy was killing them and had just taken a [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]] territory. Twenty minutes later a member replied that he supposed they could reinvite them.{{cite:fantasy-leaving-and-returning-2018-discord-private|29 Oct 2018}} The guild was readmitted that evening and gone again seven hours later.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|10/29/2018}} Taking a guild back was not a judgement about the guild; it was a way of removing it from somebody else.

Espionage stopped being an accusation and became routine, running both ways inside a fortnight. A guild admitted at the start of September was removed within about two days after screenshots showed its leader passing internal Federation material to the rival alliance, and was then attacked and reduced; in the same period a member admitted through another guild disclosed that she had come from the rival alliance and was feeding the Federation intelligence on it, and afterwards fed that alliance false information about when the Federation was least able to defend.{{cite:federation-decline-2018-discord-private|September 2018}} It was operationally useful: the leadership circulated the rival's plan to attack its two Wynn-province guilds on a named day, and claimed to hold screenshots of the rival leadership discussing how to frighten guilds out of joining the Federation at all.{{cite:federation-decline-2018-discord-private|early September 2018}} By early November the alliance was purging inactive accounts from its own Discord to make it harder for spies to sit in it.{{cite:federation-decline-2018-discord-private|November 2018}}

Governing had become a matter of compensating everyone. A proposed redraw of the territory map in September 2018 would have roughly halved the largest member guild's holdings; that guild objected angrily, the map passed by supermajority at thirteen votes of eighteen, one guild lost the region it had been fighting over for years and objected that it had earned it, and another complained that its requests had been ignored and was granted an additional territory in a follow-up vote days later.{{cite:federation-decline-2018-discord-private|territory redraw, September 2018}} Every decision produced a claimant, and every claimant a compensation.

Membership stopped being worth having. A guild leaving in late October said plainly that the alliance had given it little benefit from its territories or its free-for-all arrangements, and both sides called the departure amicable.{{cite:federation-decline-2018-discord-private|late October 2018}} In the same week — with five member guilds leaving around it — the alliance's territory committee discussed bringing in the guild that had been its principal enemy for most of its existence, and provisionally reserved territory for it on the working map pending its joining.{{cite:federation-decline-2018-discord-private|territory committee, late October 2018}}

The alliance's last territory crisis was a surplus. On 30 October 2018 its leaders counted eleven member guilds where a month earlier there had been eighteen, and the map-maker told them it held more territory than it could hand out: four guilds already over forty territories, about eighty unassigned, and guilds refusing what they were offered.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, the count, the figures and the refusals}} The map drawn in answer threw a whole region open because there was nobody to give it to, carried unanimously on 2 November and was never announced; the alliance published no territory document after 12 October 2018.{{cite:federation-more-map-than-guilds-2018-discord-private|30 Oct – 2 Nov 2018, the Ragni decision and the vote}}{{cite:federation-last-declaration-october-2018-discord-private|both announcement channels, 12 Oct – 10 Nov 2018}} It is at [[federation-the-last-map-2018|the Federation's last map]], and the open ground at [[federation-free-for-all-territories|the Federation's free-for-all territories]].

### Collapse
At around 3 AM EST on 10 Nov 2018, one guild left the Federation — and the alliance cascaded apart within a day.{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, Drew1011, 13 Nov 2018}} In the sequence of [[the-federation-dies|the Federation's death]], The Hive had left a month earlier, then Odysseia; [[Imperial]]'s exit for independence "snowballed into a major attack on Fed together with the enemy alliance [Ent]"; Arisen followed, and after 14 hours of constant warring the Aesir Pact guilds quit, ending it.{{cite:thread-236962|post #17, Ascended Kitten, 11 Nov 2018}} The tag **[Ent]** is the only identifier ever given for the opposing bloc, which is otherwise unrecorded. "What killed Federation was... internal affairs", wrote Drew1011 in his tribute, posted three days later with the full member roster.{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, Drew1011, 13 Nov 2018}} He announced the end in the alliance's own Discord that morning, counting the span three ways — "9 months. / or 267 days. / or 6,408 hours. / After so long, Federation has finally died." — and, fifteen minutes later, claimed the record: "Also yes that is a record for the longest a mega alliance has held the map, good luck beating it."{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Drew1011, 10 Nov 2018}} Two hundred and sixty-seven days back from 10 November is 16 February, which fixes the founding date from inside the alliance on the day it ended. He kept the server open rather than deleting it, and in March 2021 opened its archives and its leader channel to everyone in it.{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Drew1011, 10 Nov 2018 and 9 Mar 2021}}

## Membership
The founder's tribute names 37 member guilds and gives the count as "37 guilds. 40 if you count guilds such as LEF and DDT to be entirely different ones."{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, Drew1011, 13 Nov 2018}}

Two of the three pairs he set an arrow between were renames: DogsAmongUs to White Lotus in August 2018, and As Darkness Falls to Constellations in April.{{cite:territory-exchanges|DogsAmongUs and White Lotus, As Darkness Falls and Constellations, abutting activity windows, 2018 UTC}} The third was not. LE Flowers was dissolved on 8 April 2018 and [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]] created the same day by the same leaders, who twice refused a merger with an existing member because the new guild already existed.{{cite:le-flowers-to-diamond-deities-2018-discord-private|8 Apr 2018}}

Another of the 37, DeathReapers [DxR], held the Desert on the alliance's founding map of 21 February 2018 and took territory under that name until 13 March.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}}{{cite:territory-exchanges|DeathReapers, last exchange 13 Mar 2018 UTC}} The alliance voted on expelling it for inactivity on 4 April 2018 and then could not agree whether it had.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|4–10 Apr 2018}}

{{alliance:Federation}}

## Legacy
The Federation's fall ended 2018's era of single-bloc dominance and opened a winter interregnum in which, per the community timeline, "all guilds keep to themselves with valk owning most of Gavel" — the territorial peak of the [[Valkyrie]] network.{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, the community guild-timeline image (imgur seklYAQ)}} [[SOL]] was created in the post-collapse chaos{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, the community guild-timeline image (imgur seklYAQ)}}, and [[Luna]] was formed to counter it in February 2019, beginning [[the-alliance-wars-of-2019|the Alliance Wars of 2019]].{{cite:community-guild-timeline}} No later alliance matched the Federation's duration of total map control.

## The inactivity motion

The motion of 4 April 2018 was the alliance's second attempt to shed its dead, not its first. On 12 March the Federation had already voted to remove Death Reapers and Immortalish, on the grounds that they held no territory, were inactive and took up space, and the motion carried ten to two with eleven abstentions, or ten to one with ten if the single account sitting on two of its three options is taken out as well.{{cite:federation-voting-machinery-march-2018-discord-private|multi-option reactions across the fourteen March motions}} No account on it sat on every option, so the first figure is the one that removes only placeholders, and the second removes a mark that cannot be told apart from a guild voting twice. On 17 March a member of the leadership channel was still asking why the two guilds were in the alliance.{{cite:federation-how-many-guilds-march-2018-discord-private|12 and 17 Mar 2018, the earlier motion and the delay}} That argument, and the moratorium on admissions the alliance voted four days before it, is at [[federation-admissions-march-2018|Federation admissions in March 2018]].

The complaint was made again three weeks later. On the evening of 3 April 2018 an officer told the guild leaders that the alliance could not be made partly of guilds that had stopped playing.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|3 Apr 2018, the complaint}} Asked which, he named [[serpentem-empire|Serpentem Empire]], DeathReapers, [[dogsamongus|DogsAmongUs]] and Immortalish.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|3 Apr 2018, the four named}} Part of the trouble, he said, was not idleness at all: some members were friendly with the guilds attacking the alliance and would not attack them back.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|3 Apr 2018, the stated cause}} He asked the leaders to finish voting on the motion already open — the rule making attacks on listed enemies compulsory, put that evening by the leader of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] — so that a vote on inactive guilds could be started behind it.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|3 Apr 2018, the two motions}}{{cite:federation-obligation-to-attack-2018-discord-private|3 Apr 2018, the motion}}

The motion posted the next morning was narrower than the complaint, and it carried a test. It asked whether to expel the guilds that had not claimed or attempted to claim the territory assigned to them, or that had been assigned none because they had shown they could neither claim nor defend it.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|4 Apr 2018, the motion}} That is the alliance's own definition of a dead guild, and it is not about logging in. It named four: [[illustratus|Illustratus]], DeathReapers, Immortalish and Serpentem Empire — DogsAmongUs off the list, Illustratus on.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|4 Apr 2018, the four named}} The options were to expel all four, to expel some and say in the leaders' channel which were wrongly listed or missing, to expel none, or to abstain.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|4 Apr 2018, the four options}}

## The list was negotiable

The second option invited the motion's own list of guilds to be amended, in another room, while the vote ran. It was. Within a quarter of an hour the leaders were arguing about whether LE Flowers deserved another chance.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|4 Apr 2018, 10:19}} Forty-six minutes after the motion was posted, the officer running the vote asked two of them directly whether they would vote yes if he took LE Flowers off the list; both said they would, and he reported it removed.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|4 Apr 2018, 10:52}} Asked who else did not belong there, another leader said that DeathReapers had at any rate said it was content to hold nothing, and that LE Flowers was active.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|4 Apr 2018, who else}}

The motion as it stands names those four guilds and not LE Flowers, and neither channel records a trade. Reading the two together is this article's.

That afternoon the officer put it to DeathReapers' leader, who said he wanted his guild off the list.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|4 Apr 2018, the offer}} He asked him to go and try to take a free-for-all territory — not to take it, only to try, because he wanted to see some sign of life.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|4 Apr 2018, the activity test}} The same leader told the channel that afternoon that he chose to hold no land at all and turned up when there was fighting to do.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|4 Apr 2018, holding nothing}}

## What had happened on the map

Nothing had. None of the four named in the motion took or lost a territory in the weeks around it: Immortalish last appears on 2 March 2018, Illustratus on the 11th, DeathReapers on the 13th and Serpentem Empire on the 14th.{{cite:territory-exchanges|last exchange before 30 Apr 2018 for Illustratus, DeathReapers, Immortalish and Serpentem Empire, UTC}} None of them appears again before May, and no day between is missing from the record, so the silence is the guilds' own.{{cite:territory-exchanges|no exchange with any of the four as attacker or defender, 15 Mar – 30 Apr 2018 UTC; no absent days in that span}} Three of the four came back. Immortalish returned on 13 May 2018, eleven days after the vote's month ended; Illustratus on 29 August 2018 and DeathReapers not until 30 May 2019. Serpentem Empire's exchange of 14 March 2018 was its last of either kind.{{cite:territory-exchanges|first exchange after 30 Apr 2018 for Illustratus, DeathReapers, Immortalish and Serpentem Empire, UTC}} DeathReapers took nothing in April either, which is not proof that it never tried: only changes of ownership were logged, and an attack that failed left nothing behind.

LE Flowers, taken off the list, was in the same condition as the four left on it. Its last exchange either way was on 19 March 2018 and it never appears again.{{cite:territory-exchanges|LE Flowers, last exchange 19 Mar 2018 UTC; none thereafter}} Four days after the vote its leaders dissolved it and founded [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]] in its place.{{cite:le-flowers-to-diamond-deities-2018-discord-private|8 Apr 2018}} Setting the motion beside these figures is this article's; nobody did so at the time.

## Holding no land

Withholding territory from a guild while it kept its membership was a recognised standing that April, in three forms at once. A guild's spokesman told the leaders on 4 April that it had said it was content with no territories.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|4 Apr 2018, content with none}} [[emorians|Emorians]] was admitted by vote on 11 April on a motion recording that it had asked not to be assigned any until the next allocation.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|11 Apr 2018, the admission}} And on the 22nd, during the allocation debate, the leader of the newly rebuilt DiamondDeities said he did not think it a good idea to assign his guild territory yet, because its defences were not strong.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|22 Apr 2018, the allocation debate}} The alliance reached for the same arrangement in September, when its territory coordinator asked whether he should be assigning a member any land at all.{{cite:federation-inactive-members-2018-discord-private|10 Sep 2018}} The September argument was the second of its kind, not the first.

## Four guilds out, and nobody sure which

On 6 April 2018 the alliance's founder instructed an officer to take Illustratus, DeathReapers, Immortalish and Serpentem Empire off the ally list.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|6 Apr 2018, the removals}} An hour later, when Titans Valor's leader said Federation rules were never enforced, the founder answered that four guilds had just been expelled by a vote; the leader replied that it had taken over a week and that the vote had been redone.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|6 Apr 2018, the exchange}}

On 10 April the founder told the channel the alliance had expelled DeathReapers, Immortalish and Serpentem Empire, three former [[coalition|Coalition]] guilds.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|10 Apr 2018, the founder's account}} A member answered that DeathReapers had in fact been taken out of the motion, and the founder said he had not known; another observed that DeathReapers' own leader had not realised either.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|10 Apr 2018, the correction}} Six days after the vote the alliance had no record of its own decision.

The allocation published on 22 April lists seventeen guilds and 327 territories, and none of the four is among them.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|4/22/2018 2:34 PM}} It settles nothing: it also drops guilds nobody had voted on.

## What inactivity meant

On 28 April 2018 the leader of a small guild asked in the general channel whether the alliance's server still carried his guild's role. It had joined at the very start, he said, and had gone quiet after a war update made his war build useless; it was active again and wished to return.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|28 Apr 2018, the request}} The founder told him the guild had never been in the alliance at all; another member said it had been let in when the alliance's defences were wiped out, and had fought in the ocean.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|28 Apr 2018, the disagreement}} Nothing settled it.

What the channel could agree was the rule. An alliance built for war had no use for a member that had stopped playing, and having been in before did not mean being in after a period of inactivity.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|28 Apr 2018, the doctrine}} A guild coming back was to be voted on like any other applicant.{{cite:federation-inactive-guilds-april-2018-discord-private|28 Apr 2018, admission by vote}}

## Guilds nobody recognised

On the afternoon of 14 April 2018 a leader reported that Super Empire and [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] were attacking.{{cite:federation-unaffiliated-attackers-april-2018-discord-private|14 Apr 2018, the report}} Minutes later a member asked the war channel what the new random guilds were, listing Flosh, Nihil, Nature Kingdom and Mark RPG Clan; the reaction was bewilderment rather than alarm.{{cite:federation-unaffiliated-attackers-april-2018-discord-private|14 Apr 2018, the war channel}}

Three of the five were not new. Nihil had been taking territory since 31 March, Mark RPG Clan since 4 April and Super Empire since the 11th; only Flosh, from the 13th, and Nature Kingdom, from the 14th itself, were as recent as the channel took them all to be.{{cite:territory-exchanges|first capture by Nihil, Mark RPG Clan, The Super Empire, Flosh and Nature Kingdom, UTC}}

One of the five was fighting the alliance. Against the free-for-all list published on 24 March 2018, Super Empire made 154 contested captures in April out of 177.{{cite:territory-exchanges|The Super Empire captures, Apr 2018, FFA list of 24 Mar 2018 applied, UTC}} A hundred and forty-three were off guilds on the alliance's map of the 22nd: thirty-two from [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]], twenty-two from [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]], twenty-one from [[the-divine-swords|The Divine Swords]], sixteen each from Angelic and [[holders-of-le|Holders of LE]].{{cite:territory-exchanges|The Super Empire captures by defender, Apr 2018, FFA list of 24 Mar 2018 applied, UTC}} It concentrated on Corkus and the Ragni approaches, taking Corkus City thirteen times.{{cite:territory-exchanges|The Super Empire captures by territory, Apr 2018, FFA list of 24 Mar 2018 applied, UTC}}

The rest were mostly not. Fifty of Nature Kingdom's fifty-two captures were on free-for-all ground, twenty of them Avos Temple and seventeen Lava Lake.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Nature Kingdom captures, Apr 2018, FFA list of 24 Mar 2018 applied, UTC}} Thirty-one of Nihil's thirty-six were, twenty-four of them Lava Lake alone, and most of the guilds it traded with were not Federation members.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Nihil captures and defenders, Apr 2018, FFA list of 24 Mar 2018 applied, UTC}} Flosh and Mark RPG Clan took no free-for-all ground at all — eighteen captures of assigned land between them across the month.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Flosh and Mark RPG Clan captures, Apr 2018, FFA list of 24 Mar 2018 applied, UTC}} For two of the five, the swarm was strangers fighting over ground the alliance had declared open to anybody.

None of it held. Across the six guilds that attacked the alliance that month, 294 territories were taken in April 2018 and not one was still held thirty days later.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures by The Super Empire, Nature Kingdom, Nihil, vsauce, Flosh and Mark RPG Clan, Apr 2018, UTC}} Median holding times ran from nine minutes to thirty-nine.{{cite:territory-exchanges|median holding times for the same six guilds, Apr 2018, UTC}}

## Everyone hates us

The sixth was made for the purpose. On the evening of 14 April a member told the general channel that somebody had just created a guild called Vsauce and announced an intention to bring the Federation down; he was listening to them plan it in the general voice channel of the game's own Discord server at the time.{{cite:federation-unaffiliated-attackers-april-2018-discord-private|14 Apr 2018, Vsauce}} Another member said the alliance needed to make it an enemy, describing the plan as a revolution to be shut down.{{cite:federation-unaffiliated-attackers-april-2018-discord-private|14 Apr 2018, make them an enemy}} Ten days after the compulsory-attack vote that was not a figure of speech: listing a guild obliged every member to attack it.{{cite:federation-obligation-to-attack-2018-discord-private|4 Apr 2018, the count}} Whether it was ever listed went unrecorded.

Its whole war was eleven captures between 02:57 UTC on 15 April and 18:15 UTC on the 21st, five of them of assigned ground.{{cite:territory-exchanges|vsauce captures 15–21 Apr 2018 UTC, FFA list of 24 Mar 2018 applied}} Each was reversed within forty minutes: four hours and twenty minutes of possession altogether.{{cite:territory-exchanges|vsauce holding times, Apr 2018 UTC: 260 minutes across 11 captures}} The first falls the same evening the guild was reported created, the channel running seven hours behind the log. It never appears again. On 26 April a member remarked that its server appeared to be gone, then said in the next line that he had more likely been removed from it.{{cite:federation-unaffiliated-attackers-april-2018-discord-private|26 Apr 2018}}

The serious pressure that month was not military. On 21 April a member posted into the general channel a Wynncraft forum thread proposing a cap on how much territory a guild could hold.{{cite:federation-unaffiliated-attackers-april-2018-discord-private|21 Apr 2018, the thread}} The channel derided it and recognised it at once: one member noted with disbelief that its author had reached for anti-trust law, and another said the Federation had become a sort of monopoly.{{cite:federation-unaffiliated-attackers-april-2018-discord-private|21 Apr 2018, the reaction}} A third asked what was stopping other guilds from forming an alliance of their own, and was told that this was exactly what [[war-syndicate|War Syndicate]] had done when [[hackforums|HackForums]] was in power.{{cite:federation-unaffiliated-attackers-april-2018-discord-private|21 Apr 2018, an alliance of their own}} A member then remarked that the alliance was about two months old and that everyone hated it, and another put that down to the guilds it had refused to admit.{{cite:federation-unaffiliated-attackers-april-2018-discord-private|21 Apr 2018, everyone hates us}}

Five days later a rival was recruiting inside the alliance's own general channel, in the open, inviting a member who was looking for a guild to join its group instead.{{cite:federation-unaffiliated-attackers-april-2018-discord-private|26 Apr 2018, the recruitment}} A member set out that morning the argument being made against the alliance from outside, as he understood it: that a guild which joined it would never become the game's top guild and would be reduced to a follower.{{cite:federation-unaffiliated-attackers-april-2018-discord-private|26 Apr 2018, the argument reported}}

The death of the [[the-federation|Federation]] on 10 November 2018 ended the longest period of total map control in the recorded history of Wynncraft guild warfare. The alliance was not defeated from outside: one guild's departure at around 3 AM EST triggered a cascade that emptied it within a day{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, 13 Nov 2018}}.

{{map:2018-11-10|The map on the day of the collapse}}

## Background

The Federation formed around 16 February 2018 from the merger of the [[Coalition]] remnant and War Syndicate's remains, founded and led by [[Drew1011]]{{cite:thread-265297|post #16, Lego_DW, 4 Apr 2020}}. It wiped [[HackForums]] on 19 February, won the [[coalition-civil-war|Coalition Civil War]] on 20 March{{cite:community-guild-timeline|19 Feb and 20 Mar 2018 entries}}, and then held the entire map for 8 months and 25 days, interrupted only for roughly three days in March. At its height it counted 37–40 member guilds{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, 13 Nov 2018}}.

Strain showed late and then all at once. [[Fantasy]]'s departure was announced on 22 September and it [[fantasy-turns-on-the-federation|fought the alliance through October]] more heavily than any guild had, rejoining and leaving again on the same night of the 29th{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|9/22/2018 and 10/29/2018}}. The Hive left about a month before the end, then Odysseia{{cite:thread-236962|post #17, Ascended Kitten, 11 Nov 2018}}. Several separate wars between member guilds ignited on 1 November 2018: the pre-collapse fraying{{cite:territory-exchanges|intra-Federation wars igniting 1 Nov 2018}}.

The alliance emptied over the fortnight before it ended. The Hive left on 27 October 2018, Odysseia and TNA on the 29th, and The Divine Swords was removed on the 30th, after which the remaining leaders counted the alliance down to eleven member guilds.{{cite:federation-collapse-2018-discord-private|Federation leadership channels, 27–30 Oct 2018}} Two of those exits were disputed rather than clean: Odysseia was found to have placed former members into other Federation guilds under disguised roles after announcing it was quitting, and The Divine Swords was discovered to be run through the alt account of somebody no longer in the alliance, which its leaders called treachery.{{cite:federation-collapse-2018-discord-private|Federation leadership channels, 29–30 Oct 2018}} A member writing at the end of October described an alliance already below ten working guilds, one of its fighters at half its usual strength, and the rest in territory disputes with each other.{{cite:federation-collapse-2018-discord-private|late October 2018}}

The departures compounded one another, partly through the alliance's own rules. A standing vote had made the assigned holdings of any member guild falling below thirty territories free for allied guilds to attack, so a guild that weakened became a target for its own side.{{cite:federation-collapse-2018-discord-private|the thirty-territory rule}}{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|the thirty-territory rule, 2018}} When one member independently attacked The Hive shortly after it left, The Hive — which had been inclined to help the Federation against the rival alliance — turned its attacks on the Federation instead.{{cite:federation-collapse-2018-discord-private|Federation war channel, late October 2018}} Days before the end, Federation leaders warned each other that an individual was negotiating territory access directly with member guilds' captains rather than their leaders, bypassing the alliance's own structures.{{cite:federation-collapse-2018-discord-private|Federation leadership channels, early November 2018}}

## The alliance never said it was ending

Through 29 October 2018, as six membership changes were announced inside sixteen hours, the leaders' channel joked about which guild would go next; at 03:26 UTC on the 30th a member guild's leader said that people were now leaving because people were leaving, and that they had no faith.{{cite:federation-two-rooms-2018-discord-private|29–30 Oct 2018, the diagnosis}} No leader in the channel that day proposed a measure to stop it.{{cite:federation-two-rooms-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, no measure proposed}}

What the alliance did that evening was audit itself. At 21:28:33 UTC on 30 October a guild leader proposed that every member guild go through its own membership and confirm that everyone in it was legitimate; [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] reported clean at 21:32:55, [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] at 21:32:59, Arisen at 21:35, [[emorians|Emorians]] at 21:39, and [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] at 21:56 and again at 22:01.{{cite:federation-two-rooms-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, the audit and the reports}} One member said in passing that he thought he had found one, and not one guild reported a problem it could not settle in the same conversation.{{cite:federation-two-rooms-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018, the results}} An alliance that believed it was full of spies had asked each of its members to vouch for itself and had accepted every answer.

After [[federation-the-last-map-2018|the map of 2 November]] the leaders' channel had nothing left to do. It had not gone fourteen hours without a message since the previous July.{{cite:federation-two-rooms-2018-discord-private|the channel's gaps, 5 Jul – 5 Nov 2018}} At 19:36 UTC on 5 November it stopped and stayed stopped for twenty hours and twenty-four minutes, its longest silence in four months, and what broke it was Paladins United's leader posting a single word calling for the channel to be revived, with IceBlue Team's leader posting the same word seventeen seconds later.{{cite:federation-two-rooms-2018-discord-private|5–6 Nov 2018, the silence and the appeal}} Ninety minutes after that, in the [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]]'s council, a member asking where the leaders' chat had gone was told that this was the leaders' chat now.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-founded-2018-discord-private|6 Nov 2018}}

Nothing in the alliance's own leadership channel treated the end as coming. Searched across 1 to 9 November 2018 for any mention of the Federation dissolving, collapsing, disbanding or failing, that channel carries greetings, territory grumbles, a complaint about one guild's defences, and a proposal on the 6th to approach again a guild it had refused in October, on the ground that the opposing alliance was growing stronger and that taking the guild in would be a loss to them and a gain to the Federation.{{cite:federation-two-rooms-2018-discord-private|1–9 Nov 2018, the search and what the channel carries}} The language of the alliance dying appears only in the members' general channel and the war channel, where it is banter — one member wondering when the thing would disband, another using it as a punchline in an argument about Christmas.{{cite:federation-two-rooms-2018-discord-private|1–9 Nov 2018, the members' channels}} In the same days the Aesir council was discussing exactly that, and on 8 November was still arguing that letting the Federation fall would be a mistake.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-founded-2018-discord-private|8 Nov 2018}}

## The collapse

At around 3 AM EST on 10 November 2018 one guild left the Federation, and the rest cascaded out within a day{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, 13 Nov 2018}}. [[Imperial]]'s exit 'snowballed into a major attack together with the enemy alliance', Ascended Kitten of Imperial wrote the next day; Arisen followed; and after 14 hours of constant warring the [[Aesir Pact]] guilds — Sins of Seedia, [[DiamondDeities]] and IceBlue Team, the Federation's last defenders — quit, ending it{{cite:thread-236962|post #17, Ascended Kitten, 11 Nov 2018}}.

The alliance's own channels time the whole collapse to a single morning, and to seven hours and twelve minutes of it. Enemy-held territory stood at 108 at 00:48 on 10 November, with members already reacting to Imperial's departure; Imperial's official withdrawal statement followed at 02:55 and Arisen's at 03:36; by 07:23 the Aesir bloc was discussing whether to go and by 07:50 members were asking what the next alliance should be called; and at 08:00 its three guilds announced a joint withdrawal they had already voted on privately, the vote turning on a single holdout changing position.{{cite:federation-collapse-2018-discord-private|Federation and Aesir channels, 00:48–08:00 on 10 Nov 2018, export clock — eight hours behind UTC in November}}

Eight guilds left that morning, not two, and the alliance's own announcements channel carries all of them. [[Imperial]] was announced out at 12:26 AM and Arisen at 3:36; [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] at 8:04, the three Aesir guilds at 8:05, [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] and [[emorians|Emorians]] at 9:14, and Minerva at 10:11.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Goden, 11/10/2018 12:26 AM and 3:36 AM; Slayne 8:04 AM; 8:05 AM; Arianna 9:14 AM; faceman 10:11 AM — export clock}} Announcing the Aesir withdrawal, its author wrote that "All alliances are born and then die" and that "there was nothing wrong with federation", and that the three guilds would not be siding with anyone until further notice.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|11/10/2018 8:05 AM}} Three minutes later a member wrote in the same channel that the Federation was gone now.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Goden, 11/10/2018 8:08 AM}} That was two hours and nine minutes before its founder said anything.{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Drew1011, 11/10/2018 10:17 AM}}

Imperial's departure was announced two and a half hours before the withdrawal statement above, and members were reacting to it in between.{{cite:federation-collapse-2018-discord-private|Federation channels, 00:48 and 02:55 on 10 Nov 2018, export clock}} Between the first departure and the last, no meeting, motion or discussion of what to do appears anywhere in the leadership channel.{{cite:federation-two-rooms-2018-discord-private|10 Nov 2018, the leadership channel}} They framed that withdrawal as peaceful and mutual, and Imperial's own statement said the split was amicable and that it expected fighting against the Federation to be more enjoyable than fighting alongside it.{{cite:federation-collapse-2018-discord-private|withdrawal statements, 10 Nov 2018}}

The territory losses were visible first and the amicable language came afterwards, seven hours apart on the same morning.{{cite:federation-collapse-2018-discord-private|Federation and Aesir channels, 00:48–08:00 on 10 Nov 2018}}

Drew1011 posted a tribute three days later: 'What killed Federation was... internal affairs.'{{cite:thread-237070|post #1, 13 Nov 2018}}

## Aftermath

The succession was claimed and refused inside a minute. At about eight on the morning of 10 November, in the [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]]'s own server, a member said that the Confederation was now the new Federation and that they were the Confederation. An Aesir figure answered at once that they were independent and had no side.{{cite:after-the-federation-2018-discord-private|10 Nov 2018}} It is not a dispute about who had won but a refusal of the question.

The same room said what the alliance had been for. On 11 November a member stated that the literal reason the Federation had existed was to stop [[hackforums|HackForums]], and another said he had read the founder's own written account of it; a third remarked on the humour of one guild being powerful enough to oblige the game's most formidable guilds to band together against it, and said they should keep it that way.{{cite:after-the-federation-2018-discord-private|11 Nov 2018}} Members in the same exchange discussed which alliance that guild would attack first and concluded it disliked [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]] more than them.{{cite:after-the-federation-2018-discord-private|11 Nov 2018}} Forty seconds earlier the same members had been watching that guild take the map: it had been held back by the Federation and by nothing else, one of them said, and there was nobody left to do it.{{cite:aesir-and-hackforums-november-2018-discord-private|11 Nov 2018}} It is former members' account of their own alliance's purpose, given the day after it dissolved with nothing to gain by giving it, and it matches what the alliance did first: it wiped HackForums three days after forming.{{cite:community-guild-timeline|19 Feb 2018 entry}}


The successor was carried out the same day and planned eleven days before it. Within hours of the withdrawal the [[aesir-pact|Aesir Pact]]'s leadership had divided the Canyon region among its member guilds, assigning specific territory clusters to each, and guilds began applying to it within days — [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] petitioned to affiliate on 12 November, citing the Federation's collapse, and [[thenolifes|TheNoLifes]] joined formally on 17 November under written conditions.{{cite:federation-collapse-2018-discord-private|Aesir leadership channels, 10–17 Nov 2018}} The three-way split of the Canyon had been begun on the afternoon of 30 October, in a channel created in the pact's own server that day and described by its author as a council, on the ground that the Federation's leaders' channel could not be relied on; in the same conversation a member proposed a community-level combination with [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]] expressly to preserve the Federation's core when the Federation fell.{{cite:aesir-oligarchy-founded-2018-discord-private|30 Oct 2018}} Every guild in that room was still a Federation member, and remained one for eleven days.{{cite:aesir-membership-record-2018-discord-private|membership, October–November 2018}}

Members reflecting days afterwards concluded that nobody had inherited the alliance's position: the mega-alliance had fractured into competing groupings, and the rival that had fought it for a year was too weak to dominate what was left.{{cite:federation-decline-2018-discord-private|November 2018}}

The community timeline describes the winter 2018–19 interregnum simply: 'After Fed all guilds keep to themselves with valk owning most of Gavel'{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, the community guild-timeline image}} — [[Valkyrie]]'s guilds, Kingdom Foxes and Imperial, took more territories over that winter than any others, 11,738 and 8,447 between the Federation's dissolution and the end of February 2019.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures made by each guild, 11 Nov 2018 – 28 Feb 2019 UTC; raw counts, no free-for-all list applied}} No alliance had any territory declared open in that window: the Federation's list of 12 October 2018 was the last it published, and nothing replaced it until [[terra|Terra]] declared eighteen territories on 14 September 2019.{{cite:free-for-all-amendments-2018-2019-discord-private|the declaration of 12 Oct 2018}}{{cite:terra-alliance-announcements-discord-private|14 Sep 2019}} [[sol|SOL]] was created in the post-collapse chaos, and [[Luna]] formed on 14 February 2019 to counter it{{cite:community-guild-timeline|14 Feb 2019 entry}}, opening [[the-alliance-wars-of-2019|the alliance wars of 2019]].

Times here are the Discord export's own clock, which ran eight hours behind UTC in February 2018.

## Fifty-two minutes

The [[coalition|Coalition]] began to come apart at twelve minutes past seven on the evening of 16 February 2018, when the first of six member guilds announced in its general channel that it was withdrawing.{{cite:coalition-fracture-2018-discord-private|Coalition general channel, 16 Feb 2018}} At 20:05:07 a new Discord server was created, and that server was the Federation.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|server creation computed from the Discord id, 16 Feb 2018}} The gap is fifty-two minutes and twenty-seven seconds, and it is fixed to the second: a Discord identifier carries the moment of its own creation inside it.

The first account joined thirty-four seconds afterwards.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|join records, 16 Feb 2018}} Seven seconds later the first thing said in the room was that the alliances were merging, and the next thing said was that it was done.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018, 20:05-20:06}}

Nothing was put to a vote, and the decision had been taken before the room existed. The merger was announced inside the new server as a completed fact within its first minute, by the head of [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]].{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018, 20:05-20:06}} The Coalition's own leader did not post until 20:10:57, and his first substantive question was what the alliance he was merging with was called.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018, 20:10}} He was told twice that it had never really had a name, and that the name people used for it was a joke.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018, from 20:11}} The alliance's first recorded vote falls the next day, and lasted two minutes and twenty-nine seconds: it closed four to nothing, and two members reacted after it had closed.{{cite:federation-vote-procedure-february-2018-discord-private|first vote, 17 Feb 2018}}

The other party to the merger was not an alliance in the ordinary sense. It was an existing but unnamed grouping around Sins of Seedia, whose leader told the room that his guild had been in an alliance the whole time and that it held more member guilds than the new arrivals supposed.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018}} Four days later a member of the new alliance gave its origin as the surviving half of the Coalition joined to [[illustratus|Illustratus]] — a participant's summary made after the fact, and not the partner named on the night itself.{{cite:coalition-fracture-2018-discord-private|20 Feb 2018}}

Announcing the collapse on 10 November 2018 the alliance's founder counted its life at 267 days, which reaches back to 16 February and to the hour the server was made.{{cite:federation-public-announcements|Drew1011, 10 Nov 2018}} On the export's clock the founding falls on the evening of the 16th; in UTC it falls at 04:05 on the 17th.

## An enemy, not a membership

Eleven accounts posted 745 messages in the server between 20:05 and 22:37 that night.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|traffic over the export's local calendar day, 16 Feb 2018}} Those who stated an office stated it to one another as strangers would: the head of Sins of Seedia, a co-leader of [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]], a co-leader of [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]], and a chief of TheEnchanted, invited in at 20:52, who said he was acting as leader on the strength of being his guild's most active member rather than of holding the rank.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|introductions, 16 Feb 2018}}

What the room agreed on was an enemy. Members named [[hackforums|HackForums]], [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] and [[house-of-sentinels|House of Sentinels]] within seven minutes, and two further guilds were named and then withdrawn from the list by the same speaker.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018, 20:05-20:12}} Nobody produced a list of members. At 21:52 the chief of TheEnchanted asked to be given a list naming the allied guilds he was not to attack, saying he was still bad at remembering who counted as an ally, and none was given.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018, 21:52}} Five days later the alliance published a territorial settlement naming twenty-two member guilds.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018 9:12 PM}}

[[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] was proposed that night and refused. Asked at 20:12 whether the guild was in the alliance, the Coalition's leader said no in two successive messages, and another member said no twice more and said he did not care.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018, 20:12}} The room granted that its fighting strength was formidable and gave its objection as an inability to deal with its leader.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|16 Feb 2018, the objection}} It was inside the alliance twenty-three hours later — see [[federation-founding-membership-2018|the Federation's founding membership]].

## The drafting channel

A channel called alliance-drafting was created at 21:56:37 that night, one hour and fifty-one minutes after the server itself.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|channel creation computed from the Discord id, 16 Feb 2018}} It carried 101 messages in its whole life, eight on the 16th and ninety-three on the 17th, and its last was at 13:59 on 17 February.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|alliance-drafting, message counts and last message}} Nothing was posted in it afterwards.

Its substantive content is two exchanges: on the night a member proposed that the territories be won back before anybody divided them, and was agreed with; the following afternoon two members proposed inviting the second guild of Titans Valor's leader.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|alliance-drafting, 16-17 Feb 2018}} Four separate people asked in it what the channel was for, one of them in the last message it ever carried.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|alliance-drafting, the four questions}} A member answered that it would become a serious discussion once the alliance's leader arrived, and that until then it was people posting nonsense at each other.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|alliance-drafting, 17 Feb 2018}} The one room the alliance made in order to constitute itself never carried a constitutional discussion.

## The rooms, and the name

The permanent structure was built the next morning in forty-three minutes. Between 11:37:29 and 12:20:01 on 17 February sixteen channels were created, and their names reproduce the Coalition's server almost exactly: help-and-trade, alliance-general, war-discussion, guild-leaders, links, info-welcome, information-alliance, two announcement channels, two channels for the leaders' serialised histories, and five voice rooms.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|channel creations computed from Discord ids, 17 Feb 2018}} One was renamed rather than reproduced and two of the Coalition's were not carried across.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|the two channel lists compared}} The Coalition's own server had been assembled from the same list, in about seventy-seven minutes, five weeks earlier, in the hour after the original was destroyed.{{cite:coalition-discord-deleted-january-2018-discord-private|12 Jan 2018, 04:19-04:20 UTC}} The same room list was built from scratch twice in five weeks.

The alliance was named while those rooms were being made. With the first two channels created at 11:37:29 and 11:37:41, the founder asked the general channel at 11:37:31 for another generic name for an alliance and said he was reading synonyms.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|17 Feb 2018, 11:37}} A minute later he said this was exactly how he had chosen the name Coalition, and that the Coalition's name had been meant to be temporary.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|17 Feb 2018, 11:38}} Two members proposed Federation within seconds of each other and he adopted it a few seconds after that.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|17 Feb 2018, the two proposals}} Two members recorded the same origin independently later that day, one having searched for synonyms of alliance and the other for synonyms of the name of the alliance they had just lost.{{cite:federation-founding-week-2018-discord-private|17 Feb 2018}} He gave the same account on the forums six months afterwards.{{cite:drew1011-storytime|part 17, composed 30 Aug 2018}}

## No membership document

The info-welcome and information-alliance channels were created on 17 February and left empty. The first message in either is of 22 September 2018, seven months later, and the membership and enemy card posted then was itself revised on 1 March 2019, so its contents belong to that revision.{{cite:federation-founding-night-2018-discord-private|info-welcome and information-alliance, 17 Feb - 22 Sep 2018}} The Coalition had posted its own membership and enemy card on the day its replacement server was built.{{cite:coalition-information|posted 1/11/2018 9:33 PM}}

Some list of member guilds was being kept that February — a leader undertook on 28 February to record a subguild indented beneath its parent on it — but where it was kept was never established.{{cite:federation-vote-procedure-february-2018-discord-private|28 Feb 2018}}

The members were nonetheless reading a list in one of those two channels that February. On 22 February a member asked the leaders' channel for the ally list so that he would not attack a friend by mistake, was directed by the founder to the welcome channel, and answered that it was not there.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|22 Feb 2018, the request}} Two days later a captain of a member guild came into the general channel to ask whether the guild NYN was really an ally, was pointed at the information channel, and reported that NYN was not in it; two members told him that settled the matter.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|24 Feb 2018, the NYN exchange}}

So the alliance had at least one such list in its first fortnight, kept by one officer and amended by asking her in the open, and none of it was kept.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|the two lists and their keeper, February 2018}} Whether the February content was deleted, replaced, or posted somewhere a member merely thought of as that channel was never established.{{cite:federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018-discord-private|the February content and the September message}} The lists, and what they could not record, are at [[federation-lists-and-truces-february-2018|the alliance's lists and truces]].

The alliance never acquired a constitution either. In May 2018 a member proposed drafting a formal charter, noting that it had run on rules announced informally and never set down.{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|May 2018}}

[[the-federation|The Federation]] held every territory on the map through July 2018. It renamed itself on the 14th{{cite:federation-late-july-2018-discord-private|14 Jul 2018}}, lost four member guilds between the 22nd and the 25th{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 7/22/2018 and 7/25/2018}}, and spent the middle of the month being told by the guild that did most of its fighting that it would stop fighting for anyone else.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|17 Jul 2018}}

## A quarter of the war

On 10 July 2018 a member read the leadership's own war-activity graphs into the channel: there had been a day on which [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] fought more wars than every other member guild put together, the alliance's line and the guild's line were both climbing but not together, and he expected them to come apart within about two months.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|10 Jul 2018}} A week later the guild's Viceroy put its share of every war the alliance had ever fought at twenty-five per cent the last time he had looked, and said the balance was bad without meaning it against any other guild.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|17 Jul 2018}} A member of another guild put two guilds' combined share at thirty.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|17 Jul 2018, the thirty per cent figure}}

## The guild that stopped helping

The same day the Viceroy told the leadership channel that Kingdom Foxes would take a break from helping allies until further notice, and would fight only for its own assigned territory and for free-for-all ground.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|17 Jul 2018}} Asked why, he said the other leaders had been asked to do more for allies because his guild was carrying the heavy work, and that most had either not done so or could not.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|17 Jul 2018, the reasons given}} The quarter of the alliance's fighting his guild was doing rested, he said, on three or four people.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|17 Jul 2018, three or four people}}

Two days later the complaint went to the whole alliance. A notice copied into the announcements channel on 19 July addressed "this morning's (last night for NA) reaction time, or rather the lack thereof, of several guilds when they were tagged to reclaim the several territories that the enemy guilds held", said people had been tagged three times before anything was done, and that the tags were being "treated as spam and completely ignored".{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|1:52 PM, 7/19/2018}} The alliance had been arguing about those tags for a fortnight: on 27 June its leader reminded members that tagging the captains reached every captain in the server{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 6/27/2018}}, and on the 29th said he would mute anyone who abused them.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 6/29/2018}}

## Seventy territories too many

The suspension announced on the 17th had been meant to run a week.{{cite:federation-fox-withdrawal-july-2018-discord-private|19 Jul 2018, the intended length}} It was overtaken on the 22nd. That morning the standing leader of Kingdom Foxes' war team told the war channel that his guild holding seventy territories more than it had been assigned was the opposite of what its place in the alliance should be, that it should not have happened and should not happen again, and that he was withdrawing it from all wars until the figure came down through its allies retaking their own ground.{{cite:federation-fox-withdrawal-july-2018-discord-private|22 Jul 2018, the withdrawal}} Minutes later he put the guild at about nine tenths or more of the assigned territory of five member guilds and named them: [[dogsamongus|DogsAmongUs]], Renegade, Verinian Trials, [[the-hive|The Hive]] and [[lunatic|Lunatic]].{{cite:federation-fox-withdrawal-july-2018-discord-private|22 Jul 2018, the five guilds}} It had held most of the Light Realm as well while [[ex-nihilo|Ex Nihilo]] reclaimed.{{cite:federation-fox-withdrawal-july-2018-discord-private|22 Jul 2018, the Light Realm}} In the same message he said another member guild needed help immediately.{{cite:federation-fox-withdrawal-july-2018-discord-private|22 Jul 2018, the call for help}}

The second withdrawal did not last the morning. Within ten minutes a member asked the war channel for help in the Light Realm, and the same leader answered that he had already tagged for it.{{cite:federation-fox-withdrawal-july-2018-discord-private|22 Jul 2018, the request and the reply}} Reading that as the tagging simply continuing is this article's; what he said was that he had tagged.

Between 22 and 25 July Kingdom Foxes took between 77 and 122 territories a day outside the declared free-for-all list, against a daily range of six to 122 across the twelve days before.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures by Kingdom Foxes by UTC day, 10–25 Jul 2018, free-for-all list of 4 Jun 2018 excluded to 24 Jul and the pool amended on 25 Jul thereafter; the two give the same figures: 6–122 daily on 10–21 Jul; 122, 77, 95 and 94 on 22–25 Jul}} The announcement was made at 08:39 on the alliance's clock, seven hours behind the log's, so the figure for the 22nd straddles it. Neither this announcement nor the one of the 17th forbade the guild fighting for its own assigned ground or for free-for-all territory, so the figures are consistent with both having been kept.{{cite:federation-fox-withdrawal-july-2018-discord-private|17 and 22 Jul 2018, what the announcements permitted}}

## Thirty territories for an island

The alliance spent the first half of the month moving its own furniture. On the 8th, hours after admitting Renegade, a member set out a reassignment of five guilds in the war channel — [[angels-of-eternal|Angels of Eternal]] into [[ex-nihilo|Ex Nihilo]]'s Light Realm holdings, [[dogsamongus|DogsAmongUs]] to the North Desert, [[diamonddeities|DiamondDeities]] to Elkurn and the newest member to the ravines.{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|8 Jul 2018, the reassignment}} His reasoning was that a guild had been given ground it could not have held if anybody had attacked it{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|8 Jul 2018, the reasoning}}, and another member answered that one of the guilds named had a single active fighter.{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|8 Jul 2018, the objection}} On the 10th a third revived a scheme letting small guilds take territory off large ones for a trial week{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|10 Jul 2018, the trial week}}; asked what the problem was, he answered that one guild owned half the province.{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|10 Jul 2018, half the province}}

At ten to six on the morning of the 16th [[imperial|Imperial]] put a written proposal to the leadership channel. It offered to give up its thirty territories in Gavel — its holdings in the Light Forest, Aldorei and Cinfras — for the whole of Corkus Island including its two free-for-all zones.{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, the offer}} It would fall down the leaderboards and did not mind{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, the leaderboards}}, and the thirty released would relieve the assignment crisis and suit three guilds or more, [[lunatic|Lunatic]] being named as one that had complained of its own.{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, the case made}} Four minutes later the leader of [[fantasy|Fantasy]] said his guild would leave the Federation if it happened{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, 05:52, the threat to leave}}, and members pointed out that Fantasy already held about half the island.{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, half of Corkus}} A counter-proposal giving Imperial the island and Fantasy the canyon came from the leader of [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]], who dropped it on being told the same thing{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, the counter-proposal}}; two hours later he offered half of his own guild's territory to make up whatever Fantasy lost.{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, 08:12, the offer of compensation}}

Nesaak came up in the same hours and reached no answer either. Members proposed dividing it and the Nether territories between Imperial, [[the-hive|The Hive]] and [[iceblue-team|Ice Blue Team]]{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, Nesaak}}, and one proposed drawing the map again from scratch.{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, the redraw}} Two members answered that redistribution would not stop the attacks but move them to whichever guild absorbed the ground.{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, the answer on redistribution}}

It ended with a question put to the guild that stood to lose. Shortly after nine a member asked Fantasy's leader whether he would settle Corkus himself or put it to a ballot, and another thought a ballot unwise but said the choice was his.{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, the question}} He would not give Corkus up, he answered, and would see whether his guild could trim anything for the others.{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|16 Jul 2018, the answer}} The alliance had a voting channel, a stated threshold and a team that drew its maps, and its largest territorial dispute of the year was settled by asking the loser whether he minded.

Eight days later it was remembered as a near-collapse. Arguing on the 24th that the smaller alliances inside the Federation made member guilds less likely to leave, the founder was answered with the episode as evidence against him: Imperial had asked for Corkus, Fantasy had said it would go, and then the [[aesir-pact|Aesir]] pact had said it would go too.{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|24 Jul 2018, the pacts}} Nothing had come of it, the member said, but it had been close.{{cite:federation-corkus-dispute-july-2018-discord-private|24 Jul 2018, uncomfortably close}}

## A plurality is not two thirds

At six minutes past noon on 19 July a member put a motion to the voting channel proposing that three of the alliance's own guilds be removed.{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|19 Jul 2018, 12:06, the motion}} Kasai Shinrai [Two] had fought no war in over a month, its inactivity blamed on its leader being away{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|19 Jul 2018, the first guild}}; [[angels-of-eternal|Angels of Eternal]] brought nothing to the alliance in the way of warring{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|19 Jul 2018, the second guild}}; [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] showed no sign of being able to take ground anywhere, with a complaint about its leader's conduct the mover attributed to most people he had spoken to.{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|19 Jul 2018, the third guild}} Five options followed: expel all three, expel none, or any of the three pairs.{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|19 Jul 2018, the five options}}

The reactions the motion carried give eight accounts to expelling all three and six to expelling nobody, two to the pair of Kasai Shinrai and Angels of Eternal, one to the pair of Kasai Shinrai and Titans Valor, and none at all to the third pair.{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|19 Jul 2018, the reactions recorded on the motion}} That is seventeen marks from fifteen accounts, so at least one account marked more than one option.{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|19 Jul 2018, seventeen marks, fifteen accounts}} No account stands on every option, one of them having drawn none, and the mover marked only the option he had proposed.{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|19 Jul 2018, the mover's own mark}}

It failed on a threshold the motion never stated. At 20:37 that evening a member asked the leadership channel whether the vote had not technically passed{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|19 Jul 2018, 20:37, the question}}, and was told a kick needed two thirds, and asked whether he had not been around long enough to know it.{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|19 Jul 2018, the threshold given in answer}} Ninety minutes earlier a guild leader newly seated among the leadership had been told the rest of the conventions: that votes were supposed to run twenty-four hours, that where the outcome was obvious they were cut short, and that the voting channel was for votes and not for talking.{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|19 Jul 2018, 19:21, the conventions}} The threshold was stated for this motion, on this day, in another room.

Two of the three guilds left of their own accord three days later, and the alliance could not agree whether they had been thrown out. Asked in the war channel on the afternoon of the 22nd whether Titans Valor had been officially removed, one guild leader said it had been voted out and another corrected him: it had not been kicked, it had left.{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|22 Jul 2018, the disagreement}} Earlier that day a member had told the same channel to attack the guild, explaining that its removal would not be announced for a while and that people were used to waiting for an announcement before acting.{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|22 Jul 2018, 13:21, the delay}}

The guild named first, on the plainest grounds of the three, was still a member when the month closed. On the 30th the founder told the leadership channel he had no objection to removing Kasai Shinrai{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|30 Jul 2018, the founder on Kasai Shinrai}}, and that his complaint had been with the way the mover had compressed his argument about the other two into something he had not said; another suggested starting a vote.{{cite:federation-kick-vote-19-july-2018-discord-private|30 Jul 2018, the complaint about the motion}}

## The Alliance, and the factions

The rename of the 14th was not explained to the membership. At 05:27 a member asked the general channel what had happened to the server's name and icon, and was told they were now the Alliance and the Federation was no more{{cite:federation-guild-factions-event-2018-discord-private|14 Jul 2018, 05:27, the rename noticed}}; five minutes later another asked whether [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] and [[hackforums|HackForums]] were meant to be the rebels or the dark side.{{cite:federation-guild-factions-event-2018-discord-private|14 Jul 2018, the question about the enemy alliances}} Asked that afternoon in the war channel why the name had changed, a member answered that it was a joke.{{cite:federation-guild-factions-event-2018-discord-private|14 Jul 2018, 14:08, the explanation}} Outsiders could not tell what the new name meant either: on the 18th a member pasted into the war channel a conversation from elsewhere that ended with players following the game's guild-factions event asking whether the Alliance was the Federation.{{cite:federation-guild-factions-event-2018-discord-private|18 Jul 2018, the outside conversation}}

The event was then treated as a threat to the alliance's structure for one afternoon. On the 22nd a leader posted the forum announcement to the leadership channel: as he understood it the game would now have official alliances of guilds, each with an agenda of its own, and it needed discussing.{{cite:federation-guild-factions-event-2018-discord-private|22 Jul 2018, the question put}} Two leaders answered that their own guilds were already asking which faction they would be joining, one that members were talking about leaving to join factions.{{cite:federation-guild-factions-event-2018-discord-private|22 Jul 2018, the guilds asking}} One called the concept silly and was told it would be silly only if organised guilds and guild alliances did not already exist.{{cite:federation-guild-factions-event-2018-discord-private|22 Jul 2018, the exchange about the concept}} By mid-afternoon leaders were reporting that the comments on the post put Imperial with the rebels and that Emorians and Ice Blue Team had chosen the mercenaries.{{cite:federation-guild-factions-event-2018-discord-private|22 Jul 2018, the choices reported}}

## Four guilds out in eleven days

Late on 20 July the Kingdom of Foxes' Viceroy removed [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]]'s leader's roles on information he said he could not verify, meaning to speak to the man before deciding anything — the alliance's one suspension pending an inquiry, set out at [[alliance-governance|alliance governance]].{{cite:federation-late-july-2018-discord-private|20 Jul 2018}}

The next day the alliance's founder gave his own account of what was happening to that guild. Its leader, he said in the general channel, was fond of the Federation and devoted to Kingdom Foxes, but had been subjected to constant harassment in the leadership channel — enough to push anybody into leaving and turning on the alliance — and something like a third of those present grew upset whenever he spoke.{{cite:federation-late-july-2018-discord-private|21 Jul 2018}} In the same conversation a member reported that somebody had just left [[angels-of-eternal|Angels of Eternal]] for [[hackforums|HackForums]] to fight the alliance, and the founder answered that he had seen it the night before and had already removed that person's roles.{{cite:federation-late-july-2018-discord-private|21 Jul 2018, the defection}}

Both guilds were gone the next day: "Titans Valor [ANO] and Angels of Eternal [AoE] have left the Federation. Aphelion [Aph] has joined."{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 7/22/2018}}

A member counting that afternoon made the alliance seventeen guilds.{{cite:federation-late-july-2018-discord-private|22 Jul 2018}} Three days later members in the general channel were asking what the initials of the opposing alliance stood for, and were told it was the grouping built around [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]].{{cite:federation-late-july-2018-discord-private|25 Jul 2018}}

Two more went on the 25th. [[hall-of-fame|Hall of Fame]] and Verinian Trials left together, and the founder told the membership that they "are leaving and have partnered with CGoW, get ready to War".{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 7/25/2018}}

Verinian Trials had been voted in a month earlier to the day, on the afternoon of [[the-federation-june-crisis|25 June]].{{cite:federation-25-june-2018-discord-private|25 Jun 2018}} Two guilds arrived in the same weeks, Renegade on 7 July and Aphelion on the 22nd.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|7/7/2018 and Drew1011, 7/22/2018}}

A fifth departure was being prepared underneath. The alliance had discussed expelling a member over its leader's conduct in June and never put it to a vote{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|June 2018}}; by the end of July that guild's own active members were building a replacement guild to take its place.{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|July 2018}}

An application arrived on the last night of the month. The founder told the leadership channel that Metric [Met]'s leader had asked to join for the third day running and had written the paragraph the alliance required of applicants, that he was not sure the man was serious, and that he would investigate before starting a vote.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|31 Jul 2018}} The guild's leader had been attacking Federation members.{{cite:federation-admitting-its-attackers-2018-discord-private|2 Aug 2018, the wipes}} It was admitted on 30 August and removed three days later{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/1/2018 and 9/2/2018}} — see [[federation-admissions-august-2018|the Federation's admissions of August 2018]].

## The night of 29 July

In the last days of July the successor coalition mounted a sustained assault on the alliance's largest guild, with roughly fifteen guilds involved and five to seven attacking at once over about sixteen hours.{{cite:enemy-alliances-2018-discord-private|late July 2018}}

Shortly after midnight a Federation announcement thanked "everyone who showed up to support us tonight after being targeted for over 14 hours".{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Arianna, 12:23 AM, 7/30/2018}}

The capture log carries the same night. Between 17:00 UTC on 29 July and 07:30 UTC on 30 July, Kingdom Foxes lost 182 territories outside the alliance's declared free-for-all list, spread over 36 territories and taken by fifteen distinct guilds{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures from Kingdom Foxes, 29 Jul 17:00 UTC – 30 Jul 07:30 UTC: 182 captures, 36 territories, 15 attacking guilds. The pool in force is the list of 4 Jun 2018 as amended on 25 Jul — Avos Temple and Factory Entrance out, Guild Hall and Mine Base Plains in — and the two states give the same figures, none of the four swapped territories changing hands in the window}} — three times the losses of any other guild in that window.{{cite:territory-exchanges|losses by defending guild, 29 Jul 17:00 UTC – 30 Jul 07:30 UTC, free-for-all list of 4 Jun 2018 as amended on 25 Jul excluded}} The fighting ran in every hour from midday on the 29th to five in the morning on the 30th and then stopped.{{cite:territory-exchanges|hourly captures from Kingdom Foxes, 29–30 Jul 2018 UTC, free-for-all list of 4 Jun 2018 as amended on 25 Jul excluded}} The announcement is stamped on the alliance's own clock, seven hours behind the log's.

Identifying the besieged guild with Kingdom Foxes is this article's; a replay of the capture record to 26 July put it joint-largest in the alliance on 64 territories.{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|running totals at 26 Jul 2018}} The 29th was its heaviest day of the month, at 242 contested captures against it from twenty-five guilds.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures from Kingdom Foxes by UTC day, July 2018, free-for-all list of 4 Jun 2018 excluded to 24 Jul and as amended on 25 Jul thereafter: peak 242 on 29 Jul, 25 attacking guilds}}

## What the month came to

Across July 2018 Kingdom Foxes took 2,586 territories and lost 2,572, both counted outside the free-for-all pool as it stood on each day.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures by and from Kingdom Foxes, July 2018 UTC, against the 4 Jun 2018 list to 25 Jul and that list as amended on 25 Jul thereafter: 2,586 taken, 2,572 lost}} A net of fourteen, in the month in which it did a quarter of the alliance's fighting and told the alliance it would stop.{{cite:federation-july-2018-discord-private|17 Jul 2018}}

## What is not known

The day began with an argument between two of the alliance's senior figures, and nothing records what it was about.{{cite:federation-25-june-2018-discord-private|general channel, 25 Jun 2018}} Everything that followed was written down; the subject of the argument never was. Asked that afternoon what it had been about, the alliance's leader said to find somebody else for the full story and that he would not discuss it himself.{{cite:federation-25-june-2018-discord-private|general channel, 25 Jun 2018}}

## The day

The alliance's Discord administrator, who was also the leader of a member guild, deleted every role on the alliance's own server that morning.{{cite:federation-25-june-2018-discord-private|25 Jun 2018}} Members noticed their roles missing through the late morning, and another leader spent it repairing them, apologising in the general channel for the disruption.{{cite:federation-25-june-2018-discord-private|general channel, 25 Jun 2018}}

The alliance's leader set out the sequence himself that afternoon: an argument broke out, the administrator deleted all the alliance roles, he woke up and removed her administrator rights, a vote was started to expel her guild, and she left the server.{{cite:federation-25-june-2018-discord-private|general channel, 25 Jun 2018}} The leadership was not unanimous about the expulsion — another guild's leader said in the leaders channel that he did not wish to see her removed.{{cite:federation-25-june-2018-discord-private|leaders channel, 25 Jun 2018}}

Two further departures fell in the same afternoon. Twenty minutes after the leadership channel turned to removing inactive members, [[paladins-united|Paladins United]]'s leader announced that his guild was leaving too, giving as his reason that it did not contribute and had become inactive, and repeating the announcement in a second channel two minutes later with an invitation to stay in touch personally.{{cite:federation-25-june-2018-discord-private|25 Jun 2018}} A third leader announced his own departure a little over two hours later, without giving a reason.{{cite:federation-25-june-2018-discord-private|leaders channel, 25 Jun 2018}} Seven minutes after that the alliance announced two departures and one admission: [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] and [[kingdomphoenixes|KingdomPhoenixes]] had left, and Verinian Trials had joined.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2:17 PM, 6/25/2018}} Which of the day's three departures the second name records was never set out.

A replacement was admitted before the evening. Verinian Trials was voted in the same day on an overwhelming and unusually quick vote, and welcomed in the leaders channel with members crediting it for having helped fight off the opposing coalition in the preceding days.{{cite:federation-25-june-2018-discord-private|25 Jun 2018}} Nobody at the time connected that admission to the seat vacated hours earlier.

## What it shows

The alliance's business lived in one place. Territory assignments, admission and expulsion votes, war coordination and the membership itself all ran through the Discord server{{cite:alliance-governance-mechanics-discord-private|voting practice, 2016–2019}}{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 2/21/2018}}, and on 25 June 2018 one person removed every role in it in a morning. The alliance had no written constitution to fall back on: a member proposed drafting one the month before, noting that it had run on rules announced informally and never set down.{{cite:federation-governance-2018-discord-private|May 2018}}

[[the-federation|The Federation]] held the entire territory map at the time.

On 26 May 2018 WynnChairman opened a thread on the Wynncraft forums titled "Guild Feeding Rules Clarification (mods Read Plz)".{{cite:thread-226798|post #1, the title}} It asked the game's moderators to confirm whether [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s conduct counted as feeding and to set out a definitive criterion for what did.{{cite:thread-226798|post #1, the request}} It drew 143 posts in five days and was closed to replies at the end of them.{{cite:thread-226798-p08|post #143, 30 May 2018, and thread status}}

## The post

The opening post set out the alliance's system — each member guild assigned territories, certain other territories left open to anyone — and then described the practice under accusation. When the opposing alliance attacked a guild that could not keep up, other member guilds retook the ground and defended it lightly, so that the guild it was assigned to could reclaim it more easily and the guilds that had retaken it did not spend heavily holding land they would lose again anyway.{{cite:thread-226798|post #1}} This, the post argued, kept the enemy from accumulating territory without forcing an ally into a long reclamation.{{cite:thread-226798|post #1, the justification}}

It then quoted the server rule it was answering: "Do not use alternate guilds or sub-guilds to maintain territory."{{cite:thread-226798|post #1, quoting rule 18}} Its closing line was a denial: "All guilds in Fed are equal and none are alternate/sub guilds."{{cite:thread-226798|post #1}}

The post was drafted inside the alliance before it went out. Its author put it before the Federation's guild leaders and asked them to confirm that his account was accurate, saying he had not been able to be active.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|26 May 2018, the draft}} A leader corrected one detail, that defending lightly was about not wasting money as well as about the owner reclaiming faster.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|26 May 2018, the correction}} He said he would post it regardless if nobody answered, and linked the thread in the alliance's general channel a few minutes later.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|26 May 2018, the posting}}

The first reply said the guild rules were being rewritten and that what to do about the Federation would be decided once that was finished.{{cite:thread-226798|post #2, JuicedBananas, 26 May 2018}}

## Five weeks before the post

The argument the thread answered had been running in the alliance's own leadership channel since the middle of April. On 18 April 2018 the channel was dealing with a threat to report [[the-hive|The Hive]] for feeding, because it defended with fifty high-level defenders.{{cite:federation-feeding-before-the-controversy-2018-discord-private|18 Apr 2018, the threat}} The founder paraphrased the charge back to the channel as one the accusers' own guilds earned equally.{{cite:federation-feeding-before-the-controversy-2018-discord-private|18 Apr 2018, the charge relayed}} The leader of [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] said her guild used ten level-fifty defenders.{{cite:federation-feeding-before-the-controversy-2018-discord-private|18 Apr 2018, ten defenders}} A member said he attacked with twenty and defended low so as not to waste money, and was accused of feeding by people doing the same.{{cite:federation-feeding-before-the-controversy-2018-discord-private|18 Apr 2018, twenty defenders}} Four minutes later a member reported that the rival alliance's second had gone to a moderator of the game and was pressing for light defending to be prohibited altogether.{{cite:federation-feeding-before-the-controversy-2018-discord-private|18 Apr 2018, the approach reported}} The approach itself went unrecorded.

The substance was argued a week later, and it was about money. On 25 April 2018 the founder told the channel that it irritated him that the other side thought nobody ought to be permitted to place fewer than a thousand defenders, and asked why the game offered the option if it was not to be used.{{cite:federation-feeding-before-the-controversy-2018-discord-private|25 Apr 2018, the option}}

That morning, in the war channel, a member of [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] asked for the arrangement the alliance would deny practising five weeks later. If the others wanted to fight [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] and then feed his guild its ground back he would not object, he said, because he could not really be bothered to do it himself — and he used the critics' own word for it.{{cite:federation-feeding-before-the-controversy-2018-discord-private|25 Apr 2018, the request}} He was speaking for himself and not for his guild, which was struggling: the rival alliance's second kept him and his guild's leader on a friends list and waited for them to log off before attacking.{{cite:federation-feeding-before-the-controversy-2018-discord-private|25 Apr 2018, the friends list}}

Two months on it was still being negotiated in the open, and the negotiation was about who bore the cost. At 11:12 on 27 June 2018 a member told the war channel's fighters to start taking their territories back or he would go back to defending with hundreds of defenders.{{cite:federation-feeding-before-the-controversy-2018-discord-private|27 Jun 2018, the ultimatum}} Another answered that it was hard to take ground back with three guilds attacking.{{cite:federation-feeding-before-the-controversy-2018-discord-private|27 Jun 2018, three guilds}} The first replied that it was the other who had asked him to defend lightly in the first place, and that BuildCraftia was now taking the territories.{{cite:federation-feeding-before-the-controversy-2018-discord-private|27 Jun 2018, the answer}}

The other side used the same word for the opposite behaviour. Writing out her account of the war in a Federation channel on 5 November 2018, a former war leader of BuildCraftia described holding [[holders-of-le|Holders of LE]]'s assigned territory alone earlier in the year, and called [[sins-of-seedia|Sins of Seedia]] and [[imperial|Imperial]] the worst feeders, for defences of about three thousand that she could not beat.{{cite:federation-feeding-before-the-controversy-2018-discord-private|5 Nov 2018, the account}} The events in it are not dated by the telling.

Setting the April exchanges beside the statement of 26 May is this article's; nobody in the leadership channel referred back to them.

## The denial beside the alliance's own leadership channel

Setting the closing line against what the alliance's leaders had been saying among themselves is this article's; nobody in the thread did it.

Six weeks earlier the same server rule had been posted into the Federation's leadership channel by one of its officers and pinned there by the founder.{{cite:subguild-rule-and-the-games-staff-2018-discord-private|10 Apr 2018}} One member guild had been created as another's subguild: [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] established [[illustratus|Illustratus]] [Ius] on 16 February 2018.{{cite:gdoc-1fev2xrzlfbeo2ll|Feb 2018}} In April the founder had directed that Kasai Shinrai be struck from a vote count, because counting it would have given Titans Valor's leader two votes.{{cite:federation-obligation-to-attack-2018-discord-private|4 Apr 2018, the count}} In July a member guild's leader described the device in plain terms, saying he might keep his own guild alive on a second account while joining a better one, and naming the founder and House of Legends [HoL] as where he had seen it done.{{cite:titans-valor-departure-july-2018-discord-private|10 Jul 2018, the second account}} Two days later a leader told the same channel that House of Legends had become a community guild and was no longer in the alliance.{{cite:titans-valor-departure-july-2018-discord-private|12 Jul 2018, House of Legends}} In August the founder stated the terms on which such guilds were tolerated: that they left allies alone and held no assigned territory.{{cite:federation-libertas-2018-discord-private|12 Aug 2018}}

None of that shows the denial to have been false. The rule forbade using a second guild to hold territory, and no Federation guild was shown to have been used that way. Beside the public statement that the alliance contained no subguilds stands a leadership channel treating them as ordinary business in the same months, before the post and after it. The practice and the rule are described at [[territory-warfare|territory warfare]].

## Nobody could define the word

About ninety minutes after the post went up, an officer had the war channel tagged and asked members to send him privately what they thought feeding meant, with examples, because he wanted more than one opinion.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|26 May 2018, the canvass}} A member suggested he collect answers from outside the alliance too, so that they would not all lean one way.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|26 May 2018, the suggestion}} The request was pinned so that it would not be lost.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|26 May 2018, pinned}}

The next afternoon its advisory channel produced two definitions that cannot both hold. One member said feeding was defending a captured territory barely or not at all in the hope that a particular guild would reclaim it — which is what the public post had just described the alliance doing.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|27 May 2018, the first definition}} Asked what barely defending meant, he said he could not say, that the published rule was too vague to work from, and that the alliance should not have to invent a definition of its own.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|27 May 2018, too vague}} Another member said it meant running guilds whose only function was to feed a larger one, and that since the Federation had no subguilds and no single guild collecting their territory, what it did was not feeding.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|27 May 2018, the second definition}} Both speakers were ordinary members, and both treated the question as settled.

The public thread had the same problem. "There's a very grey line between outright feeding and defending lightly", wrote ChuckeyTheBear, of a guild fighting the alliance.{{cite:thread-226798|post #5, ChuckeyTheBear, 26 May 2018}} He set out a siege in which his side found a Federation guild's recaptured territories defended with a hundred low-level mobs, switched to attacking the recapturing guild instead, and then met defences of nine hundred.{{cite:thread-226798|post #5, the siege}}

## The administrator's answer

The argument closed on 30 May with a post by the Wynncraft administrator jpresent. He wrote that he had been reworking guilds, polling the community and monitoring the wars and defences being used, and that the proposed changes to the rules would clash with what he was developing, so the war rules would not change.{{cite:thread-226798-p08|post #143, jpresent, 30 May 2018}} Keeping track of who was feeding whom would be an immense task the moderation team could not handle, and the practice did not damage other guilds as much as it looked.{{cite:thread-226798-p08|post #143, the reasons given}} Future updates would discourage feeding and alliances somewhat, but he did not see the need to ban it from the game.{{cite:thread-226798-p08|post #143, future updates}} One prohibition he restated: "Do not purposefully keep a territory under attack between two guilds for the purpose of making sure other guilds can't take it."{{cite:thread-226798-p08|post #143, the rule kept}} The thread was locked at that post.{{cite:thread-226798-p08|thread status, not open for further replies}}

The alliance had been holding an answer of its own for weeks. On 31 May a [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom of Foxes]] officer told the leadership channel that she had obtained the official interpretation of the rule as it then stood, and had not raised it because pressure to change the rule had been building both to and from the moderators.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|31 May 2018, the interpretation}} As the argument grew, she said, a senior member of the game's staff had had to step in and say that feeding was officially not a problem and not something to regulate.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|31 May 2018, the ruling reported}} The member who had written the public post answered that he had not known it existed, and asked why nobody had raised it at any point in the preceding week.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|31 May 2018, the reply}}

Its leaders then stopped using the word. Later that day the officer said she disliked the term because as far as she was concerned it described nothing that existed.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|31 May 2018, the term}} The founder agreed, and said that was why he always set it in quotation marks; the author of the public post said he had been doing the same.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|31 May 2018, the quotation marks}}

## Why the ground moved downward

The reason the alliance moved territory between its members was stated plainly a month later. On 30 June 2018, after another guild had been admitted and a member complained that his own had gained barely anything in the last three map updates, another member answered that with each new guild admitted the alliance found it steadily harder to raise any one guild's holdings.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|30 Jun 2018, the difficulty}} The founder said Kingdom of Foxes had had no increase across those updates either, and had handed roughly nine of its territories to other member guilds so that a guild smaller than his own might hold a few.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|30 Jun 2018, the nine territories}} The small guilds expanding mattered more, he said, than his own picking up a little extra experience.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|30 Jun 2018, the reason given}} Another member observed that if every guild kept increasing, one guild would eventually hold the whole map.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|30 Jun 2018, the whole map}}

Those figures cannot be checked against the maps in question. The last two allocations the alliance published in full gave Kingdom of Foxes twenty-nine territories on 10 May 2018 and thirty-nine on 4 June, a rise of ten.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|5/10/2018 and 6/4/2018}} The four allocations issued between 4 June and the conversation went out as images without counts, so what they assigned to any guild went unwritten.{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|6/16, 6/21, 6/25 and 6/30/2018}}

## The word turned outward

By August the alliance was using the term against its enemies. On 6 August 2018 members reported in the war channel that [[hackforums|HackForums]] had gained more than twenty territories in a short period by what they called sniping or feeding, and that other guilds were letting it take ground from them.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|6 Aug 2018, the reports}} The opposing alliance's leader had said his side would play the same game.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|6 Aug 2018, the enemy leader}} A general call went out in the channel to break it up, and within about forty minutes members were reporting the guild back down to single figures.{{cite:federation-feeding-controversy-2018-discord-private|6 Aug 2018, the response}}

The capture log carries the same three hours. Between 18:00 and 21:00 UTC on 6 August HackForums ran twenty-three territories ahead of where it began, peaking at 19:27, and finished the window level.{{cite:territory-exchanges|HackForums captures and losses, 6 Aug 2018 18:00–21:00 UTC, free-for-all list of 6 Aug 2018 excluded: peak net +23 at 19:27, net 0 at 21:00}} Eleven Federation guilds took ground from it inside those three hours.{{cite:territory-exchanges|guilds capturing from HackForums, 6 Aug 2018 18:00–21:00 UTC, free-for-all list of 6 Aug 2018 excluded: 11 distinct attackers}} The messages are stamped on the alliance's own clock, seven hours behind the log's.

## The practice conceded

A month after that the alliance made the practice a term of a treaty. On 8 September 2018 its founder put to the guild leaders an arrangement under which [[titans-valor|Titans Valor]] would attack the Confederation while Federation guilds left its territories alone, except when reclaiming their own assigned ground.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the motion}} A leader objected in the leadership channel that the alliance ought not to be actively encouraging the handing over of territory.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the objection}}

The founder did not defend the distinction. He answered that the alliance did it constantly, asked whether anybody was still pretending otherwise, and said it was entirely within the rules.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the answer}} The objector tried to separate the cases — that the outside guild would exist for no other purpose, where no guild inside the alliance did — and then accepted the point.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the distinction}} A second leader said he would vote against unless the guild stopped taking Federation ground, and was told that stopping it was what the treaty was for.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the condition}} The motion carried by eleven votes to three.{{cite:federation-titans-valor-treaty-september-2018-discord-private|8 Sep 2018, the tally recorded on the motion}}

The treaty was announced to the alliance's war role the next afternoon: "A treaty has been established with Titans Valor [ANO]. They will only attack Confed and help us regain our territories."{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/9/2018 1:36 PM}} Thirteen minutes later the weekly bulletin added a term the announcement had left out — "don't attack them unless you're reclaiming your assigneds or they have ffa."{{cite:federation-alliance-announcements|Drew1011, 9/9/2018 1:49 PM}} Setting the answer of 8 September beside the denial of 26 May and the quotation marks of 31 May is this article's; nobody in the leadership channel referred back to either.

## The instruments and what they met

[[terra|Terra]] sorted every guild it knew of into five classes — allies, the neutral canyon grouping, distant allies, plain neutrals, and enemies with their subguilds — and it had a voting procedure for admissions and a form on which member guilds registered the areas they wanted.{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|27 Sep 2019}}{{cite:terra-institutions-2019-discord-private|September 2019}} All of it assumed that the thing being classified was a guild with a leadership that could be spoken to.

The first of the new guilds was not new. Three days after the raid the ambassador reported Luminosity moving in several places at once and identified it as the guild a chief of the defeated [[buildcraftia|BuildCraftia]] had split off to found.{{cite:the-map-refilled-2019-discord-private|war channel, 10 Sep 2019}} It had taken its first territory on 2 September, five days before the raid, and it fought through the raid itself, taking thirty-five on the 7th.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Luminosity captures by day, 1–20 Sep 2019, UTC; no free-for-all list was published for the window, so nothing is netted out}} Whether it was aligned with anyone was never established.

## Reconstitution

FinalFront left Terra on or before 28 September 2019 and was entered among the neutrals.{{cite:the-map-refilled-2019-discord-private|late Sep 2019}} Its last capture fell on 28 September, a week after a day of sixty.{{cite:territory-exchanges|FinalFront captures by day, 15 Sep – 15 Nov 2019, UTC}} Two days after it stopped, a guild called Solis Crudelia took its first territory.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Solis Crudelia's first capture, 30 Sep 2019, UTC}}

Terra classified it before it could identify it. Asked in the war channel on 8 October who Solis Crudelia were, the ambassador answered that this was the new guild of FinalFront's former leader, that it had no real alignment, and that they had wanted more freedom and to be clear of what remained of the old guild.{{cite:the-map-refilled-2019-discord-private|war channel, 8 Oct 2019}} Asked whether Terra therefore counted them neutral, he said yes so long as they did not trouble the alliance too much, and that they had already been entered as neutral on the guild list about a week earlier, when the guild first appeared.{{cite:the-map-refilled-2019-discord-private|war channel, 8 Oct 2019}} It took 1,399 territories in the three months to the end of the year.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Solis Crudelia captures by month, Sep–Dec 2019, UTC; the eighteen free-for-all territories declared 14 Sep 2019 are not netted out}}

## Names out of the previous era

On 20 September a member of the war channel asked outright who was using HHU, and the answers came over two nights as recollection. One member said it was a fairly old guild and that a friend of one of Terra's leaders had run it; the ambassador said he did not know who they were and had last seen the tag months earlier; [[thenolifes|TheNoLifes]]' leader said it was a very old guild that had belonged to [[valkyrie|Valkyrie]] in the period when [[holders-of-le|Holders of LE]] and [[house-of-sentinels|House of Sentinels]] were members of it.{{cite:the-map-refilled-2019-discord-private|war channel, 20 and 21 Sep 2019}} An alliance three weeks old was reconstructing its own prehistory in a war channel because a name had come back onto the map.

Late on the 21st, on the export's clock of UTC-07:00, a member reported that HHU had been wiped by [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] and a subguild.{{cite:the-map-refilled-2019-discord-private|war channel, 21 Sep 2019}} Eight guilds took ground from HaHaUnited between the 20th and the 26th, [[the-simple-ones|The Simple Ones]] thirty-two of it and Kingdom Foxes thirteen; the guild's own busiest day of the year, fifty captures, fell on the 22nd.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures by and against HaHaUnited, 20–26 Sep 2019, UTC; the eighteen free-for-all territories declared 14 Sep 2019 are not netted out}}

Two names from before [[the-federation|the Federation]]'s fall came back to the map in the same month. House of Sentinels, which had left its last alliance in March 2018 and gone neutral, took 152 territories in October 2019 and 87 in November.{{cite:territory-exchanges|House of Sentinels captures by month, Jan 2018 – Dec 2020, UTC; the eighteen free-for-all territories declared 14 Sep 2019 are not netted out}} [[nethers-ascent|Nether's Ascent]], which had left the Federation in October 2018 and taken nothing at all between February and September 2019, took 816 territories in October, 1,646 in November and 1,016 in December — 2,462 of them spread across 266 different territories in the two autumn months alone.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Nethers Ascent captures by month, and distinct territories Oct–Nov 2019, UTC; the eighteen free-for-all territories declared 14 Sep 2019 are not netted out}} Neither guild held a single territory at the end of either month.{{cite:territory-exchanges|holdings reconstructed from the last exchange on each territory, 31 Oct and 30 Nov 2019, UTC}}

## The grouping that changed sides

On 16 October MasterWynnLords reported to Terra's leadership that The Simple Ones' holdings were now Nether's Ascent's, and the ambassador answered that The Simple Ones had been broken by a new grouping of three guilds, naming Nether's Ascent, [[blacklisted|Blacklisted]] and one other.{{cite:the-map-refilled-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 16 Oct 2019}} The Simple Ones had left Terra for neutrality on 21 September.{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 21 Sep 2019}} Nether's Ascent took 341 territories from it that October and Blacklisted 149, the two largest takers of its ground.{{cite:territory-exchanges|captures with The Simple Ones as defender, by attacker, Oct 2019, UTC; the eighteen free-for-all territories declared 14 Sep 2019 are not netted out}} Three weeks later the same grouping, with one further party, was reported to Terra's war advisory as fighting on Terra's side.{{cite:terra-november-collapse-2019-discord-private|war advisory, 2 Nov 2019}}

## Ground without defenders

Terra ended September holding more of the map than its members could stand on. During the debate on the map revision on 29 September the discord-management lead told the voting-discussion channel that the neutral canyon bloc had left a third of the map without a sea border{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|voting-discussion channel, 29 Sep 2019, the sea-border estimate}}, and that the alliance had too few territories to give out against too many guilds needing upgrading.{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|voting-discussion channel, 29 Sep 2019, territories against guilds}} The fraction was his own estimate. The bloc had formed in the open a week earlier: on 21 September [[the-simple-ones|The Simple Ones]]' leader announced in the war channel that his guild and several others he named would declare neutrality{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|war channel, 21 Sep 2019, the neutrality announcement}}, that nobody in his guild was willing to war, and that he would take in members of allied guilds locked out of warring by a game fault.{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|war channel, 21 Sep 2019, the offer to house locked-out members}}

Four answers were tried. The first was to take ground back from the guilds that had gone. On 24 September the ambassador asked the leadership channel what was to be done about the canyon bloc drawing land away, said [[iceblue-team|IceBlue Team]] had just joined it, and suggested that guilds deserving expansion might take a couple of bites out of it without costing it much.{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 24 Sep 2019}} Two days later, when [[hackforums|HackForums]]' leader made the same argument in public, the ambassador opposed it{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|war channel, 26 Sep 2019}}, and a member of the general channel answered on arithmetic: with [[paladins-united|Paladins United]] and [[blacklisted|Blacklisted]] still attacking, a war with the canyon guilds as well would cripple the alliance.{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|alliance-general channel, 26 Sep 2019}} Others warned that [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]] would be a serious problem if attacked, and that any reduction should be negotiated with the guilds concerned first.{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|alliance-general channel, 26 Sep 2019, the warnings against acting}} Nothing was taken. Reading the two days against each other is this article's.

The second was to open ground nobody would defend. Between 25 and 26 September ThisIsMurica's leader asked in the advisors' channel whether MasterWynnLords meant to enforce its claim on Legendary Island, and offered to hold it himself to keep a neutral guild out{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|advisors' channel, 25-26 Sep 2019, the offer to hold the territory}}; MasterWynnLords' leader answered that he did not mind who held it, since it generated no experience and only cost his guild bank to defend.{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|advisors' channel, 25-26 Sep 2019, the owner's answer}} The ambassador said the alliance might as well turn it into a free-for-all, unless somebody moved into that region at the next map change and felt like defending it.{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|advisors' channel, 26 Sep 2019}} A leader in the same conversation said [[thenolifes|TheNoLifes]] had joined [[the-federation|the Federation]] on the condition of receiving that territory.{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|advisors' channel, 26 Sep 2019, the Federation condition}}

The instrument was proposed again as a penalty. On 2 October, the day after TheNoLifes entered hibernation to wait for a game update, a member of the advisors' channel said nobody was to touch its claim, and the ambassador answered that it should be opened as a free-for-all with the ground reserved for the guild's return.{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|advisors' channel, 2 Oct 2019}} Later that morning he said it was not fair for a guild idle pending an update to hold the Mirror Cave territory for months without helping against the enemy alliance, and that Terra criticised the canyon bloc for exactly that kind of sitting on land.{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|advisors' channel, 2 Oct 2019, the fairness argument}} The alliance amended its free-for-all declaration eight days later; whether that territory entered the pool is not recoverable here, the names of the amendments being held outside this repository.{{cite:terra-alliance-announcements-discord-private|the declaration of 14 Sep 2019 and its amendments, 30 Sep - 11 Dec 2019}}

The third was redistribution by decision. On 28 October the ambassador announced in the leadership channel, and pinned, that after discussion between the managers and certain guild leaders the six swamp areas released by [[the-mage-legacy|Mage Legacy]] would go back to [[emorians|Emorians]] and HackForums{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 28 Oct 2019, the swamp areas}}, and the guild's six remaining eastern claims to [[shadowfall|ShadowFall]], as a compromise satisfying all three.{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 28 Oct 2019, the eastern claims}} There was no vote, in an alliance that had put two territory maps to its membership the month before. ShadowFall held fifteen territories on 28 October and twenty-three on the 31st.{{cite:territory-exchanges|ShadowFall holdings from the last exchange on each territory at 00:00 on 28 and 31 Oct 2019, UTC; holdings include the free-for-all ground declared 14 Sep 2019 as amended, whose names are held outside this repository}}

The fourth was a tenancy. On 30 October the ambassador told the leadership channel that Wrath of Poseidon's central Corkus claims passed to HackForums with immediate effect, because a strong high-level guild holding ground was better than ground defended by fillers, and that HackForums was holding a third member's ravine claims for the same reason{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 17:13 on 30 Oct 2019}}; two ocean territories came across from Spectral Cabbage as part of the arrangement.{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 30 Oct 2019, the Spectral Cabbage ocean territories}} He called the ocean arrangement a stronghold occupation and said the guild was struggling badly{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 18:55 on 30 Oct 2019}}, and stated that the occupation was not permanent and that the ground would be returned on that guild's request once it had rebuilt.{{cite:terra-undefended-land-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 19:12 on 30 Oct 2019}} Possession moved and title did not.

Both guilds whose ground was disposed of had already lost it. Wrath of Poseidon held twenty-four territories through 22 October, one on the 24th and none from the 25th, five days before its Corkus claims were transferred{{cite:territory-exchanges|Wrath Of Poseidon holdings from the last exchange on each territory at 00:00 on 22, 24, 25 and 30 Oct 2019, UTC; holdings include the free-for-all ground declared 14 Sep 2019 as amended, whose names are held outside this repository}}; Mage Legacy held thirteen on the 22nd and none from the 25th, three days before its claims were divided.{{cite:territory-exchanges|The Mage Legacy holdings, same reconstruction at 00:00 on 22, 25 and 28 Oct 2019, UTC; same caveat on free-for-all ground}} Reading the announcements against the holdings is this article's.

## A tag that was one man

On 5 October a member of the war channel asked who Hyp were and why they were permitted to hold ThisIsMurica's Corkus claim while attacking another guild, and said he was confused; another asked for help clearing them out of Corkus.{{cite:the-map-refilled-2019-discord-private|war channel, 5 Oct 2019}} The ambassador answered with a warning rather than an identification. A rogue member of [[lunatic|Lunatic]], earlier inside [[imperial|Imperial]] attacking Terra, was now in the subguild Hyperion Faction and using it against the neutral canyon guilds, and anyone who hit Hyperion Faction might draw retaliation for it.{{cite:the-map-refilled-2019-discord-private|war channel, 5 Oct 2019}} He said he did not understand why the subguild's owner had admitted the man.{{cite:the-map-refilled-2019-discord-private|war channel, 5 Oct 2019}} A member answered that Hyperion Faction was holding their land and asked what else they were supposed to do.{{cite:the-map-refilled-2019-discord-private|war channel, 5 Oct 2019}} The guild took thirty-six territories that month, all of them between the 1st and the 7th, and none again until January.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Hyperion Faction captures by day, Oct 2019 – Jan 2020, UTC; the eighteen free-for-all territories declared 14 Sep 2019 are not netted out}}

## The last application

The last guild to seek admission asked for no territory. On 31 October the voting-discussion channel took up an application from Merashold; asked whether they wanted a claim, a leader answered that she did not believe so, that the guild was not strong yet and that she was working with them to build it up, and another leader said that if they were not asking for territory he had no objection.{{cite:the-map-refilled-2019-discord-private|voting channel, 31 Oct 2019}} [[avicia|Avicia]]'s founder reported that Merashold had originally intended to fight Avicia for Terra's claim in Aldorei and had stopped only after Terra spoke to them, and judged them reasonable and capable enough.{{cite:the-map-refilled-2019-discord-private|voting channel, 31 Oct 2019}} Whether it was admitted went unrecorded.

The guild was not new and did not stay small. Merashold had been taking territory since September 2018, and November 2019 was its busiest month on the map, at 169 captures.{{cite:territory-exchanges|Merashold captures by month, Sep 2018 – Mar 2020, UTC; the eighteen free-for-all territories declared 14 Sep 2019 are not netted out}} Two days after the application was discussed, Terra was attacked and came apart ([[terra-falls|Terra falls]]).

## What was on the map at the end

On 2 December the alliance's discord-management lead reported that TheNoLifes was returning from the hibernation it had entered on 2 October, its two most active members with it, and the channel concluded that the guild The Tempest had therefore proved short-lived.{{cite:the-map-refilled-2019-discord-private|2 Dec 2019}}{{cite:terra-october-2019-discord-private|leadership channel, 2 Oct 2019}} The Tempest held forty-three territories the day before that exchange, third on the map behind [[imperial|Imperial]] and [[kingdom-foxes|Kingdom Foxes]], and went on taking ground until December 2020.{{cite:territory-exchanges|holdings at 1 Dec 2019 and The Tempest captures by month, Nov 2019 – Dec 2020, UTC}}

Terra was dissolved on 30 December 2019.{{cite:terra-alliance-2019-discord-private|30 Dec 2019}} Imperial had held three territories on 1 November; it held fifty-six at the end of that month and fifty-one on 30 December, more of the map than any other guild on both days.{{cite:territory-exchanges|holdings reconstructed from the last exchange on each territory at 1 Nov, 30 Nov and 30 Dec 2019, UTC}} By the end of January 2020 Kingdom Foxes led with sixty-eight and Imperial was second with fifty-six.{{cite:territory-exchanges|same reconstruction at 31 Jan 2020, UTC}}

[[tapu-fini|Tapu_Fini]] opened the alliance's thread on 26 June 2016{{cite:forums-wynncraft-com-forums-guilds-72-prefix-id-82-order-post-date-direction-asc|thread listing: The Separatists, Tapu_Fini, 26 Jun 2016}}, three weeks after [[the-banhammer|The Banhammer]] — the second of the two anti-[[hackforums|HackForums]] blocs of [[early-guild-era|the early guild era]] to open that month.

## History

Its opening post gave the earliest written description of the bloc that held the territory map, framing it as a duumvirate: "a deadly enemy rose from the defeated corpse of [[ordo-malleus|Ordo Malleus]]. This enemy, known only has 'Hack Forums' quickly established their rule through military might. Young aspiring guilds were crushed under the might of the tryhards... Soon after, another guild, [[libertas|Libertas]], joined the surge... Many distinguished scholars compare this rule of two to the ancient guilds of 'The Nether's Ascent' and 'Kangronomicon'."{{cite:thread-133666|post #1, 26 Jun 2016}}

Enrolment was deliberately loose. A player could join The Anarchy directly "regardless of level or class", or "pledge your guild to The Separatists, and you way withdraw that allegiance whenever you feel the need. No guild is too small or too low level, heck, we are level 13! Strength in numbers!"{{cite:thread-133666|post #1, 26 Jun 2016}} The Anarchy is described as the founding guild, a "Combat Guild" led by supermariowifi with the chiefs comrade_doge and superACE.{{cite:thread-133666|post #1, 26 Jun 2016}} The post closed with a disclaimer: "this is all for fun, no real hate to the Hack Forums and Libertas players, just to their 'Own everything' policy."{{cite:thread-133666|post #1, 26 Jun 2016}}

The reception was uniformly sceptical. WhettoWen of UltimateXeons pointed to earlier attempts — "To make a long story short: It didn't work, all those guilds no longer have any territory"{{cite:thread-133666|post #10, 27 Jun 2016}} — and a fellow member, valvoid, spelled out his own guild's position: "our alliance with Hax and Lir (we are UXs) is gonna let the three guilds keep their territory (we are like the only three guilds with any real territory) without anybody really taking it unless one of the guilds leaves or breaks the alliance."{{cite:thread-133666|post #13, 27 Jun 2016}} qqcora corrected the terminology — "ur alliance with hax and lir is a mutual land agreement" — and directed readers to the Astrafterí Affinity thread as "the actual alliance".{{cite:thread-133666|post #16, 28 Jun 2016}} [[drew1011|Drew1011]] wrote of HackForums' readmission to [[ordo-malleus|Ordo Malleus]]: "Hax was one of the original guilds, although we kicked them out, and then for some reason, let them back in."{{cite:thread-133666|post #6, 27 Jun 2016}}

The founder's own expectations were modest: "Luck will definitely be needed to take down hax lol"{{cite:thread-133666|post #4, 26 Jun 2016}} and "even if we don't beat them, at least we can be an annoyance."{{cite:thread-133666|post #11, 27 Jun 2016}} Challenged on 28 June to say whether the alliance had ever attacked the bloc, Tapu_Fini answered: "I mean lir and hax. And we have. Usually around Ragni. Katoa ranch we tend to attack a lot."{{cite:thread-133666-p02|post #23, 28 Jun 2016}} Tomix, replying for the bloc, disputed it — "I've never seen your guild try to take territory from us,weird heh"{{cite:thread-133666-p02|post #22, 28 Jun 2016}} — and WhettoWen's comment on the state of affairs was "it should stay that way", which Tapu_Fini accepted: "Fine by me. You guys seem nice enough anyways."{{cite:thread-133666-p02|posts #21 and #23, 28 Jun 2016}}

## Membership

No guild other than The Anarchy was ever named as a member, and none was recorded as pledging.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}

## Legacy

No dissolution or outcome was ever recorded; the alliance was last mentioned on 1 July 2016.{{cite:thread-133666-p02|post #36, 1 Jul 2016}} It was one of the two blocs behind the [[hackforums|HackForums]] chief Mrussell75's remark on 30 June 2016 that "right now there is 2 anti hax alliances", the other being [[the-banhammer|The Banhammer]].{{cite:thread-134638|post #13, 30 Jun 2016}} Neither bloc changed the map: in September 2016 HackForums still held 146 of roughly 332 territories.{{cite:pre2018-territory-snapshots|pre2018-territory-snapshots.json — Wayback Machine capture of the Wynncraft v1 API guild leaderboard, 23 Sep 2016}}

The Simple Ones [ILQ] was created on 5 Feb 2019 by Goatylicious, Saethar, GrandAdmiralAce_ and _Infected_gaming, "as a way to find more people to play and have fun with"{{cite:thread-255067|post #1 (OP as last edited 16 Sep 2020)}}{{cite:wynncraft-api|Wynncraft API v3/guild, retrieved 2 Sep 2026}} — nine days before [[Luna]] formed. It joined Luna around the alliance's founding, though only its departure was documented.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}

## History

### Founding and the Aetherius merger (2019)

Three days after it was founded, on 8 February 2019, the guild merged with XeraAndromeda's [Aeu] The Aetherius{{cite:thread-255067|post #1 (OP as last edited 16 Sep 2020)}}. XeraAndromeda held the guild thereafter: the thread's roster of September 2020 gives her as owner under the title "Empress", GrandAdmiralAce_ as "Crown Prince" and chief, and four more officers as a "Parliament" of barons and baronesses{{cite:thread-255067|post #1 (OP as last edited 16 Sep 2020)}}. The same roster claims membership of a "Wynnic Union community alliance"{{cite:thread-255067|post #1 (OP as last edited 16 Sep 2020); the alliance appears in no other archived source}}.

### The defection that decided a war (2019)

The Simple Ones signed the Treaty of Ternaves on 6 Jul 2019 with [[Kingdom Foxes]] and ThisIsMurica, splitting the territory of the dissolved Celestial Tigers.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} Two months later came its decisive act in [[the-alliance-wars-of-2019|the alliance wars of 2019]]: on 8 Sep 2019, mid-war, it defected from Luna to [[Terra]] — and Terra took control of the map the same day.{{cite:thread-265297|post #1, the community guild-timeline image (imgur seklYAQ)}} It then co-founded the [[Council of Canyon Kingdoms]] on 15 Sep 2019, staying to its 23 Nov collapse, and co-founded [[Horologium]] on 1 Dec 2019, which died with [[DiamondDeities]]' disbandment on 8 Jan 2020.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}

### Divisions and the Admiralty

The guild ran a second, separate application thread from May 2019, which describes it in September that year as a level 48 guild "part of the Terra alliance", requiring applicants to hold a level 80 account and join the Discord and to be willing to fight in wars; Goatylicious was named as the officer reviewing applications, and results were given by Discord rather than on the thread.{{cite:thread-250066|post #1, opening post as last edited 14 Sep 2019}}

Its main thread shows the guild organised into named divisions that applicants chose between and could join more than one of — a Royal Fleet for warring, an Adveneria and a Citizenry.{{cite:thread-255067-p02|p2 #22–26, Oct 2019}}{{cite:thread-255067-p04|p4 #70, 19 Dec 2019}} Admissions were announced in the language of a state: "The Admiralty has granted you a Trial Citizenship in our guild", with a trial period of about a week before full membership.{{cite:thread-255067-p02|p2 #35, TheOtherJo, 9 Nov 2019}}{{cite:thread-255067-p03|p3 #41, TheOtherJo, 16 Nov 2019}} The imperial styling was already in use in November 2019, nine months before the Empire of Nemract was formed{{cite:thread-255067-p02|p2 #38, TheOtherJo, 15 Nov 2019}}, and the guild's officers carried titles of their own, one chief styling himself of the "ILQ Boat Club" and "ILQ Snake Pit".{{cite:thread-255067-p02|p2 #31, H0nour, 5 Nov 2019}} By May 2020 XeraAndromeda was processing the applications herself.{{cite:thread-255067-p08|p8 #147–154, May 2020}}

### The Empire of Nemract (2020-2021)

The guild negotiated its way out of [[terra|Terra]] in September 2019 rather than being expelled from it. Its leader asked on the 19th only that the guild be allowed to exist, offered to return a named territory and to make no war, and was told it might exist with a reduced land count and that Terra would take its lands if it could not hold them.{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|19 Sep 2019}} It left on the evening of the 21st, a day early and on warm terms; two minutes afterwards a Terra leader said it was time to eat ILQ.{{cite:terra-mid-september-2019-discord-private|21 Sep 2019}}

On 17 Aug 2020 The Simple Ones formed the [[Empire of Nemract]], a political union with Mystic Woods later branded 'The Empire of Nemract | ILQ + Aeu', whose empress held court titles ('Empress of Nemract, Queen of Aphelion').{{cite:thread-278123|Wynncraft forums thread 278123, Empire of Nemract branding}} The union was last mentioned in August 2021; no war between the two guilds was ever recorded, and the union may have persisted quietly. They kept taking ground from one another all the same, twenty-eight times between the union's declaration and June 2021.{{cite:territory-exchanges|exchanges with The Simple Ones and Mystic Woods on opposite sides, by month, 17 Aug 2020 – 30 Jun 2021 UTC, raw: 28}} On 21 May 2021 ILQ signed the Treaty of Damask, under which Nefarious Ravens declared independence.{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}}

### The blocs (2021-2023)

The Simple Ones was a founding member of [[Valhalla]] on 11 May 2021 and followed it into [[Vanir]].{{cite:chronicle-records|Chronicle alliance and event records}} At the start of Season 8 (autumn 2022) it joined the renamed 'Smtn else' — later [[smtn elf]] — remaining until the alliance's quiet end in mid-2023.{{cite:titantimes-github-io-vol6|vol. 6, 12 Oct 2022}}

## Alliances

- [[Luna]] — c. Feb 2019 – 8 Sep 2019 (defected)
- [[Terra]] — 8 Sep 2019 – 15 Sep 2019
- [[Council of Canyon Kingdoms]] — 15 Sep 2019 – 23 Nov 2019 (co-founder)
- [[Horologium]] — 1 Dec 2019 – 8 Jan 2020 (co-founder)
- [[Empire of Nemract]] — 17 Aug 2020 – at least Aug 2021 (political union){{cite:thread-278123|Wynncraft forums thread 278123, Empire of Nemract branding}}
- [[Valhalla]] — 11 May 2021 – 28 Nov 2021 (founding member)
